window.GIQ={"meta":{"tier":1,"lede":"","top":75,"fold":0,"nonotes":1},"lectures":[{"id":"HIST-11","t":"Digestive tract (H15A, H15B)"},{"id":"HIST-12","t":"Glands of the digestive system (H16)"}],"groups":[{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Lamina propria carries blood and lymph vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: lamina propria is loose connective tissue with blood and lymph vessels","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Mucosa labelled: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosa above the submucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6","lg":[["GC","goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["IGl","intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn)"],["red bands","muscularis mucosae, colored in"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"],["BGl","Brunner (duodenal) glands in the submucosa: pale mucous glands, duodenum only"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-54-1","s":"Which of these layers of the digestive system contains blood and lymphatic vessels?","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Muscularis mucosa","C. Muscularis externa","D. Adventitia","E. Epithelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lamina propria","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lamina propria.</b> The lecture's layer table describes the lamina propria as loose connective tissue holding <b>blood and lymphatic vessels</b> (in the small intestine, the lacteals of the villi). The submucosa also has large vessels, but it is not a choice.<br>B. Muscularis mucosae: thin smooth muscle that moves the mucosa.<br>C. Muscularis externa: thick muscle coat for peristalsis.<br>D. Adventitia: outer connective tissue binding the organ to its neighbors.<br>E. Epithelium: has no blood vessels; it absorbs and secretes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":54,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":115,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Lamina propria is part of the mucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Mucosa = epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosa, labelled over submucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6","lg":[["GC","goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["IGl","intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn)"],["red bands","muscularis mucosae, colored in"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"],["BGl","Brunner (duodenal) glands in the submucosa: pale mucous glands, duodenum only"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-4.jpg","cap":"Layer diagram: mucosa includes lining epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-9-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman presents with recurrent diarrhea, fatigue, and weight loss. A biopsy of her small intestine reveals immune cell infiltration and inflammation in the lamina propria. This inflammation primarily affects which of the following layers of the GI tract?","o":["A. Submucosa","B. Serosa","C. Mucosa","D. Muscularis externa"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucosa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Mucosa.</b> The mucosa has three parts: the <b>epithelium, the lamina propria</b> and the muscularis mucosae. So inflammation in the lamina propria is inflammation of the mucosa.<br>A. Submucosa: connective tissue below the muscularis mucosae, outside the mucosa.<br>B. Serosa: outermost covering with mesothelium.<br>D. Muscularis externa: the thick inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":9,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 2"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Adventitia: esophageal outer coat, no mesothelium","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-11.jpg","cap":"Adventitia: thick connective tissue, no mesothelium; serosa: thin, mesothelium-covered","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section: adventitia outside; only distal one to two centimeters has serosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-5-1","s":"A 54-year-old man presents with dysphagia and unintentional weight loss. An upper endoscopy reveals a mass in the esophagus. Biopsy of the affected tissue shows no serosa and a thick layer of connective tissue directly connecting the esophagus to adjacent structures. Based on the GI tract's histological organization, which of the following best describes the outermost layer of this patient's esophagus?","o":["A. Adventitia","B. Serosa","C. Muscularis mucosae","D. Submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Adventitia","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Adventitia.</b> The biopsy gives two clues: there is no serosa, and a thick layer of connective tissue ties the esophagus directly to the structures around it. That is what an <b>adventitia</b> is. The esophagus runs through the neck and chest, where it is bound to its neighbors and does not hang free in a body cavity. So its outer coat has <b>no mesothelium</b>. Only its last 1 to 2 cm, below the diaphragm, lies in the peritoneal cavity and has a serosa.</p><p><b>Serosa and adventitia compared.</b></p><ul><li><b>Serosa:</b> thin loose connective tissue covered by <b>mesothelium</b>, a simple squamous epithelium (one layer of flat cells). It gives the organ a smooth, finished edge. It covers organs that hang free in the peritoneal cavity: the stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse colon and sigmoid colon.</li><li><b>Adventitia:</b> thicker loose connective tissue with vessels, nerves and fat, and no mesothelium. It has no finished edge and blends into the tissue next to it. It covers organs bound directly to their neighbors: most of the esophagus, and the parts of the gut behind the peritoneum, such as parts 2 to 4 of the duodenum.</li></ul><p>A <b>mesentery</b> is a sheet of connective tissue with mesothelium on both sides. It holds the loops of gut, carries their vessels and is continuous with the serosa.</p><p><b>The four layers of the digestive tube, from the lumen outward.</b></p><ol><li><b>Mucosa.</b> It has three parts. The epithelium faces the lumen, the open space inside the tube. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with small blood and lymph vessels and many immune cells. In a villus of the small intestine, the central lymph vessel is called a lacteal. The muscularis mucosae is a thin sheet of smooth muscle that moves the mucosa on its own.</li><li><b>Submucosa.</b> Connective tissue with the larger vessels and the submucosal (Meissner) nerve plexus. It holds glands in only two places: the esophagus and the duodenum.</li><li><b>Muscularis externa.</b> Usually two layers of smooth muscle, inner circular and outer longitudinal, with the myenteric (Auerbach) nerve plexus between them. The stomach adds a third, oblique layer. The upper esophagus has skeletal muscle.</li><li><b>Serosa or adventitia.</b> The outer coat.</li></ol><p><b>The epithelium is the layer that changes most.</b> It is stratified squamous where rough food rubs: the oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus and anal canal. It is simple columnar where the tube secretes and absorbs: the stomach, small intestine and large intestine.</p><p><b>Where the layers come from in the embryo.</b> Endoderm gives the epithelium of the gut tube and of the glands that grow from it, the liver and pancreas. Mesoderm gives the connective tissue, smooth muscle, blood vessels and mesentery. Ectoderm gives the lining of the mouth and the salivary glands. The nerve cells of the gut wall come from the neural crest.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 10 (Terminology)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":5,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Epithelium varies most along the tract","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-5.jpg","cap":"Stratified squamous mucosa lines mouth, oropharynx, esophagus and anal canal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Simple columnar mucosa lines stomach, small and large intestine","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6","lg":[["GC","goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["IGl","intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn)"],["red bands","muscularis mucosae, colored in"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"],["BGl","Brunner (duodenal) glands in the submucosa: pale mucous glands, duodenum only"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-47-1","s":"The GI tract is compartmentalized into organs that are specialized for digestion of food and absorption of nutrients. Most variation and specialization along the length of the GI tract occur in which of the following tissue layers?","o":["A. Epithelium of mucosa","B. Lamina propria","C. Muscularis externa","D. Muscularis mucosae","E. Submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epithelium of mucosa","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Epithelium of the mucosa.</b> The lining changes most along the tract: <b>stratified squamous</b> in the mouth, esophagus and anal canal; <b>simple columnar</b> with different cell types in the stomach, small intestine and colon. Each organ's special job comes from its epithelium.<br>B. Lamina propria: similar loose connective tissue throughout.<br>C. Muscularis externa: two layers almost everywhere (three in the stomach).<br>D. Muscularis mucosae: thin and similar.<br>E. Submucosa: similar; glands only in the esophagus and duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 40 (Q- The GI tract is compartmentalized into organs that are specialized for digest)","H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":47,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":211,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 3"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Myenteric plexus: muscularis externa, controls motility","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-6.jpg","cap":"Myenteric plexus between inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: myenteric (Auerbach) plexus inside the muscle coat","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-144-1","s":"The Myenteric (Auerbach’s) plexus is located in which layer of the digestive tract?","o":["A. Mucosa","B. Submucosa","C. Muscularis externa","D. Adventitia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Muscularis externa.</b> The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies <b>between the inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layers</b> of the muscularis externa, where it drives peristalsis.<br>A. Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosae; no main nerve plexus.<br>B. Submucosa: holds the other plexus, the submucosal (Meissner) plexus.<br>D. Adventitia: outer connective tissue coat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":144,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 4"},{"id":"F3952-77-1","s":"A 62-year-old woman presents with complaints of persistent abdominal pain and irregular bowel movements. During your examination, you explain the structure of the gastrointestinal tract and how various layers contribute to its function, particularly emphasizing the role of the myenteric plexus in regulating gut motility. You discuss how the layer of the GI wall containing the autonomic neurons of the myenteric plexus is predominantly composed of a specific tissue type that facilitates these neural functions. In the wall of the gastrointestinal tract, the layer containing the autonomic neurons of the myenteric plexus is characterized by the abundance of what tissue?","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Epithelia of different types in different GI regions","C. Smooth muscle","D. Dense regular connective tissue","E. Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Smooth muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Smooth muscle.</b> The myenteric plexus sits inside the <b>muscularis externa</b>, between its inner circular and outer longitudinal layers, and that layer is smooth muscle (except in the upper esophagus).<br>A. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue of the mucosa.<br>B. Epithelia that differ by region: the lining layer of the mucosa.<br>D. Dense regular connective tissue: tendons and ligaments, not the gut wall.<br>E. Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT): immune cells in the lamina propria and submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 6 (100x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":77,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 5"},{"id":"F3950-7-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman complains of intermittent abdominal pain and bloating. She is diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). During a colonoscopy, the mucosa appears normal. The patient's symptoms are attributed to uncoordinated contractions of smooth muscle. Which structure is most likely involved in coordinating the abnormal contractions in this patient?","o":["A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","B. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","C. Muscularis mucosae","D. Serosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.</b> This nerve network lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and <b>coordinates gut motility</b> (peristalsis). Faulty signaling here gives uncoordinated contractions while the mucosa looks normal.<br>B. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus: mainly controls secretion and local blood flow.<br>C. Muscularis mucosae: thin muscle that moves the mucosa; not a nerve center.<br>D. Serosa: outer covering with mesothelium; no motor control.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":7,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 6"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Meissner plexus lies in the submucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) nerve plexus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-40-1","s":"While working as a pathology resident, you and your colleague come across the following slide of the colon. You note some unexpected tissue (white arrow) that it appears to be neural tissue, and your colleague believes it to be pathological, but you know that it is actually...","o":["A. Myenteric plexus - found in between the inner and outer layers of the muscularis externa","B. Meissner's plexus - found in the submucosa","C. Auerbach's plexus - found in the subepithelium","D. Peyer's patches - found in the submucosa"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Meissner's plexus - found in the submucosa","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Meissner's plexus - found in the submucosa.</b> The upper right of the picture is colon mucosa: straight crypts full of pale goblet cells. The white arrow points to a small, pale cluster of large cells with round nuclei. The cluster sits in the connective tissue just beneath the mucosa, between the crypts and the pink smooth muscle. These large cells are <b>ganglion cells</b>, the cell bodies of nerve cells. The layer just under the mucosa is the <b>submucosa</b>, and the nerve network of the submucosa is the <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b>. It is a normal part of the gut wall.</p><p><b>The gut has its own nervous system.</b> It is called the enteric nervous system. It is made of two networks of nerve cells and fibers inside the wall. It can run the gut on its own, independent of the brain and spinal cord. Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves only adjust it.</p><p><b>The two plexuses.</b></p><ul><li><b>Submucosal (Meissner) plexus:</b> lies in the submucosa, just under the muscularis mucosae, the thin sheet of smooth muscle at the base of the mucosa. It controls gland secretion, local blood flow and the movements of the mucosa.</li><li><b>Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus:</b> lies in the muscularis externa, the thick outer muscle coat. It sits in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and the outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers. It controls the contraction of that muscle: peristalsis (the wave that pushes food along) and the rest of gut motility.</li></ul><p>\"Myenteric\" and \"Auerbach\" are two names for one plexus. \"Submucosal\" and \"Meissner\" are two names for the other.</p><p><b>How to name a plexus under the microscope.</b> The two plexuses look the same. Each is a small, pale cluster of large nerve cell bodies with nerve fibers, set in connective tissue. So the cells alone cannot tell you which plexus it is. Name the layer first. A cluster in the submucosa, close under the mucosa, is Meissner. A cluster between the two layers of the muscularis externa is Auerbach.</p><p><b>Nerve tissue and lymphoid tissue look different.</b> Ganglion cells are few, large and pale, each with a round nucleus. Lymphoid nodules, such as the Peyer patches of the ileum, are large, dark blue masses of small, tightly packed lymphocytes.</p><p><b>When a plexus fails.</b> Without myenteric nerve cells, the muscle cannot relax and coordinate. At the lower esophageal sphincter this causes achalasia: the sphincter stays shut and food piles up above it.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-40.jpg","in":"Colon mucosa with goblet-cell-rich crypts above; white arrow points to a small cluster of pale ganglion/nerve cells in the connective tissue just beneath the mucosa (submucosa), above smooth muscle.","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":40,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":82,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":180,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["white arrow","ganglion cells of the submucosal (Meissner) plexus (large pale cells, round nuclei)"]],"n":"2"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Oral mucosa has no muscularis mucosae","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-24.jpg","cap":"Internal lip: epithelium on lamina propria, with no muscularis mucosae beneath","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 24","lg":[["circle","area enlarged in the right panel"],["arrowheads","tall connective tissue papillae poking into the epithelium"],["EP","epithelium (very thick stratified squamous)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["arrows","flat surface cells that still keep their nuclei (parakeratinized surface)"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-15.jpg","cap":"Esophagus by contrast: muscularis mucosae labelled under the lamina propria","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-207-1","s":"13. All of the following structures may be found in oral mucous membrane except","o":["A. lamina propria","B. basal lamina","C. stratified squamous epithelium","D. muscularis mucosae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"muscularis mucosae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis mucosae.</b> Oral mucosa is just <b>stratified squamous epithelium on a basal lamina plus a lamina propria</b>. It has no muscularis mucosae, so the lamina propria blends into the submucosa or sits on bone. A muscularis mucosae first appears in the esophagus.<br>A. Lamina propria: under every oral epithelium.<br>B. Basal lamina: under every epithelium.<br>C. Stratified squamous epithelium: lines the whole oral cavity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)","H15A slide 26 (Oral Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 7"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Nonkeratinized and parakeratinized surface cells keep nuclei","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-26.jpg","cap":"Oral epithelium: parakeratinized surface keeps nuclei; keratinized surface loses them","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-16-1","s":"Does the outermost layer of non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the oral cavity contain nuclei?","o":["A. Yes","B. No"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Yes.</b> In nonkeratinized (and parakeratinized) oral epithelium the flat surface cells <b>keep their nuclei</b> as they are shed. Only fully keratinized epithelium, like skin, has dead surface cells with no nuclei.<br>Common mix-up: \"stratified squamous\" does not mean keratinized. The cheeks, soft palate and floor of the mouth are nonkeratinized, and their top cells are nucleated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":16,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 8"},{"id":"F0754-150-1","s":"What are parakeratinized cells?","o":["A. Epithelial cells with keratin and no nucleus","B. Epithelial cells with no keratin","C. Epithelial cells keratinized, but can still see the nucleus","D. Epithelial cells with kerato-hyaline granules next to the nucleus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Epithelial cells keratinized, but can still see the nucleus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Keratinized cells that still show a nucleus.</b> Parakeratinized epithelium makes keratin at the surface, but its surface cells <b>keep small, shrunken nuclei</b>. Fully (ortho)keratinized cells, as in skin, lose their nuclei.<br>A. Keratin and no nucleus: full keratinization.<br>B. No keratin: nonkeratinized epithelium.<br>D. Keratohyalin granules beside the nucleus: cells of the granular layer, a step on the way to keratin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":150,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 9"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Keratinized mucosa: gingiva and hard palate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-28.jpg","cap":"Palate: masticatory mucosa over hard palate and gingiva; soft palate behind","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G015-27.jpg","cap":"Keratinized: gum, hard palate; nonkeratinized: soft palate, lips, cheeks, floor","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-1","s":"19. A structure in the oral cavity that is covered for the most part by stratified squamous keratinized epithelium is the","o":["A. hard palate","B. soft palate","C. cheek","D. tooth"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"hard palate","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Hard palate.</b> Keratin is laid down where the lining takes the most force. During chewing, food is pressed and ground against the <b>hard palate</b> and the <b>gingiva</b> (the gums). These surfaces are called masticatory mucosa, meaning chewing mucosa. They are covered by <b>keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b>, a many-layered epithelium whose top cells are dead, flat and full of the tough protein keratin.</p><p><b>The lining of the mouth.</b> The mouth is lined by oral mucosa: a stratified squamous epithelium on a lamina propria, which is a layer of connective tissue. The mouth has <b>no muscularis mucosae</b>. The lamina propria runs straight into a submucosa where there is one, or onto bone or muscle.</p><p><b>The three kinds of surface.</b></p><ul><li><b>Keratinized:</b> the surface cells are dead and flat, full of keratin, with no nuclei. A granular layer with dark keratohyalin granules lies just below them. It covers the gingiva and hard palate, and also the tips of the filiform papillae of the tongue.</li><li><b>Parakeratinized:</b> keratin is made, but the flat surface cells keep small, dark nuclei. It covers parts of the gingiva and hard palate and patches of the lip.</li><li><b>Nonkeratinized:</b> living flat cells with nuclei all the way to the surface, which stays wet. It covers the soft palate, inner lips, cheeks, floor of the mouth, underside of the tongue and pharynx.</li></ul><p><b>Mucoperiosteum.</b> Over the hard palate and the attached gingiva, the lamina propria is fused directly to the periosteum, the covering of the bone underneath. This firm layer is called mucoperiosteum. It keeps the lining from sliding during chewing. The soft palate, cheeks and floor of the mouth have a loose submucosa instead, so their lining moves.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1,"n":"3"},{"id":"F1221-226-2","s":"17. Mucoperiosteum may be found in which one of the following regions?","o":["A. soft palate","B. hard palate","C. lip","D. cheek","E. tongue"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"hard palate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hard palate.</b> Mucoperiosteum means the mucosa's lamina propria is <b>fused directly to the periosteum of bone</b>. That happens in masticatory mucosa lying on bone: the hard palate (and gingiva).<br>A. Soft palate: lining mucosa over muscle, no bone.<br>C. Lip: skeletal muscle core.<br>D. Cheek: lining mucosa over the buccinator muscle.<br>E. Tongue: mucosa bound to skeletal muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1221-235-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 10"},{"id":"F3950-12-1","s":"A 35-year-old man presents with a persistent ulcer on his hard palate that has not healed for several weeks. On examination, the lesion appears well-defined, and the surrounding tissue is firm and resilient to abrasion. Biopsy reveals stratified squamous epithelium with keratinization. Which of the following is the most likely location of this lesion?","o":["A. Soft palate","B. Gingiva","C. Cheeks","D. Floor of the mouth"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gingiva (gum)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gingiva.</b> A firm, abrasion-resistant lining with <b>keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> is masticatory mucosa. It covers only the <b>gingiva (gums) and the hard palate</b>, where the lamina propria sits right on bone; gingiva is the only such site offered.<br>A. Soft palate: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>C. Cheeks: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>D. Floor of the mouth: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":12,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"F1221-235-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 11"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Lip core is skeletal muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-9.jpg","cap":"Lip: skeletal muscle core (M) between oral mucosa and skin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G015-25.jpg","cap":"Lip summary: skeletal muscle core; external, vermilion and internal surfaces","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-170-1","s":"Q1. What is true about the structure of the lips?","o":["A. Composed of smooth muscle with some areas of simple squamous non keratinized epithelium","B. Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of smooth muscle","C. Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of skeletal muscle","D. Composed of Burt’s Bees"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of skeletal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Partly parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium over a skeletal muscle core.</b> The lip's core is <b>skeletal muscle</b> (orbicularis oris), which makes it so mobile. All three surfaces are stratified squamous: keratinized skin outside, the thin vermilion, and oral mucosa inside.<br>A. Smooth muscle and simple squamous: both wrong; the lip has skeletal muscle and stratified epithelium.<br>B. Smooth muscle core: wrong muscle type.<br>D. Joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 25 (The lips)","H15A slide 24 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":163,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 12"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Lip sagittal section: three surfaces","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-9.jpg","cap":"Lip sagittal section: oral mucosa inside, skin with follicles outside, vermilion tip","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G015-21.jpg","cap":"Low-power lip labelled: oral mucosa, skin, hair follicle, vermilion, muscle core","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["G","minor salivary (labial) glands"],["F","hair follicles"],["M","skeletal muscle core"],["OM","oral mucosa (inner surface)"],["S","skin (outer surface)"],["V","vermilion zone (red margin of the lip)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-33-1","s":"What is this? Be specific on the left and right.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The lips: left = internal portion (oral mucosa); right = external portion (thin skin with hair follicles); vermillion zone at the tip","a":null,"why":"<b>Lip, sagittal section.</b> <b>Left (internal portion):</b> thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous oral mucosa with minor salivary glands beneath and no hair. <b>Right (external portion):</b> thin skin with <b>hair follicles</b> and sebaceous glands. <b>Tip (vermilion zone):</b> very thin keratinized epithelium over capillary loops, no glands. The core is skeletal muscle.<br>Common mix-up: the side with hair follicles is always the skin (outer) side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-33.jpg","in":"Sagittal section of lip: thick stratified squamous epithelium on the left surface, skeletal muscle core, minor glands, hair follicles and sebaceous glands on the right surface; boxes cover the labels.","tg":["H15A slide 21 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":33,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":121,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["F","hair follicle (skin side of the lip)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 13"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"External lip: thin skin with hair follicles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-22.jpg","cap":"External lip: thin epidermis, hair follicle and sebaceous gland in the dermis","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 22","lg":[["circle","area enlarged in the right panel"],["EP","epidermis (thin skin, keratinized)"],["BV","blood vessels"],["HF","hair follicle"],["SGl","sebaceous gland (pale, foamy cells)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["M","melanin pigment in basal-layer cells (red-brown granules)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels compared: external skin, vermilion zone, internal oral mucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10","lg":[["lines (left panel)","labels cut off: purple lobules = labial (minor salivary) glands; red bundles = skeletal muscle"],["G","minor salivary (labial) glands"],["F","hair follicles"],["M","skeletal muscle core (orbicularis oris)"],["OM","oral mucosa: inner surface, thick nonkeratinized epithelium"],["S","skin: outer surface"],["V","vermilion zone: red edge, very thin keratinized epithelium"],["EP","epidermis"],["BV","blood vessels"],["HF","hair follicle"],["SGl","sebaceous gland"],["(skin) circle","epidermis with its short dermal papillae"],["(vermilion) circle","tall dermal papillae reaching close to the surface"],["(vermilion) arrowheads","the tall dermal papillae, full of capillaries (why the lip looks red)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (dark granules under the thin keratin)"],["MC","Meissner corpuscle: touch receptor in a dermal papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-7-1","s":"The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. External portion","B. Vermillion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"External portion","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. External portion.</b> The picture is <b>thin skin</b>. The left panel shows a thin epidermis (EP) on a dermis that holds a <b>hair follicle (HF)</b> and a <b>sebaceous gland (SGl)</b>, the group of pale, foamy cells at the bottom. The right panel enlarges the circled area. It shows a stratum granulosum (SG), the layer of cells with dark keratohyalin granules that lies just under the keratin. It also shows melanin (M), the red-brown pigment in the deepest cells. BV marks blood vessels. Hair follicles and sebaceous glands are found on only one surface of the lip: the outer, skin side.</p><p><b>How a lip is built.</b> A lip cut from front to back has a core of skeletal muscle, the orbicularis oris. Three different surfaces cover it. Going from the face into the mouth, they are the external portion, the vermilion zone and the internal portion.</p><p><b>The three surfaces.</b></p><ul><li><b>External portion (skin side):</b> thin skin. A thin keratinized epidermis on a dermis with hair follicles, sebaceous glands and sweat glands.</li><li><b>Vermilion zone (the red margin):</b> a very thin keratinized epidermis. It is the transition from skin to oral mucosa. Tall dermal papillae (fingers of dermis that push up into the epidermis) bring loops of capillaries close to the surface, and that makes the pink-red color. It has many sensory nerve endings (Meissner corpuscles). It has no hair follicles and no glands, so only saliva keeps it moist, and it dries and cracks easily.</li><li><b>Internal portion (mouth side):</b> oral mucosa. A thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium whose surface cells keep their nuclei. Some areas are parakeratinized, which means keratin is made but the surface cells still have small nuclei. Minor salivary glands, the labial glands, lie below it. It has no hair.</li></ul><p><b>Telling the three apart in a section.</b> Look at what lies under the epithelium. Hair follicles or skin glands in the dermis mean the external portion. A thin epidermis with vessels pushed up close to the surface, and nothing else in the connective tissue below, means the vermilion. A very thick epithelium with nucleated surface cells and mucous glands below means the internal portion.</p><p><b>Cold sores.</b> Cold sores (herpes labialis) are clusters of painful vesicles, or small blisters, on the lip that break into ulcers. The cause is herpes simplex virus type 1. The virus kills the infected epithelial cells, then hides in the sensory ganglion and can return. Keep four other mouth infections apart from it:</p><ul><li><b>Epstein-Barr virus:</b> infectious mononucleosis and hairy leukoplakia of the tongue.</li><li><b>Human herpesvirus 8:</b> Kaposi sarcoma.</li><li><b><i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i>:</b> strep throat.</li><li><b><i>Borrelia vincentii</i>:</b> trench mouth, a gum infection that kills tissue.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-7.jpg","in":"Thin skin: thin epidermis (EP) with stratum granulosum (SG) and melanin (M), hair follicle (HF) and sebaceous gland (SGl) in the dermis.","tg":["H15A slide 22 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":9,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":11,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":9,"f":"F0745"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":129,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle","area shown enlarged in the right panel"],["EP","epidermis (thin skin, keratinized)"],["BV","blood vessels"],["HF","hair follicle"],["SGl","sebaceous gland (pale foamy cells)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (dark keratohyalin granules)"],["M","melanin pigment in basal-layer cells (red-brown granules)"]],"n":"4"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Vermilion zone: thin epidermis, capillaries, no glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-23.jpg","cap":"Vermilion zone: thin epidermis, tall papillae with vessels near the surface","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 23","lg":[["circle","area enlarged in the right panel"],["EP","epidermis (thin, lightly keratinized)"],["arrowheads","tall dermal papillae reaching close to the surface"],["BV","blood vessels (rich capillaries give the red color)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["MC","Meissner corpuscle (touch receptor in a dermal papilla)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels: vermilion is thin keratinized epithelium over capillary loops","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10","lg":[["lines (left panel)","labels cut off: purple lobules = labial (minor salivary) glands; red bundles = skeletal muscle"],["G","minor salivary (labial) glands"],["F","hair follicles"],["M","skeletal muscle core (orbicularis oris)"],["OM","oral mucosa: inner surface, thick nonkeratinized epithelium"],["S","skin: outer surface"],["V","vermilion zone: red edge, very thin keratinized epithelium"],["EP","epidermis"],["BV","blood vessels"],["HF","hair follicle"],["SGl","sebaceous gland"],["(skin) circle","epidermis with its short dermal papillae"],["(vermilion) circle","tall dermal papillae reaching close to the surface"],["(vermilion) arrowheads","the tall dermal papillae, full of capillaries (why the lip looks red)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (dark granules under the thin keratin)"],["MC","Meissner corpuscle: touch receptor in a dermal papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-133-1","s":"Q3. The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. Vermillion","B. External portion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vermillion","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vermilion.</b> A thin epidermis, <b>tall dermal papillae carrying blood vessels close to the surface</b> (the red color), and <b>no hair follicles or glands</b>: that is the vermilion zone.<br>B. External portion: thin skin plus hair follicles and sebaceous glands.<br>C. Internal portion: very thick nonkeratinized epithelium whose flat surface cells keep their nuclei.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-133.jpg","in":"H&E: thin keratinized epithelium (EP) with a tall dermal papilla (circled, arrowheads) carrying blood vessels (BV) close to the surface; no hair follicles or glands; inset shows stratum granulosum (SG) and a Merkel cell (MC).","tg":["H15A slide 23 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":133,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["circle","area shown enlarged in the right panel"],["arrowheads","tall dermal papillae reaching close to the surface"],["EP","epidermis (thin, keratinized)"],["BV","blood vessels (many, close to the surface: red color)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (thin granular layer under thin keratin)"],["MC","Meissner corpuscle (touch receptor in a dermal papilla)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 14"},{"id":"F3950-14-1","s":"A 40-year-old woman complains of recurrent dryness and cracking of her lips, particularly in the red area between her skin and the oral mucosa. She notes that the dryness worsens in cold weather. Examination reveals a dry, erythematous region without visible sweat or salivary glands. Which area of the lips is affected?","o":["A. Internal mucous surface","B. Vermilion zone","C. Outer surface","D. Floor of the mouth"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vermilion zone","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vermilion zone.</b> The red part of the lip has a very thin keratinized epidermis over <b>capillary loops</b> (the pink-red color) and <b>no sweat or oil glands</b>. Only saliva from the tongue keeps it moist, so it dries and cracks in cold weather.<br>A. Internal mucous surface: thick wet oral mucosa with minor salivary glands.<br>C. Outer surface: thin skin with hair follicles, sebaceous and sweat glands.<br>D. Floor of the mouth: nonkeratinized lining mucosa, not part of the lip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 23 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":14,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 15"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Internal lip: thick oral mucosa, nucleated surface","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-24.jpg","cap":"Internal lip: very thick epithelium; arrows on flat surface cells keeping nuclei","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 24","lg":[["circle","area enlarged in the right panel"],["arrowheads","tall connective tissue papillae poking into the epithelium"],["EP","epithelium (very thick stratified squamous)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["arrows","flat surface cells that still keep their nuclei (parakeratinized surface)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels: internal oral mucosa, nonkeratinized stratified squamous with minor glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10","lg":[["lines (left panel)","labels cut off: purple lobules = labial (minor salivary) glands; red bundles = skeletal muscle"],["G","minor salivary (labial) glands"],["F","hair follicles"],["M","skeletal muscle core (orbicularis oris)"],["OM","oral mucosa: inner surface, thick nonkeratinized epithelium"],["S","skin: outer surface"],["V","vermilion zone: red edge, very thin keratinized epithelium"],["EP","epidermis"],["BV","blood vessels"],["HF","hair follicle"],["SGl","sebaceous gland"],["(skin) circle","epidermis with its short dermal papillae"],["(vermilion) circle","tall dermal papillae reaching close to the surface"],["(vermilion) arrowheads","the tall dermal papillae, full of capillaries (why the lip looks red)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (dark granules under the thin keratin)"],["MC","Meissner corpuscle: touch receptor in a dermal papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-131-1","s":"Q2. The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. Vermillion","B. External portion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal portion","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Internal portion.</b> A <b>very thick stratified squamous epithelium</b> with no hair follicles or glands, and flat surface cells that <b>keep their nuclei</b> (arrows): oral mucosa lining the inside of the lip.<br>A. Vermilion: thin epidermis with capillaries close to the surface.<br>B. External portion: thin skin with hair follicles and sebaceous glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-131.jpg","in":"H&E: very thick stratified squamous epithelium (EP, boxed in red) with tall connective-tissue papillae (arrowheads), no adnexa; inset of surface with stratum granulosum (SG) and arrows on flattened surface cells that retain nuclei.","tg":["H15A slide 24 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":131,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["circle","area shown enlarged in the right panel"],["arrowheads","tall narrow connective tissue papillae poking into the epithelium"],["red box","the very thick epithelium (key clue)"],["EP","epithelium (very thick stratified squamous)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["arrows","flat surface cells that still keep their nuclei (parakeratinized surface)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 16"},{"id":"F0745-8-1","s":"Lip: What are the arrows pointing to? Is this internal or external lip?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Arrows: parakeratinized cells (nuclei still present in the keratin layer); internal portion of the lip (thick stratified squamous oral mucosa)","a":null,"why":"<b>Internal portion of the lip.</b> The epithelium is very thick stratified squamous with tall connective tissue papillae and <b>no hair follicles or glands</b>: oral mucosa. The arrows mark flat surface cells that <b>still have nuclei</b> (parakeratinized). Fully keratinized skin cells lose their nuclei.<br>Common mix-up: the vermilion zone also lacks hair, but its epithelium is thin with capillaries close to the surface.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-8.jpg","in":"Two H&E panels of lip: thick stratified squamous epithelium (EP) with tall connective-tissue papillae and no hair follicles/glands; high-power inset of the surface (SG) with arrows on flattened superficial cells that still retain nuclei.","tg":["H15A slide 24 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)","H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":8,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["circle","area shown enlarged in the right panel"],["arrowheads","tall connective tissue papillae of the lamina propria"],["EP","epithelium (very thick stratified squamous)"],["SG","stratum granulosum (cells with dark keratohyalin granules)"],["arrows","flat surface cells that still keep their nuclei (parakeratinized surface)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 17"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Cold sores: herpes simplex virus type 1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-46.jpg","cap":"Herpes simplex type 1 cold sore on the lip","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-66-1","s":"A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physical examination reveals several vesicles and ulcers in the lesion area. Infection with which of the following pathogens is the common cause of cold sores?","o":["A. Borrelia vincentii","B. Epstein-Barr virus","C. Herpes simplex virus type 1","D. Human herpes virus 8","E. Streptococcus pyogenes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Herpes simplex virus type 1","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Herpes simplex virus type 1.</b> It infects lip epithelium and kills the cells, giving <b>painful clustered vesicles and ulcers</b> (cold sores). The virus hides in nearby nerves and returns with stress, fever or illness.<br>A. Borrelia vincentii: trench mouth (necrotizing gingivitis).<br>B. Epstein-Barr virus: hairy leukoplakia on the sides of the tongue, and mononucleosis.<br>D. Human herpesvirus 8: Kaposi sarcoma.<br>E. Streptococcus pyogenes: pharyngitis and tonsillitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 44 (Q1- A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physi)","H15A slide 45 (Q1- A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physi)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":66,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 18"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Tongue: skeletal muscle in three planes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-20.jpg","cap":"Tongue core: striated bundles cut obliquely (O), across (C), lengthwise (L)","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G015-38.jpg","cap":"Tongue: mass of skeletal muscle intersecting in all three planes","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-35-1","s":"Sam got drunk last night and decided that it was a good idea to get a tongue piercing. What is true about the tongue?","o":["A. Composed of a mass of skeletal muscle in 3 different planes","B. Composed of skeletal muscle circumferentially layering on top of each other","C. Composed of smooth muscle with a Myenteric plexus","D. Composed of dense regular CT"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Composed of a mass of skeletal muscle in 3 different planes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. A mass of skeletal muscle in three planes.</b> The tongue is a mass of <b>skeletal (striated) muscle</b> whose bundles run and cross in <b>all three planes</b>. That interlacing gives the tongue its great mobility; mucosa covers it.<br>B. Circumferential layers: describes gut-type muscle coats, not the tongue.<br>C. Smooth muscle with a myenteric plexus: the gut wall from the lower esophagus down.<br>D. Dense regular connective tissue: tendons and ligaments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":35,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":166,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 19"},{"id":"F0754-153-1","s":"How are the muscle fibers of the tongue organized?","o":["A. Circular, oblique and longitudinal","B. Circular and longitudinal","C. Longitudinal","D. Circular, oblique, longitudinal and vertically"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Circular, oblique and longitudinal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Circular, oblique and longitudinal.</b> This is the intended \"three planes\" answer: the lecture says tongue muscle fibers <b>intersect in all three planes</b>, so in a section the bundles look cut lengthwise, across and obliquely. Strictly, the intrinsic fibers run <b>longitudinally, transversely and vertically</b>.<br>B. Circular and longitudinal: the two-layer muscularis externa of the gut.<br>C. Longitudinal only: one plane.<br>D. Four directions: there are three planes, not four.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed A is the intended 'three planes' choice, but its wording is inaccurate: tongue skeletal muscle bundles run longitudinally, transversely and vertically, interlacing at right angles (G015:38 'intersect in all three planes'); there is no circular/oblique layer, so no option is strictly correct."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 20"},{"id":"F0755-216-1","s":"What direction does the muscle fibers of the tongue arranged in ?","o":["A. Longitudinal","B. Longitudinal and circular","C. Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique.</b> This is the intended \"three directions\" answer: tongue skeletal muscle bundles <b>cross in all three planes</b>. Strictly, the intrinsic fibers run <b>longitudinally, transversely and vertically</b>, so in sections they look cut lengthwise, across and obliquely.<br>A. Longitudinal only: one plane.<br>B. Longitudinal and circular: the two-layer muscularis externa of the gut.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed C is the intended 'three directions' choice, but tongue skeletal muscle fibres run longitudinally, transversely and vertically (G015:38 'intersect in all three planes'), not circumferentially/obliquely; no option is worded correctly."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":216,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 21"},{"id":"F3955-13-1","s":"A tongue is examined at autopsy (shown in the image). Identify the tissue indicated by the letters:","o":["A. Dense irregular connective tissue","B. Dense regular connective tissue","C. Peripheral nerve","D. Skeletal muscle","E. Smooth muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Skeletal muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Skeletal muscle.</b> The letters mark bundles of long, pink, multinucleated <b>striated fibers cut in three directions</b>: O (oblique), C (cross) and L (lengthwise). Skeletal fibers crossing in all three planes are the signature of the tongue.<br>A. Dense irregular connective tissue: wavy collagen with few nuclei.<br>B. Dense regular connective tissue: parallel collagen with flat nuclei, as in tendons.<br>C. Peripheral nerve: wavy axons wrapped in perineurium.<br>E. Smooth muscle: small spindle cells with central nuclei.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-13.jpg","in":"Bundles of pink striated fibers cut in three planes: O (oblique), C (cross), L (longitudinal).","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":13,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":70,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":68,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":135,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["O","skeletal muscle bundle cut obliquely"],["C","skeletal muscle bundle cut in cross-section"],["L","skeletal muscle bundle cut lengthwise"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 22"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Glands drain into lingual, not palatine, tonsil crypts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-31.jpg","cap":"Tongue root: palatine tonsils at the sides, lingual tonsil behind the papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-190-3","s":"12. All of the following characterize palatine tonsils except","o":["A. stratified squamous epithelium","B. ducts of mucous glands open into crypts","C. crypts","D. lymphatic nodules"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ducts of mucous glands open into crypts","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ducts of mucous glands opening into crypts.</b> That describes the <b>lingual tonsil</b>, whose shallow crypts are flushed by mucous glands. Palatine tonsils have no such glands, so debris collects in their deep crypts.<br>A. Stratified squamous epithelium: covers the palatine tonsil and lines its crypts.<br>C. Crypts: deep, branching crypts are typical of palatine tonsils.<br>D. Lymphatic nodules: line the crypts of every tonsil.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 23"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Filiform papillae: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types: filiform panel pointed and keratinized, without taste buds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Tongue surface: pointed filiform (FI) beside mushroom-shaped fungiform (F) papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-219-1","s":"Which type of papillae has no taste buds?","o":["A. Fungiform","B. Filiform","C. Vallate","D. Foliate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Filiform.</b> Filiform papillae are pointed and <b>heavily keratinized and have no taste buds</b>; their job is friction to move food.<br>A. Fungiform: a few taste buds on the top.<br>C. Vallate: hundreds of taste buds on the side walls.<br>D. Foliate: taste buds in the clefts between the ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":219,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 24"},{"id":"F3950-22-1","s":"Which of the following papillae are most likely responsible for providing the rough texture that facilitates food movement during mastication?","o":["A. Fungiform papillae","B. Vallate papillae","C. Filiform papillae","D. Foliate papillae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Filiform papillae.</b> They are the most numerous papillae: <b>long, pointed and heavily keratinized</b>, giving the tongue a rough surface that grips food during chewing. They have no taste buds.<br>A. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped and scattered, with a few taste buds.<br>B. Vallate: eight to twelve large papillae in a V near the sulcus terminalis, full of taste buds.<br>D. Foliate: ridges on the sides of the tongue; small in adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":22,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 25"},{"id":"F3980-71-1","s":"Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Fungiform papillae","C. Circumvallate papillae","D. Foliate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> Rows of <b>slender, pointed, cone-shaped papillae with heavily keratinized tips</b> and no taste buds are filiform. They are the most numerous papillae and make the tongue rough.<br>B. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized, a few taste buds on top.<br>C. Circumvallate: one huge papilla sunk in a trench, taste buds on its sides.<br>D. Foliate: parallel ridges with taste buds in the clefts between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-71.jpg","in":"Dorsal tongue surface with a row of pointed, conical papillae capped by keratinized squamous epithelium, no taste buds; skeletal muscle below.","tg":["H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":17,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":19,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":21,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":23,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":141,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["lines","filiform papillae (pointed, keratinized tips)"]],"like":"G018-49-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 26"},{"id":"G018-49-1","s":"Q2. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> The picture shows a row of slender, pointed cones that all lean the same way. Each tip is capped by a pale, flaky layer of <b>keratin</b>. The dark purple epithelium holds no taste buds, and there is no trench or cleft beside the papillae. Pointed, keratinized and without taste buds describes one papilla only: the filiform. The pink bundles of skeletal muscle at the bottom confirm that this is tongue.</p><p><b>What filiform papillae do.</b> They are the most numerous papillae. They stand in rows over the whole top surface of the front two-thirds of the tongue. Their hard keratin tips give the tongue its rough texture and grip food by friction during chewing. Their job is mechanical. They do not taste.</p><p><b>The four papillae.</b> A papilla is a raised fold of the epithelium with a core of connective tissue.</p><ul><li><b>Filiform:</b> pointed cones with heavily keratinized tips. No taste buds.</li><li><b>Fungiform:</b> mushroom-shaped, with a narrow stalk and a broad round top. Scattered singly among the filiform papillae. Thin keratin and a few taste buds on top.</li><li><b>Circumvallate:</b> the largest. 8 to 12 domes in a V at the back, each ringed by a deep trench. No keratin. Many taste buds in the side walls.</li><li><b>Foliate:</b> rows of parallel, leaf-like ridges on the sides of the back of the tongue, separated by deep clefts. No keratin. Many taste buds in the walls of the clefts.</li></ul><p><b>The body of the tongue.</b> The tongue is a mass of skeletal (striated) muscle. Its fibers run in all three planes: lengthwise, across and up-and-down. They cross one another. So any one section shows bundles cut lengthwise, across and at a slant. This arrangement gives the tongue its great mobility for taste, chewing, swallowing and speech. Connective tissue and fat separate the bundles. In the embryo, the muscle comes from the occipital myotomes, and the lining comes from the pharyngeal (branchial) arches.</p><p><b>The two surfaces of the tongue.</b> The underside (ventral surface) is smooth, with a thin nonkeratinized lining. The top (dorsal surface) is rough. A V-shaped groove called the sulcus terminalis divides the top into two parts. The front two-thirds carries the papillae. The back one-third, the root, carries the lingual tonsil.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-49.jpg","in":"Row of pointed, slanted papillae capped with keratin; no taste buds. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Fungiform papilla: mushroom shape, connective tissue core","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Fungiform papilla: mushroom-shaped with connective tissue core, taste buds on top","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-12.jpg","cap":"Fungiform (F) papilla among pointed filiform (FI) papillae","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 12","lg":[["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom shape, few taste buds)"],["Fl","filiform papillae (pointed, keratinized, no taste buds)"],["SSE","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core (lamina propria)"],["TB","taste buds on the side walls of the papilla"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-139-1","s":"Q6. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fungiform papillae","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fungiform papillae.</b> A single <b>mushroom-shaped</b> papilla with a broad, lightly keratinized top and a <b>connective tissue core</b>, standing among pointed filiform papillae. Its few taste buds sit on the top surface (arrows).<br>A. Filiform: the pointed papillae around it.<br>B. Foliate: rows of parallel ridges with taste buds in deep clefts.<br>D. Circumvallate: very large, sunk in a trench, with glands below.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-139.jpg","in":"H&E: a mushroom-shaped papilla with a connective-tissue core, between pointed papillae; arrows on its dorsal epithelium (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":139,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","connective tissue core sending branches (secondary papillae) up into the epithelium"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 27"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Circumvallate papilla: trench, taste buds, glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla: trench, taste buds (TB) on walls, glands (GL) below","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with taste buds on its walls and serous glands beneath","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-137-1","s":"Q5. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> One <b>large dome sunk below the surface and ringed by a deep trench</b>, with <b>taste buds on its side walls</b> and <b>serous glands</b> beneath that empty into the trench.<br>A. Filiform: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds.<br>B. Foliate: several parallel ridges with taste buds in the clefts.<br>C. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, stands above the surface, few taste buds on top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-137.jpg","in":"H&E: a large dome-shaped papilla sunk below the surface and surrounded by a deep trench, with taste buds in its lateral walls and serous glands beneath (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":137,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-48-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 28"},{"id":"F0722-123-1","s":"What is this? Be specific.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Tongue – circumvallate papilla","a":null,"why":"<b>Circumvallate (vallate) papilla of the tongue.</b> It is the <b>largest papilla</b>, sunk below the surface and ringed by a deep <b>trench</b>. Taste buds (labelled TB) line its side walls, and small serous (von Ebner) glands (labelled GL) below empty into the trench to rinse it. Eight to twelve sit in a V just in front of the sulcus terminalis.<br>Common mix-up: fungiform papillae are mushroom-shaped and stand above the surface, with taste buds on top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-123.jpg","in":"Large dome papilla sunk in a trench with taste buds (TB) on its walls, glands (GL) at the base of the trench, stratified squamous surface (SS).","tg":["H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":123,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["TB","taste buds (arrows) on the sides of the papilla"],["CT","connective tissue core of the papilla"],["GL","von Ebner glands (small serous salivary glands draining into the moat)"]],"like":"G018-48-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 29"},{"id":"G018-48-1","s":"Q1. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> The picture shows one very large papilla with a broad, flat top. It does not rise above its neighbors. It sits level with the surface, and a deep, narrow <b>trench</b> runs down each side of it. The label lines mark <b>taste buds</b> in the side wall that faces the trench. Under the papilla lie dark purple clusters labeled <b>serous glands</b>. One big dome in a moat, with taste buds in its walls and serous glands below it, is a circumvallate (vallate) papilla.</p><p><b>Facts about circumvallate papillae.</b> They are the largest papillae, and there are only 8 to 12 of them. They stand in a V-shaped row just in front of the sulcus terminalis, the V-shaped groove that separates the front two-thirds of the tongue from the back one-third. They have no keratin. They hold more taste buds than any other papilla, about 250 each, in the epithelium of the side walls that face the trench.</p><p><b>Von Ebner glands.</b> The serous glands in the picture are the von Ebner glands. \"Serous\" means that they make a thin, watery fluid. They are small salivary glands that lie in the tongue under the circumvallate papillae.</p><ul><li>Their ducts empty into the base of the trench.</li><li>The steady flow rinses the trench. It clears away old food so that the taste buds can sense new tastes.</li><li>They also secrete lingual lipase, an enzyme that begins the digestion of fat.</li></ul><p><b>Why the shape fits the job.</b> Dissolved food runs into the trench and bathes the taste buds in its walls. The fluid from the glands then washes it out. So the trench is a small tasting chamber that is filled and rinsed again and again.</p><p><b>Behind the V: the root of the tongue and the tonsils.</b> The back one-third of the tongue has no papillae. It carries the lingual tonsil. Two tonsils sit at the back of the mouth. Both are covered by stratified squamous epithelium, both have crypts (pits that dip in from the surface), and both have lymphoid nodules (round clusters of immune cells) around the crypts.</p><ul><li><b>Lingual tonsil:</b> at the root of the tongue. Its crypts are shallow. Ducts of mucous glands open into the crypts and flush them clean.</li><li><b>Palatine tonsil:</b> on the side walls of the oropharynx. Its crypts are deep and branching, and the epithelium lines them too. No glands drain into them, so debris collects there.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-48.jpg","in":"One large flat-topped papilla in a deep trench; taste buds labeled on its walls; serous glands labeled below. The name of the papilla is blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Von Ebner glands flush vallate papillae","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with serous (von Ebner) glands opening into its trench","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-24-1","s":"Which of the following structures is most directly involved in flushing the vallate papillae to facilitate gustation?","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Von Ebner glands","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Sulcus terminalis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Von Ebner glands","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Von Ebner glands.</b> These <b>serous salivary glands</b> sit under the circumvallate (vallate) papillae and empty into the trench around each papilla. Their watery secretion <b>flushes the trench</b> so the taste buds can sample new tastes.<br>A. Filiform papillae: most numerous; keratinized, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform papillae: mushroom-shaped, a few taste buds, no glands of their own.<br>D. Sulcus terminalis: the V-shaped groove between the front two-thirds and back third of the tongue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"'Supplemental Slide 126 Tongue': von Ebner's glands and their duct beneath a vallate papilla (not needed to answer).","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":24,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 30"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges with taste-bud clefts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges, taste buds in clefts, serous glands below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-33.jpg","cap":"Diagrams of filiform, fungiform, vallate and foliate papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-143-1","s":"Q8. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> A <b>row of parallel leaf-like ridges</b> separated by deep clefts, with <b>taste buds in the cleft walls</b> and serous glands and ducts below. Foliate papillae sit on the sides of the tongue and are best developed in children.<br>A. Filiform: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom shape.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla in a ring-shaped trench, not a row of ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-143.jpg","in":"H&E: a series of parallel leaf-like ridges separated by deep clefts (arrows), with taste buds in the cleft walls, ducts and serous glands below (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":143,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","deep clefts between the parallel ridges"],["cut-off bottom label","striated muscle"]],"like":"G018-50-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 31"},{"id":"G018-50-1","s":"Q3. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> The picture shows several broad ridges standing side by side, all about the same height. The black arrows at the top mark the narrow, deep <b>clefts</b> between them. The label \"taste buds\" points to pale ovals in the walls of a cleft. \"Ducts\" open into the bottoms of the clefts, and \"serous glands\" lie deeper, next to the pink skeletal muscle of the tongue. A row of parallel ridges with taste buds in the cleft walls is foliate papillae. \"Foliate\" means leaf-like: the ridges lie side by side like the leaves of a closed book.</p><p><b>Facts about foliate papillae.</b> They sit on the sides of the back of the tongue. They have no keratin. They carry many taste buds, all in the walls of the clefts. They are well developed in children and small in adults. Serous glands, which make a watery fluid, drain into the clefts through ducts. The fluid rinses the clefts so that the taste buds can sense new tastes.</p><p><b>Foliate and circumvallate look alike, so count.</b> Both have deep grooves, taste buds in the groove walls and serous glands below. A circumvallate papilla is <b>one</b> large dome with a trench around it. Foliate papillae are a <b>row</b> of equal ridges with a cleft between each pair.</p><p><b>The fungiform papilla.</b> It is shaped like a mushroom, with a narrow stalk and a broad, round top around a core of connective tissue. Fungiform papillae are scattered singly among the filiform papillae, with more of them near the tip of the tongue. Their keratin is thin. They carry a few taste buds on the upper surface. A fungiform papilla rises above the surface and has no trench around it.</p><p><b>Naming a papilla in a section.</b></p><ul><li>Rows of pointed, keratinized spikes: <b>filiform</b>.</li><li>One broad mushroom among spikes: <b>fungiform</b>.</li><li>One large dome in a trench, with glands below: <b>circumvallate</b>.</li><li>A row of parallel ridges with clefts: <b>foliate</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Where the taste buds are.</b> The side walls of the circumvallate papillae have the most, about 250 per papilla. The walls of the foliate clefts have many. The tops of the fungiform papillae have a few. The filiform papillae have none. So taste buds face a trench or a cleft in two of the four papillae, and both of those have rinsing glands beneath them.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-50.jpg","in":"Several parallel ridges separated by narrow clefts (arrows); taste buds, ducts and serous glands labeled. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["lines at bottom (label cut off)","striated (skeletal) muscle"]],"n":"7"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Four papilla types side by side","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types side by side: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17","lg":[["(c) arrows","deep clefts between the foliate ridges"],["(b) arrows","secondary connective tissue papillae pushing up into the epithelium"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-34.jpg","cap":"Pointed filiform papilla (A) next to mushroom-shaped fungiform papilla (B)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-13-1","s":"Tongue Papillae (Filiform, Fungiform, Vallate, Foliate): identify the papillae labelled A–E.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: filiform; B: fungiform; C: fungiform; D: filiform; E: vallate","a":null,"why":"<b>A filiform, B fungiform, C fungiform, D filiform, E vallate.</b> <b>Filiform</b> (A, D): slender, pointed, heavily keratinized, no taste buds. <b>Fungiform</b> (B, C): mushroom-shaped with a broad top and a connective tissue core. <b>Vallate</b> (E): one large papilla sunk in a trench, taste buds on its walls and glands at its base. Foliate papillae (parallel ridges) are not shown.<br>Common mix-up: pointed tip = filiform; rounded cap = fungiform.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-13.jpg","in":"Three tongue micrographs: (left) a pointed keratinized papilla A next to a mushroom-shaped papilla B; (bottom) dorsal surface with a broad rounded papilla (arrow C) and a slender pointed papilla (arrow D); (right) a large papilla sunk in a trench with taste buds (TB) and glands (GL) in its walls (arrow E).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":13,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","filiform papilla (pointed, keratinized tip)"],["B","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, broad connective tissue core)"],["C","fungiform papilla"],["D","filiform papilla"],["E","vallate papilla (sunk in a moat, taste buds on its sides)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["TB","taste buds"],["GL","von Ebner glands (small serous salivary glands draining into the moat)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 32"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Taste buds line vallate papilla walls","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-37.jpg","cap":"Taste buds: pale oval cell clusters within the papilla epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 37","lg":[["arrow","taste pore with the taste cells' microvilli"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla walls lined by taste buds (TB) facing the trench","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35","lg":[["a","dorsal tongue surface with filiform and fungiform papillae"],["F","fungiform papilla (mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized)"],["FI","filiform papillae (pointed, heavily keratinized)"],["SS","stratified squamous epithelium"],["CT","connective tissue core"],["b","one large vallate papilla"],["TB","taste buds on the papilla's side walls"],["GL","small serous (von Ebner) glands draining into the trench"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-179-1","s":"First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue. The sides of a circumvallate papilla are examined. Identify the oval pale-stained structure indicated by the arrow.","o":["A. Circumvallate papilla","B. Fungiform papilla","C. Mucous gland","D. Serous salivary gland","E. Taste bud"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Taste bud","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Taste bud.</b> A <b>pale oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells inside the stratified epithelium</b> on the side of a circumvallate papilla, opening toward the trench, is a taste bud.<br>A. Circumvallate papilla: the whole structure being examined, not the oval inside it.<br>B. Fungiform papilla: a different papilla type.<br>C. Mucous gland: lies in the connective tissue below, with pale bubbly cells.<br>D. Serous (von Ebner) gland: lies below the papilla in connective tissue and empties into the trench.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"H&E of the lateral wall of a circumvallate papilla: the arrow points to a pale oval intraepithelial structure of spindle-shaped cells opening onto the trench.","tg":["H15A slide 49 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 50 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 33"},{"id":"F0755-218-1","s":"Identify the pale-stain oval structure. What is it and what papillae is the slide depicting?","o":["A. Supporting cell: filiform","B. Taste bud: Vallate","C. Goblet cell: Funfigorm","D. Supporting cell: Foliate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Taste bud: Vallate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Taste bud; vallate papilla.</b> The arrow marks a <b>pale oval taste bud</b> inside the epithelium on the wall of a papilla bordered by a <b>deep trench</b>, which makes it a vallate (circumvallate) papilla.<br>A. Supporting cell; filiform: filiform papillae have no taste buds.<br>C. Goblet cell; fungiform: tongue epithelium has no goblet cells.<br>D. Supporting cell; foliate: the arrow marks a whole bud, and foliate papillae have clefts between ridges, not a ring-shaped trench.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-218.jpg","in":"H&E of the lateral wall of a papilla bordered by a deep trench: the arrow points to a pale oval intraepithelial taste bud.","tg":["H15A slide 49 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 50 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":218,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","taste bud (pale oval cell cluster in the epithelium)"]],"like":"G118-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 34"},{"id":"F3952-82-1","s":"During a charity event, renowned chef and television personality Gordon Ramsay mingles with guests while sharing insights about his culinary experiences and the importance of taste in cooking. As he passionately describes the flavors that can elevate a dish, he emphasizes the role of the tongue in detecting different tastes, highlighting the various structures involved in this process. When asked where taste buds are found in the highest concentration in the oral cavity, he confidently explains the specific type of papillae that plays a crucial role in flavor perception. In which of the following structures of the oral cavity would taste buds be localized in the highest concentration?","o":["A. Fungiform papillae","B. Gingiva","C. Filiform papillae","D. Ventral surface of the tongue","E. Vallate papillae"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vallate papillae","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vallate papillae.</b> About two hundred fifty taste buds line the side walls of <b>each vallate papilla</b>, the highest concentration in the mouth. Serous von Ebner glands rinse their trenches.<br>A. Fungiform papillae: only a few taste buds on top.<br>B. Gingiva: keratinized gum, no taste buds.<br>C. Filiform papillae: keratinized, no taste buds.<br>D. Ventral surface of the tongue: smooth lining mucosa without papillae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":82,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 35"},{"id":"G118-18-1","s":"The structures labeled B in the image below are present in which region of the GI tract?","o":["A. Tongue","B. Small Intestines","C. Appendix","D. Lips"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Tongue","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Tongue.</b> The two arrows labeled B point to pale, oval, barrel-shaped groups of cells that sit inside a stratified squamous epithelium. Several of them are lined up in a row along the wall that faces the deep cleft, C. These are <b>taste buds</b>. Pale ovals lined up in the wall of a cleft or trench is the look of a papilla of the tongue, so the organ is the tongue.</p><p><b>What a taste bud is.</b> A taste bud is a pale, oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells set inside the stratified epithelium. It reaches from the basal lamina, the thin sheet under the epithelium, up to the surface. There it opens through a small hole called the taste pore (gustatory pore). It looks pale because its long cells stain more lightly than the epithelial cells packed around it.</p><p><b>Where taste buds are found.</b></p><ul><li><b>Circumvallate papillae</b> (the large domes ringed by a trench): in the side walls. These have the most, about 250 per papilla.</li><li><b>Foliate papillae</b> (the parallel ridges on the sides of the tongue): in the walls of the clefts.</li><li><b>Fungiform papillae</b> (the mushroom-shaped ones): a few, on the top.</li><li><b>Outside the tongue:</b> scattered in the soft palate, pharynx and epiglottis.</li></ul><p><b>Where there are none.</b> The filiform papillae, the pointed keratinized cones of the tongue, have none. The lips have none, although the lips have the same kind of epithelium. The stomach, small intestine and appendix have none either. They are lined by a single layer of columnar cells, so they have no stratified epithelium for a taste bud to sit in.</p><p><b>The cells inside a taste bud.</b></p><ul><li><b>Gustatory (taste) cells:</b> long, pale sensory cells. Their microvilli reach into the taste pore, and their base makes a synapse with a sensory nerve fiber.</li><li><b>Supporting cells:</b> long cells that stand between the taste cells.</li><li><b>Basal cells:</b> small stem cells at the base of the bud. They replace the taste cells, which live only about 10 days.</li></ul><p><b>Why the cleft matters.</b> Food dissolved in saliva runs into the cleft and reaches the taste pores in its walls. Serous glands, which make a watery fluid, empty into the bottom of the cleft and rinse it. That clears old food so that new tastes can be sensed.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-18.jpg","in":"Stratified squamous epithelium on both sides of a deep cleft (C) beside a papilla of the tongue; the two arrows labeled B point to pale, oval, barrel-shaped groups of cells inside the epithelium (taste buds).","tg":["H15A slide 37 (The tongue)","H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":18,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Taste bud cells and microvilli in the pore","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-36.jpg","cap":"Taste bud diagram: gustatory pore, microvilli, support, gustatory and basal cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G018-18.jpg","cap":"Taste bud: gustatory microvilli reach the taste pore; supporting and basal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 18","lg":[["pink arrows","lead from each boxed area to its enlargement"],["orange arrow","taste buds (pale ovals) in the side wall of the papilla"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-156-1","s":"What do the gustatory cells have that helps with absorption of chemicals, helping for taste identification?","o":["A. Cilia","B. Stereocilia","C. Microvilli","D. Goblet cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Microvilli","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Microvilli.</b> Each gustatory cell ends in <b>microvilli that project into the taste pore</b>. Tastants dissolved in saliva bind receptors on these microvilli.<br>A. Cilia: motile hairs of airway epithelium.<br>B. Stereocilia: long microvilli of the epididymis and inner-ear hair cells.<br>D. Goblet cells: mucus-secreting cells; not in taste buds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":156,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3955-26-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 36"},{"id":"F0747-50-1","s":"Taste buds: Identify the structures above (the three blank labels on the taste-bud diagram, top to bottom, between 'Gustatory pore' and 'Basal cell'). Check your answers by sliding the boxes...","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) Microvilli (at the gustatory pore); (2) Support (sustentacular) cell; (3) Gustatory (taste) cell","a":null,"why":"<b>Microvilli, supporting cell, gustatory (taste) cell.</b> <b>Gustatory cells</b> send apical microvilli up into the gustatory pore, where tastants dissolved in saliva touch them. <b>Supporting cells</b> sit between them. <b>Basal cells</b> at the bottom are stem cells that replace taste cells every seven to ten days, and afferent nerve fibers synapse on the gustatory cells.<br>Common mix-up: taste cells carry microvilli, not cilia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0747-50.jpg","in":"Line drawing of a taste bud in lingual epithelium (Junqueira) beside an H&E taste bud; labels shown: Lingual epithelium, Gustatory pore, Basal cell, Afferent nervous fiber; three purple boxes hide the labels for the apical microvilli, a dark spindle-shaped cell and a pale spindle-shaped cell.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Histology E2 2023 TA: GI part only","sl":50,"f":"F0747"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3955-26-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 37"},{"id":"F3955-26-1","s":"Name the structures numbered as #1, #2 and #3, respectively:","o":["A. Supporting cells; Cilia; Sensory cells","B. Supporting cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells","C. Goblet cells; Stereocilia; Supporting cells","D. Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","E. Basal cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells.</b> The picture is one taste bud at high power. Take the three numbers one at a time.</p><ul><li><b>#1</b> has two arrows on long, pale cells with large, light nuclei in the middle of the bud. These are the <b>sensory (gustatory, or taste) cells</b>.</li><li><b>#2</b> points to the very top of the bud, where fine, hair-like processes stick up into the opening at the surface. The opening is the taste pore. The processes inside it are the <b>microvilli</b> of the taste cells, and the arrow is on them.</li><li><b>#3</b> points to a small, dark cell at the bottom edge of the bud, just above the band labeled \"nerve\". This is a <b>basal cell</b>.</li></ul><p><b>The cells of a taste bud and their jobs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Gustatory (taste, sensory) cells:</b> long, pale cells that run the full height of the bud. Their top ends carry microvilli. Their bottom ends make synapses with nerve fibers.</li><li><b>Supporting (sustentacular) cells:</b> long cells that stand between the taste cells.</li><li><b>Basal cells:</b> small stem cells at the base of the bud. Taste cells live only about 10 days, and the basal cells divide to replace them.</li><li><b>Afferent (sensory) nerve fibers:</b> they enter the bud at its base and carry the signal toward the brain.</li></ul><p><b>The surface processes are microvilli.</b> Microvilli are short, finger-like folds of the cell membrane that do not move. On a taste cell they reach into the taste pore. They are not cilia, which are the longer, beating hairs of the airway lining. They are not stereocilia, which are the very long processes found in the epididymis and the inner ear.</p><p><b>How tasting works, step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Food dissolves in saliva.</li><li>The dissolved substances enter the taste pore.</li><li>They bind receptors on the microvilli of the taste cells.</li><li>The taste cell signals across the synapse at its base to an afferent nerve fiber.</li></ol><p><b>The tastes.</b> Five are certain: salty, sweet, bitter, sour and umami, the taste of glutamate. Water, calcium and fat may be extra tastes.</p><p><b>Where the bud sits.</b> A taste bud is a pale oval set inside a stratified squamous epithelium. It reaches from the basal lamina, the thin sheet under the epithelium, to the surface. Most taste buds are in the walls of the circumvallate and foliate papillae of the tongue.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-51.jpg","in":"High-power taste bud: #1 on elongated pale cells inside the bud, #2 at the apical opening where processes project into the pore, #3 on a small cell at the bud base above the nerve.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":72,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","sensory (gustatory) cells: pale, round nuclei"],["2","gustatory microvilli in the taste pore"],["3","basal (stem) cell"]],"n":"9"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Enamel covers the crown","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Tooth diagram: enamel covers the crown; cementum covers the root","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-28-1","s":"A woman presents to the dentist with a cavity. It has penetrated the outermost layer of the tooth. What is the outermost layer of the tooth?","o":["A. Enamel","B. Dentin","C. Periodontal ligament","D. Cementum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enamel","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enamel.</b> The crown is covered by <b>enamel</b>, the hardest tissue in the body (about ninety-six percent hydroxyapatite), made by ameloblasts. A cavity must cross enamel before it reaches dentin.<br>B. Dentin: the bulk of the tooth, under the enamel and cementum.<br>C. Periodontal ligament: collagen fibers holding the root in its socket.<br>D. Cementum: covers the root, not the crown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)","H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":28,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 38"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Tooth mineral: hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-42.jpg","cap":"Teeth text: dentine is seventy percent hydroxyapatite by wet weight","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-3","s":"23. The major inorganic component of dentin is","o":["A. collagen","B. lactate","C. hydroxyapatite","D. lipid","E. desmosine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"hydroxyapatite","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hydroxyapatite.</b> Dentin is a calcified tissue harder than bone, about <b>seventy percent hydroxyapatite</b> (calcium phosphate crystals).<br>A. Collagen: the main organic part (type I).<br>B. Lactate: a metabolite.<br>D. Lipid: trace only.<br>E. Desmosine: a cross-link of elastin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 39"},{"id":"F1221-190-2","s":"11. The major inorganic constituent of enamel by weight is","o":["A. collagen","B. sodium","C. phosphate","D. carbonate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"phosphate","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phosphate.</b> Enamel is about ninety-six percent <b>calcium hydroxyapatite</b>, a calcium phosphate crystal. By weight, phosphate is its largest inorganic part, and calcium is not offered.<br>A. Collagen: organic, and enamel has none.<br>B. Sodium: a trace ion.<br>D. Carbonate: a minor substitute in the crystal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","n":"11 extra 40"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Collagen: in dentin, absent from enamel","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-42.jpg","cap":"Ameloblasts secrete enamel matrix; dentine is ninety percent type I collagen","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-2","s":"20. The major organic component of dentin is","o":["A. collagen","B. lactate","C. hydroxyapatite","D. lipid","E. desmosine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"collagen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Collagen.</b> Odontoblasts secrete predentin made mostly of <b>type I collagen</b> (the lecture says about ninety percent of the organic part), which then mineralizes.<br>B. Lactate: a metabolite, not matrix.<br>C. Hydroxyapatite: the inorganic mineral, about seventy percent of dentin.<br>D. Lipid: trace only.<br>E. Desmosine: a cross-link of elastin, not dentin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 41"},{"id":"F1221-226-3","s":"18. All of the following cell types may produce collagen fibers except?","o":["A. odontoblasts","B. ameloblasts","C. cementoblasts","D. osteoblasts","E. Chondroblasts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ameloblasts","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ameloblasts.</b> Ameloblasts secrete an enamel matrix of proteins such as amelogenin with <b>no collagen</b>. All the others build collagen-based matrices.<br>A. Odontoblasts: type I collagen of dentin.<br>C. Cementoblasts: collagen of cementum.<br>D. Osteoblasts: type I collagen of bone (osteoid).<br>E. Chondroblasts: type II collagen of cartilage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 42"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Cementoblasts first make cementoid","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Cementum layer over root dentin, laid down by cementoblasts","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-226-1","s":"16. Cementoblasts first produce","o":["A. secondary cementum","B. Sharpey's fibers","C. cementoid","D. primary cementum","E. cementocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"cementoid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cementoid.</b> Like osteoblasts making osteoid and odontoblasts making predentin, cementoblasts first secrete an <b>uncalcified matrix, cementoid</b>, which then mineralizes into cementum.<br>A. Secondary cementum: later, cellular cementum.<br>B. Sharpey's fibers: ligament collagen trapped in cementum and bone.<br>D. Primary cementum: the first mineralized (acellular) layer, formed from cementoid.<br>E. Cementocytes: cementoblasts that became trapped in their matrix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 43"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Periodontal ligament inserts into cementum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Periodontal ligament between root cementum and alveolar bone","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-190-1","s":"10. Most principal fibers of the periodontal ligament insert into","o":["A. enamel","B. cementum","C. dentin","D. free gingiva"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"cementum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cementum.</b> The periodontal ligament's collagen bundles (Sharpey fibers) run from the <b>cementum of the root to the alveolar bone</b>, slinging the tooth in its socket.<br>A. Enamel: covers the crown above the gum; nothing inserts there.<br>C. Dentin: lies under the cementum; the fibers do not reach it.<br>D. Free gingiva: gum tissue held by separate gingival fibers, not the principal ligament fibers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 44"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Scurvy loosens teeth: collagen needs vitamin C","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Tooth diagram: periodontal ligament anchors root cementum to alveolar bone","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Vitamin C (ascorbate) is needed for prolyl hydroxylation of collagen","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G031-11.jpg","cap":"Scurvy: sore, spongy, bleeding gums and petechiae","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-179-3","s":"9. Avitaminosis (scurvy) results in a loosening of the teeth in the sockets chiefly because ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is needed by","o":["A. osteoblasts to form bone","B. cells to produce collagen","C. odontoblasts to form dentin","D. cells to fight viruses","E. cementoblasts to form cementum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"cells to produce collagen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cells need vitamin C to make collagen.</b> Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is the cofactor for the prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases that finish collagen. The <b>periodontal ligament is collagen with fast turnover</b>, so in scurvy it weakens and teeth loosen; gums get spongy and bleed.<br>A. Osteoblasts forming bone: bone suffers too, but loose teeth come from the ligament.<br>C. Odontoblasts forming dentin: dentin does not anchor the tooth.<br>D. Fighting viruses: not the reason.<br>E. Cementoblasts: cementum is only the root's covering.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 45"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous lining","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-55.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 55"},{"src":"sl/G018-23.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, submucosal mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23","lg":[["SS","stratified squamous epithelium, nonkeratinized"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-24.jpg","cap":"Esophagus transmural: squamous lining over inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-179-2","s":"8. In humans, the lining epithelium of the esophagus is","o":["A. transitional","B. simple columnar","C. stratified squamous, keratinized","D. stratified squamous, non-keratinized","E. stratified cuboidal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"stratified squamous, non-keratinized","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stratified squamous, nonkeratinized.</b> The esophagus has a thick, wet, <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b> lining that resists friction from swallowed food.<br>A. Transitional: urinary tract.<br>B. Simple columnar: stomach and intestines (and Barrett esophagus, which is abnormal).<br>C. Keratinized: skin, gingiva and hard palate.<br>E. Stratified cuboidal: sweat gland ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 46"},{"id":"F1221-166-1","s":"4. Which of the following lacks goblet cells?","o":["A. nasal cavity","B. ileum","C. trachea","D. esophagus","E. vermiform appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"esophagus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Esophagus.</b> The esophagus is lined by <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b>, which has no goblet cells; its mucus comes from submucosal esophageal glands.<br>A. Nasal cavity: respiratory epithelium with goblet cells.<br>B. Ileum: many goblet cells (they increase from duodenum to ileum).<br>C. Trachea: respiratory epithelium with goblet cells.<br>E. Appendix: colon-type crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 47"},{"id":"F3980-64-1","s":"From what organ of the digestive tract is this section?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Stomach","C. Duodenum","D. Jejunum","E. Ileum","F. Large intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Esophagus.</b> A <b>thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (surface cells keep their nuclei), with connective tissue papillae pushing up into it, lines the esophagus. Every other choice has simple columnar epithelium.<br>B. Stomach: surface mucous cells and pits.<br>C. Duodenum: villi, plus Brunner glands.<br>D. Jejunum: tall villi, prominent plicae.<br>E. Ileum: villi with Peyer's patches.<br>F. Large intestine: straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-64.jpg","in":"Thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with papillae of lamina propria pushing up into it.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":135,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 48"},{"id":"F3955-28-1","s":"From which organ could this section could have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophagus.</b> A <b>thick stratified squamous epithelium whose surface cells keep their nuclei</b> (nonkeratinized) is esophageal mucosa.<br>A. Outer lip: thin keratinized skin with hair follicles; its surface cells have no nuclei.<br>B. Stomach: simple columnar epithelium with pits.<br>D. Jejunum: villi lined by enterocytes and goblet cells.<br>E. Colon: straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-28.jpg","in":"High power: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with nucleated surface cells over lamina propria.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":34,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":38,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":147,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 49"},{"id":"F0052-149-1","s":"Q11. From which organ could this section could have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophagus.</b> A <b>stratified squamous lining</b>, a submucosa with mucous glands, and a <b>very thick muscularis externa</b> of interlacing bundles fit the esophagus. The squamous lining alone rules out every gut organ below it.<br>A. Outer lip: thin skin with hair follicles over a skeletal muscle core; no muscularis externa.<br>B. Stomach: simple columnar epithelium with pits.<br>D. Jejunum: villi.<br>E. Colon: crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-149.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: stratified squamous epithelium on the right, submucosa with glands, and very thick muscularis externa of interlacing bundles to the left.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":149,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G018-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 50"},{"id":"G018-21-1","s":"From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer surface of lip","B. Inner surface of lip","C. Tongue","D. Oral mucosa","E. Esophagus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Esophagus.</b> The low-power picture shows a whole wall built in layers. On top is a thick stratified squamous epithelium. Under it lies connective tissue with vessels and a gland. Deeper still are <b>two layers of muscle at right angles</b>. The first is cut along its length and shows long pink bands. The second is cut across and shows bundles of round and polygonal profiles. The high-power picture shows that the flat surface cells still have nuclei, so the epithelium is <b>nonkeratinized</b>. A nonkeratinized stratified squamous lining over a muscle coat in two neat layers is the esophagus. The thick fibers are skeletal muscle, which places the section in the upper third.</p><p><b>The esophagus.</b> It is a muscular tube about 25 cm (10 inches) long that carries food from the pharynx to the stomach. An upper and a lower sphincter close its two ends.</p><p><b>Its wall, layer by layer.</b></p><ul><li><b>Epithelium:</b> thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous. It resists the rubbing of food. Like every stratified squamous lining, it has no goblet cells.</li><li><b>Lamina propria:</b> loose connective tissue. Near the stomach it holds small mucous glands, the esophageal cardiac glands.</li><li><b>Muscularis mucosae:</b> a single layer of longitudinal smooth muscle, incomplete near the top.</li><li><b>Submucosa:</b> holds the esophageal glands. These are mucous glands whose ducts cross the mucosa to lubricate the lumen. It also holds a rich plexus of veins.</li><li><b>Muscularis externa:</b> inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The muscle is skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third and smooth in the lower third.</li><li><b>Outer coat:</b> adventitia, connective tissue with no mesothelium, in the neck and chest. A serosa covers only the last 1 to 2 cm, below the diaphragm.</li></ul><p><b>What sets the esophagus apart from the mouth.</b> The inner lip, cheek and tongue have the same nonkeratinized epithelium. But the mouth has no muscularis mucosae and no muscle coat in two layers at right angles. The muscle of the lip is one sheet, the orbicularis oris, with salivary glands under the lining. The muscle of the tongue runs in all three planes, and its top surface carries papillae. The outer surface of the lip is thin skin with hair follicles.</p><p><b>Esophageal varices.</b> When pressure in the portal vein rises (portal hypertension, for example from alcoholic cirrhosis), the veins of the submucosa swell into varices. They can burst and cause vomiting of blood.</p><p><b>Infections of the lining.</b> In people with weak immunity, for example acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), the yeast <i>Candida</i> can grow on the lining. It forms raised white plaques on red, swollen mucosa and makes swallowing painful. Herpes simplex virus instead makes small punched-out ulcers.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-21.jpg","in":"Two pictures: low power of a wall with stratified squamous epithelium on one side, glands and vessels beneath, then two layers of skeletal muscle at right angles; high power of the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 53 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":21,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Candida esophagitis: white plaques, immunocompromised","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-107.jpg","cap":"Endoscopy: patchy white Candida plaques on red esophageal mucosa","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 107"},{"src":"sl/F3955-109.jpg","cap":"Candida hyphae in esophageal squamous debris; silver stain turns fungus black","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 109"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-104-1","s":"A 30-year-old man with AIDS complains of severe pain on swallowing. Upper GI endoscopy shows elevated, white plaques on a hyperemic and edematous esophageal mucosa. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?","o":["A. Barrett esophagus","B. Candida esophagitis","C. Herpetic esophagitis","D. Reflux esophagitis","E. Squamous cell carcinoma in situ"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Candida esophagitis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Candida esophagitis.</b> A patient with AIDS (acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), <b>painful swallowing</b> and <b>raised white plaques</b> on red mucosa has Candida esophagitis. The plaques are fungal hyphae, fibrin and dead cells.<br>A. Barrett esophagus: salmon-pink columnar patches from reflux.<br>C. Herpetic esophagitis: vesicles and small punched-out ulcers.<br>D. Reflux esophagitis: redness and erosions near the stomach junction.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma in situ: a flat or irregular lesion, not white plaques.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":104,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 51"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Esophageal varices: dilated submucosal veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: submucosa holds the large blood and lymphatic vessels","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G015-58.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: mucosa, muscularis mucosae, then submucosa with gland and duct","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-28-1","s":"A 45-year-old male alcoholic with a history of portal hypertension presents with vomiting of blood (hematemesis) and hypotension. He denies any history of vomiting non-blood material or retching prior to vomiting blood. During the work-up he dies suddenly. Which of the following histologic changes is most likely to be seen in a biopsy specimen taken from his esophagus?","o":["A. Metaplastic columnar epithelium","B. Decreased ganglion cells in myenteric plexus","C. Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa","D. Mucosal outpouchings","E. Numerous intraepithelial neutrophils"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa.</b> An alcoholic with portal hypertension who vomits blood without retching has <b>esophageal varices</b>: swollen veins in the submucosa of the lower esophagus that link the portal and systemic circulations. They can burst and bleed fatally.<br>A. Metaplastic columnar epithelium: Barrett esophagus from acid reflux.<br>B. Fewer myenteric ganglion cells: achalasia.<br>D. Mucosal outpouchings: diverticula.<br>E. Many neutrophils in the epithelium: acute esophagitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 52"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Esophagus thirds: skeletal, mixed, smooth","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Diagram: upper third skeletal, middle mixed, lower third smooth muscle","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle third: big skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section; text lists the muscularis externa by thirds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-1","s":"1. The esophagus is subdivided into three portions along its course on the basis of a transition in the composition of the","o":["A. submucosa","B. mucosal layer","C. adventitia","D. muscularis externa","E. none of the above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"muscularis externa","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis externa.</b> The esophagus is divided into thirds by the <b>muscle type of its muscularis externa</b>: skeletal (upper), mixed (middle), smooth (lower).<br>A. Submucosa: has mucous glands all along; does not define the thirds.<br>B. Mucosal layer: nonkeratinized stratified squamous throughout.<br>C. Adventitia: outer coat all the way to the diaphragm.<br>E. None of the above: D is correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 53"},{"id":"F0754-158-1","s":"The bottom ⅓ of the esophagus’ muscularis externa is composed of what type of muscle?","o":["A. Skeletal and smooth muscle","B. Skeletal muscle","C. Smooth muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Smooth muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Smooth muscle.</b> The lower third of the esophageal muscularis externa is <b>smooth muscle only</b>, like the rest of the gut below it.<br>A. Skeletal and smooth: the middle third.<br>B. Skeletal: the upper third, continuous with the pharyngeal muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":158,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 54"},{"id":"F3950-34-1","s":"A 58-year-old male presents to the clinic with a 3-month history of progressive dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) for solids and liquids. He reports occasional chest pain and regurgitation of food. Upon examination, the patient appears to have lost weight and shows signs of dehydration. A barium swallow study reveals a significant narrowing in the lower third of the esophagus. Which of the following best describes the muscular composition of the esophagus in this patient?","o":["A. The entire esophagus is composed of smooth muscle.","B. The upper third is composed of skeletal muscle, the middle third is a mix of skeletal and smooth muscle, and the lower third is composed of smooth muscle.","C. The entire esophagus is composed of skeletal muscle.","D. The upper third is composed of smooth muscle, the middle third is skeletal muscle, and the lower third is composed of striated muscle."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The upper third is composed of skeletal muscle, the middle third is a mix of skeletal and smooth muscle, and the lower third is composed of smooth muscle.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Upper third skeletal, middle third mixed, lower third smooth.</b> Swallowing starts with voluntary (skeletal) muscle and finishes with involuntary peristalsis, so the <b>muscularis externa shifts from skeletal to smooth</b> along the esophagus.<br>A. All smooth: wrong for the upper two thirds.<br>C. All skeletal: wrong for the lower two thirds.<br>D. Smooth on top, striated at the bottom: the order is reversed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":34,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 55"},{"id":"F3955-36-1","s":"What portion of the esophagus does this section corresponds to?","o":["A. Upper third","B. Middle third","C. Lower third"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle third","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle third.</b> The muscularis externa here <b>mixes large pink skeletal fibers</b> (nuclei at the edge of each fiber) <b>with bundles of small smooth muscle cells</b> (one central nucleus each). A mix of both types occurs only in the middle third.<br>A. Upper third: skeletal fibers only.<br>C. Lower third: smooth muscle only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-52.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa with large pink cross-cut skeletal fibers (peripheral nuclei) mixed with smooth muscle bundles (central nuclei).","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":36,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 56"},{"id":"F0745-20-1","s":"Name the layers, what section is this from (upper, mid, or lower)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Middle third. Left: inner circular smooth muscle layer of the muscularis externa (cut lengthwise); middle: thin connective tissue with nerve bundles (myenteric plexus zone); right: outer longitudinal layer cut across, mixing large skeletal fibers with smooth muscle bundles","a":null,"why":"<b>Middle third of the esophagus.</b> The right layer mixes <b>large pink skeletal fibers with small smooth muscle cells</b>, which happens only in the middle third. In this cross-section the left band of smooth muscle cut lengthwise is the <b>inner circular layer</b>; the loose tissue with nerves between the layers is the <b>myenteric plexus</b> zone; the right is the <b>outer longitudinal layer</b>, cut across.<br>Common mix-up: the left band is not muscularis mucosae, a thin layer right under the epithelium.","ex":"","fl":["The source labeled the left band muscularis mucosae and the middle zone submucosa, but this micrograph is a cross-section of the muscularis externa alone: inner circular smooth muscle, the myenteric plexus zone, and an outer layer of mixed skeletal and smooth fibers."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F0745-20.jpg","in":"H&E of esophageal wall: band of smooth muscle at far left, loose connective tissue with vessels/nerves in the middle, and a muscle layer at right containing large cross-cut striated fibres mixed with smooth muscle bundles.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":20,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 57"},{"id":"G118-12-1","s":"Which segement of the esophagus is characterized by this type of muscularis externa?","o":["A. Top 1/3","B. Mid 1/3","C. Bottom 1/3"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mid 1/3","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Mid 1/3.</b> The picture shows muscle cut lengthwise, and it holds two kinds of muscle in one field. On the right are wide, straight fibers crossed by fine, regular stripes called <b>cross-striations</b>. Their nuclei are pushed to the edge of each fiber. That is skeletal muscle. On the left are thin, spindle-shaped cells with <b>no stripes</b> and one long nucleus in the middle of each cell. That is smooth muscle. Skeletal and smooth muscle side by side are found only in the middle third of the esophagus.</p><p><b>The esophagus in thirds.</b> The muscularis externa is the thick outer muscle coat of the tube. In the esophagus its muscle type changes from top to bottom.</p><ul><li><b>Upper third:</b> skeletal muscle. Swallowing starts as a voluntary act.</li><li><b>Middle third:</b> skeletal and smooth muscle mixed.</li><li><b>Lower third:</b> smooth muscle only, like the rest of the gut.</li></ul><p>The esophagus is the only part of the digestive tube with skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa. The external anal sphincter is skeletal too, but it is a separate sphincter muscle.</p><p><b>Telling the two muscles apart.</b></p><ul><li><b>Skeletal fibers:</b> large, pink and cross-striated. Each fiber has many nuclei, and they lie at its edge. Cut lengthwise, the fibers are wide bands crossed by fine stripes.</li><li><b>Smooth muscle cells:</b> small spindles, wide in the middle and tapered at both ends. Each has one central nucleus and no stripes.</li></ul><p><b>Why the muscle changes.</b> The top of the esophagus continues from the pharynx, where a swallow begins under voluntary control. That needs skeletal muscle. Further down, the gut's own nerve network takes over and moves the food by automatic waves called peristalsis. That is the work of smooth muscle.</p><p><b>Reading a cross-section of the tube.</b> The muscularis externa has an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer.</p><ul><li>The inner circular layer is cut lengthwise, so its cells look long.</li><li>The outer longitudinal layer is cut across, so its fibers look like round or polygonal profiles.</li><li>The myenteric (Auerbach) nerve plexus sits in the thin connective tissue between the two layers.</li></ul><p>Do not confuse the muscularis externa with the muscularis mucosae. The muscularis mucosae is the thin band of smooth muscle right under the epithelium, and it is smooth muscle at every level.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-12.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa cut lengthwise, H&E: on the right, wide fibers with cross-striations and nuclei at their edges (skeletal muscle); on the left, spindle cells with no striations and long central nuclei (smooth muscle). Both muscle types in one field.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":12,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Striated muscularis externa means esophagus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Esophageal muscularis externa by thirds: skeletal, skeletal plus smooth, smooth","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle esophagus: large skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-166-2","s":"5. Which one of the following has skeletal muscle in the muscularis externa?","o":["A. colon","B. appendix","C. stomach","D. esophagus","E. small intestine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"esophagus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Esophagus.</b> It is the only part of the digestive tube with <b>skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa</b>: all skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third.<br>A. Colon: smooth muscle; outer layer forms teniae coli.<br>B. Appendix: smooth muscle.<br>C. Stomach: three layers of smooth muscle.<br>E. Small intestine: smooth muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 58"},{"id":"F3980-52-1","s":"You are working in a pathology lab and are given an unlabeled slide from the GI system. The mucosa and submucosa are poorly preserved, with only the thick muscularis well-stained, showing striated fibers. Which region of the GI tract is this most likely?","o":["A. Pyloric sphincter","B. Esophagus","C. Colon","D. Corpus of the stomach","E. Ileum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Esophagus.</b> The esophagus is the only part of the tube with <b>striated (skeletal) muscle in its muscularis externa</b>: skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third, smooth in the lower third.<br>A. Pyloric sphincter: thickened smooth circular muscle.<br>C. Colon: smooth muscle; outer layer in three teniae coli.<br>D. Body of the stomach: three smooth muscle layers.<br>E. Ileum: smooth muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":52,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":139,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 59"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Achalasia: myenteric plexus lost at LES","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between muscle layers; submucosal (Meissner) plexus","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G016-6.jpg","cap":"Myenteric plexus between inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, 100x","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-37-1","s":"A 49-year-old woman presents to hospital with increasing problems swallowing food (progressive dysphasia). X-ray studies with contrast reveal that she has a markedly dilated esophagus above the level of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). No lesions are seen within the lumen of the esophagus. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this disorder?","o":["A. Decreased LES resting pressure","B. Absence of myenteric plexus in the body of the esophagus","C. Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES","D. Absence of submucosal plexus in the body of the esophagus","E. Absence of submucosal plexus at the LES"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES.</b> LES stands for lower esophageal sphincter, the ring of circular muscle that closes the bottom of the esophagus. Her esophagus is widely dilated above the sphincter, and nothing is blocking the lumen. So the sphincter itself is failing to open. This is <b>achalasia</b>. The sphincter depends on the nerve cells of the <b>myenteric plexus</b> to make it relax during a swallow. When those nerve cells are lost at the sphincter, it stays shut.</p><p><b>The chain of events.</b></p><ol><li>At rest, the circular muscle at the lower end stays contracted. This is the lower esophageal sphincter. It keeps stomach contents out of the esophagus.</li><li>During a normal swallow, nerve cells of the myenteric plexus make the sphincter relax, and the food passes into the stomach.</li><li>In achalasia, the myenteric nerve cells at the sphincter are lost. The sphincter cannot relax.</li><li>Food piles up above it, and the esophagus above the sphincter widens.</li></ol><p><b>How it presents.</b> Swallowing gets steadily harder, for <b>both solids and liquids</b>. Difficulty swallowing is called dysphagia. A contrast X-ray shows a wide esophagus that narrows to a point at the sphincter, a \"bird's beak\" shape. No mass is seen, because the problem is a muscle that will not open.</p><p><b>Why the myenteric plexus.</b> The gut wall holds two nerve networks, and each has its own job.</p><ul><li><b>Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus:</b> lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, the thick outer muscle coat. It controls the contraction and relaxation of that muscle: peristalsis (the wave that pushes food along) and the rest of gut motility.</li><li><b>Submucosal (Meissner) plexus:</b> lies in the submucosa, just under the mucosa. It controls gland secretion, local blood flow and the movements of the mucosa.</li></ul><p>A sphincter is part of the muscularis externa, so its relaxation is a job of the myenteric plexus.</p><p><b>Why at the sphincter.</b> The widening begins right above the sphincter. That places the failure at the sphincter, and not in the body of the esophagus above it.</p><p><b>The sphincter pressure in achalasia is high.</b> A sphincter that cannot relax holds a high pressure. A weak sphincter with low pressure is the opposite problem. It lets acid flow back up from the stomach and causes gastroesophageal reflux disease, with heartburn and an esophagus of normal width.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 5 (ENTERIC NERVOUS SYSTEM)","H15B slide 6 (100x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":37,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Esophagogastric junction: squamous to columnar","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-63.jpg","cap":"Esophagogastric junction: squamous esophagus meets columnar cardia with gastric pits","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 63","lg":[["E","esophagus (stratified squamous epithelium)"],["C","cardia of the stomach (simple columnar epithelium)"],["GP","gastric pits"],["CG","cardiac glands"],["ECG","esophageal cardiac glands (mucous)"],["arrow (lower, unlabeled)","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle strands)"],["SM","submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G017-7.jpg","cap":"Squamocolumnar junction on histology and endoscopy: Z-line between white and tan","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 7","lg":[["Es","esophagus"],["SE, SSE","stratified squamous epithelium (esophagus)"],["CS","cardiac region of the stomach"],["CE, SCE","simple columnar epithelium (stomach)"],["arrows","gastric pits in the cardiac mucosa"],["box","junction area enlarged in the inset"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["ECG","esophageal cardiac glands"],["EG","esophageal glands in the submucosa"],["Sm","submucosa"],["ME","muscularis externa"],["Se","serosa"],["red triangle","stratified squamous epithelium (white, esophagus)"],["black arrow (endoscopy)","Z-line (squamocolumnar junction)"],["green star","simple columnar epithelium (tan, stomach)"],["yellow dot","lumen leading to the stomach"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-4-1","s":"At the esophagogastric junction, the epithelial lining of the digestive tract undergoes an abrupt change. Which epithelial types are present on each side of this junction?","o":["A. Simple columnar and stratified squamous nonkeratinized","B. Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar","C. Stratified squamous keratinized and simple cuboidal","D. Simple cuboidal and pseudostratified columnar","E. Transitional and stratified squamous nonkeratinized"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar","a":"B","why":"<b>A and B. Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar.</b> At the esophagogastric junction the esophagus's <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b> epithelium changes abruptly to the stomach's <b>simple columnar</b> epithelium. A and B name the same pair (B in esophagus-to-stomach order).<br>C. Keratinized and cuboidal: neither lines this junction.<br>D. Cuboidal and pseudostratified columnar: duct and airway types.<br>E. Transitional: urinary tract only.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only B, but A names the same two epithelia in reverse order and the stem sets no order, so A and B are both correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":4,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-31-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 60"},{"id":"F3980-31-1","s":"Where is this?","o":["A. Transition from esophagus to cardia of stomach","B. Transition from cardia to body of stomach","C. Transition from pylorus to duodenum","D. Transition from rectum to anus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Transition from esophagus to cardia of stomach","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Transition from esophagus to cardia of stomach.</b> The picture shows two different linings that meet at one sharp line. On the left is a thick, many-layered pink epithelium with flat cells at its surface: <b>stratified squamous epithelium</b>. On the right the lining suddenly becomes a single row of tall, pale cells that dips down into pits, with round, pale glands below it: <b>simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits and mucous glands</b>. Esophagus on one side and stomach on the other is the esophagogastric junction, where the esophagus joins the cardia of the stomach.</p><p><b>The two sides.</b></p><ul><li><b>Esophagus side:</b> nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, built to resist the rubbing of food.</li><li><b>Stomach side:</b> simple columnar epithelium of surface mucous cells, which make protective mucus. The sheet dips down as gastric pits, and glands open into the bottoms of the pits. There are no goblet cells.</li></ul><p><b>What else lies at the junction.</b> The change of lining is abrupt, with no gradual zone. Mucus-secreting glands open on both sides: the esophageal cardiac glands in the esophagus and the cardiac glands in the stomach. Strands of muscularis mucosae, the thin smooth muscle of the mucosa, separate the mucosa from the submucosa. The junction lies at the lower esophageal sphincter.</p><p><b>The cardia.</b> The cardia is the narrow ring of stomach, 1.5 to 3 cm wide, around the entry of the esophagus. Its glands are short, coiled mucous glands. Its job is protection by mucus.</p><p><b>The junctions of the digestive tube, and how each looks.</b></p><ul><li><b>Esophagus to stomach:</b> stratified squamous meets simple columnar. The columnar side has gastric pits and no goblet cells.</li><li><b>Rectum to anal canal (the pectinate line):</b> the only other place where simple columnar meets stratified squamous. Here the columnar side has straight crypts packed with goblet cells.</li><li><b>Stomach to duodenum, and ileum to cecum:</b> two columnar linings meet. No squamous epithelium is present. In the duodenum, villi appear and glands fill the submucosa.</li><li><b>Cardia to body of the stomach:</b> both sides are gastric mucosa with the same columnar surface. Only the glands change.</li></ul><p><b>Why this line matters.</b> The normal esophagus is squamous all the way down to the junction. After years of acid reflux, columnar epithelium with goblet cells can replace the squamous lining just above the junction. That change is called Barrett esophagus.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-31.jpg","in":"Abrupt junction: thick stratified squamous epithelium on the left meets simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits and mucous glands on the right.","tg":["H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":31,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":74,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":223,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":169,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"},{"id":"F0755-189-1","s":"Digital slides illustrating various organs of the GI tract are examined in the histology laboratory. The specimen shown in the image was obtained from which of the following anatomic locations?","o":["A. Esophagogastric junction","B. Fundus of the stomach","C. Gastroduodenal junction","D. Ileocecal junction","E. Pylorus of the stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagogastric junction","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Esophagogastric junction.</b> The section shows <b>stratified squamous epithelium (right) changing abruptly to glandular gastric (cardiac) mucosa with pits (left)</b>, here with a lymphoid aggregate at the junction.<br>B. Fundus: simple columnar throughout, with deep glands full of parietal cells.<br>C. Gastroduodenal junction: gastric pits change to villi and Brunner glands; no squamous epithelium.<br>D. Ileocecal junction: villi change to colonic crypts.<br>E. Pylorus: deep pits and short coiled mucous glands, no squamous epithelium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-189.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: stratified squamous epithelium at the right end of the section abruptly continuous with glandular (gastric cardiac) mucosa to the left; a lymphoid aggregate at the junction; thick muscularis below.","tg":["H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 82 (Q4- Digital slides illustrating various organs of the GI tract are examined in t)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":189,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-31-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 61"},{"id":"F4081-8-1","s":"A 45-year-old man undergoes an upper endoscopy for dysphagia. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows a transition from non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Which of the following normal anatomic transitions in epithelial type occurs at the same boundary?","o":["A. Simple columnar epithelium → simple cuboidal epithelium","B. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","C. Simple columnar epithelium → non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","D. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → simple columnar epithelium","E. Simple cuboidal epithelium → stratified columnar epithelium"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → simple columnar epithelium","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous → simple columnar.</b> The biopsy shows Barrett esophagus at the distal end. The normal boundary there is the <b>esophagogastric junction</b>, where esophageal stratified squamous epithelium gives way to the stomach's simple columnar epithelium.<br>A. Columnar → cuboidal: not a gut junction.<br>B. Squamous → ciliated pseudostratified: an airway junction (pharynx to larynx or nasal cavity).<br>C. Columnar → squamous: the anal canal's order (rectum to anus), reversed here.<br>E. Cuboidal → stratified columnar: duct epithelia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":8,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-31-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 62"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: symptoms, treatment, biopsy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-57.jpg","cap":"Normal esophageal mucosa: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 57","lg":[["SS","nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium"],["LP","lamina propria"],["MM","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle)"],["D","ducts of the esophageal glands"],["GL","esophageal mucous glands in the submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Reflux disease progression: normal lining, Barrett, dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-2-1","s":"A 35 year-old man comes to your office complaining of several months of heartburn unresponsive to oral antacids. He noted an intermittent burning sensation in the center of his chest, particularly following dinner when he typically sits in his recliner to watch a favorite TV show. Occasionally he notes a sour taste in his mouth and a sensation of something stuck in his throat. He keeps a container of TUMS by his chair because they typically provide some relief. Other than TUMS, he is not currently taking any medication. He gave no history of cancer, radiation therapy or immunosuppressive conditions. He loves coffee and drinks four or five cups per day. He often has a beer while watching TV after dinner. On physical examination, he was noted to be overweight (BMI 29); no additional contributory findings were identified. What is your impression and what are your recommendations?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Reflux esophagitis / gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD); lifestyle modifications (smaller dinner, sit upright, cut back on beer and coffee), a proton pump inhibitor, return in 8 weeks","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) with reflux esophagitis.</b> Burning chest pain after big meals while reclining, a sour taste, and relief from antacids mean stomach acid is flowing back through a weak lower esophageal sphincter; coffee, alcohol and extra weight worsen it. Treat with <b>lifestyle changes</b> (smaller dinner, sit upright, less beer and coffee) plus a <b>proton pump inhibitor</b>, and recheck in eight weeks.<br>Common mix-up: untreated reflux can lead to Barrett esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-8-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 63"},{"id":"G017-8-1","s":"A 35-year-old man with months of heartburn after dinner has an upper endoscopy. The esophageal biopsy shows stratified squamous epithelium with a thickened basal cell layer, vascular papillae that reach up toward the surface, and scattered eosinophils within the epithelium. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Barrett esophagus","B. Reflux esophagitis","C. Esophageal adenocarcinoma","D. Candida esophagitis","E. Achalasia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Reflux esophagitis","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Reflux esophagitis.</b> Two parts of the story fit together. He has had months of heartburn after dinner, which is the symptom of acid flowing back from the stomach. And the biopsy shows the three changes that acid produces in the squamous lining: a <b>thickened basal cell layer</b>, <b>papillae that reach up toward the surface</b> and <b>eosinophils inside the epithelium</b>. The epithelium is still stratified squamous. It is inflamed, but it has not changed into another type. That is reflux esophagitis.</p><p><b>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</b> Acid from the stomach flows back into the lower esophagus when the lower esophageal sphincter does not close well.</p><ul><li><b>Triggers:</b> large meals, lying down after eating, obesity, coffee, alcohol and smoking.</li><li><b>Symptoms:</b> heartburn, which is a burning pain behind the breastbone, and a sour taste. Antacids ease them.</li><li><b>Treatment:</b> lifestyle changes come first: smaller meals, no eating before lying down, weight loss, less coffee and alcohol. Then come acid-lowering drugs, the proton pump inhibitors, which block the pump that secretes acid in the stomach.</li></ul><p><b>The three biopsy changes, and what each means.</b></p><ol><li><b>Basal cell hyperplasia.</b> The basal layer is the deepest layer, where new cells are made. Acid destroys surface cells faster, so the basal layer divides more and grows thicker.</li><li><b>Elongated papillae.</b> The papillae are fingers of lamina propria (the connective tissue under the epithelium), with their small vessels, that push up into the epithelium. They stretch up toward the surface.</li><li><b>Inflammatory cells inside the epithelium.</b> Eosinophils, neutrophils and lymphocytes appear between the squamous cells.</li></ol><p><b>The normal lining, for comparison.</b> The esophagus is lined by thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Its basal layer is thin, its papillae are short, and it holds no inflammatory cells. Under it lie the lamina propria and the muscularis mucosae, a thin sheet of smooth muscle.</p><p><b>Other causes of esophagitis to rule out.</b></p><ul><li><b>Eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis:</b> an allergic inflammation that crowds the epithelium with eosinophils.</li><li><b>Infections:</b> <i>Candida</i> (a yeast that forms white plaques), herpes simplex virus (punched-out ulcers) and cytomegalovirus.</li><li><b>Pills</b> that stick in the esophagus.</li><li><b>Radiation.</b></li></ul><p><b>Where years of reflux can lead.</b> The squamous lining of the lower esophagus can be replaced by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. That is Barrett esophagus. It is a separate diagnosis, because there the epithelium itself has changed type.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Left: normal esophagus (SSE, LP, MM). Right: biopsy of stratified squamous epithelium with elongated vascular papillae reaching toward the surface, thickened basal cell zone, and scattered intraepithelial eosinophils (labels are given in the stem).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":6,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: differential diagnosis","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G017-4-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-11: 35-year-old overweight man, months of post-dinner heartburn while reclining, sour taste, relieved by TUMS; coffee and beer after dinner.] In 8 weeks he returns noting his symptoms are still present; he has developed an occasional cough. You explain that in most cases of GERD, his symptoms should have responded to the treatment you prescribed. Since they did not, additional investigation is prudent to exclude the possibility of another cause. What are other diagnostic possibilities (differential diagnosis)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Eosinophilic esophagitis; infection; medications; pill esophagitis; radiation injury","a":null,"why":"<b>Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection, medications (pill esophagitis), and radiation injury.</b> Heartburn that does not respond to a proton pump inhibitor may not be acid reflux. Infections include fungi (<b>Candida</b>) and viruses (herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus). Pills that injure the lining include NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), doxycycline and ferrous sulfate.<br>Common mix-up: Barrett esophagus is a complication of reflux, not another cause of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 64"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Metaplasia: one mature epithelium replaces another","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Metaplasia example: squamous esophagus replaced by columnar (Barrett) epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-12-1","s":"In cigarette smokers, ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the airways may be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. In chronic reflux esophagitis, stratified squamous epithelium lining the esophagus may be replaced by simple columnar epithelium. This replacement of the one epithelial type by another epithelial type in response to injury is referred to as:","o":["A. Neoplasia","B. Metaplasia","C. Dysplasia","D. Differentiation","E. Quiescence"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Metaplasia","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Metaplasia.</b> Both examples in the question follow one pattern. A normal, mature epithelium meets a long-lasting injury, and a different normal, mature epithelium takes its place. The replacement of one mature cell type by another mature type is <b>metaplasia</b>. The new type is the one that withstands that injury better.</p><p><b>The two examples explained.</b></p><ul><li><b>The smoker's airway.</b> The normal lining is ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium, one layer of tall cells that looks like several layers. Its cilia sweep mucus and dust out of the airway. Smoke injures it, and it is replaced by stratified squamous epithelium, which is tougher. The cost is that the cleaning cilia are lost.</li><li><b>The acid-washed esophagus.</b> The normal lining is stratified squamous epithelium. Years of acid reflux replace it, just above the junction with the stomach, with simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. This is the intestinal type of lining. Columnar cells resist acid better. The condition is called <b>Barrett esophagus</b>.</li></ul><p><b>How the switch happens.</b> A mature cell does not turn into another mature cell. The stem cells of the epithelium are reprogrammed by the chronic injury. The new cells they produce then mature along a different line.</p><p><b>Metaplasia can reverse.</b> If the injury stops, the original epithelium can return. If the injury continues, the changed epithelium can move on to dysplasia.</p><p><b>The words to keep apart.</b></p><ul><li><b>Metaplasia:</b> one mature cell type is replaced by another mature type. The cells look normal. They are simply not the type that belongs in that place.</li><li><b>Dysplasia:</b> disordered growth of abnormal cells. It is a precancer.</li><li><b>Neoplasia:</b> new, uncontrolled growth. It is a tumor.</li><li><b>Differentiation:</b> the normal maturing of a cell into its specialized form.</li><li><b>Quiescence:</b> a resting state in which a cell is not dividing.</li></ul><p><b>Why metaplasia in the esophagus matters.</b> The normal esophagus is squamous all the way down to the junction with the stomach. Barrett epithelium can pass through low-grade dysplasia and then high-grade dysplasia to adenocarcinoma, a cancer of gland-forming columnar cells. So metaplasia protects against the injury, but it is also the first step on a path toward cancer.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":8,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Columnar esophageal lining means Barrett metaplasia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus beside abnormal columnar (Barrett) esophagus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-162-1","s":"GERD induces esophageal metaplasia into what type of cells?","o":["A. Simple columnar","B. Simple cuboidal","C. Simple Squamous","D. Simple urothelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Simple columnar.</b> In Barrett esophagus, acid reflux turns the esophageal <b>stratified squamous epithelium into simple columnar epithelium</b> (intestinal type, with goblet cells), so the lower esophagus starts to look like intestine.<br>B. Simple cuboidal: lines small ducts and kidney tubules.<br>C. Simple squamous: mesothelium and endothelium.<br>D. Urothelium: the transitional epithelium of the urinary tract.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":162,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 65"},{"id":"F3950-32-1","s":"A man comes in complaining of acid reflux. During the biopsy of the esophagus you find columnar epithelial cells in the lining of the esophagus. Is this normal?","o":["A. Yes","B. No"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"No","a":"B","why":"<b>B. No.</b> The esophagus is normally lined by <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> all the way to the esophagogastric junction, where it changes abruptly to the stomach's simple columnar epithelium. Columnar cells in the esophageal lining after long acid reflux mean <b>Barrett esophagus</b>, a metaplasia that raises adenocarcinoma risk.<br>Common mix-up: columnar cells belong just below the junction (cardia), not above it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":32,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 66"},{"id":"F0753-75-1","s":"What happens at this junction (esophagogastric junction) if a person has untreated GERD?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Barrett's esophagus – metaplasia of the distal esophageal stratified squamous epithelium into simple columnar (intestinal-type, goblet-cell-containing) epithelium","a":null,"why":"<b>Barrett esophagus.</b> Normally the junction is an abrupt change from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium. With untreated gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), acid makes the <b>distal esophageal squamous lining turn into simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (metaplasia), so the columnar zone creeps up the esophagus and adenocarcinoma risk rises.<br>Common mix-up: the change is squamous to columnar, not the reverse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":75,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":183,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 67"},{"id":"F0755-224-1","s":"In which location does Esophageal metaplasia tend to occur? What change of epithelium do you seen in esophageal metaplasia?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: esophagogastric junction (transition between esophagus and cardia of stomach); B: changes from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium","a":null,"why":"<b>Distal esophagus at the esophagogastric junction; stratified squamous becomes simple columnar.</b> Acid refluxing from the stomach hits the lowest esophagus first, so Barrett metaplasia starts there and creeps upward. The squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the metaplasia is in the esophagus, not in the stomach's cardia, which is normally columnar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Diagram of normal lining → Barrett's esophagus → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma, and an H&E of abnormal (columnar) esophagus.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":224,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 68"},{"id":"F4081-19-1","s":"Identify which is normal and which is metaplastic, its location and cause of disease","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = normal esophagus (nonkeratinized stratified squamous); right = abnormal (metaplastic) esophagus: columnar epithelium with goblet cells = Barrett's esophagus (distal esophagus), caused by GERD","a":null,"why":"<b>Left normal, right metaplastic (Barrett esophagus).</b> The left panel shows the normal <b>thick stratified squamous</b> lining. The right shows <b>glandular columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b>. It forms in the <b>distal esophagus near the esophagogastric junction</b> after long-standing acid reflux (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>Common mix-up: goblet cells are never normal in the esophagus or stomach; they mark intestinal-type metaplasia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-19.jpg","in":"Left: thick stratified squamous epithelium; right: glandular columnar epithelium with many goblet cells.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":19,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 69"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Heartburn plus red distal mucosa: Barrett esophagus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus versus abnormal columnar Barrett mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-10-1","s":"Three years after his reflux was controlled with a proton pump inhibitor, a man has a surveillance endoscopy. It shows salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extending up from the gastroesophageal junction into the pale squamous lining. The biopsy shows simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Reflux esophagitis","B. Barrett esophagus","C. Eosinophilic esophagitis","D. Hiatal hernia","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Barrett esophagus.</b> Two findings make the diagnosis. On endoscopy, <b>salmon-colored tongues</b> of mucosa reach up from the gastroesophageal junction into the pale squamous lining. On biopsy, the lower esophagus is lined by <b>simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> where stratified squamous epithelium should be. Columnar epithelium with goblet cells in the esophagus is Barrett esophagus. His years of reflux are the cause.</p><p><b>What Barrett esophagus is.</b> It is a metaplasia: one mature cell type is replaced by another that better withstands a chronic injury. Refluxed gastric acid (and bile) washes over the distal (lower) esophagus for years. The stratified squamous lining just above the junction turns into intestinal-type columnar epithelium, because columnar cells resist acid better. It is not caused by the stomach bacterium <i>Helicobacter pylori</i>, and it is not a motility problem.</p><p><b>How to recognize it.</b></p><ul><li><b>Story:</b> long-standing heartburn, worse after meals or lying down.</li><li><b>Endoscopy:</b> salmon-pink or red, velvety mucosa reaching up from the junction into the lower esophagus. Normal squamous lining is pale pinkish-white.</li><li><b>Biopsy:</b> columnar cells with goblet cells where squamous epithelium should be.</li></ul><p><b>Goblet cells are the key.</b> A goblet cell is a mucus-filled cell shaped like a wine glass, and it belongs to the intestine. Stomach mucosa, including the cardia, has no goblet cells. So columnar mucosa with goblet cells in the esophagus is intestinal metaplasia. It is not stomach lining that has moved upward.</p><p><b>Where Barrett can lead.</b></p><ol><li>Barrett esophagus (metaplasia).</li><li>Low-grade dysplasia. Dysplasia is disordered growth of abnormal cells, a precancer.</li><li>High-grade dysplasia.</li><li>Invasive adenocarcinoma, a cancer of gland-forming columnar cells.</li></ol><p>This is the reason for surveillance endoscopy. A proton pump inhibitor, a drug that lowers stomach acid, controls the symptoms. The changed lining still has to be checked for dysplasia.</p><p><b>The two cancers of the esophagus.</b></p><ul><li><b>Adenocarcinoma:</b> starts from Barrett (columnar) epithelium. It arises in the lower third. Main risk factors: chronic reflux and Barrett esophagus, obesity and smoking.</li><li><b>Squamous cell carcinoma:</b> starts from the normal squamous epithelium. It arises in the upper and middle thirds. Main risk factors: smoking and alcohol.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Endoscopy: salmon-coloured tongues of columnar mucosa (green stars) extending up into pale squamous mucosa (red triangle). Biopsy: columnar epithelium with goblet cells (stars) beside stratified squamous epithelium (triangle).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":7,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"},{"id":"F3961-42-1","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with increasing \"heartburn,\" especially after eating or when lying down. Endoscopic examination finds a red velvety plaque located the distal esophagus. Biopsies from this area, taken approximately 4 cm proximal to the gastroesophageal junction reveal metaplastic columnar epithelium as seen in the next slide. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Acquired achalasia","B. Barrett's esophagus","C. Hamartomatous polyp","D. Metastatic adenocarcinoma","E. Reflux esophagitis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett's esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett's esophagus.</b> Reflux symptoms, a <b>red velvety plaque</b> a few centimeters above the gastroesophageal junction, and <b>metaplastic columnar epithelium</b> on biopsy define Barrett esophagus.<br>A. Acquired achalasia: the lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax after loss of myenteric neurons.<br>C. Hamartomatous polyp: a disorganized growth of normal tissues.<br>D. Metastatic adenocarcinoma: cancer from elsewhere; nothing invasive is described.<br>E. Reflux esophagitis: inflamed but still squamous lining, no columnar change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":42,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 70"},{"id":"F3955-95-1","s":"A 55-year-old smoker comes in complaining of heartburn especially at night and before meals. These pains have been present for at least 20 years but seem to have worsened in the last two months. On endoscopy, you see an area of velvety red mucosa in the distal esophagus. An esophageal biopsy is taken. See the accompanying figure. The pathologic condition shown in this figure most likely represents which of the following?","o":["A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","B. Barrett esophagus","C. Candidiasis of the esophagus","D. Displaced gastric cardia mucosa","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett esophagus.</b> Decades of heartburn and <b>velvety red mucosa in the distal esophagus</b>, with squamous epithelium beside <b>glandular columnar mucosa with goblet cells</b>, is Barrett metaplasia.<br>A. Adenocarcinoma: invasive malignant glands; no invasion is described.<br>C. Candidiasis: white plaques with yeast and hyphae.<br>D. Displaced gastric cardia mucosa: gastric mucosa has no goblet cells.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: malignant squamous cells, not columnar glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Squamous epithelium (left) abruptly next to glandular columnar mucosa with goblet-cell-type glands (right).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":95,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 71"},{"id":"F3980-68-1","s":"A 55-year-old presents to his primary physician for chest pain. Endoscopy shows pink mucosa extending 5 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Biopsy on the lower esophagus shows nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells. What is the cause of this patient's condition?","o":["A. Uncontrolled growth of esophageal epithelium","B. H. pylori infection","C. Esophageal exposure to gastric acid","D. Incomplete relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal exposure to gastric acid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophageal exposure to gastric acid.</b> Pink columnar mucosa reaching up the lower esophagus, with <b>goblet cells</b> on biopsy, is Barrett esophagus: <b>metaplasia caused by long-term acid reflux</b> (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>A. Uncontrolled growth: describes cancer (neoplasia), not metaplasia.<br>B. Helicobacter pylori: gastritis and peptic ulcers of the stomach and duodenum.<br>D. Incomplete relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter: achalasia, which causes trouble swallowing, not acid damage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":68,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":126,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 72"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Barrett progresses to adenocarcinoma","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Normal lining, Barrett, low- then high-grade dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-39-1","s":"Barrett's Esophagus: What can this cause?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Invasive carcinoma (adenocarcinoma) – via low- to high-grade dysplasia","a":null,"why":"<b>Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.</b> Barrett esophagus can progress step by step: <b>normal squamous lining → Barrett metaplasia → low-grade dysplasia → high-grade dysplasia → invasive adenocarcinoma</b>. That is why these patients get surveillance endoscopy.<br>Common mix-up: squamous cell carcinoma comes from the squamous lining (smoking, alcohol), not from Barrett.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Side-by-side H&E: normal non-keratinized stratified squamous esophageal mucosa vs columnar glandular (Barrett) mucosa.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":39,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 73"},{"id":"F3955-88-1","s":"A 58-year-old man has a long history of heavy smoking, alcohol abuse, and severe heartburn. Two years ago, esophagoscopy revealed an area of ulceration 3 cm above the esophago-gastric junction. Biopsies of the ulcer edge revealed patches of intestinal type epithelium. Recently, the patient has experienced difficulty in swallowing solid foods and has lost 10 kg in body weight. The best working diagnosis is which of the following?","o":["A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","B. Bronchogenic carcinoma with invasion of the esophagus","C. Esophageal varices","D. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the oropharynx with obstruction"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.</b> Severe heartburn and patches of <b>intestinal-type epithelium</b> near the esophagogastric junction mean <b>Barrett esophagus</b>; new trouble swallowing solids and weight loss mean it has progressed to adenocarcinoma.<br>B. Bronchogenic carcinoma: a lung cancer, not linked to Barrett.<br>C. Varices: bleeding veins from portal hypertension.<br>D. Squamous cell carcinoma: arises from squamous lining (smoking, alcohol), usually mid-esophagus; Barrett leads to adenocarcinoma.<br>E. Oropharyngeal carcinoma: wrong site.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":88,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 74"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Rugae are stomach folds","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-29.jpg","cap":"Stomach transmural section: rugae, pits and glands, submucosa, muscularis, serosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G015-69.jpg","cap":"Stomach section with rugae, gastric pits and glands labelled","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-191-1","s":"Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Large intestine","C. Rectum/anal canal","D. Small intestine","E. Stomach"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Stomach.</b> <b>Rugae</b> are the large longitudinal folds of the stomach's mucosa and submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills.<br>A. Esophagus: longitudinal folds when collapsed, but not called rugae.<br>B. Large intestine: semilunar folds between haustra.<br>C. Rectum/anal canal: transverse rectal folds and anal columns.<br>D. Small intestine: plicae circulares, permanent circular folds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 84 (Q5- Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":191,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 75"},{"id":"F1211-197-1","s":"What area of the digestive tract is depicted histologically?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The Stomach! (rugae are the large folds in the stomach)","a":null,"why":"<b>Stomach.</b> The mucosa is thrown into <b>large folds with submucosal cores (rugae)</b>; it is thick and glandular with <b>no villi</b>, and a thick muscularis externa lies below. Rugae occur only in the stomach and flatten when it fills.<br>Common mix-up: plicae circulares of the small intestine are also folds with a submucosal core, but they are covered with villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1211-197.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: thick glandular mucosa thrown into large folds with submucosal cores, over a thick muscularis externa; no villi.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 84 (Q5- Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":197,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":199,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":201,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 76"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Stomach muscularis: oblique, circular, longitudinal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-68.jpg","cap":"Stomach diagram: longitudinal, circular and oblique muscle layers","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 68"},{"src":"sl/G018-27.jpg","cap":"Stomach muscularis externa: inner oblique, middle circular, outer longitudinal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-222-1","s":"The muscular layers of the stomach are arranged in which orientation?","o":["A. Longitudinal and circular","B. Circular only","C. Longitudinal and oblique","D. Longitudinal, circular and oblique"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Longitudinal, circular and oblique","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Longitudinal, circular and oblique.</b> The muscularis externa is the thick outer muscle coat of the digestive tube. In most of the tube it has two layers. The stomach has <b>three</b>. From the inside out they are the <b>inner oblique</b>, the <b>middle circular</b> and the <b>outer longitudinal</b> layers. All three are smooth muscle.</p><p><b>Why the stomach has a third layer.</b> The stomach does more than push food along. It churns. It mixes food with acid and enzymes until the food becomes a soupy paste called chyme. Muscle that runs in three directions can squeeze and knead from every side, so the extra oblique layer helps the churning.</p><p><b>The muscle coat along the tube.</b></p><ul><li><b>Usual plan:</b> inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle. The myenteric (Auerbach) nerve plexus lies between the two layers and controls their contraction.</li><li><b>Stomach:</b> adds the inner oblique layer. At the outlet, the circular layer thickens into the pyloric sphincter.</li><li><b>Esophagus:</b> two layers, but the muscle is skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third and smooth in the lower third.</li></ul><p><b>The stomach as a whole.</b> It is the widest part of the tube and holds about 1.5 L. It has four regions: the cardia, fundus, body and pylorus.</p><p><b>The wall of the stomach, layer by layer.</b></p><ol><li><b>Mucosa.</b> The lining is a simple columnar epithelium of surface mucous cells, each with a pale cap of mucus. The stomach has no goblet cells. The surface dips into funnel-shaped gastric pits. Gastric glands, the tubular glands that secrete the gastric juice, open into the bottoms of the pits. The lamina propria (the connective tissue of the mucosa) is squeezed into thin strips between the packed glands. A muscularis mucosae, a thin sheet of smooth muscle, lies under the glands.</li><li><b>Submucosa.</b> Connective tissue. It forms the core of the rugae.</li><li><b>Muscularis externa.</b> The three layers: oblique, circular and longitudinal.</li><li><b>Serosa.</b> The stomach hangs free in the peritoneal cavity. So its outer coat is a serosa, which is thin connective tissue covered by mesothelium, a single layer of flat cells. It is not an adventitia.</li></ol><p><b>Rugae.</b> Rugae are large folds of the mucosa with a core of submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills, which lets it expand. Only the stomach has rugae. The small intestine has permanent circular folds instead, which do not flatten.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":222,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Stomach: simple columnar surface mucous cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-70.jpg","cap":"Gastric mucosa: simple columnar surface mucous cells (arrow), no goblet cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 70"},{"src":"sl/G015-71.jpg","cap":"Gastric pits lined by surface mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 71","lg":[["A","gastric pit lined by surface mucous cells"],["B","gastric gland starting at the base of the pit"],["inset","stomach surface seen by scanning electron microscope"],["P","gastric pit opening (inset)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-153-1","s":"Q13. From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stomach.</b> Tall <b>simple columnar cells, each with a pale cap of mucus</b> (surface mucous cells), line every surface and pit, and there are <b>no goblet cells or villi</b>: gastric mucosa.<br>A. Outer lip: thin keratinized skin.<br>C. Esophagus: stratified squamous epithelium.<br>D. Jejunum: villi with enterocytes and scattered goblet cells.<br>E. Colon: straight crypts packed with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-153.jpg","in":"H&E: tall simple columnar epithelium with pale apical mucus cups (arrow) lining pits/folds, over lamina propria; no goblet cells or villi.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","surface mucous cells (pale mucus cap in every cell, no goblet cells)"]],"like":"G018-53-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 77"},{"id":"G018-53-1","s":"Q6. Which cell type is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Surface mucous cell","C. Enterocyte","D. Mucous neck cell","E. Chief cell"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Surface mucous cell","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Surface mucous cell.</b> The arrow touches the lining at the very top of the mucosa, where it faces the lumen. The cells there are tall and columnar. Their nuclei line up in a row at the base, and the top of each cell is <b>pale and clear</b> because it is filled with mucus. The same sheet of cells runs down into the pits on both sides of the arrow, with no break and no goblet cells. One continuous sheet of pale-topped columnar cells on the surface is the stomach's <b>surface mucous cells</b>.</p><p><b>What surface mucous cells do.</b> They make a thick, alkaline layer of mucus that coats the whole lining. The layer protects the stomach from its own acid and enzymes. The mucus does not take up the usual pink and purple stains, which is why the top of each cell looks empty.</p><p><b>Pits and glands.</b> A gastric pit is a funnel-shaped dip of the surface epithelium into the mucosa. Surface mucous cells line the pits as well as the surface. The gastric glands are tubular glands, deeper in the mucosa, that secrete the gastric juice. They open into the bottoms of the pits. The lamina propria, the connective tissue of the mucosa, is squeezed into thin strips between the pits and glands. In the picture it is the tissue with many small nuclei and small vessels between the folds of epithelium.</p><p><b>The cells of the gastric mucosa, from the surface down.</b></p><ul><li><b>Surface mucous cells:</b> on the surface and in the pits. Pale mucus cap. Protective mucus.</li><li><b>Mucous neck cells:</b> in the neck of the gland, just below the pit.</li><li><b>Parietal cells:</b> large pink cells in the glands of the fundus and body. They make acid.</li><li><b>Chief cells:</b> purple (basophilic) cells at the base of those glands. They make pepsinogen, the inactive form of the enzyme pepsin.</li><li><b>Enteroendocrine cells:</b> small hormone-making cells scattered deep in the glands, with their granules at the base. An example is the G cell, which makes gastrin. They need special stains to be seen.</li></ul><p><b>The stomach surface compared with the intestinal surface.</b> The intestine is lined by enterocytes. These are absorptive cells with a brush border, and goblet cells are scattered among them on villi. The stomach has no goblet cells and no villi. Every cell on its surface is a mucous cell.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-53.jpg","in":"Gastric surface and pits lined by tall columnar cells with pale apical cytoplasm; arrow on the surface lining.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","surface mucous cell (pale mucus cup at the apex, stomach surface)"]],"n":"18"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Cardia: mucous zone at the esophageal entry","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-67.jpg","cap":"Stomach regions: cardia, fundus, body, pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 67"},{"src":"sl/G015-73.jpg","cap":"Cardia makes mucus; body and fundus make acid; pylorus makes mucus, gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 73"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-52-1","s":"A medical student is studying the anatomy of the stomach and learns about its four main regions. During a practical examination, she is asked to identify the region that primarily functions in mucus production and transitions between the esophagus and the stomach. Which region is she identifying?","o":["A. Cardia","B. Body","C. Fundus","D. Pylorus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cardia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cardia.</b> The cardia is a narrow ring, a few centimeters wide, where the esophagus enters the stomach. Its <b>short coiled glands make mucus</b> that protects against acid.<br>B. Body: oxyntic glands making acid (parietal cells) and pepsinogen (chief cells).<br>C. Fundus: the same oxyntic glands as the body.<br>D. Pylorus: also mucous glands (plus gastrin-making G cells), but it is the exit into the duodenum, not the entry.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Stomach regions diagram with cardia circled (on the answer slide).","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":52,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 78"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Gastrin comes from pyloric G cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-34.jpg","cap":"Pyloric mucosa: mucous glands and gastrin-secreting G cells; no parietal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G015-80.jpg","cap":"Pyloric cells: high concentration of gastrin-producing G cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 80","lg":[["P","deep gastric pits"],["G","short coiled pyloric glands (not G cells)"],["LP","lamina propria"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-166-1","s":"In which region of the stomach is gastrin produced?","o":["A. Cardia","B. Body","C. Fundus","D. Pylorus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pylorus","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Pylorus.</b> Gastrin is made by <b>G cells</b>. G cells are enteroendocrine cells, the hormone-making cells scattered in the lining of the gut. They live in the glands of the <b>pylorus</b>, the last part of the stomach, which is made up of the antrum and the pyloric canal. No other region of the stomach has them.</p><p><b>What gastrin does.</b> G cells release gastrin into the blood, and not into the lumen. The blood carries it to the body and fundus. There it tells the glands to make acid. It also makes the stomach move more. So the pylorus makes no acid itself, but it drives the acid-making part of the stomach.</p><p><b>The three kinds of gastric mucosa.</b> To tell them apart, compare the depth of the pits with the length of the glands.</p><ul><li><b>Cardia:</b> a narrow ring, 1.5 to 3 cm wide, around the entry of the esophagus. Fairly long pits. Short, coiled mucous glands. Mucous cells, few or no parietal and chief cells, and no G cells. Job: protection by mucus.</li><li><b>Fundus and body:</b> most of the stomach. This is the oxyntic, or acid-making, mucosa. Short pits, about a quarter of the mucosa. Long, straight, tightly packed glands. Parietal cells (pink) make acid, and chief cells (purple) make pepsinogen, the inactive form of the enzyme pepsin. Job: digestion.</li><li><b>Pylorus:</b> deep pits, about half of the mucosa. Short, branched and coiled mucous glands. Mucous cells that make mucus and lysozyme (an enzyme that kills bacteria), plus G cells. No parietal or chief cells. Job: mucus to protect the duodenum, and gastrin.</li></ul><p><b>The trend along the stomach.</b> Going from the esophagus toward the duodenum, the pits get deeper. The glands get shorter, but more branched and coiled.</p><p><b>Cardia and pylorus look alike.</b> Both have pale, coiled mucous glands and no pink parietal cells. The cardia mucosa looks like the pyloric mucosa but has no G cells.</p><p><b>Why G cells are hard to see.</b> They are small and scattered among the mucous cells of the glands. Their granules sit at the base of the cell, on the side of the blood vessels, because the hormone leaves toward the blood. Special stains are needed to pick them out.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 80 (Mucous cells, Chief Cells, Parietal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":166,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Stomach regions: pit depth versus gland length","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-75.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture labels: cardia, body/fundus, pylorus (same pictures as the question)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 75","lg":[["arrowhead (cardia panel)","where the pit's mucous lining meets the gland (neck)"],["P","deep gastric pits"],["G","short coiled pyloric glands (not G cells)"],["LP","lamina propria"]]},{"src":"sl/G015-73.jpg","cap":"Cardia, body, pylorus glands: pits deepen and glands shorten toward pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/G018-34.jpg","cap":"Pyloric mucosa: deep pits over short coiled mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-78-1","s":"What organ is this from?","o":["A. Tongue","B. Esophagus","C. Stomach","D. Small Intestine","E. Large Intestine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach (pylorus).</b> <b>Long, deep gastric pits</b> lined by pale surface mucous cells over <b>short, coiled mucous glands</b>: pyloric mucosa, with no villi and no goblet cells.<br>A. Tongue: stratified squamous epithelium over skeletal muscle.<br>B. Esophagus: stratified squamous epithelium.<br>D. Small intestine: villi with enterocytes and goblet cells.<br>E. Large intestine: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, flat surface.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-78.jpg","in":"H&E of gastric mucosa with long, deep pits lined by pale mucous columnar cells and short coiled glands beneath.","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 74 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":78,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":187,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":174,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-5-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 79"},{"id":"F3980-66-1","s":"Name the Parts of the Stomach: [three micrographs, left, middle, right]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: pylorus. Middle: cardia. Right: body/fundus (the labels on 2026 lecture slide G015:75, which uses these same pictures).","a":null,"why":"<b>Left: pylorus. Middle: cardia. Right: body/fundus.</b> The 2026 lecture (slide G015:75) labels these same three pictures this way, so learn these labels for the exam.<br><b>Pylorus:</b> deep pits over short, coiled, pale mucous glands.<br><b>Body/fundus:</b> shallow pits over long, tightly packed glands, then the thick muscularis externa below.<br><b>Cardia:</b> the middle picture, as the lecture labels it. Pits are lined by surface mucous cells.<br>Common mix-up: the stomach has <b>no goblet cells</b>; goblet cells start in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":["Middle image is body/fundus (oxyntic) mucosa, not cardia: short pits over long straight glands packed with pink (acidophilic) parietal cells with round central nuclei (checked at high resolution). Cardiac glands are short, coiled, pale mucous glands (G015:73). The stomach also has no goblet cells; cardia has mucous gland cells.","Middle image is body/fundus (oxyntic) mucosa, not cardia: short pits over long straight glands packed with pink (acidophilic) parietal cells with round central nuclei (same picture as F3980 slide 66, checked at high resolution). Cardiac glands are short, coiled, pale mucous glands (G015:73). The stomach also has no goblet cells; cardia has mucous gland cells.","The source said the cardia is \"mostly goblet cells\". The stomach has no goblet cells; the pale cells lining the pits are surface mucous cells."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F3980-66.jpg","in":"Left: deep branching pits over short coiled pale glands (pylorus). Middle: short pits lined by surface mucous cells over long straight glands of pink parietal cells; arrowhead at pit-gland junction. Right: shallow pits over thick layer of deep glands with pink parietal cells above and purple chief cells at the base, muscularis below.","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 75 (Cardia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":66,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":133,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["P","gastric pits (deep)"],["G","pyloric glands (short, coiled)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["arrowhead","middle picture: where a gastric pit opens into its gland (surface mucous cells end, gland cells begin)"]],"like":"G118-5-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 80"},{"id":"G118-5-1","s":"Identify the organ in the photomicrograph below.","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Stomach","C. Jejunum","D. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Stomach.</b> Start at the surface. It is one sheet of tall, pale cells: <b>surface mucous cells</b>. The sheet dips down into deep, funnel-shaped <b>gastric pits</b>, which take up about the upper half of the mucosa. Under the pits lie short, coiled glands made of pale mucous cells, cut as round and oval rings. A pink band of smooth muscle, the muscularis mucosae, runs along the bottom. There are no goblet cells, no villi and no stratified epithelium. Pits lined by surface mucous cells, with glands opening below them, is the stomach.</p><p><b>The surface that every part of the stomach shares.</b> The stomach has three kinds of mucosa, but all three have the same surface: a sheet of tall, pale surface mucous cells that dips into pits, with no goblet cells and no villi. So the surface alone names the organ. The pits and glands then tell you the region.</p><p><b>Naming the region by pit depth and gland length.</b></p><ul><li><b>Cardia:</b> fairly long pits over short, coiled mucous glands.</li><li><b>Fundus and body:</b> short pits, about a quarter of the mucosa, over long, straight, tightly packed glands. The glands hold pink parietal cells, which make acid, and purple chief cells, which make pepsinogen.</li><li><b>Pylorus:</b> deep pits, about half of the mucosa, over short, branched and coiled mucous glands. G cells here release the hormone gastrin.</li></ul><p>This picture has deep pits over pale, coiled mucous glands and no pink parietal cells. That is the look of the pyloric region. It is still stomach.</p><p><b>A pit is not a villus.</b> The tall folds at the top of this picture can look like villi. They are the walls between neighboring pits. A pit is a dip into the mucosa, with the open space in the middle. A villus is a finger that sticks out into the lumen, with connective tissue in the middle. The folds here are covered only by mucous cells, with no goblet cells among them.</p><p><b>How the look-alike organs appear.</b></p><ul><li><b>Colon:</b> a flat surface over straight, tube-like crypts packed with goblet cells.</li><li><b>Jejunum:</b> villi covered by absorptive cells and goblet cells.</li><li><b>Esophagus:</b> a thick stratified squamous epithelium, with no pits.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-5.jpg","in":"Stomach mucosa, H&E: surface lined by a simple columnar epithelium of pale mucous cells that dips into deep pits; short coiled pale mucous glands under the pits (the look of the pyloric region); no goblet cells, no villi, no stratified squamous epithelium; muscularis mucosae at the bottom. Nothing is marked.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":5,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Parietal cells: acid and intrinsic factor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands: pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78","lg":[["box (50x)","area enlarged in the 100x panel"],["box (100x)","gland bases enlarged in the 400x panel"],["PC","parietal cells (pink, round central nucleus)"],["CC","chief cells (purple, at the gland bases)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-129-1","s":"Which of the following cell type aids in absorption of vitamin B12?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Enteroendocrine cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Parietal cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Parietal cells.</b> Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it; they also secrete hydrochloric acid. Losing them causes pernicious anemia.<br>A. Chief cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase.<br>B. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.<br>C. Paneth cells: antibacterial lysozyme and defensins in small-intestine crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":129,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 81"},{"id":"F3950-48-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain and nausea after a heavy meal. An endoscopy reveals a significant amount of gastric acid in the lumen of his stomach. A biopsy shows that the mucosa is composed of a simple columnar epithelium with deep invaginations forming gastric pits. Which type of gastric cell is primarily responsible for secreting the hydrochloric acid observed in this patient?","o":["A. Mucous neck cells","B. Chief (zymogenic) cells","C. Parietal (oxyntic) cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Parietal (oxyntic) cells.</b> Large, pink (acidophilic) parietal cells in the body and fundus glands secrete <b>hydrochloric acid</b> into their intracellular canaliculi, plus intrinsic factor.<br>A. Mucous neck cells: watery, more acidic mucus; the stem-cell zone.<br>B. Chief (zymogenic) cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":48,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 82"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Chief cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Purple chief cells (CC) at gland bases, pink parietal cells above","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78","lg":[["box (50x)","area enlarged in the 100x panel"],["box (100x)","gland bases enlarged in the 400x panel"],["PC","parietal cells (pink, round central nucleus)"],["CC","chief cells (purple, at the gland bases)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-50-1","s":"A 30-year-old female presents with complaints of indigestion and difficulty digesting fatty foods. Upon examination, her medical history reveals she has had several episodes of gastric discomfort following high-fat meals. In the stomach, triglycerides are initially digested by which of the following secretions?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Amylase","C. Gastric lipase","D. Hydrochloric acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric lipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastric lipase.</b> Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and a <b>lipase precursor</b>; gastric lipase begins triglyceride digestion in the stomach.<br>A. Pepsin: digests proteins.<br>B. Amylase: digests starch; comes from saliva and pancreas, not the stomach.<br>D. Hydrochloric acid: lowers pH and activates pepsinogen, but does not split triglycerides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":50,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 83"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Gastric gland cells and their secretions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: surface mucous, neck mucous, parietal, chief and G cells with secretions","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph of gastric gland: parietal, chief and enteroendocrine cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-170-1","s":"Match the mucosal cell with the secretion: Epithelial Mucous Cell; Mucous neck cell; Parietal Cell; Chief Cell; Entero-Endocrine (G) Cell","o":["A. Pepsinogen and Lipase precursor","B. Peptide hormones (e.g. serotonin and gastrin)","C. HCl and Intrinsic factor for Vit 12 absorption","D. Alkaline mucous","E. Acidic mucous"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Epithelial mucous cell – D (alkaline mucous); Mucous neck cell – E (acidic mucous); Parietal cell – C (HCl and intrinsic factor); Chief cell – A (pepsinogen and lipase precursor); Entero-endocrine (G) cell – B (peptide hormones)","a":null,"why":"<b>Surface mucous cell → D (alkaline mucus); mucous neck cell → E (acidic mucus); parietal cell → C (HCl, hydrochloric acid, and intrinsic factor); chief cell → A (pepsinogen, lipase precursor); enteroendocrine (G) cell → B (peptide hormones).</b> On H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), parietal cells are pink, chief cells purple, mucous cells pale, and enteroendocrine cells need special stains.<br>Common mix-up: surface cells make the thick <b>alkaline</b> protective layer; neck cells make thinner, more acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 80 (Mucous cells, Chief Cells, Parietal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 84"},{"id":"F3952-86-1","s":"At a renowned culinary festival, the late Anthony Bourdain captivates the crowd with stories from his adventures around the globe, exploring the intricate relationship between food and culture. As he prepares a flavorful broth inspired by his travels, he explains how the body’s digestive system works to break down the delicious meals we enjoy, particularly highlighting the role of gastric glands in the stomach. When asked about the cells responsible for producing digestive enzymes and their unique cellular structures, he describes a specific type of cell characterized by a well-developed rough endoplasmic reticulum and few Golgi complexes, emphasizing its importance in the digestive process. Those cells are most likely examples of what cell?","o":["A. Neck mucous cell","B. Parietal cell","C. Chief cell","D. Neuroendocrine cell","E. Stem cell"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chief cell","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Chief cell.</b> Chief cells make the stomach's digestive <b>enzymes (pepsinogen and lipase precursor)</b>, so they are packed with <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER)</b> and stain purple (basophilic) at the base of fundic glands. (The stem's 'few Golgi' is off; chief cells also have a good Golgi.)<br>A. Neck mucous cell: makes acidic mucus, not enzymes.<br>B. Parietal cell: makes acid and intrinsic factor; full of mitochondria, stains pink.<br>D. Neuroendocrine cell: makes hormones, stored in small basal granules.<br>E. Stem cell: undifferentiated, few organelles; it divides to replace cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":86,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 85"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Parietal pink, chief purple on H&E","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands, 400x: pale-pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78","lg":[["box (50x)","area enlarged in the 100x panel"],["box (100x)","gland bases enlarged in the 400x panel"],["PC","parietal cells (pink, round central nucleus)"],["CC","chief cells (purple, at the gland bases)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-30.jpg","cap":"Fundus/body mucosa: pits, neck and glands; parietal (P) and chief (C) cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30","lg":[["GP","gastric pits"],["SM","surface mucous cells"],["MN","mucous neck cells"],["P","parietal cells (big, pink, round 'fried egg')"],["C","chief cells (purple, basophilic, deep in the glands)"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["boxes","areas enlarged at right"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-58-1","s":"A biopsy is obtained from the stomach. A and B correspond respectively to:","o":["A. Parietal cell; Chief cell","B. Chief cell; Parietal cell","C. Chief cell; Enteroendocrine cell","D. Parietal cell; Enteroendocrine cell"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cell; Chief cell","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Parietal cell; chief cell.</b> <b>A</b> is a large, round, <b>pale-pink (acidophilic)</b> cell with a central nucleus in the upper gland: a parietal cell. <b>B</b> is a <b>dark-purple (basophilic)</b> cell lining the deep gland: a chief cell.<br>B. Chief cell; parietal cell: reversed. Pink cells are parietal, purple cells are chief.<br>C. Chief cell; enteroendocrine cell: A is pink, so not a chief cell.<br>D. Parietal cell; enteroendocrine cell: B is dark purple; enteroendocrine cells are small and pale.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-58.jpg","in":"Gastric glands: A = pale pink cell with central nucleus bulging from the gland wall; B = dark purple basophilic cell lining the deeper gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":58,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":192,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":155,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (pale pink, round central nucleus)"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, at the gland base)"]],"like":"G018-54-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 86"},{"id":"F0745-30-1","s":"What are these cells? What do they secrete?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: Parietal (oxyntic) cells – HCl and intrinsic factor; B: Chief cells – pepsinogen and lipase precursor","a":null,"why":"<b>A = parietal (oxyntic) cell; B = chief cell.</b> A is a large, round, <b>pink (acidophilic)</b> cell with a central nucleus in the upper gland. It secretes <b>hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, needed to absorb vitamin B12. B is a <b>purple (basophilic)</b> cell at the gland base. It secretes <b>pepsinogen</b> and <b>lipase precursor</b>.<br>Common mix-up: students swap them. Pink = acid-making parietal cell; purple = enzyme-making chief cell, rich in rough endoplasmic reticulum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-30.jpg","in":"H&E of fundic gastric glands: arrow A on a pale-pink (acidophilic) rounded cell with central nucleus; arrow B on a dark-purple (basophilic) cell at the gland base.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":30,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (pale pink, round central nucleus)"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, at the gland base)"]],"like":"G018-54-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 87"},{"id":"G018-54-1","s":"Q7. The cell indicated by A produces:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Intrinsic factor.</b> Arrow A points to a large, round cell that stains <b>pink</b> and has a round nucleus in its center. It bulges from the side of a long gastric gland. That look belongs to the <b>parietal cell</b>. Parietal cells make two things: hydrochloric acid (HCl) and <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Arrow B points to a smaller, <b>purple</b> cell at the bottom of a gland, which is a chief cell.</p><p><b>Why the parietal cell is pink.</b> Pumping acid costs a lot of energy, so the parietal cell is packed with mitochondria. Mitochondria take up eosin, the pink dye of the routine hematoxylin and eosin stain. A cell that stains pink is called acidophilic. The nucleus sits in the middle, so the cell looks like a fried egg. Parietal cells are also called oxyntic cells. They are most common in the upper half of the gland.</p><p><b>What intrinsic factor does.</b> Intrinsic factor is a protein that binds vitamin B12 in the gut. The ileum, the last part of the small intestine, absorbs vitamin B12 only when it is bound to intrinsic factor. So a person who loses the parietal cells runs short of vitamin B12.</p><p><b>The three regions of a gland in the body or fundus.</b></p><ul><li><b>Pit (top):</b> surface mucous cells. They are tall columnar cells with a pale cap of mucus. Their thick, sticky, alkaline mucus clings to the surface and protects it from acid.</li><li><b>Neck (isthmus):</b> mucous neck cells, which make a more watery, less alkaline mucus, and stem cells, which divide and replace all the other cells.</li><li><b>Body and base:</b> parietal cells, mostly in the upper half, and chief cells at the base.</li></ul><p><b>Other secretions and the cells that make them.</b></p><ul><li><b>Pepsinogen:</b> chief cells, the purple cells at the gland base (cell B). Acid turns it into pepsin, which digests protein.</li><li><b>Histamine:</b> enterochromaffin-like cells, small hormone cells scattered in the glands. Histamine acts on nearby parietal cells and makes them secrete more acid.</li><li><b>Gastrin:</b> G cells in the glands of the pylorus. It raises acid secretion and stomach movement.</li><li><b>Lysozyme:</b> Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of the small intestine. It kills bacteria.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-54.jpg","in":"Fundic glands: arrow A on a round pink cell with a central nucleus; arrow B on a purple (basophilic) cell lower in the gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (pink, round, central nucleus): HCl and intrinsic factor"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, gland base): pepsinogen"]],"n":"21"},{"id":"G118-6-1","s":"Which of the following is secreted by the cells at the tips of the arrows?","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Secretin","C. CCK","D. HCL"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"HCL","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. HCl (hydrochloric acid).</b> Each arrow ends on a large, round, <b>pale pink</b> cell with a round nucleus in its center. These cells sit in the deep part of the gastric glands, just above the pink band of smooth muscle at the bottom of the picture (the muscularis mucosae). Large, round and pink with a central nucleus means <b>parietal cell</b>. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid, and also intrinsic factor, the protein the ileum needs in order to absorb vitamin B12.</p><p><b>Go by size and color, not by position.</b> With the routine hematoxylin and eosin stain, two cell types fill the deep part of the gland. The big, round, pink cells are parietal cells. The smaller blue-purple cells squeezed between them are chief cells, which make pepsinogen. Parietal cells are most numerous in the upper half of the gland and chief cells at the base. But deep in a gland the two are often mixed, as they are here.</p><p><b>How the parietal cell makes acid.</b> The acid comes from a pump in the cell membrane called the H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> pump, or proton pump. It pushes hydrogen ions (H<sup>+</sup>) out of the cell in exchange for potassium ions (K<sup>+</sup>). The cell changes its shape when it starts to work.</p><ul><li><b>At rest:</b> the pumps are stored inside the cell, in small membrane sacs called tubulovesicles near the top of the cell. The cell has only short channels and few microvilli.</li><li><b>At work:</b> the tubulovesicles fuse with the cell membrane. They form deep channels that run into the cell, called intracellular canaliculi, lined with many microvilli. This greatly enlarges the membrane that carries the pumps, so much more acid is secreted.</li></ul><p>The many mitochondria supply the energy for the pumps. They are also the reason the cell stains pink.</p><p><b>What the acid is for.</b> Acid turns pepsinogen from the chief cells into pepsin, the enzyme that starts protein digestion. Starch is not digested in the stomach. Its enzyme, amylase, comes from the salivary glands and the pancreas.</p><p><b>Hormones that come from the small intestine.</b> Secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) are not products of the gastric glands. Small, pale hormone cells in the lining of the small intestine release them when acid, fat and protein arrive from the stomach.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-6.jpg","in":"Base of gastric (fundic) glands, H&E: four arrows on large, round, pale pink (acidophilic) cells with a central round nucleus (parietal cells); smaller blue-purple cells (chief cells) lie between them; muscularis mucosae at the bottom.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 79 (Parietal Cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":6,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"},{"id":"G118-7-1","s":"Identify the cells at the tip of the arrows?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Enteroendocrine cells","C. Parietal cells","D. Mucous cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chief cells","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Chief cells.</b> The arrows end on the small, <b>blue-purple</b> cells that are squeezed between the large pink cells. They lie deep in the gastric glands, close to the band of smooth muscle at the bottom of the picture. Small and blue-purple, deep in a gastric gland, means <b>chief cell</b>. The large, round, pink cells around them are parietal cells.</p><p><b>Why chief cells are blue-purple.</b> A chief cell is a protein factory. It is full of rough endoplasmic reticulum, the stacks of membrane studded with ribosomes that build proteins for export. Ribosomes contain RNA, which binds hematoxylin, the blue dye of the routine hematoxylin and eosin stain. A cell that stains blue this way is called basophilic. The finished protein is stored in zymogen granules at the top of the cell, ready to be released into the gland. A zymogen is an enzyme stored in an inactive form, so chief cells are also called zymogenic cells.</p><p><b>What chief cells secrete.</b></p><ul><li><b>Pepsinogen.</b> It is inactive when released. Acid in the stomach lumen turns it into pepsin, which digests protein.</li><li><b>Gastric lipase</b>, released as a precursor. It starts the digestion of fat.</li></ul><p><b>The other cells of the gland and how each looks.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parietal cells:</b> large, round and pink, with a central nucleus. They make hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor.</li><li><b>Surface mucous cells:</b> tall columnar cells on the surface and in the pits, with a pale cap of mucus on top. Their thick alkaline mucus shields the lining from acid.</li><li><b>Mucous neck cells:</b> smaller pale cells in the narrow neck of the gland. Their mucus is more watery.</li><li><b>Stem cells:</b> small dividing cells in the neck. They replace all the others.</li><li><b>Enteroendocrine cells:</b> hormone-making cells scattered singly in the glands. They are pale and easy to miss, and special stains are needed to show them.</li></ul><p><b>A quick rule for the deep gland.</b> Two colors fill the deep part of a gland of the body or fundus. Pink and large is parietal. Purple and small is chief. Chief cells gather at the base, but the two types are often mixed, so color is the safer guide.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-7.jpg","in":"Same field of gastric (fundic) glands as Kahoot question 6, but the four arrows are now on the small blue-purple (basophilic) cells that lie between the large pink parietal cells (chief cells).","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":7,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":16,"label":"Enteroendocrine cells: special stains, serotonin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells: seen with chromium, silver or immunostains; serotonin increases motility","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: enteroendocrine cell (E) with basal granules beside parietal, chief cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-32-1","s":"How can we find the EE (enteroendocrine) cells?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Immunostaining","a":null,"why":"<b>With special stains, mainly immunostaining (immunohistochemistry).</b> Enteroendocrine (EE) cells are small and stain poorly on routine H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin). They show up with <b>antibody stains for their hormones</b>, or with <b>chromium or silver salts</b>.<br>Common mix-up: a pale cell at the gland base on H&amp;E may be an EE cell, but only the special stain proves it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Same fundic-gland micrograph (parietal A, chief B); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":32,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-13-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 88"},{"id":"F1211-193-1","s":"BONUS: What cell does the red arrow point to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A poorly stained G cell (enteroendocrine cell)","a":null,"why":"<b>An enteroendocrine (EE) cell.</b> The red arrow marks a <b>small cell with pale, almost unstained cytoplasm</b> in the deep gland, among dark-purple chief cells. EE cells stain poorly on H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin) and need <b>special stains</b> to be seen clearly. They secrete hormones such as serotonin and gastrin.<br>Common mix-up: parietal cells are also light, but they are large, round and pink, with a central nucleus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1211-193.jpg","in":"Same gastric-gland micrograph: the red arrow points to a small cell with pale, unstained cytoplasm at the base of a gland among dark chief cells.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","parietal cell (big, pink, round 'fried egg')"],["B","chief cell (purple, basophilic, deep in the gland)"],["red arrow","enteroendocrine cell (pale, poorly stained cytoplasm)"]],"like":"G017-13-1","kind":"harder","n":"11 extra 89"},{"id":"G017-13-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric cardia mucosa. On his immunostained preparation, he can observe scattered cells in the glandular portion not seen in the H&E preparation. Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract.</b> The clue is that the cells are scattered through the glands and show up only with an immunostain. An immunostain uses an antibody to color one chosen protein, here a hormone. Cells that are missed with the routine hematoxylin and eosin stain and appear only with special stains are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>, the hormone cells of the gut. One kind, the enterochromaffin cell, releases <b>serotonin</b>, which increases the movement (motility) of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract. Gastrin, from another kind, increases motility too.</p><p><b>What enteroendocrine cells are.</b> They are small cells scattered one by one among the other gland cells, from the stomach to the colon. Their hormone granules sit at the <b>base</b> of the cell, on the side away from the lumen. So they release their product into the blood (endocrine) or onto neighboring cells (paracrine), not into the lumen.</p><p><b>How they are shown.</b> With the routine stain they look pale and are easy to miss. Three special methods bring them out:</p><ul><li><b>Chromium salts:</b> the cells that react are called enterochromaffin cells.</li><li><b>Silver salts:</b> the cells that react are called argentaffin cells.</li><li><b>Immunostaining</b> (immunohistochemistry): an antibody against the hormone itself.</li></ul><p><b>Open and closed types.</b> An open-type cell reaches the lumen and has microvilli that sense the nutrients there, much as a taste cell does. A closed-type cell does not reach the lumen.</p><p><b>The main cells and their products.</b></p><ul><li><b>Enterochromaffin cell:</b> serotonin, which increases gut motility.</li><li><b>Enterochromaffin-like cell:</b> histamine. It acts on nearby parietal cells and makes them secrete more acid.</li><li><b>G cell</b> (in the pylorus): gastrin, which increases acid secretion and motility.</li><li><b>Cells of the small intestine:</b> cholecystokinin, secretin and others.</li></ul><p><b>The gland cells that the routine stain does show.</b> Stem cells in the neck of the gland divide and replace old or damaged cells. Surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells secrete mucus. Parietal cells are large and pink. They make acid and intrinsic factor, which is needed to absorb vitamin B12, and with the electron microscope they show deep channels called intracellular canaliculi. Chief cells are purple and make pepsinogen.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostain of gastric glands with scattered dark brown-stained single cells in the glandular epithelium (enteroendocrine cells); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"24"},{"id":"F3961-7-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric mucosa. He can observe a set of cells from the glandular portion which had not been observed in the H&E preparation (see the figure below). Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Their secretions may increase gastrointestinal (GI) motility.</b> Gland cells seen only with <b>immunostaining</b>, not on routine H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>. Some of them (enterochromaffin cells) secrete <b>serotonin</b>, which increases gut motility.<br>B. Replacing old cells: stem cells in the gland neck (isthmus).<br>C. Secreting mucus: surface and neck mucous cells.<br>D. Needed for vitamin B12 absorption: parietal cells (intrinsic factor).<br>E. Intracellular canaliculi: a feature of acid-secreting parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostained gastric mucosa (per slide 8: gastrin stain highlighting G cells in antral mucosa); not needed to answer. Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":5,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-13-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 90"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Pernicious anemia: no B12, large red cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: no parietal-cell intrinsic factor, so B12 deficiency and large red cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"},{"src":"sl/G017-17.jpg","cap":"Smear: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and Howell-Jolly body in B12 deficiency","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-21-1","s":"In pernicious anemia, the parietal cells no longer secrete intrinsic factor. Which vitamin becomes deficient, and what type of anemia results?","o":["A. Folate; microcytic anemia","B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","C. Iron; microcytic hypochromic anemia","D. Vitamin B6; sideroblastic anemia","E. Vitamin B12; microcytic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Vitamin B12; megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia.</b> Intrinsic factor is a protein made by the parietal cells of the stomach. It binds vitamin B12, and the ileum (the last part of the small intestine) absorbs the vitamin only in that bound form. Without intrinsic factor, vitamin B12 passes through unabsorbed and the body runs short of it. The red cells then come out too large, which is megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia.</p><p><b>The chain of events in pernicious anemia.</b></p><ol><li>Parietal cells are destroyed or bypassed.</li><li>No intrinsic factor is made.</li><li>Vitamin B12 is not absorbed, and deficiency follows.</li><li>Marrow cells need vitamin B12 to make DNA. The young red cells keep growing but cannot copy their DNA and divide on time, so they become very large. These large precursors are called megaloblasts.</li><li>The red cells that reach the blood are large and too few. \"Macrocytic\" means large-celled.</li></ol><p><b>What the blood smear shows.</b> Large, oval red cells. It also shows hypersegmented neutrophils, which are white cells whose nucleus has 6 or more lobes.</p><p><b>The size clue for any anemia.</b> Lack of vitamin B12 or folate gives <b>big</b> red cells, because both vitamins are needed to make DNA. Lack of iron gives <b>small</b> red cells, because iron is needed to make hemoglobin.</p><p><b>Other effects of the deficiency.</b></p><ul><li>Fatigue, from the anemia.</li><li>A smooth, red tongue.</li><li>Diarrhea.</li><li>Nerve damage: numbness, tingling and poor balance.</li></ul><p><b>Why the parietal cells are lost.</b></p><ul><li><b>Autoimmune atrophic gastritis</b> (true pernicious anemia): antibodies attack the parietal cells and intrinsic factor itself. The mucosa of the body and fundus of the stomach thins.</li><li><b>Stomach bypass surgery:</b> food skips most of the stomach, so the parietal cells no longer supply intrinsic factor.</li></ul><p><b>The parietal cell under the microscope.</b> It is the large, round, pink cell with a central nucleus in the glands of the body and fundus. Besides intrinsic factor it makes hydrochloric acid.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"},{"id":"F3980-37-1","s":"A woman comes into your office complaining of weakness and fatigue. She has a past history of stomach bypass surgery. You do some tests and find that she is deficient in vitamin B12. You decide to do a blood smear. What will you see?","o":["A. Crescent shaped red blood cells","B. Small red blood cells","C. Big red blood cells","D. Fragmented red blood cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Big red blood cells (Megaloblastic anemia)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Big red blood cells.</b> After stomach bypass, the <b>parietal cells</b> that make intrinsic factor no longer serve the food stream, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed. Low B12 slows DNA synthesis in the marrow, and red cells come out <b>large</b>: megaloblastic anemia.<br>A. Crescent (sickle) cells: sickle cell disease.<br>B. Small red cells: iron deficiency.<br>D. Fragmented red cells (schistocytes): cells torn in damaged small vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":37,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":195,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 91"},{"id":"F0755-227-1","s":"A patient presents to your office with symptoms of fatigue, diarrhea, loss of appetite. He stated that a few months ago he had a procedure done which you ultimately found out was stomach bypass and he has lost about 45 lbs. The symptoms began after the surgery. Which condition represents the clinical presentation? What in addition would a CBC exam show?","o":["A. Barrett’s esophagus; parietal cells","B. Pernicious Anemia; Megaloblastic anemia","C. No condition, the symptoms are due to the surgery","D. Vitamin B 12 deficiency, sideroblastic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pernicious Anemia; Megaloblastic anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pernicious anemia; megaloblastic anemia.</b> Stomach bypass cuts off the <b>parietal cells</b>, so there is no <b>intrinsic factor</b> and vitamin B12 is not absorbed. The CBC (complete blood count) would show <b>large red cells</b>: megaloblastic anemia.<br>A. Barrett's esophagus: columnar metaplasia from acid reflux, not from bypass.<br>C. 'No condition': the symptoms have a clear cause.<br>D. Sideroblastic anemia: iron stuck in red-cell mitochondria; B12 deficiency gives megaloblastic, not sideroblastic, anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":227,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 92"},{"id":"G017-16-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] Given the principal complaint of weakness, you decide to investigate her hematologic status first. You order a complete blood count with manual differential. Her red cell indices were notable for a Hb of 10 g/dL, Hct of 33 and MCV of 110 fL. An image from the peripheral blood smear is presented here. Are there significant findings?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes: megaloblastic anemia.</b> The smear shows <b>oval macrocytes</b> (big, oval red cells), a <b>hypersegmented neutrophil</b> (too many nuclear lobes), and a <b>Howell-Jolly body</b> (a small nuclear leftover inside a red cell). With an MCV (mean corpuscular volume, the average red cell size) of one hundred ten, this fits vitamin B12 deficiency.<br>Common mix-up: iron deficiency gives small, pale red cells, not big oval ones.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G017-16.jpg","in":"Peripheral blood smear with large oval red cells, a neutrophil with many nuclear lobes, and a red cell containing a small dark nuclear remnant (Howell-Jolly body).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 93"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Pernicious anemia: parietal cells lost","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: intrinsic factor lost; stomach bypass surgery can also cause it","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-21-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman has fatigue and a smooth, red tongue. Labs show macrocytic anemia. Her vitamin B12 level is very low. Further testing shows antibodies against intrinsic factor. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her anemia?","o":["A. Poor dietary intake of B12","B. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","C. Chronic blood loss","D. Folate deficiency","E. Iron deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells.</b> <b>Antibodies against intrinsic factor (IF)</b> mean pernicious anemia. The immune attack wipes out parietal cells, which make IF, so B12 is not absorbed. Result: macrocytic anemia and a smooth red tongue.<br>A. Poor diet: rare, and it would not make anti-IF antibodies.<br>C. Chronic blood loss: iron deficiency, small red cells.<br>D. Folate deficiency: also macrocytic, but B12 would be normal.<br>E. Iron deficiency: small, pale red cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":21,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-19-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 94"},{"id":"G017-19-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis has megaloblastic anemia from low vitamin B12. Endoscopy shows diffuse atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; Helicobacter pylori is negative. Which pattern of gastritis and which antibodies fit this autoimmune pernicious anemia?","o":["A. Antrum-predominant gastritis; antibodies to gastrin-secreting G cells","B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","C. Helicobacter pylori gastritis of the antrum; antibodies to urease","D. Acute erosive gastritis; antibodies to chief cell pepsinogen","E. Atrophic gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to tissue transglutaminase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Atrophic autoimmune gastritis of the fundus and body; antibodies to the parietal cell H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase (proton pump) and to intrinsic factor.</b> She has megaloblastic anemia from low vitamin B12, atrophic gastritis limited to the fundus and body, a negative test for Helicobacter pylori, and two other autoimmune diseases. This is autoimmune atrophic gastritis, the cause of true pernicious anemia. Her antibodies have two targets. One is the H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase, the proton pump that the parietal cell uses to secrete acid. The other is intrinsic factor.</p><p><b>Why the fundus and body.</b> Parietal cells live in the glands of the fundus and body. That is where the attack falls, and that is where the mucosa thins. \"Atrophic\" means wasted or thinned. The antrum, the part near the pylorus, is spared. Its G cells sense that acid is low and release more gastrin.</p><p><b>From antibodies to anemia.</b></p><ol><li>The parietal cells are destroyed. Acid and intrinsic factor are no longer made.</li><li>Intrinsic factor normally binds vitamin B12 so that the ileum can absorb it. Without it, vitamin B12 is not absorbed.</li><li>Marrow cells cannot make DNA normally without vitamin B12. Red cell precursors grow large, which is megaloblastic anemia.</li></ol><p><b>The company it keeps.</b> Autoimmune gastritis often comes with other autoimmune diseases. Type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis are the typical partners, and she has both.</p><p><b>The second cause of pernicious anemia.</b> Stomach bypass surgery. Food skips most of the stomach, so the parietal cells no longer supply intrinsic factor. No antibodies are involved.</p><p><b>How Helicobacter pylori gastritis looks.</b> Helicobacter pylori is a curved, rod-shaped bacterium that lives in the mucus on the stomach surface. It is the usual cause of chronic gastritis and of peptic ulcer disease. It mostly affects the antrum, and it comes with no autoantibodies. Long-term infection builds lymphoid tissue in the mucosa. That raises the risk of two cancers: a gastric lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT lymphoma) and gastric adenocarcinoma.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"},{"id":"G017-23-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] An image from the gastric body biopsy is presented below with an image from a more normal gastric body for comparison. Absence of which cell type in the patient's gastric body is most likely responsible for her clinical findings?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Surface mucous cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Parietal cells.</b> All of her findings trace back to low vitamin B12. A normal gastric body is packed with tall glands full of parietal cells. In autoimmune atrophic gastritis the biopsy shows a thin mucosa with few, sparse glands and an inflamed lamina propria. The cells that have been lost are the <b>parietal cells</b>. They make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, the protein that lets the ileum absorb vitamin B12.</p><p><b>Each finding explained.</b></p><ul><li><b>Fatigue, pale conjunctiva, heart rate of 100, hemoglobin of 10:</b> anemia. With less hemoglobin to carry oxygen she is tired and pale, and the heart beats faster to make up for it.</li><li><b>Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) of 110 fL (femtoliters):</b> the MCV is the average size of a red cell, and hers is large. Without vitamin B12 the marrow cannot make DNA normally, so red cell precursors grow large. This is megaloblastic anemia.</li><li><b>Numbness, tingling and unsteadiness:</b> nerve damage from the lack of vitamin B12.</li><li><b>Diarrhea:</b> part of the same deficiency. A smooth, red tongue is another sign.</li><li><b>Type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis:</b> autoimmune diseases that often travel with autoimmune gastritis.</li></ul><p><b>What the immune attack hits.</b> Antibodies target the proton pump of the parietal cell (the H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> pump that secretes acid) and intrinsic factor. Parietal cells live in the fundus and body, so that is where the mucosa thins.</p><p><b>What each cell of a body gland makes.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parietal cells</b> (large, round, pink): hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor.</li><li><b>Chief cells</b> (purple, at the gland base): pepsinogen, which acid turns into pepsin to digest protein.</li><li><b>Surface mucous cells:</b> thick alkaline mucus that protects the lining.</li><li><b>Mucous neck cells:</b> a more watery mucus.</li><li><b>Enteroendocrine cells:</b> hormones and local signals, such as serotonin and histamine.</li></ul><p>Of all these products, only intrinsic factor is needed to absorb vitamin B12.</p><p><b>A different stomach lesion: the smooth muscle tumor.</b> A leiomyoma is a benign tumor of smooth muscle, usually from the muscularis externa. It forms a small, well-defined mass under the mucosa. It is made of bland, spindle-shaped cells in bundles, and it stains for the smooth muscle markers desmin and smooth muscle actin. Its look-alike is the gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST). A GIST arises from the pacemaker cells of the gut wall, the interstitial cells of Cajal, and it stains for KIT (also called CD117).</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Biopsy (chronic atrophic gastritis): thin mucosa with few, sparse glands and inflamed lamina propria vs normal body mucosa packed with tall oxyntic glands.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":23,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":15,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"},{"id":"F3961-12-1","s":"A 42-year-old man presents to your office six months after his stomach bypass surgery. He has lost 40 pounds since the surgery and is happy with the results. However, he reports feeling constantly fatigued and irritable and has also noticed that his tongue appears to be swollen. A CBC examination yields the photomicrograph below (megaloblastic anemia). An underlying problem to which of the following cells could best explain this patient's clinical presentation?","o":["A. Chief Cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Goblet Cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells.</b> After stomach bypass, food skips most of the stomach, so the <b>parietal cells</b> no longer supply <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Without it, vitamin B12 is not absorbed: megaloblastic anemia, fatigue and a swollen tongue.<br>A. Chief cells: make pepsinogen and lipase; not needed for B12.<br>C. Goblet cells: intestinal mucus.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones.<br>E. Mucous neck cells: acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Peripheral smear (per notes: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes, Howell-Jolly body); stem states megaloblastic anemia, so not needed. Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":8,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-19-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 95"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"H. pylori gastritis raises gastric lymphoma risk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Peptic ulcer disease is usually caused by Helicobacter pylori","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-91-1","s":"Harold, a fifty-eight year-old grocery store manager, had recently been waking up in the middle of the night with abdominal pain. This was happening several nights a week. He was also experiencing occasional discomfort in the middle of the afternoon. Harold decided to schedule an appointment with his physician. An endoscopic procedure was performed, and biopsies taken. See the accompanying figure. If left untreated, the patient would be at an increased risk for which of the following?","o":["A. Gastric melanoma","B. Gastric nerve sheath tumor","C. Gastric lymphoma","D. Gastric neuroblastoma","E. Gastric Squamous cell carcinoma"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric lymphoma","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastric lymphoma.</b> The curved rods in the surface mucus are <b>Helicobacter pylori</b>, the usual cause of peptic ulcer disease. Long-term infection makes gastric lymphoid tissue grow, raising the risk of <b>MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphoma</b>, and of gastric adenocarcinoma.<br>A. Melanoma: a melanocyte cancer, not linked to H. pylori.<br>B. Nerve sheath tumor: from Schwann cells; no infection link.<br>D. Neuroblastoma: a childhood neural crest tumor, usually adrenal.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: the stomach has simple columnar epithelium, not squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-91.jpg","in":"High-power gastric surface mucous cells with many small curved/spiral rods in the mucus over the epithelium (Helicobacter pylori).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":91,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 96"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Gastric leiomyoma: benign smooth muscle tumor","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-126.jpg","cap":"Leiomyoma: bundles of spindle cells, positive for smooth muscle myosin","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 126"},{"src":"sl/G018-27.jpg","cap":"Stomach wall: muscularis externa of oblique, circular and longitudinal smooth muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-124-1","s":"A 54-years-old male patient presented to his primary care physician with continuing epigastric discomfort and heart burn. He stated that he had been experiencing these same symptoms for the past 8 months. He stated that taking OTC medicines did help with the discomfort but then he decided to stop taking them. At times, he did experience moderate nausea. His general physical examination was unremarkable. No palpable mass in epigastrium was detected on abdominal examination. Upper GI endoscopy showed submucosal lesion in the cardia of the stomach. A CT scan revealed a 2.5 x 3.0 cm mass situated in that area. A laparoscopic wedge resection of the mass was successfully performed. The mass was sent to pathology for histological examination. [Slides 125-126: gastric submucosal lesion; composed of bundles of elongated cells with oblong nuclei; cells positive for smooth muscle myosin heavy chain.] Based on the clinical findings and endoscopic image obtained plus the histological photomicrographs, the most accurate diagnosis for this lesion is which of the following?","o":["A. Gastric lipoma","B. Gastric schwannoma","C. Gastric leiomyosarcoma","D. Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E. Gastric leiomyoma"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric leiomyoma","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Gastric leiomyoma.</b> A small, well-defined submucosal mass of <b>bland spindle cells in bundles</b> that stain for <b>smooth muscle myosin</b> is a benign smooth muscle tumor. It arises from the muscularis and is common in the cardia.<br>A. Lipoma: mature fat cells.<br>B. Schwannoma: Schwann (nerve sheath) cells; stains for S-100, not smooth muscle myosin.<br>C. Leiomyosarcoma: smooth muscle too, but atypical nuclei and many mitoses.<br>D. GIST (gastrointestinal stromal tumor): from interstitial cells of Cajal; stains for KIT (CD117).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Bland spindle cells in interlacing bundles (H&E) staining brown for smooth muscle myosin heavy chain.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":124,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 97"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Plicae circulares: small intestine, not stomach","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Small intestine surface: plicae circulares, then villi, then microvilli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G016-12.jpg","cap":"Small intestine folds (plicae) carry villi; villi carry microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-2","s":"2. Plicae circularis (valves of Kerkring) are present in","o":["A. stomach","B. ileum","C. duodenum","D. jejunum","E. all but the stomach"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"all but the stomach","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All but the stomach.</b> <b>Plicae circulares</b> are permanent circular folds of mucosa with a <b>submucosal core</b>. They line the <b>duodenum, jejunum and ileum</b> and are best developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: has <b>rugae</b> instead, folds that flatten when the stomach fills.<br>B. Ileum: has plicae, but so do the other two segments.<br>C. Duodenum: has plicae, but so do the others.<br>D. Jejunum: best plicae, but not the only place.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 98"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: fold with submucosal core = plica","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares (P) with thin submucosal cores (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Definitions: plica has submucosal core; villus has lamina propria core; microvilli apical","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-16-1","s":"Identify (the structure circled in red)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Plicae circulares!","a":null,"why":"<b>Plica circularis (circular fold).</b> The red circle encloses one whole tall fold. Its core is <b>submucosa (SM)</b> rising from above the muscularis, and its surface carries many <b>villi (V)</b>. Plicae are best developed in the <b>jejunum</b>.<br>Common mix-up: a single villus is far smaller and has only a lamina propria core; a plica is a fold of mucosa plus submucosa carrying many villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-16.jpg","in":"Low-power small intestine (Junqueira): tall folds each covered by many villi (V), with cores of submucosa (SM) arising above the muscularis (M) and serosa (S); one whole fold, from its submucosal core up through its villi, is circled in red; P labels another fold.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":16,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["red circle","one plica circularis (circular fold) with its submucosal core"],["P","plica (another circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (core of each fold)"],["V","villi (arrow) covering the folds"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 99"},{"id":"F3955-70-1","s":"Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circulares.</b> The oval circles a whole <b>fold with a core of submucosa</b>, and the fold is covered by many villi. A fold that carries villi is a plica.<br>B. Microvilli: tiny projections on single cells, seen only as the brush border at high power.<br>C. Villi: the small finger-like projections on the fold, with only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds, which carry no villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-70.jpg","in":"Red oval around one whole large fold with a submucosal core that carries many villi (a plica).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":70,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":72,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":167,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["red oval","one plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core, covered by villi)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 100"},{"id":"G018-58-1","s":"Q11. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circularis.</b> The circle holds a whole fold that has a core of submucosa (connective tissue) and is covered with many villi. Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent circular folds of mucosa plus submucosa: the largest of the three ways the small intestine enlarges its surface (plicae, then villi, then microvilli).<br>B. Brush border (microvilli): far too small to see at this power; it is the striped edge on top of each absorptive cell.<br>C. Villi: the many finger-like projections on the surface of the fold; each has only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall, which flatten out when the stomach fills.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-58.jpg","in":"Low power small intestine; an oval circles one entire tall fold (with a connective-tissue core) covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":58,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["circle","plica circularis: mucosa fold with a submucosa core, covered by villi"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 101"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: finger-like projection = villus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Light-microscope level: intestinal villi with lamina propria cores","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Villi: finger-like fronds of columnar epithelium around a lamina propria core","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-169-1","s":"Q21. Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villi","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Villi.</b> The small oval outlines one <b>finger-like projection</b> of simple columnar epithelium with a <b>lamina propria core</b>, one of many sitting on a larger fold.<br>A. Plicae circulares: the big fold underneath, with a submucosal core.<br>B. Microvilli: too small to see one by one; they form the brush border of each cell.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds without villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-169.jpg","in":"Higher-power H&E of a fold covered by many finger-like projections; a small red oval outlines one projection of epithelium with a lamina-propria core.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":169,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["red oval","one villus (finger of mucosa with a lamina propria core)"]],"like":"G018-59-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 102"},{"id":"G018-59-1","s":"Q12. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. MicrovillIi","C. Villus","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villus","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Villus.</b> The oval is drawn around one slender, finger-like projection. It is covered by a single layer of epithelium and has a thin core of connective tissue. It sticks out from the side of a much larger fold, next to many projections just like it. One such finger of mucosa is a <b>villus</b>. The large fold that carries all of these villi, with the pale core running up its middle, is a plica circularis.</p><p><b>The three levels of folding.</b> The small intestine is built to absorb, so it enlarges its surface at three levels. Tell them apart by size and by what fills the core.</p><ul><li><b>Plicae circulares</b> (valves of Kerckring): permanent circular folds, large enough to see with the naked eye. The core is <b>submucosa</b>. Each fold is covered with many villi.</li><li><b>Villi:</b> finger-like or leaf-like projections of the mucosa, seen with the light microscope. The core is <b>lamina propria</b>.</li><li><b>Microvilli:</b> tiny projections on top of each absorptive cell, seen one by one only with the electron microscope. The core is <b>actin filaments</b>. Together they form the brush border, a thin fuzzy pink line on the cell tops.</li></ul><p><b>Inside a villus.</b> The surface is one layer of columnar cells: mostly absorptive cells (enterocytes), with goblet cells between them. The core of lamina propria holds blood capillaries, a central lacteal (a lymph capillary that takes up absorbed fat) and strands of smooth muscle.</p><p><b>The crypts in this picture.</b> At the foot of the villi the epithelium dips down into short tubes called crypts of Lieberkühn. Here they are the small, dark, round and oval profiles lined up along the base of the villi. They stay in the mucosa and end at the muscularis mucosae, the thin muscle sheet at the bottom of the mucosa.</p><p><b>Facts about the plicae.</b> They begin in the duodenum, are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and fade out in the distal ileum. They are permanent and do not flatten when the gut fills. The folds of the stomach are called rugae. Rugae do flatten, and they carry gastric pits, not villi. So a fold that carries villi always means small intestine.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-59.jpg","in":"Medium power: one fold with many finger-like projections; an oval (and arrow) on a single projection.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":59,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["circle and arrow","one villus: finger of lamina propria covered by enterocytes"]],"n":"28"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Brush border = microvilli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Brush border on enterocytes, and its microvilli by electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-74-1","s":"Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microvilli","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Microvilli.</b> The arrow points to the thin, fuzzy, <b>pink striated (brush) border</b> on the apical surface of columnar absorptive cells. Under the light microscope, the brush border is the packed <b>microvilli</b>.<br>A. Plicae circulares: large folds with a submucosal core, seen at low power.<br>C. Villi: whole finger-like projections with a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-74.jpg","in":"High power of columnar absorptive cells; arrow on the pink fuzzy striated (brush) border at the apical surface.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":74,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":171,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","brush border (microvilli) on the enterocyte surface"]],"like":"G018-60-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 103"},{"id":"G018-60-1","s":"Q13. Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brush border (microvilli)","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Brush border (microvilli).</b> This is a high-power view of the tip of a villus. The lining is a single row of tall columnar cells with dark oval nuclei. The arrow touches the thin, even, pink band that runs along the free top surface of these cells, facing the lumen. That band is the <b>brush border</b>, also called the striated border. It is made of <b>microvilli</b> packed side by side.</p><p><b>What microvilli are.</b> A microvillus is a tiny finger of the cell membrane with a core of actin filaments. Each absorptive cell (enterocyte) carries thousands of them on its top surface. One microvillus is too small to see with the light microscope. Only the electron microscope shows them one by one. With the light microscope they blur together into the fuzzy pink line seen here.</p><p><b>What the brush border does.</b></p><ul><li><b>More surface.</b> It greatly enlarges the membrane available for absorption.</li><li><b>Terminal digestion.</b> Enzymes fixed in the brush border split sugars and short peptides into their final small units, right at the cell surface. The cell then absorbs the products.</li></ul><p><b>Three sizes of surface enlargement.</b> The names are easy to mix up, so sort them by scale.</p><ul><li><b>Plicae circulares:</b> large permanent folds seen with the naked eye. Core of submucosa.</li><li><b>Villi:</b> finger-like projections of mucosa, each made of many cells. Core of lamina propria.</li><li><b>Microvilli:</b> projections of one cell's membrane. Core of actin.</li></ul><p>A band that lies on top of single cells can only be the smallest of the three. The number of villi and microvilli falls along the small intestine: most in the duodenum, fewer in the jejunum, fewest in the ileum.</p><p><b>The crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> Between the villi the epithelium dips down to form simple tubular glands, the crypts (intestinal glands). They reach as far as the muscularis mucosae, the thin muscle sheet at the bottom of the mucosa. So they lie in the mucosa, not in the submucosa. They hold the stem cells that renew the lining, the Paneth cells and many hormone-making enteroendocrine cells. Crypts are present along the whole small intestine and the large intestine. The colon keeps its crypts but has no villi. The esophagus has no crypts, and the stomach has gastric glands instead.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-60.jpg","in":"High power of a villus tip: arrow on the pink apical border of the columnar cells.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":60,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrow","brush border (microvilli) on enterocytes"]],"n":"29"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Crypts: all small intestine and colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Small intestine diagram: crypts of Lieberkühn dip down between the villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: simple columnar intestinal glands (crypts) with goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-60-1","s":"Which gastrointestinal organ(s) contain Crypts of Lieberkühn?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Fundus of Stomach","C. Duodenum","D. Jejunum","E. Ileum","F. Large intestine"],"g":["C","D","E","F"],"k":["C","D","E","F"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum, Jejunum, Ileum, Large intestine","a":"C,D,E,F","why":"<b>C, D, E, F. Duodenum, jejunum, ileum and large intestine.</b> <b>Crypts of Lieberkühn</b> are tubular glands of the mucosa that dip down between the villi all along the small intestine. The <b>large intestine lacks villi but keeps its crypts</b>.<br>A. Esophagus: stratified squamous lining; its mucous glands sit in the submucosa, not crypts.<br>B. Fundus of stomach: gastric pits leading to gastric glands (parietal and chief cells), not intestinal crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":60,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":137,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 104"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: glands at mucosa base = crypts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: crypts sit above the muscularis mucosae; Brunner glands lie below it","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Crypt of Lieberkühn: tubular gland between villi, lined by intestinal cell types","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-49-1","s":"Identify the glandular structures located between the lines (shown in the image).","o":["A. Brunner glands","B. Cardiac glands","C. Crypts of Lieberkühn","D. Fundic glands","E. Pyloric glands"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The lines bracket <b>tubular glands at the base of the mucosa</b>, between the villus bases and the muscularis mucosae. Villi above mean small intestine; the submucosa below holds only vessels (arrows), no glands.<br>A. Brunner glands: pale mucous glands below the muscularis mucosae, in the submucosa.<br>B. Cardiac glands: stomach, next to the esophagus.<br>D. Fundic glands: stomach glands of parietal and chief cells.<br>E. Pyloric glands: coiled mucous glands of the stomach outlet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-49.jpg","in":"Small intestine with tall villi; two black lines bracket the tubular glands in the lower mucosa between villus bases and muscularis mucosae; arrows mark vessels in the gland-free submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 43 (Q3-  Identify the glandular structures located between the lines (shown in the i)","H15B slide 18 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":49,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":213,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["lines","crypt layer between them (crypts of Lieberkühn = intestinal glands)"],["arrows","vessels in the submucosa (right one full of red blood cells)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 105"},{"id":"F0754-177-1","s":"What are these?","o":["A. Crypts of Lieberkuhn","B. Brunners Glands","C. Goblet Cells","D. Plicae circulares","E. Payer’s Patches"],"g":["A"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brunners Glands","a":"B","why":"<b>A. Crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The arrows end on <b>tubular glands at the base of the mucosa</b>, between the villi and just above the muscularis mucosae. They are lined by columnar cells with goblet cells: intestinal crypts.<br>B. Brunner glands: pale, foamy mucous lobules <b>below</b> the muscularis mucosae, in the duodenal submucosa; none are in this field.<br>C. Goblet cells: single pale cells inside these glands, not whole glands.<br>D. Plicae circulares: large folds with a submucosal core.<br>E. Peyer's patches: dark-blue lymphoid nodules of the ileum.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed B (Brunner's glands), but the arrows point to tubular glands inside the mucosa between the villus bases, above the muscularis mucosae: crypts of Lieberkühn."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F0754-177.jpg","in":"H&E of duodenal wall: tall villi above; arrows point to pale-staining glands lying deep to the crypts, below the muscularis mucosae in the submucosa (Brunner's glands).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","crypts of Lieberkühn (intestinal glands) at the base of the mucosa, above the muscularis mucosae"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 106"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Enterocytes: terminal digestion and absorption","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte microvilli enlarge surface for terminal digestion and absorption","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Enterocytes: brush border of microvilli by light and electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-64-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with chronic diarrhea and weight loss (Celiac Disease). A biopsy of the small intestine reveals a significant reduction in villi and enterocytes. Which of the following best describes the primary function of the cells that are most affected in this condition?","o":["A. Secretion of mucus for lubrication","B. Absorption of nutrients","C. Production of antimicrobial peptides","D. Hormonal regulation of digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of nutrients","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Absorption of nutrients.</b> Celiac disease destroys villi and the <b>enterocytes</b> on them. Enterocytes are tall columnar cells whose <b>microvilli</b> finish digestion and absorb nutrients, so losing them causes diarrhea and weight loss.<br>A. Mucus for lubrication: goblet cells.<br>C. Antimicrobial peptides: Paneth cells.<br>D. Hormones: enteroendocrine cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":64,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-30-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 107"},{"id":"G017-30-1","s":"A sample of small intestinal mucosa from a patient with celiac disease and a sample of normal small intestinal mucosa for comparison are illustrated below. Given the histologically apparent structural alterations of the small intestinal mucosa seen here (celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia), what normal functions would you expect to be most abnormal in the patient with celiac disease?","o":["A. Concentration of bile","B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","C. Water resorption","D. Initiation of carbohydrate digestion","E. Initiation of protein and triglyceride digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption.</b> Normal mucosa has tall villi over short crypts. In celiac disease the surface is flat, with no villi (villous atrophy), and the crypts are long (crypt hyperplasia). Villi are covered by enterocytes, the absorptive cells, and each enterocyte carries a brush border of microvilli. When the villi are lost, most of the absorbing surface and most of the brush-border enzymes are lost with them. So the two jobs of the enterocyte fail: the last step of digestion and the absorption of nutrients.</p><p><b>Celiac disease.</b> It is an immune reaction to gluten, a protein of wheat. It flattens the villi, deepens the crypts and fills the epithelium with lymphocytes. With fewer enterocytes and less brush border, nutrients are poorly absorbed. The patient has chronic diarrhea and weight loss.</p><p><b>The enterocyte.</b> It is a tall columnar cell with its nucleus near the base and a dense brush border on top. It is by far the most numerous of the five cell types of the lining. The others are goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells and stem cells.</p><ul><li><b>Terminal digestion:</b> enzymes in the brush border split sugars and short peptides into their final units, right at the cell surface.</li><li><b>Absorption:</b> the cell then takes up the products. Tight junctions seal neighboring cells together, so absorbed material passes through the cells.</li><li><b>Life cycle:</b> stem cells in the crypts divide. The new cells climb the villus and are shed at its tip after about 5 days. Cells do not divide on the villus itself.</li></ul><p><b>How enterocytes absorb fat.</b></p><ol><li>In the lumen, pancreatic lipase splits triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides. Bile salts carry them in tiny droplets called micelles.</li><li>These diffuse into the enterocyte. No active transport is needed.</li><li>In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum they are rebuilt into triglycerides.</li><li>Proteins are added in the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi. The finished packages are called chylomicrons.</li><li>Chylomicrons leave the base of the cell and enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the core of the villus, not the blood capillaries. They reach the blood later through the thoracic duct.</li></ol><p><b>Goblet cells.</b> They are scattered among the enterocytes and secrete mucins, which form the protective mucus layer. Their number rises along the gut: fewest in the duodenum, more in the jejunum and ileum, and most in the colon.</p><p><b>Where the earlier and later steps happen.</b> Starch digestion starts in the mouth with salivary amylase. Protein and fat digestion start in the stomach with pepsin and gastric lipase. Bile is concentrated in the gallbladder. Absorbing water to form stool is the main job of the colon.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Celiac mucosa: flat surface with no villi and long hyperplastic crypts; normal mucosa: tall villi over short crypts (labels in stem).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":30,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":24,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":48,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"},{"id":"F4081-11-1","s":"A 21-year-old woman undergoes endoscopy for chronic diarrhea. Biopsies of different segments of her GI tract are compared to normal histology. One sample shows simple columnar epithelium with tall cells possessing dense microvilli forming a prominent brush border, along with enzymes anchored to this border that complete the final steps of carbohydrate and protein digestion. Which of the following regions of the digestive tract normally has this apical surface modification and associated function?","o":["A. Esophagus – Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium for protection from abrasion","B. Stomach – Simple columnar epithelium with surface mucous cells for acid protection","C. Duodenum – Villi with microvilli that increase absorptive surface area and contain brush-border enzymes","D. Colon – Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells for mucus secretion and water reabsorption","E. Anal canal – Stratified squamous epithelium for increased friction resistance"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum – Villi with microvilli that increase absorptive surface area and contain brush-border enzymes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenum: villi with microvilli and brush-border enzymes.</b> Tall columnar <b>enterocytes</b> with a dense <b>microvillus brush border</b> cover small-intestine villi. Enzymes anchored in that border finish carbohydrate and protein digestion.<br>A. Esophagus: stratified squamous, no microvilli.<br>B. Stomach: surface mucous cells make alkaline mucus; no brush border.<br>D. Colon: no villi; short, sparse microvilli; it absorbs water, it does not finish digestion.<br>E. Anal canal: stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":11,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-30-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 108"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Enterocytes re-make triglycerides","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-40.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte remakes triglycerides in smooth endoplasmic reticulum; chylomicrons exit to lacteal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40","lg":[["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum (packed with small lipid droplets in a)"],["(a) arrows","lipid globules leaving the cell into the space between cells"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["1","bile acids and lipase split fats into glycerol, fatty acids, monoglycerides"],["2","triglycerides remade in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","triglyceride + protein coat = chylomicron"],["4","chylomicrons enter the lacteal (lymph)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-66-1","s":"A 50-year-old male patient is diagnosed with a condition affecting the epithelial cells in the ileum, characterized by an inability to transport lipids properly. Upon further evaluation, it is found that the patient has a deficiency in chylomicron formation. Which cell type in the small intestine is primarily responsible for the reesterification of lipids into triglycerides?","o":["A. Goblet cells","B. M Cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Enterocytes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterocytes","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Enterocytes.</b> Fatty acids and monoglycerides diffuse into enterocytes. There they are <b>re-made into triglycerides in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b>, coated with protein in the Golgi, and sent out as <b>chylomicrons</b> to the lacteals.<br>A. Goblet cells: secrete mucus.<br>B. M (microfold) cells: carry antigens to immune cells.<br>C. Paneth cells: secrete lysozyme and defensins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":66,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 109"},{"id":"F3952-75-1","s":"A 28-year-old man presents to the clinic with symptoms of malabsorption, including weight loss and persistent diarrhea. During your evaluation, you discuss the role of absorptive cells in the small intestine, emphasizing their importance in nutrient absorption, particularly lipids, and the synthesis of triglycerides from these absorbed lipids. You explain that these specialized cells play a crucial role in the digestive process, helping maintain overall health. Which of the following is true of the absorptive cells of the small intestine?","o":["A. Also called enteroendocrine cells","B. Have many microvilli covering their basal surfaces","C. Absorb lipids by active transport","D. Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids","E. Undergo mitosis at tips of villi and are sloughed off into crypts"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids.</b> <b>Enterocytes</b> re-make triglycerides in their smooth endoplasmic reticulum and package them as chylomicrons for the lacteals.<br>A. Enteroendocrine cells: a different, hormone-making cell.<br>B. Microvilli cover the <b>apical</b> (lumen) surface, not the basal one.<br>C. Lipids enter by <b>passive diffusion</b> (and carriers), not active transport.<br>E. Reversed: cells divide in the <b>crypts</b>, move up, and are shed at villus tips.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":75,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 110"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Fat leaves villi via lacteals","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-40.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte packs triglycerides into chylomicrons that enter the lymphatic lacteal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40","lg":[["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum (packed with small lipid droplets in a)"],["(a) arrows","lipid globules leaving the cell into the space between cells"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["1","bile acids and lipase split fats into glycerol, fatty acids, monoglycerides"],["2","triglycerides remade in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","triglyceride + protein coat = chylomicron"],["4","chylomicrons enter the lacteal (lymph)"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-12.jpg","cap":"Villus diagram: central lacteal beside arteriole and venule","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-205-1","s":"Q7. What is true about fat absorption in the GI tract?","o":["A. Fat is taken up by sinusoidal capillaries in the villi","B. Fat is absorbed via the GLUT2 transporter","C. Fat is taken up via fenestrated capillaries in the villi","D. Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi.</b> Enterocytes re-make fats into triglycerides, pack them into <b>chylomicrons</b>, and release them at their base. Chylomicrons are too big for blood capillaries, so they enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymphatic capillary in the center of each villus.<br>A. Sinusoidal capillaries: in liver and bone marrow, not villi.<br>B. GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2): moves sugars, not fat.<br>C. Fenestrated villus capillaries: take up sugars, amino acids and short-chain fatty acids, not chylomicrons.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":205,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":187,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 111"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Crypt stem cells mostly make enterocytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Regenerative stem cells at crypt base renew enterocytes, goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-67-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and diarrhea that has persisted for several weeks. During the examination, you discuss the structure and function of her small intestine, explaining how nutrients are absorbed and how the intestinal mucosa is lined with various cell types that contribute to this process. You highlight the role of intestinal glands, including the presence of stem cells in the crypts, and you focus on the predominant cell type that arises from these stem cells, which is crucial for nutrient absorption. Intestinal glands (or crypts) in the mucosa of the small intestine include stem cells. What is the most abundant cell type derived from these stem cells?","o":["A. Enterocytes","B. Goblet cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Chief cells","E. Parietal cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterocytes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enterocytes.</b> Stem cells in the <b>crypts</b> make every small-intestine lining cell, and the most numerous are the absorptive <b>enterocytes</b>. They climb the villus and are shed at its tip in about a week.<br>B. Goblet cells: made by the same stem cells, but far fewer.<br>C. Paneth cells: few, at the crypt base.<br>D. Chief cells: stomach.<br>E. Parietal cells: stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":67,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 112"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Paneth granules: lysozyme and defensins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: innate immunity; granules release lysozyme, defensins, phospholipase A2","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells packed with bright red apical granules (arrow)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-232-1","s":"What cells do you find in the Crypts of Lieberkuhn whose function is to secrete definsins and lysozomes?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Paneth cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Paneth cells.</b> They sit at the <b>base of the crypts of Lieberkühn</b> and have <b>large red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b>. The granules release <b>defensins, lysozyme and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria (innate immunity) and keep the crypt clean.<br>Common mix-up: goblet cells also line the crypts, but they secrete mucus and look pale, not red.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"High-power H&E of a crypt base with cells containing bright red apical granules (not needed to answer).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":232,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 113"},{"id":"G017-27-1","s":"The granules within the cells at the pointer are rich in which of the following substances?","o":["A. Defensins","B. Cholecystokinin","C. Pepsin","D. IgA","E. Mucin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Defensins","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Defensins.</b> The picture is a high-power view of the bottom of an intestinal crypt. The arrows point to clusters of large, <b>bright red granules</b>. The granules fill the tops of the cells at the very base of the crypt, on the side that faces the crypt lumen. Cells at the crypt base with bright red (eosinophilic) granules at the top are <b>Paneth cells</b>. Their granules hold substances that kill bacteria: <b>defensins</b>, lysozyme and phospholipase A2.</p><p><b>What Paneth cells do.</b> They belong to innate immunity, the built-in defense that works without antibodies. They release their granules into the crypt lumen. This keeps the crypt and the stem cells that live in it free of microbes. It also controls the number of bacteria in the gut.</p><p><b>The cells of a crypt and how to tell them apart.</b></p><ul><li><b>Paneth cells:</b> base of the crypt. Pyramid-shaped, packed with large bright red granules at the top.</li><li><b>Stem cells:</b> lower part of the crypt. Small cells, often caught dividing (mitotic figures). They renew the whole lining every few days: mostly enterocytes, plus goblet, Paneth and enteroendocrine cells.</li><li><b>Enteroendocrine cells:</b> crypts and villi. Pale, with granules at the <b>base</b> of the cell. Special stains are needed to show them. They make hormones.</li><li><b>Goblet cells:</b> crypts and villi. A pale, mucus-filled cup with the nucleus at the base. They make mucin.</li></ul><p><b>Two look-alikes with granules.</b> The chief cell of the stomach also stores granules at its top. But it is purple and lies in a gastric gland, and it makes pepsinogen, which becomes pepsin. The enteroendocrine cell is pale, and its granules sit at the base.</p><p><b>Where immunoglobulin A (IgA) comes from.</b> This antibody of the gut is made by plasma cells in the lamina propria, the connective tissue around the crypts.</p><p><b>Hormones of the small intestine.</b> They come from the enteroendocrine cells.</p><ul><li><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK):</b> released when fat and protein reach the duodenum. It makes the gallbladder contract and release bile, and it makes the acinar cells of the pancreas secrete enzymes. It also slows the stomach and signals fullness.</li><li><b>Secretin:</b> released when acid reaches the duodenum. It makes the pancreatic ducts secrete bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid.</li><li><b>Glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) and peptide YY:</b> signal fullness (satiety) to the brain.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G017-27.jpg","in":"High-power crypt base of small intestine; arrows point to cells packed with bright red/magenta apical granules (Paneth cells).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":27,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":24,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":17,"f":"F0731"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":19,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["arrows","Paneth cell granules (bright red, crypt base; defensins, lysozyme)"]],"n":"31"},{"id":"F3952-71-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman presents with recurrent gastrointestinal infections and unexplained weight loss. During your examination, you discuss the role of various intestinal cells in maintaining gut health, particularly the function of Paneth cells located in the crypts of the small intestine. You explain that these cells play a vital role in secreting antimicrobial peptides that help regulate the intestinal microbiome, and you consider how a reduction in their numbers could impact her gastrointestinal health. Which of the following would most likely result from a reduction in the number of Paneth cells?","o":["A. Thinning of the glycocalyx","B. Reduced breakdown of fats","C. Elevated levels of undigested proteins","D. Decreased mucus in the intestine","E. Increased number of intestinal bacteria"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased number of intestinal bacteria","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Increased number of intestinal bacteria.</b> <b>Paneth cells</b> at the crypt base release <b>lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria and keep the crypt clean. Fewer Paneth cells, more bacteria.<br>A. Glycocalyx: made by enterocytes.<br>B. Fat breakdown: pancreatic lipase and bile.<br>C. Protein digestion: pancreatic proteases and brush-border peptidases.<br>D. Mucus: goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-27-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 114"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Paneth cells: red granules at crypt base","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-17.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells with red granules (A); circled cell is dividing","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17","lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: crypt-base cells packed with bright red apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18","lg":[["arrow","Paneth cell granules (large, bright red, toward the lumen)"],["arrowhead","enteroendocrine cell (pale, finely granular cytoplasm, wedged between crypt cells)"],["M","mast cell in the lamina propria (packed with purple-red granules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-14-1","s":"Identify [the cell indicated by the arrow / blank label].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Paneth cells (at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn, small intestine)","a":null,"why":"<b>Paneth cells.</b> The arrow marks cells at the <b>base of a crypt of Lieberkühn</b> packed with <b>large, bright red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b>. The granules hold lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2, which kill bacteria.<br>Common mix-up: stomach chief cells are also granular, but their cytoplasm is purple (basophilic) and they line gastric glands, not intestinal crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-14.jpg","in":"Base of a small-intestinal crypt: arrow (blank label) points to cells packed with large bright red apical granules flanking the crypt lumen (A).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":14,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow (label hidden by the gray box)","a Paneth cell (nucleus at its base, red granules toward the lumen)"],["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 115"},{"id":"F3955-60-1","s":"Name the cell shown by the letter A:","o":["A. Chief cell","B. Parietal cell","C. Enteroendocrine cell","D. Paneth cell"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Paneth cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Paneth cell.</b> A labels cells at the <b>base of an intestinal crypt</b> packed with <b>large, bright red apical granules</b>: Paneth cells, whose granules hold lysozyme and defensins. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Chief cell: stomach gland cell with purple (basophilic) cytoplasm.<br>B. Parietal cell: stomach; large, pale pink, central nucleus, no coarse red granules.<br>C. Enteroendocrine cell: small and pale on H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), with granules at its base, not its apex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-60.jpg","in":"Crypt base with cells full of large bright red apical granules (A); a circled cell elsewhere.","tg":["H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":60,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":157,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 116"},{"id":"F0754-179-1","s":"(Micrograph with four labelled prompts) B. What types of cells are these? C. What types of cells are these? A. What is labeled A? D. What is this called? (hint- found in the small intestine)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"B. Paneth cells; C. stem cell proliferating; A. lysozymes and defensins; D. Crypts of Lieberkuhn","a":null,"why":"<b>B = Paneth cells; C = a dividing stem cell; A = Paneth granules (lysozyme, defensins); D = a crypt of Lieberkühn.</b> Paneth cells sit at the <b>very base of the crypt</b>, packed with <b>bright red apical granules</b>. The circled cell higher up shows a <b>mitotic figure</b>: crypt stem cells divide to renew the lining.<br>Common mix-up: Paneth granules are red (eosinophilic); stomach chief cells have purple (basophilic) cytoplasm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-179.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of the base of a small-intestinal crypt: arrow B to cells at the crypt base packed with bright red apical granules; A marks those red granules; circle C on a mitotic/proliferating nucleus higher up the crypt wall; D = the whole gland.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["B arrow","Paneth cells at the crypt base"],["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cell granules (red; lysozyme, defensins)"],["C circle","dividing stem cell (mitotic figure)"],["D","the whole gland: crypt of Lieberkühn (small intestine)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 117"},{"id":"G018-55-1","s":"Q8. The granules in the cell indicated by the letter “A” contain:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lysozyme","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lysozyme.</b> Cell A sits at the base of a crypt of Lieberkühn and is packed with large, bright red (eosinophilic) granules: a Paneth cell. Its granules hold antibacterial proteins: lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: chief cells of the stomach.<br>B. Intrinsic factor: parietal cells of the stomach.<br>C. Histamine: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach.<br>D. Gastrin: G cells of the pyloric antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-55.jpg","in":"Base of an intestinal crypt: cell A filled with large bright red granules; a mitotic figure is circled nearby.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A (arrowheads)","Paneth cells: big red granules (lysozyme, defensins) at the crypt base"],["circle","dividing cell (mitotic figure: clumped chromosomes with a clear halo); crypts are where new epithelial cells are made"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 118"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Intestinal enteroendocrine cells: CCK and secretin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells secrete cholecystokinin and secretin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-41.jpg","cap":"Open enteroendocrine cell; its hormones (cholecystokinin, secretin) control digestive secretion","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-12-1","s":"Which of the following small intestine cell types secretes CCK and secretin?","o":["A. Regenerative cells","B. Paneth cells","C. Enteroendocrine cells","D. Goblet cells","E. Surface absorptive cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Enteroendocrine cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Enteroendocrine (EE) cells.</b> Small-intestine EE cells secrete <b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>, which releases pancreatic enzymes and bile, and <b>secretin</b>, which releases pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>A. Regenerative cells: stem cells at the crypt base.<br>B. Paneth cells: lysozyme and defensins.<br>D. Goblet cells: mucus.<br>E. Surface absorptive cells (enterocytes): absorb nutrients.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":12,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 119"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Brunner glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"sl/G017-25.jpg","cap":"Brunner gland mucus, pH eight to nine-and-a-half, neutralizes acid chyme","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-14-1","s":"Brunner’s glands are a defining feature of which portion of intestine?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Cecum","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Duodenum.</b> <b>Brunner glands</b> are branched mucous glands in the <b>submucosa</b> that make alkaline mucus to neutralize stomach acid. Among the intestines they are <b>exclusive to the duodenum</b>.<br>B. Jejunum: prominent plicae, thin submucosa without glands.<br>C. Ileum: Peyer's patches.<br>D. Cecum: large-intestine mucosa, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: crypts and goblet cells, no submucosal glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labelled micrograph of duodenal wall showing Brunner's glands in the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae (labels give the answer away; not needed).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":14,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 120"},{"id":"F3952-69-1","s":"A 60-year-old man presents to the clinic with complaints of persistent abdominal discomfort and changes in bowel habits. During the physical examination, you discuss the anatomy of his gastrointestinal tract and highlight the various secretory functions that occur along its length. You explain that specific regions of the digestive tract contain large mucous glands in their submucosa, which play a crucial role in lubrication and protection of the intestinal lining. Large mucous glands are present in the submucosa of what digestive tract region?","o":["A. Corpus of the stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Pyloric stomach","D. Ileum","E. Large intestine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Large mucous glands in the <b>submucosa</b> of the intestine are <b>Brunner glands</b>. They make alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme and, among the intestines, are found only in the duodenum.<br>A. Corpus of the stomach: glands in the mucosa that secrete acid and enzymes.<br>C. Pyloric stomach: mucous glands, but in the mucosa, not the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer's patches, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Large intestine: crypts in the mucosa only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":69,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-24-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 121"},{"id":"G017-24-1","s":"Histological evaluation of a portion of the gastrointestinal tract from a 55-year-old man with long-term gastrointestinal complaints shows ramified, tubular glands located in the submucosal layer. The glands contain secretions with pH close to 9.0. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract matches these findings?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Duodenum.</b> Three clues point to one structure. The glands are branched (\"ramified\") and tubular. They lie in the <b>submucosa</b>. Their secretion is strongly alkaline, with a pH near 9. These are <b>Brunner glands</b>, and Brunner glands exist only in the duodenum.</p><p><b>Brunner glands.</b></p><ul><li><b>Type:</b> branched tubular mucous glands. Their cells look pale because they are filled with mucus.</li><li><b>Place:</b> the submucosa of the duodenum. Their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae, the thin muscle sheet at the bottom of the mucosa, and open into the crypts.</li><li><b>Product:</b> alkaline mucus, pH about 9.</li><li><b>Job:</b> the stomach empties acid chyme (the mix of food and gastric juice) into the duodenum. The alkaline mucus neutralizes that acid and protects the duodenal lining.</li></ul><p><b>Why glands in the submucosa are such a strong clue.</b> Almost all glands of the digestive tube lie in the mucosa. The gastric glands of the stomach and the crypts of the small intestine and colon all end at the muscularis mucosae. Glands in the submucosa are the exception. The esophagus has mucous glands there, and so does the duodenum. Of the stomach and all the parts of the intestine, the duodenum is the only one. So villi above and pale mucous glands filling the submucosa mean duodenum.</p><p><b>Other features of the duodenum.</b> Like the rest of the small intestine it has villi and crypts. Its villi are broad and leaf-like. Its outer coat is serosa on the first part and adventitia on parts 2 to 4.</p><p><b>What the rest of the small intestine has in its place.</b> The jejunum has a thin, plain submucosa with no glands, under the tallest and most numerous folds (plicae circulares). The ileum has Peyer patches, which are large clusters of lymphoid nodules, not glands.</p><p><b>The pylorus is the look-alike.</b> The pylorus of the stomach also has pale mucous glands. But they sit in the mucosa, under deep pits, and the surface has no villi.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Small intestine with villi (mucosa) above and lobules of pale branched glands filling the submucosa (Brunner glands); stem gives the finding.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":24,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Micrograph: submucosal glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19","lg":[["dashed line","boundary between villi (above) and intestinal crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct crossing the muscularis mucosae into a crypt"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-62-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Leaf-like <b>villi</b> show it is small intestine, and the submucosa below is <b>packed with lobules of pale mucous glands</b>. Submucosal mucous glands in the intestine are <b>Brunner glands</b>, found only in the duodenum.<br>A. Gallbladder: folded mucosa, no villi, no submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches), no glands.<br>D. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.<br>E. Jejunum: tall plicae with a thin, gland-free submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-62.jpg","in":"Villi over a submucosa filled with lobules of pale mucous glands (Brunner glands).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":62,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":64,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":66,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":68,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":80,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":159,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-4-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 122"},{"id":"F0052-161-1","s":"Q17. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Villi line the edge, so it is small intestine. Under the mucosa, the <b>submucosa is filled with lobules of pale-staining mucous glands</b>: <b>Brunner glands</b>, the mark of the duodenum.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi and no submucosal glands.<br>C. Ileum: dark lymphoid nodules instead of glands.<br>D. Colon: no villi.<br>E. Jejunum: tall plicae, thin gland-free submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-161.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: villous mucosa curving along the right/bottom with lobules of pale submucosal glands beneath it and muscularis externa at upper left.","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 19 (Duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":161,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-4-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 123"},{"id":"G018-56-1","s":"Q9. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> The surface has villi, so this is small intestine, and the submucosa is filled with a thick layer of pale mucous glands: the Brunner (submucosal) glands. Brunner glands are found only in the duodenum; their alkaline mucus helps neutralize the acid coming from the stomach.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits and glands, no villi.<br>C. Jejunum: tall plicae and villi, but no glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer patches (large lymphoid nodules), no submucosal glands.<br>E. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-56.jpg","in":"Low power: villous mucosa with a broad band of pale glands below the muscularis mucosae, then muscularis externa.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-4-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 124"},{"id":"G118-4-1","s":"Which segment of the GI tract is presented in the image below?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Duodenum.</b> Read the picture from left to right. On the left is the darker mucosa, with its crypts packed side by side. At the lower right is a solid pink band of smooth muscle, the muscularis externa. The layer between them is the <b>submucosa</b>. Here it is filled with large lobules of pale, coiled glands with open lumens, and the three arrows point to them. Pale mucous glands that fill the submucosa of the intestine are <b>Brunner glands</b>, and only the duodenum has them.</p><p><b>How to be sure the glands are in the submucosa.</b> The wall of the digestive tube has four layers, from the lumen outward:</p><ol><li><b>Mucosa:</b> the epithelium, the lamina propria (loose connective tissue) and the muscularis mucosae (a thin sheet of smooth muscle).</li><li><b>Submucosa:</b> denser connective tissue with larger blood vessels.</li><li><b>Muscularis externa:</b> the thick coat of smooth muscle.</li><li><b>Serosa or adventitia:</b> the outer covering.</li></ol><p>The crypts of Lieberkühn are the intestine's own glands. They are straight tubes that end at the muscularis mucosae, so they stay in the mucosa. Any gland beyond the muscularis mucosae lies in the submucosa. In the intestine that is always a Brunner gland.</p><p><b>What Brunner glands do.</b> They are branched tubular mucous glands. Their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and empty into the crypts. They secrete alkaline mucus with a pH of about 9. It neutralizes the acid chyme that arrives from the stomach and protects the lining of the duodenum.</p><p><b>How each stretch of intestine is recognized.</b></p><ul><li><b>Duodenum:</b> villi plus pale mucous glands filling the submucosa.</li><li><b>Jejunum:</b> villi on tall folds with thin cores, and nothing in the submucosa.</li><li><b>Ileum:</b> villi plus Peyer patches. These are large lymphoid nodules: solid, dark-blue balls of small cells with no lumens.</li><li><b>Colon:</b> a flat surface with no villi, over straight crypts full of goblet cells.</li></ul><p><b>The pylorus of the stomach.</b> It also has pale mucous glands, but they sit in the mucosa under deep pits, and there are no villi and no crypts.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-4.jpg","in":"Wall of the small intestine, H&E: darker mucosa with crypts on the left; three arrows from a blacked-out label point to large lobules of pale, coiled glands that fill the submucosa (Brunner's glands); muscularis externa at lower right.","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":4,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Jejunum: thin submucosa, no glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Only the duodenum has submucosal glands; jejunum has prominent plicae","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-174-1","s":"True or false? The submucosa of the jejunum is thin/non-remarkable, has no glands, and prominent plicae circularis","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"True","a":"A","why":"<b>A. True.</b> The jejunum has a <b>thin, unremarkable submucosa with no glands</b> and the <b>most prominent plicae circulares</b> of the gut. Its mucosa has villi, crypts and a rising number of goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the duodenum also has plicae, but its submucosa is filled with Brunner glands; the ileum has Peyer's patches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":174,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 125"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Micrograph: tall plicae, no glands → jejunum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21","lg":[["V","villi covering each fold"],["P","plica circularis (circular fold)"],["SM","submucosa (blue-green core of each fold)"],["M","muscularis externa (inner circular, outer longitudinal)"],["S","serosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-165-1","s":"Q19. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Jejunum.</b> Several <b>tall, finger-like plicae circulares</b> rise from the wall, each with a <b>thin submucosal core</b> and covered by villi. There are <b>no submucosal glands</b> and no Peyer's patches. Prominent plicae plus a bare submucosa mean jejunum.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches).<br>D. Colon: no villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-165.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: several tall, finger-like plicae circulares with thin submucosal cores, each covered by villi; no submucosal glands or Peyer's patches.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":165,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 126"},{"id":"F0052-163-1","s":"Q18. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Jejunum.</b> The wall throws up <b>tall folds (plicae circulares)</b> with <b>thin submucosal cores</b>, each covered by villi. There are <b>no submucosal glands</b> and no large lymphoid nodules.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi or plicae.<br>B. Duodenum: submucosa full of Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: Peyer's patches (dark lymphoid nodules).<br>D. Colon: no villi; flat surface with straight crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-163.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: tall branching folds (plicae) with thin submucosal cores covered by villi; no submucosal glands or large lymphoid aggregates; muscularis and mesentery at top.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":163,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 127"},{"id":"G018-57-1","s":"Q10. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> Tall, well-developed plicae circulares (folds with a submucosal core), each covered with villi. The submucosa is thin and has no glands, and the lymphoid nodules are small. Plicae are most developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: rugae and gastric pits, no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: would show Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: would show large Peyer patches; its plicae are smaller.<br>E. Colon: no villi or plicae; straight crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-57.jpg","in":"Low power: several tall folds of mucosa with a submucosal core, each covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-10-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 128"},{"id":"G118-10-1","s":"The structute indicated by the bracket is characteristic of which organ?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Jejunum","C. Colon","D. Appendix"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Jejunum.</b> The bracket spans one whole tall fold, from its base to its tip. The fold has a pale pink core that rises from the submucosa, and its whole surface is covered with many slender villi. A permanent fold with a core of submucosa, covered with villi, is a <b>plica circularis</b>. Plicae are tallest and most numerous in the <b>jejunum</b>. The rest of the picture agrees: the submucosa under the folds is thin and plain, with no glands and no lymphoid nodules.</p><p><b>Plicae circulares.</b> They are also called valves of Kerckring. They are circular folds of the mucosa with its submucosa, large enough to see with the naked eye. They begin in the duodenum, reach their greatest height and number in the jejunum, and fade out in the distal ileum. They are permanent and do not flatten when the gut fills.</p><p><b>A fold that carries villi means small intestine.</b></p><ul><li><b>Stomach:</b> its folds are called rugae. They flatten when the stomach fills, and they are covered with gastric pits, not villi.</li><li><b>Colon:</b> no plicae and no villi. The surface is flat, over straight crypts full of goblet cells.</li><li><b>Appendix:</b> no plicae and no villi. It is a small tube with a tiny lumen ringed by lymphoid follicles.</li></ul><p><b>Which part of the small intestine.</b> All three parts have villi and crypts. Look for the signature of each.</p><ul><li><b>Duodenum:</b> pale mucous glands (Brunner glands) fill the submucosa. The villi are broad and leaf-like.</li><li><b>Jejunum:</b> nothing fills the submucosa. Tall plicae with thin cores, and long, finger-like villi.</li><li><b>Ileum:</b> large lymphoid nodules (Peyer patches) in the lamina propria and submucosa. Shorter villi.</li></ul><p><b>Two gradients along the intestine.</b> The absorbing surface falls along the way. Villi and microvilli are most numerous in the duodenum, fewer in the jejunum and fewer still in the ileum, and the colon has no villi. Goblet cells do the opposite. They rise from duodenum to jejunum to ileum and are most numerous in the colon, because the contents get drier and need more mucus.</p><p><b>The other layers in this picture.</b> The small dark rings at the foot of the villi are crypts of Lieberkühn cut across. The solid pink band along the bottom is the muscularis externa. The outer coat of the jejunum is serosa.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-10.jpg","in":"Small intestine, H&E, low power: tall permanent folds with a pale core of submucosa, each covered with many villi; the bracket spans one whole fold (plica circularis); thin submucosa with no glands and no lymphoid nodules.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":10,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":22,"label":"Ileum: Peyer's patches with M cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-23.jpg","cap":"Ileum: dark Peyer's patch lymphoid nodules beneath the villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 23","lg":[["dashed line (top picture)","outline of the Peyer's patch (merged lymphoid nodules)"],["large box (bottom picture)","one lymphoid nodule and the mucosa above it"],["small box (bottom picture)","lower edge of a nodule against the submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-24.jpg","cap":"Scanning electron micrograph: M-cell domes over Peyer patches, ringed by villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-181-1","s":"Which of these contains M cells, peyer’s patches, and plicae circularis?","o":["A. Appendix","B. Ileum","C. Colon","D. Jejunum","E. Duodenum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Ileum.</b> The three features meet in only one place. <b>Peyer patches</b> are the signature of the ileum. <b>M cells</b> are the special epithelial cells that cover Peyer patches, so they are found there too. Plicae circulares, the permanent folds of the small intestine, are still present in the ileum and fade out only in its last part.</p><p><b>Peyer patches.</b> They are large clusters of lymphoid nodules. They lie in the lamina propria and reach into the submucosa. Over each nodule the epithelium forms a dome with few or no villi. So a section of ileum shows villi plus large, dark, round nodules of lymphocytes. The ileum also has shorter villi and more goblet cells than the rest of the small intestine.</p><p><b>M (microfold) cells.</b> They cover the dome over each nodule. They do not absorb nutrients. They take up antigens and microbes from the lumen and hand them to the immune cells waiting underneath. This starts the gut's immune response.</p><p><b>Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT).</b> This is the immune tissue built into the gut lining. It is made of lymphocytes, plasma cells that secrete immunoglobulin A (IgA), antigen-presenting cells and lymphoid nodules, plus lymphocytes inside the epithelium. It holds about 70% of the body's immune cells. Its large collections are the tonsils, the Peyer patches and the appendix.</p><p><b>The appendix, the other lymphoid organ of the gut.</b> The appendix is a blind pouch off the cecum. It is a small round tube with a tiny, often star-shaped lumen. Its mucosa is like the colon's, with crypts and no villi, but with fewer and shorter glands. Large lymphoid follicles, many with pale germinal centers, form a ring around the lumen. It has no plicae. Its outer longitudinal muscle is a complete layer, without the three bands (teniae coli) of the colon. So lymphoid nodules together with villi mean ileum. A ring of follicles around a tiny lumen with no villi means appendix.</p><p><b>Two diseases of the appendix.</b></p><ul><li><b>Acute appendicitis:</b> the lumen is blocked and then inflamed. Neutrophils spread through the wall into the muscularis externa. Pain starts around the navel and moves to the right lower abdomen. It is most common in teenagers and young adults.</li><li><b>Neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor:</b> a small, firm, yellow nodule, usually at the tip. It is made of nests of uniform cells with hormone granules, so it arises from enteroendocrine cells. The appendix is one of its most common sites.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":181,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"},{"id":"F4081-16-1","s":"Identify [this SEM image].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ileum: Peyer patch surface showing M (microfold) cells (dome epithelium surrounded by villi)","a":null,"why":"<b>Ileum: the dome over a Peyer patch, covered by M (microfold) cells.</b> In this scanning electron micrograph, round <b>domes with a fine net-like surface</b> sit among leaf-like villi. The domes are the epithelium over Peyer patch lymphoid nodules; their surface cells are M cells, which pass antigens to immune cells below.<br>Common mix-up: the leaf-like shapes around the domes are ordinary villi, not M cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-16.jpg","in":"Scanning electron micrograph: several round domes with a fine net-like surface, each ringed by leaf-like villi.","tg":["H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)","H15B slide 24 (Peyer patch showing M (Microfold) cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":16,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0754-181-1","kind":"harder","n":"11 extra 129"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":22,"label":"M cells pass antigens to immune cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-24.jpg","cap":"M cells endocytose antigens and pass them to pocketed lymphocytes and macrophages","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Microfold (M) cells with intraepithelial pocket of lymphocytes and antigen-presenting cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42","lg":[["V","villi"],["N","lymphoid nodules of the Peyer patch"],["(a) arrow","germinal center of a nodule"],["B","brush border of enterocytes"],["E","enterocytes (darker cytoplasm)"],["L","lymphocytes in the M cell's pocket"],["D","dendritic (antigen-presenting) cells"],["BM","basement membrane (porous under M cells)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-20-1","s":"Which of the following describes M-cells?","o":["A. Secrete a protective mucus layer made up of high molecular weight glycoproteins","B. Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages","C. Secrete cholecystokinin and secretin","D. Produce lysozyme and regulate the bacterial line up","E. Contain microvilli and facilitate absorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages.</b> <b>M (microfold) cells</b> cover Peyer's patches in the ileum. Their base has deep pockets holding lymphocytes and macrophages, so antigens taken from the lumen go straight to immune cells.<br>A. Protective mucus: goblet cells.<br>C. Cholecystokinin and secretin: enteroendocrine cells.<br>D. Lysozyme and bacterial control: Paneth cells.<br>E. Microvilli and absorption: enterocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 24 (Peyer patch showing M (Microfold) cells)","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":20,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-45-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 130"},{"id":"F3980-45-1","s":"What cell works by sampling antigen from the intestinal lumen and trafficking it across the epithelium to immune cells?","o":["A. Macrophages","B. Enterocytes","C. Microfold cells","D. Mesothelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Microfold cells - aka M Cells","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Microfold cells.</b> The question describes one job: taking antigen from the lumen and carrying it across the epithelium to immune cells. That is the job of the <b>microfold (M) cell</b>. An M cell is an epithelial cell, part of the lining itself. So it touches the lumen on one side and the immune cells on the other.</p><p><b>Where M cells are.</b> They sit in the epithelium that covers Peyer patches. Peyer patches are large clusters of lymphoid nodules in the ileum. They lie in the lamina propria and reach into the submucosa. Over each nodule the epithelium forms a dome with few or no villi, and M cells cover this dome.</p><p><b>How an M cell is built.</b></p><ul><li><b>Top:</b> short folds, called microfolds, in place of a brush border. The cell does not absorb nutrients.</li><li><b>Base:</b> hollowed into a pocket. Lymphocytes and macrophages sit inside this pocket, right under the cell.</li></ul><p><b>How antigen sampling works.</b></p><ol><li>The M cell takes up antigens and microbes from the lumen by endocytosis, which means wrapping them in a small sac of membrane.</li><li>It carries them across the cell.</li><li>It releases them into the pocket at its base, to the lymphocytes and macrophages there.</li><li>These immune cells start the gut's immune response and spread the information. The result is immunoglobulin A (IgA), the antibody that the plasma cells of the gut secrete.</li></ol><p><b>The neighbors of the M cell.</b></p><ul><li><b>Enterocytes:</b> the ordinary absorptive cells of the lining. They have a brush border, finish digestion and absorb nutrients.</li><li><b>Macrophages:</b> immune cells under the epithelium. They receive the antigen from the M cell.</li><li><b>Mesothelial cells:</b> flat cells that cover the serosa, on the outer surface of the gut wall.</li></ul><p><b>The larger system.</b> Peyer patches are part of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT), the immune tissue of the gut lining. MALT holds about 70% of the body's immune cells, mostly B cells, IgA-secreting plasma cells and CD4<sup>+</sup> helper T cells. Its large collections are the tonsils, the Peyer patches and the appendix.</p><p><b>Other jobs of the ileum.</b> It reabsorbs bile acids, and it absorbs vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":45,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":190,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":208,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"},{"id":"F3950-68-1","s":"A patient presents with recurrent infections and a diagnosis of an immunodeficiency disorder. Further investigation reveals the presence of Peyer patches in the ileum that are not functioning properly. Which cell type in the intestinal mucosa is primarily responsible for sampling antigens and microorganisms to maintain mucosal immunity?","o":["A. Enterocytes","B. Goblet cells","C. Paneth cells","D. M (microfold) cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"M (microfold) cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. M (microfold) cells.</b> M cells cover the <b>Peyer patches</b> of the ileum. They sample antigens and microbes from the lumen and pass them to the lymphocytes and macrophages in the patch, starting mucosal immunity.<br>A. Enterocytes: absorb nutrients.<br>B. Goblet cells: make mucus.<br>C. Paneth cells: release lysozyme and defensins to kill bacteria; they do not hand antigens to immune cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":68,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-45-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 131"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colon absorbs water and electrolytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colonocytes: short microvilli, dilated intercellular spaces, a sign of water transport","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-30.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: transports sodium actively, water passively; forms stool","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-79-1","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of intermittent abdominal pain and changes in bowel habits. Upon examination, he is noted to have increased bowel sounds and tenderness in the lower abdomen. A colonoscopy reveals significant diverticulosis in the sigmoid colon. Which of the following is a primary function of the large intestine that may be compromised in this patient?","o":["A. Absorption of nutrients","B. Production of bile","C. Absorption of water and electrolytes","D. Secretion of digestive enzymes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of water and electrolytes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Absorption of water and electrolytes.</b> Colon absorptive cells <b>pump sodium actively, and water follows passively</b>, turning liquid chyme into stool.<br>A. Nutrient absorption: small intestine.<br>B. Bile production: liver.<br>D. Digestive enzymes: pancreas, stomach and small-intestine brush border; the colon makes mucus, not enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":79,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 132"},{"id":"F3952-84-1","s":"At a health and wellness summit, actress and wellness advocate Gwyneth Paltrow speaks passionately about the importance of gut health and its impact on overall well-being. As she discusses her dietary choices and the role of various nutrients in digestion, she highlights the specific functions of the cells lining the large intestine. When asked about the primary role of colonocytes, she confidently shares their key function. What is the main function of colonocytes in the large intestine?","o":["A. Reuptake of mucus secreted by goblet cells","B. Absorption of amino acids produced by proteolytic enzymes","C. Absorption of water and electrolytes","D. Neutralization of acid produced in the stomach","E. Protection against friction produced by feces"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of water and electrolytes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Absorption of water and electrolytes.</b> Colonocytes (colon absorptive cells) <b>pump sodium actively, and water follows passively</b>. Their short microvilli and wide spaces between cells are signs of water transport.<br>A. Reuptake of mucus: mucus is not reabsorbed; it lubricates stool.<br>B. Amino acid absorption: small-intestine enterocytes.<br>D. Acid neutralization: Brunner glands and pancreatic bicarbonate in the duodenum.<br>E. Protection from friction: goblet-cell mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":84,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 133"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic goblet cells lubricate stool","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-29.jpg","cap":"Colon gland at high power: many pale, bubbly goblet cells (arrowhead)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands: goblet cells beside columnar absorptive cells (colonocytes)","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-81-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of diarrhea and abdominal cramping. She reports having increased mucus in her stools and a sensation of incomplete evacuation. Histological examination of a biopsy from the colon reveals an increase in goblet cells. What is the primary role of the goblet cells in the colon?","o":["A. Absorption of water","B. Lubrication of fecal matter","C. Production of digestive enzymes","D. Secretion of hormones"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lubrication of fecal matter","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lubrication of fecal matter.</b> Colonic <b>goblet cells</b> secrete mucus that lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining. More goblet cells, more mucus in the stool.<br>A. Water absorption: the columnar absorptive cells (colonocytes).<br>C. Digestive enzymes: not made in the colon.<br>D. Hormones: enteroendocrine cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":81,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 134"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic mucosa: simple columnar, goblet cells, no villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Colon wall: simple columnar epithelium, goblet cells, intestinal glands, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa lacks villi; tubular glands of colonocytes and goblet cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-20-1","s":"A 40-year-old woman undergoes an investigative colonoscopy. During the exam, you notice inflammatory changes involving the mucosa of the entire colon with sparing of the rectum. Which of the following epithelial cell types would be seen in a biopsy taken from the adjacent uninvolved intestinal mucosa?","o":["A. Simple cuboidal epithelium","B. Simple squamous epithelium","C. Simple columnar epithelium","D. Stratified cuboidal epithelium","E. Stratified squamous epithelium"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Simple columnar epithelium.</b> The biopsy comes from uninvolved mucosa, so it shows the normal lining of the intestine. That lining is <b>one layer of tall columnar cells</b>: absorptive cells with goblet cells among them. This is true of the colon, of the rectum and of the small intestine. \"Simple\" means a single layer. \"Columnar\" means the cells are taller than they are wide.</p><p><b>Why a single layer.</b> A single layer of cells suits absorption and secretion, because a substance has to cross only one cell. Linings built for friction have many layers.</p><p><b>The colon's job.</b> Nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine. The colon absorbs water and electrolytes and forms the stool. Its absorptive cells actively pump sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) out of the lumen. Water follows passively by osmosis.</p><p><b>The colon's mucosa.</b></p><ul><li><b>Surface:</b> smooth and flat. No villi and no plicae.</li><li><b>Crypts of Lieberkühn:</b> straight, closely packed tubular glands that reach down to the muscularis mucosae. Cut across, they look like rows of round rings.</li><li><b>Colonocytes:</b> the columnar absorptive cells. They have short microvilli.</li><li><b>Goblet cells:</b> very many, more than anywhere else in the tube. Their mucus lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining.</li><li><b>Others:</b> a few enteroendocrine (hormone) cells, and stem cells at the base of the crypts.</li><li><b>Lamina propria:</b> rich in lymphocytes and lymphoid nodules.</li></ul><p><b>The lining of each part of the tube.</b></p><ul><li><b>Esophagus:</b> nonkeratinized stratified squamous, which is many layers of cells with flat ones on top.</li><li><b>Stomach:</b> simple columnar, made of surface mucous cells, with no goblet cells.</li><li><b>Small intestine:</b> simple columnar with goblet cells, on villi.</li><li><b>Colon, appendix and rectum:</b> simple columnar with no villi. The colon has the most goblet cells.</li><li><b>Anal canal:</b> the lining changes from simple columnar to nonkeratinized stratified squamous, and lower down to keratinized stratified squamous (skin).</li></ul><p><b>Where the other simple and cuboidal epithelia are found.</b> Simple squamous epithelium, one layer of flat cells, lines blood vessels and covers the serosa. Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and kidney tubules. Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines the ducts of sweat glands and salivary glands.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 28 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":20,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":15,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"},{"id":"F3950-83-1","s":"A 60-year-old male undergoes a colonoscopy as part of a routine screening. The procedure reveals multiple polyps in the colon. The physician explains that the mucosa of the large intestine is distinct from that of the small intestine. Which of the following statements is true regarding the mucosal structure of the large intestine?","o":["A. The mucosa contains villi for increased surface area.","B. The mucosa has major folds throughout its length.","C. The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.","D. The mucosa is primarily responsible for nutrient absorption."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.</b> Colonic mucosa is <b>simple columnar epithelium</b> of absorptive cells (colonocytes) and many goblet cells, forming straight crypts.<br>A. Villi: absent in the large intestine.<br>B. Major folds throughout: no; the colon has no plicae, and only the rectum has real folds.<br>D. Nutrient absorption: small intestine; the colon absorbs water and salts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":83,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3961-20-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 135"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Micrograph: pale mucus-filled cells = goblet cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-29.jpg","cap":"Colon gland at high power: pale, bubbly goblet cells (arrowhead)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands lined by colonocytes and goblet cells, stained for mucus","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-37-1","s":"What are the cells in the squares?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Goblet cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Goblet cells.</b> The boxed glands are lined mostly by <b>pale, rounded, mucus-filled cells</b> with the nucleus pushed to the base. In the colon, goblet cells are <b>very numerous</b>; their mucus lubricates stool.<br>Common mix-up: the thin pink columnar cells between them are the absorptive cells (colonocytes).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-37.jpg","in":"Same colon micrograph: the two boxes enclose glands whose epithelium is dominated by pale, mucus-filled cells.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":37,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","openings of the glands on the flat surface (no villi)"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["boxes","goblet cells filling the glands (pale, mucus-filled)"],["Muc","mucosa (bracket)"],["SubM","submucosa (bracket)"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 136"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic gland stem cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon tubular glands are lined by colonocytes, goblet cells and stem cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Regenerative stem cell layer at the crypt base renews the epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-39-1","s":"What are these cells?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Stem Cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Stem (regenerative) cells.</b> In these colon glands, the arrowhead marks a pale, bubbly goblet cell. The arrows mark cells with <b>narrow, dark, crowded nuclei and no mucus droplet</b> in the lower gland wall, the stem-cell zone. They divide to replace the gland's absorptive and goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the clear, bubbly cells are goblet cells; the stem cells sit between and below them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-39.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of colonic glands: pale goblet cells (arrowhead) and four long arrows pointing to small, dark, narrow nuclei among the columnar cells along the gland walls/base.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 18 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":39,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","stem (regenerative) cells: narrow dark nuclei, no mucus"],["arrowhead","goblet cell (pale mucus droplets)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 137"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Micrograph: crypts, goblet cells, no villi → colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-28.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: flat surface, straight glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 28","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the flat surface"],["box (upper right)","surface epithelium and gland tops"],["Gl","straight intestinal glands (crypts), full of goblet cells"],["LP","lamina propria"],["box (lower left)","gland bases"],["Muc","mucosa"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-27.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, lymphoid tissue below","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-76-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The mucosa has a <b>flat surface with no villi</b> and is filled with <b>straight tubular crypts packed with goblet cells</b>.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits over glands of pink parietal and purple chief cells; no goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi and Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-76.jpg","in":"Flat mucosa with straight tubular crypts full of goblet cells, no villi.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":76,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":78,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":80,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 138"},{"id":"F0749-35-1","s":"What part of the intestines is this?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Large intestine; Colon","a":null,"why":"<b>Large intestine (colon).</b> The surface is <b>flat, with no villi</b>; the small arrows mark gland openings. The mucosa is filled with <b>straight tubular glands (crypts) packed with goblet cells</b>, above a thin muscularis mucosae (MM) and the submucosa.<br>Common mix-up: small-intestine mucosa also has crypts, but villi project above them; here there are none.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-35.jpg","in":"H&E of mucosa with a flat surface (arrows at gland openings), no villi, straight tubular glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, lamina propria (LP), muscularis mucosae (MM) and submucosa (SubM); two glands boxed.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":35,"f":"F0749"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":189,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","openings of the glands on the flat surface (no villi)"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["boxes","goblet cells filling the glands (pale, mucus-filled)"],["Muc","mucosa (bracket)"],["SubM","submucosa (bracket)"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"]],"like":"G118-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 139"},{"id":"F0052-175-1","s":"Q24. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The mucosa is crowded with <b>round crypt profiles</b> (straight tubular glands cut across), with a <b>flat surface and no villi</b>. That is colonic mucosa.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits over glands of pink parietal and purple chief cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi and Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: villi and Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-175.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: mucosa crowded with crypts cut transversely and longitudinally (round gland profiles), flat surface, no villi.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 140"},{"id":"F0052-177-1","s":"Q25. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> Broad mucosal folds are lined by <b>straight, parallel tubular glands (crypts)</b> that open on a <b>flat surface with no villi</b>.<br>A. Stomach: rugae also fold its mucosa, but its glands are pink parietal and purple chief cells under gastric pits.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-177.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: broad mucosal folds lined by straight glands with flat luminal surface (no villi), lamina propria/submucosa cores.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 28 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 141"},{"id":"G018-61-1","s":"Q14. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The surface is flat with no villi, and the mucosa is packed with straight tubular crypts full of pale goblet cells. That pattern is the large intestine, whose main jobs are absorbing water and making mucus.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits leading into glands with parietal and chief cells, few goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-61.jpg","in":"Mucosa of straight tubular glands crowded with pale goblet cells; flat surface; muscularis mucosae and submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":61,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 142"},{"id":"G118-3-1","s":"Which of the following features is characteristic of the organ presented?","o":["A. Rugae","B. Colonocytes","C. Vili","D. Peyer's Patches"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Colonocytes","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Colonocytes.</b> Nothing is marked, so name the organ first. The surface at the top is flat, with only shallow dips where the crypts open, and it has <b>no villi</b>. Under it, straight tube-shaped crypts stand side by side and run down to the muscularis mucosae at the bottom. The crypts are packed with pale, round, bubble-like <b>goblet cells</b>. A flat surface, straight crypts and very many goblet cells mean <b>colon</b>. The colon's own absorptive cells are called <b>colonocytes</b>.</p><p><b>Colonocytes.</b> They are simple columnar cells that line the surface and the crypts, between the goblet cells. They absorb water and electrolytes. They pump sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) out of the lumen actively, and water follows passively by osmosis. They have short microvilli. Wide spaces between neighboring cells are a sign of this water transport.</p><p><b>The other cells in this picture.</b></p><ul><li><b>Goblet cells:</b> pale, round, mucus-filled cups with the nucleus squeezed to the base. The colon has more of them than any other part of the tube. Their mucus lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining.</li><li><b>Stem cells:</b> small, dark cells at the bottom of the crypts, sometimes caught dividing. They renew the lining.</li><li><b>Enteroendocrine cells:</b> a few hormone cells, hard to see with this stain.</li><li><b>Lamina propria:</b> the tissue between the crypts, dotted with small dark nuclei. Many are lymphocytes. The colon is rich in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT), the immune tissue of the gut lining.</li></ul><p><b>Features that name other organs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Rugae:</b> the large folds of the stomach. The stomach surface has gastric pits and no goblet cells.</li><li><b>Villi:</b> the finger-like projections of the small intestine.</li><li><b>Peyer patches:</b> large clusters of lymphoid nodules in the ileum, which also has villi.</li></ul><p><b>Two gradients that explain this picture.</b> Along the intestine the absorbing surface falls. Villi are most numerous in the duodenum, fewer in the jejunum and ileum, and absent in the colon. Goblet cells rise in the same direction and are most numerous in the colon, because the contents get drier and need more lubrication.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-3.jpg","in":"Colon mucosa, H&E: flat surface with no villi; straight tubular crypts of Lieberkühn packed with pale goblet cells; muscularis mucosae at the bottom. Nothing is marked: the organ has to be recognized first.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":3,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Teniae coli: colon's outer longitudinal muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine tunics: outer longitudinal muscularis forms the tenia coli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: outer muscularis in three longitudinal ribbons, the taenia coli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-118-1","s":"In which layer of the GI tract wall are teniae coli found?","o":["A. Epithelial layer","B. Lamina propria","C. Adventitia","D. Muscularis mucosa","E. Muscularis externa"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Muscularis externa.</b> In the colon, the <b>outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa</b> is gathered into <b>three bands, the teniae coli</b>.<br>A. Epithelium: simple columnar lining with goblet cells.<br>B. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue rich in lymphoid tissue.<br>C. Adventitia: outer connective tissue where there is no serosa.<br>D. Muscularis mucosa: a thin muscle sheet at the base of the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":118,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-33-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 143"},{"id":"F3952-80-1","s":"A 70-year-old man presents with abdominal distension and changes in bowel habits. During your physical examination, you discuss the anatomy of the large intestine and its unique features, including the teniae coli, which are visible along its outer surface. You explain that these structures are formed by a specific layer of the intestinal tract wall, which plays a critical role in the motility and overall function of the large intestine. The teniae coli of the large intestine represent an organ-specific specialization of which layer of the intestinal tract wall?","o":["A. Epithelium","B. Lamina propria","C. Muscularis mucosa","D. Muscularis externa","E. Serosa"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis externa.</b> <b>Teniae coli</b> are the colon's <b>outer longitudinal smooth muscle layer</b>, gathered into three ribbons.<br>A. Epithelium: simple columnar cells and goblet cells.<br>B. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue with lymphoid tissue.<br>C. Muscularis mucosa: thin muscle under the crypts.<br>E. Serosa: the outer covering of connective tissue and mesothelium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":80,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-33-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 144"},{"id":"F3961-17-1","s":"The teniae coli is an organ-specific specialization that forms three longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this organization?","o":["A. Esophagus, muscularis externa","B. Stomach, muscularis mucosa","C. Ileum, muscularis mucosa","D. Duodenum, muscoraris mucosa","E. Colon, muscularis externa"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, muscularis externa","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Colon, muscularis externa.</b> The <b>teniae coli</b> are the colon's outer longitudinal muscle gathered into three bands, so they belong to the <b>muscularis externa</b>.<br>A. Esophagus: full outer longitudinal layer, no teniae.<br>B. Stomach: adds an inner oblique layer, not teniae; and the muscularis mucosa is the wrong layer.<br>C. Ileum: full longitudinal layer; muscularis mucosa is the wrong layer.<br>D. Duodenum: same; no teniae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":17,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":13,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":2,"like":"G017-33-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 145"},{"id":"G017-33-1","s":"In the gastrointestinal tract, the muscularis propria consists of an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle with an intervening neural plexus (Auerbach). Exceptions to this general plan are seen in the stomach where an inner oblique layer of smooth muscle is added and in a second location where the outer layer is condensed into three longitudinal ribbons (taenia) of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this modified organization and what plane of section would be required to demonstrate it?","o":["A. Esophagus, longitudinal section","B. Ileum, transverse section","C. Colon, longitudinal section","D. Colon, transverse section","E. Anal canal, external sphincter, transverse section"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, transverse section","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Colon, transverse section.</b> The organ whose outer longitudinal muscle is gathered into three ribbons is the <b>colon</b>. The ribbons are the <b>teniae coli</b>, and they run along the length of the colon. Because they run lengthwise, a <b>transverse section</b> (a cut across the tube) passes through all three at once. They appear as three separate thick bundles of muscle, spaced around the outside of the wall.</p><p><b>The general plan of the muscle coat.</b> The muscularis propria is another name for the muscularis externa, the thick muscle coat of the gut wall. It has two layers of smooth muscle. The fibers of the inner layer are circular: they wrap around the tube. The fibers of the outer layer are longitudinal: they run along the tube. Between the two layers lies the Auerbach plexus, also called the myenteric plexus, a network of nerve cells that controls the muscle. The stomach adds a third, inner oblique layer. In the esophagus the coat is skeletal muscle at the top, mixed in the middle and smooth muscle at the bottom.</p><p><b>Teniae coli.</b></p><ul><li>The outer longitudinal layer is gathered into three narrow ribbons. Between the ribbons the longitudinal layer is very thin.</li><li>The inner circular layer stays complete all the way around.</li><li>The teniae are shorter than the colon itself. They bunch the wall into a row of pouches called haustra.</li><li>The appendix and the rectum have no teniae. There the longitudinal layer is a complete coat again.</li></ul><p><b>What the cross-section looks like.</b> From the lumen outward: mucosa with straight crypts, submucosa, a complete ring of circular muscle, and then, at three points, a thick bundle of longitudinal muscle cut across.</p><p><b>The outer coat.</b> Where the colon hangs free (transverse and sigmoid colon) it is covered by serosa. Where it is fixed to the back wall of the abdomen (ascending and descending colon) it has adventitia.</p><p><b>Diverticula.</b> Diverticula are small pouches of mucosa and submucosa pushed out through weak spots in the muscularis externa. The weak spots are where blood vessels pass through the wall, between the teniae. They are most common in the sigmoid colon of older adults, where the pressure inside is high, and with a low-fiber diet. Diverticulosis, which means having the pouches, can bleed: sudden, painless, bright red blood from the rectum. Diverticulitis, which means inflamed pouches, causes pain in the left lower abdomen and fever.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Eroschenko drawing of a transverse section of colon wall with intestinal glands, muscularis externa and a boxed label 'Taeniae coli' pointing at the thickened outer longitudinal band.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":33,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":25,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Large intestine: crypts and teniae, no villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-30.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: sodium active, water passive; crypts, no villi; goblet cells, MALT, teniae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine wall: intestinal glands, lymphatic nodule, tenia coli in muscularis","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-191-1","s":"What is true of the large intestine?","o":["A. Reabsorption of water actively","B. Passively transport Na+","C. No microvilli, villi, or crypts","D. Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkuhn","E. No MALT, many goblet cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkuhn","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The large intestine keeps its <b>crypts</b> (but lacks villi), and its outer longitudinal muscle forms three <b>teniae coli</b>.<br>A. Water is absorbed <b>passively</b>, not actively.<br>B. Sodium is transported <b>actively</b>, not passively.<br>C. It has crypts; only the villi are missing.<br>E. MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) is <b>abundant</b> in the lamina propria, not absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":191,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 146"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Diverticula bulge between teniae coli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Teniae coli: three longitudinal muscle ribbons; diverticula bulge out between them","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/F3955-100.jpg","cap":"Opened colon: multiple diverticulum openings (arrows) in the mucosa","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 100","lg":[["arrows","diverticulum openings in the mucosa"],["arrowhead","diverticulum opening with dark material sticking out of it"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-99-1","s":"A 62-year-old man presents after fainting at home approximately one hour ago. He says that for the past day he has had increasing left-sided abdominal pain, and he also has noted bright red blood in his stool. He denies any history of vomiting or diarrhea. Physical examination finds a low-grade fever and gross blood on rectal examination. Further work-up finds abnormalities in his colon that are similar in appearance to the abnormalities seen in the gross picture of his colon seen in the next slide. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his GI bleeding?","o":["A. Angiodysplasia","B. Appendicitis","C. Diverticulosis","D. Hemorrhoids","E. Intussusception"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Diverticulosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Diverticulosis.</b> An older man with <b>left-sided pain</b> and <b>bright red rectal bleeding</b> fits sigmoid diverticula. <b>Diverticula</b> are outpouchings of mucosa and submucosa that push out <b>between the teniae coli</b>, where the outer muscle layer is missing.<br>A. Angiodysplasia: dilated vessels, usually right colon, painless.<br>B. Appendicitis: right lower pain, no rectal bleeding.<br>D. Hemorrhoids: dilated anal veins; small, painless bleeds.<br>E. Intussusception: telescoping bowel, mostly in young children.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened colon with multiple small dark pouch openings in the mucosa between folds (arrows) = diverticula.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":99,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 147"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphoid follicles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-32.jpg","cap":"Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphatic nodules in mucosa and submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Appendix: blind evagination from cecum and a significant part of MALT","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42","lg":[["V","villi"],["N","lymphoid nodules of the Peyer patch"],["(a) arrow","germinal center of a nodule"],["B","brush border of enterocytes"],["E","enterocytes (darker cytoplasm)"],["L","lymphocytes in the M cell's pocket"],["D","dendritic (antigen-presenting) cells"],["BM","basement membrane (porous under M cells)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-41-1","s":"What is this?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix.</b> A small tube with a <b>narrow, star-shaped lumen</b>, ringed by <b>large lymphoid nodules</b> in the mucosa and submucosa, with few glands and a complete muscularis externa (no teniae coli). It is a blind pouch off the cecum and a big part of MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).<br>Common mix-up: the ileum also has lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches), but it has villi and a wide lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-41.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E cross-section of a small tube with a narrow star-shaped lumen, a ring of large dark lymphatic nodules in mucosa/submucosa, and muscularis externa (labels: lymphatic nodules, mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa).","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":41,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 148"},{"id":"F0755-233-1","s":"What organ is depicted and what is it its distinguishing feature?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix – MALT","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix; distinguishing feature: abundant MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).</b> Large <b>lymphoid follicles with germinal centers</b> (arrow) crowd the lamina propria and submucosa, while the mucosa has <b>few glands</b>. It also has a tiny lumen and no teniae coli.<br>Common mix-up: Peyer's patches of the ileum look similar, but the ileum has villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-233.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: large lymphoid follicles with germinal centres (arrow) filling the lamina propria/submucosa beneath a mucosa with few intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":233,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","lymphoid nodule with pale germinal center (MALT)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 149"},{"id":"F0754-193-1","s":"What is this? Where is it found?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix/ MALT – blind evagination off the cecum","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix, a blind pouch off the cecum.</b> Its lamina propria and submucosa are filled with <b>large lymphoid follicles with pale germinal centers</b> (arrow), and the mucosa has <b>few, short glands</b>. That makes the appendix a significant part of MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).<br>Common mix-up: the colon also has MALT, but only small amounts between many crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-193.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: large lymphoid follicles with germinal centres (arrow) filling the lamina propria/submucosa beneath a mucosa with few, short intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","lymphoid nodule with pale germinal center (MALT)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 150"},{"id":"F0749-42-1","s":"Appendix. Whats the arrow pointing to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"MALT","a":null,"why":"<b>MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue): a lymphoid follicle.</b> The arrow marks a large, round cluster of lymphocytes with a <b>pale germinal center</b>, filling the lamina propria and submucosa below the glands. These follicles make the appendix a major part of MALT.<br>Common mix-up: the pale center is not a gland; it is the germinal center of the follicle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-42.jpg","in":"Appendix cross-section plus a higher-power H&E: the arrow points to a large pale-centred lymphoid follicle (germinal centre) beneath intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":42,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrow","lymphoid nodule with pale germinal center (MALT)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 151"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Acute appendicitis: right lower quadrant pain","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-113.jpg","cap":"Acute appendicitis: swollen red appendix and inflamed wall at medium power","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 113"},{"src":"sl/G016-32.jpg","cap":"Normal appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphatic nodules in mucosa and submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-112-1","s":"You are called to the Surgical Pathology cutting/grossing room to examine a piece of tissue. The surgical procedure was done for right lower quadrant pain, which persisted and worsened over a period of 4 days, in a 17-year-old male. The gross and microscopic features/figures are both shown for this excised tissue on the next slide. The most likely diagnosis is which of the following:","o":["A. Crohn's disease","B. Ulcerative colitis","C. Adenomatous polyp","D. Acute appendicitis","E. Colonic adenocarcinoma"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute appendicitis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Acute appendicitis.</b> A teenager with <b>right lower quadrant pain worsening over days</b> has acute appendicitis. The removed appendix is swollen and red, with inflammation through its wall. The appendix is a blind pouch off the cecum, rich in lymphoid tissue.<br>A. Crohn's disease: chronic and patchy, often the ileum; granulomas.<br>B. Ulcerative colitis: starts in the rectum; bloody diarrhea.<br>C. Adenomatous polyp: benign colon growth, usually silent.<br>E. Colonic adenocarcinoma: older adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Gross: swollen, red, congested appendix; micro: wall diffusely infiltrated by inflammatory cells.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":112,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 152"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Appendix carcinoid: enteroendocrine cell tumor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Gastric cell types: enteroendocrine cells need special stains; EC cells secrete serotonin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/F3955-119.jpg","cap":"Appendix carcinoid: nests of uniform salt-and-pepper cells; synaptophysin-positive stain","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-118-1","s":"A 42-year-old female presents to the outpatient clinic with complaints of recurrent right lower abdominal pain that has been experienced over a two-year duration. Physical examination revealed a minimal degree of rebound tenderness. It was decided to take the patient to surgery for removal of her appendix. The patient underwent successful appendectomy and recovered four days later. Subsequently, the tip of the appendix was carefully examined with special attention paid to the tip of the appendix. See the next slide for a photomicrograph of the appendiceal tip. The tan-yellow appendiceal mass within the appendix and localized to the tip, measuring 1 cm in length and 0.5 cm in diameter, was diagnosed by histopathological examination. Follow-up examination one year after surgery revealed that the patient was well with no discomfort. [Slide 119: This lesion was submucosal, nodular with a characteristic nested pattern. The cells were small and uniform, with round nuclei, central prominent nucleoli and speckled chromatin pattern (salt and pepper nuclei). The lesional cells were also positive for synaptophysin.] After careful examination of all the histological sections, the pathologist determined that the most correct diagnosis for this patient was which of the following?","o":["A. Acute appendicitis","B. Appendiceal lipoma","C. Appendiceal leiomyoma","D. Neuroendocrine tumor (carcinoid tumor)","E. Appendiceal adenocarcinoma"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Neuroendocrine tumor (carcinoid tumor)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor.</b> A small yellow nodule at the appendix tip made of <b>nests of uniform cells</b> with <b>salt-and-pepper chromatin</b> that stain for <b>synaptophysin</b> (a neuroendocrine marker) is a carcinoid. It arises from <b>enteroendocrine cells</b> (enterochromaffin cells make serotonin).<br>A. Acute appendicitis: neutrophils through the wall, not a nested tumor.<br>B. Lipoma: mature fat cells.<br>C. Leiomyoma: bundles of spindle-shaped smooth muscle cells.<br>E. Adenocarcinoma: malignant glands from the lining epithelium, not synaptophysin-positive nests.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Nests of small uniform cells in the submucosa (H&E) that stain brown with synaptophysin.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":118,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 153"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Rectoanal junction: columnar to stratified squamous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-36.jpg","cap":"Rectoanal junction: columnar glandular lining changes abruptly to stratified squamous","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 36","lg":[["line","rectoanal junction: columnar epithelium with glands (left), stratified squamous (right)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-46.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: columnar lining above dentate line, stratified squamous below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 46","lg":[["C","colorectal zone: simple columnar epithelium with glands (label cut from 'CRZ')"],["box","start of the anal transitional zone: simple columnar meets stratified cuboidal, anal gland below"],["S","squamous zone: stratified squamous epithelium (label cut from 'SQZ')"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-73-1","s":"A 55-year-old man presents with rectal bleeding and discomfort during bowel movements. During your examination, you discuss the anatomy of the anal canal and its role in defecation, emphasizing the importance of the epithelial lining in maintaining integrity and function. You explain that as one moves from the rectum into the anal canal, there is a significant change in the type of epithelium lining the gut, which plays a critical role in protecting against mechanical stress and pathogens. Moving from the rectum, what shift in epithelial types occurs in the lining of the anal canal?","o":["A. From simple columnar to simple squamous","B. From simple squamous to simple columnar","C. From simple squamous to stratified squamous","D. From simple columnar to stratified squamous","E. From simple cuboidal to stratified cuboidal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"From simple columnar to stratified squamous","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. From simple columnar to stratified squamous.</b> The rectum is lined like the colon: one layer of tall columnar cells with many goblet cells, dipping into crypts. At the <b>pectinate line</b> of the anal canal this lining changes to <b>stratified squamous epithelium</b>, which is many layers of cells with flat ones on top. A single layer suits absorption and mucus secretion. Many layers resist the friction of passing stool, which is the protection the question describes.</p><p><b>Two lines, three linings.</b> Going down from the rectum:</p><ol><li><b>Above the pectinate line</b> (rectum and upper anal canal): simple columnar epithelium with crypts and goblet cells.</li><li><b>At the pectinate (dentate) line:</b> simple columnar changes to nonkeratinized stratified squamous. This is the rectoanal junction. A narrow transition zone of stratified cuboidal cells lies along it.</li><li><b>Between the pectinate line and Hilton's line:</b> nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. It is moist and has no surface layer of keratin, the tough protein of skin.</li><li><b>At Hilton's white line</b> (also called the anocutaneous or intersphincteric line): nonkeratinized changes to keratinized stratified squamous.</li><li><b>Below Hilton's line:</b> keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. This is skin, with hair and glands.</li></ol><p><b>The wall of the anal canal.</b></p><ul><li><b>Submucosa:</b> anal glands, and a plexus (network) of veins. These veins can swell and become varicose. Swollen anal veins are hemorrhoids, which bleed and cause discomfort with bowel movements.</li><li><b>Internal anal sphincter:</b> the thickened inner circular layer of smooth muscle. It is involuntary and is supplied by autonomic nerves.</li><li><b>External anal sphincter:</b> skeletal muscle outside it. It is voluntary and is supplied by somatic nerves.</li></ul><p>So this short region has both autonomic and somatic nerve supply.</p><p><b>The same pattern at both ends of the tube.</b> The digestive tube is lined by stratified squamous epithelium where friction is greatest: the esophagus at the top and the lower anal canal at the bottom. Everything between, from the stomach to the rectum, is lined by simple columnar epithelium. Simple squamous epithelium, a single layer of flat cells, lines blood vessels and covers the serosa on the outside of the gut.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":73,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"},{"id":"F3950-85-1","s":"A 48-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a complaint of rectal bleeding and discomfort during bowel movements. She reports that the bleeding occurs occasionally and is associated with straining during defecation. On physical examination, the physician notes the presence of hemorrhoids and recommends a colonoscopy to evaluate for any underlying pathology. During the colonoscopy, the physician observes that the mucosal lining of the rectum shows a distinct transition as it approaches the anal canal. The physician explains to the patient that this change in epithelial lining is an important anatomical feature of the anal-rectal junction. Which of the following best describes the histological change that occurs at the anal-rectal junction?","o":["A. Transition from stratified squamous epithelium to keratinized epithelium","B. Transition from simple columnar epithelium to stratified squamous epithelium","C. Transition from stratified cuboidal epithelium to transitional epithelium","D. Transition from simple squamous epithelium to simple cuboidal epithelium","E. Transition from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Transition from simple columnar epithelium to stratified squamous epithelium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium.</b> Rectal mucosa is <b>simple columnar with many goblet cells</b>. At the anorectal junction (pectinate line) it changes abruptly to <b>stratified squamous</b>.<br>A. Non-keratinized to keratinized squamous: that is Hilton's line, lower in the anal canal.<br>C. Transitional epithelium: urinary tract.<br>D. Simple squamous to simple cuboidal: not in the gut lining.<br>E. Reversed direction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":85,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3952-73-1","kind":"repeat","n":"11 extra 154"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Pectinate line: columnar meets squamous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-36.jpg","cap":"Rectoanal junction: glandular columnar epithelium meets stratified squamous along drawn line","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 36","lg":[["line","rectoanal junction: columnar epithelium with glands (left), stratified squamous (right)"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-45.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: squamocolumnar junction at the pectinate (dentate) line","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-33-1","s":"What is this line called? What is it dividing?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pectinate line – divides the colon/rectum (simple columnar with glands, left top) from the anus (stratified squamous, right bottom) at the rectoanal junction","a":null,"why":"<b>Pectinate (dentate) line.</b> It marks the <b>rectoanal junction</b>. Upper left: <b>simple columnar epithelium with glands and goblet cells</b> (rectum). Lower right: <b>non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (anal canal). The change is abrupt.<br>Common mix-up: Hilton's (white) line is lower; there non-keratinized squamous becomes keratinized skin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-33.jpg","in":"H&E of rectoanal junction: colonic glands with goblet cells at upper left, stratified squamous epithelium at right; a diagonal line drawn across the junction.","tg":["H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":33,"f":"F0749"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":195,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["black line","rectoanal junction (at the pectinate line)"],["upper left of line","rectum: simple columnar epithelium, glands with goblet cells"],["right of line","anal canal: stratified squamous epithelium"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 155"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Hilton's line: squamous becomes keratinized","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-46.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: columnar, dentate line, non-keratinized squamous, Hilton's line, then skin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 46","lg":[["C","colorectal zone: simple columnar epithelium with glands (label cut from 'CRZ')"],["box","start of the anal transitional zone: simple columnar meets stratified cuboidal, anal gland below"],["S","squamous zone: stratified squamous epithelium (label cut from 'SQZ')"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-38.jpg","cap":"Pectinate line: columnar to non-keratinized squamous; Hilton's line: to keratinized","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-196-1","s":"What is the name of the transition from stratified squamous non-keratinized to stratified squamous keratinized?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Hilton's line","a":null,"why":"<b>Hilton's (white) line.</b> In the anal canal, <b>non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> becomes <b>keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (skin) at Hilton's line, below the pectinate line.<br>Common mix-up: the pectinate (dentate) line is the higher change, from simple columnar to non-keratinized stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":196,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 156"},{"id":"F0755-234-1","s":"At the rectoanal junction, what specific transition in epithelium do you normally see? What is the name of the line that divides the two? What does Hilton's line represent?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: columnar to non-keratinized squamous epithelium – pectinate line; B: Hilton's line = transition from non-keratinized to keratinized (stratified squamous)","a":null,"why":"<b>Simple columnar → non-keratinized stratified squamous, at the pectinate (dentate) line. Hilton's (white) line marks non-keratinized → keratinized stratified squamous.</b> So the anal canal has two lines: first the glandular rectal lining turns squamous, then the squamous lining becomes skin.<br>Common mix-up: keratin does not start at the pectinate line; it starts only below Hilton's line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":234,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 157"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Internal smooth vs external skeletal anal sphincter","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-47.jpg","cap":"Internal sphincter (A) smooth muscle; external sphincter (B) striated skeletal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/F3955-86.jpg","cap":"Anal canal section: internal sphincter inside, external skeletal sphincter outside","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 86","lg":[["CRZ","colorectal zone (simple columnar epithelium, crypts)"],["gray box","start of the anal transition zone, where rectal columnar epithelium ends"],["ATZ","anal transition zone (stratified columnar/cuboidal epithelium)"],["pink dotted box","pectinate line"],["SQZ","squamous zone (stratified squamous epithelium)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-84-1","s":"A biopsy from the anus is obtained. Name the structures labeled as #1 and #2","o":["A. Internal anal sphincter; External anal sphincter","B. External anal sphincter; Internal anal sphincter"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal anal sphincter; External anal sphincter","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Internal anal sphincter; external anal sphincter.</b> #1 is <b>smooth muscle</b> (spindle cells, central nuclei) next to the lining: the <b>internal</b> sphincter, a thickening of the inner circular layer. #2 is <b>skeletal muscle</b> (fibers cut across, nuclei at the edge) further out: the voluntary <b>external</b> sphincter.<br>B. Reversed: the external sphincter is skeletal muscle and lies outside the internal one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-84.jpg","in":"#1 = band of smooth muscle (spindle cells, central nuclei) next to the epithelium; #2 = skeletal muscle fibers cut in cross-section further out.","tg":["H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":84,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["1","internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle, spindle-shaped cells)"],["2","external anal sphincter (skeletal muscle cut across, nuclei at the edge)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 158"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Goblet cells increase toward the colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Goblet cell frequency rises: duodenum, then jejunum, then ileum","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands: colonocytes and many goblet cells, few enteroendocrine cells, stem cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44","lg":[["arrows","gland openings on the surface"],["boxes","top: surface and gland tops, many goblet cells; bottom: gland bases at the muscularis mucosae"],["Gl","intestinal glands (crypts)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["bracket (right edge)","mucosa (label cut off)"],["SubM","submucosa"],["MM","muscularis mucosae"],["(d) L","folded cell-membrane leaflets in widened spaces between cells (water transport)"],["(c) G","goblet cells"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-18-1","s":"Which portion of the intestine contains the greatest number of goblet cells?","o":["A. Pylorus","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ileum.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase along the gut</b>: fewest in the duodenum, more in the jejunum, most in the ileum, and even more in the colon. Among these choices, the ileum has the most.<br>A. Pylorus: stomach; surface mucous cells, no goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: fewest goblet cells of the small intestine.<br>C. Jejunum: more than the duodenum, fewer than the ileum.<br>E. Appendix: few glands; its mucosa is crowded by lymphoid tissue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":18,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","n":"11 extra 159"},{"id":"F1221-179-1","s":"7. Goblet cells are most numerous in the walls of the","o":["A. villi","B. gastric glands","C. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the ileum","D. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the duodenum","E. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the colon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Crypts of Lieberkühn in the colon.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase along the gut</b>: few in the duodenum, more in the jejunum and ileum, and <b>most in the colon</b>, where their mucus lubricates stool.<br>A. Villi: mostly absorptive enterocytes with scattered goblet cells.<br>B. Gastric glands: no goblet cells; stomach mucus comes from surface and neck mucous cells.<br>C. Ileal crypts: many, but fewer than the colon.<br>D. Duodenal crypts: the fewest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 160"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Intestinal segments: distinguishing features","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36","lg":[["dashed line","border between villi (above) and crypts (below)"],["arrow","Brunner gland duct emptying into the base of a crypt"],["P","plica circularis (fold with a submucosa core)"],["SM","submucosa"],["M","muscularis externa"],["large box","one Peyer patch lymphoid nodule with the mucosa over it"],["small box","edge of a lymphoid nodule against the submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G016-9.jpg","cap":"Distinguishing features: Brunner glands, jejunal plicae, ileal Peyer's patches, colon lacks villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-201-1","s":"On your first day of your internal medicine clinical rotation, the crabby attending insists on quizzing all of the medical students on unique histological features of the intestinal tract in order to pass time while waiting for his assistant to bring him coffee. Identify and match the characteristic features with their correct location before Dr. Grumpy’s coffee arrives: Brunner's glands; Lots of goblet cells, no villi; Peyer's patches and M cells; Plicae circularis, absence of glands","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Colon"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Brunner's glands → Duodenum; Lots of goblet cells, no villi → Colon; Peyer's patches and M cells → Ileum; Plicae circularis, absence of glands → Jejunum","a":null,"why":"<b>Brunner glands → duodenum; many goblet cells, no villi → colon; Peyer's patches and M (microfold) cells → ileum; plicae circulares with no glands → jejunum.</b> Each segment has one tag: duodenum = <b>submucosal glands</b>; jejunum = <b>tallest plicae, thin bare submucosa</b>; ileum = <b>lymphoid nodules</b>; colon = <b>crypts full of goblet cells, no villi</b>.<br>Common mix-up: plicae exist in all three small-intestine segments, but only the jejunum has them as its main tag.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":201,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":183,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":43,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 161"},{"id":"F0755-231-1","s":"Distinguishing features Quiz: (1) In comparison to the duodenum doe the large intestine have more or less goblet cells present? (2) What type of gland is specific to the Duodenum and what is the function? (3) Do the frequency/amount of villi/microvilli increase or decrease as you pass through the small intestine (4) What is the distinguishing feature of the Jejunum? (5) What is the distinguishing feature of the Ileum?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) More; (2) Brunner's glands – alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme; (3) Decrease; (4) Prominent plicae circulares; (5) Peyer's patches","a":null,"why":"<b>(1) More; (2) Brunner glands, alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme; (3) decrease; (4) prominent plicae circulares; (5) Peyer's patches.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase</b> along the gut and are most numerous in the colon. Villi and microvilli do the opposite: most in the duodenum, fewest in the ileum. Brunner glands sit in the duodenal <b>submucosa</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the two gradients run opposite ways; absorptive surface falls along the gut while goblet cells rise.","ex":"","fl":["Part (1): correct answer More. The large intestine has MORE goblet cells than the duodenum: goblet cells increase distally (duodenum < jejunum < ileum, G016:13) and the colon has 'numerous goblet cells' (G016:30; F0749 slide 32). The key 'Less' is wrong.","The source keyed part 1 as 'Less', but the large intestine has more goblet cells than the duodenum, because goblet cells increase toward the colon."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":231,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 162"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"GI epithelial cells and their products","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Stomach cell types: surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, enteroendocrine","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Small intestine cell types: enterocyte, goblet, enteroendocrine, stem, Paneth","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Peyer patch microfold (M) cells pass antigens to lymphocytes","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42","lg":[["V","villi"],["N","lymphoid nodules of the Peyer patch"],["(a) arrow","germinal center of a nodule"],["B","brush border of enterocytes"],["E","enterocytes (darker cytoplasm)"],["L","lymphocytes in the M cell's pocket"],["D","dendritic (antigen-presenting) cells"],["BM","basement membrane (porous under M cells)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-62-1","s":"Match the cell to its function","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Paneth cell","C. Goblet cell","D. Chief cell","E. Parietal cell","F. Microfold Cell","G. Stem cell","H. Enterocyte","I. a. Production of lysozyme & defensins","J. b. Regenerates other epithelial cells","K. c. Production of Mucus","L. d. Absorbs nutrients","M. e. Production of peptide hormones","N. f. Production of HCl and intrinsic factor","O. g. Production of pepsinogen and lipase precursor","P. h. Transports antigens from lumen"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1-e, 2-a, 3-c, 4-g, 5-f, 6-h, 7-b, 8-d","a":null,"why":"<b>Enteroendocrine - peptide hormones; Paneth - lysozyme and defensins; goblet - mucus; chief - pepsinogen and lipase precursor; parietal - hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor; microfold (M) cell - carries antigens from the lumen to immune cells; stem cell - regenerates the epithelium; enterocyte - absorbs nutrients.</b><br>Common mix-up: chief cells (basophilic, purple) make enzymes; parietal cells (acidophilic, pink) make acid and intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":62,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":131,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 163"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Squamocolumnar junctions: cardia and pectinate line","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-63.jpg","cap":"Esophagogastric junction: stratified squamous to simple columnar with gastric pits","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 63","lg":[["E","esophagus (stratified squamous epithelium)"],["C","cardia of the stomach (simple columnar epithelium)"],["GP","gastric pits"],["CG","cardiac glands"],["ECG","esophageal cardiac glands (mucous)"],["arrow (lower, unlabeled)","muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle strands)"],["SM","submucosa"]]},{"src":"sl/G018-45.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: squamocolumnar junction at the pectinate (dentate) line","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-57-1","s":"An unknown gastrointestinal slide shows a zone of transitional tissue from simple columnar epithelium to non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with abundant mucous-producing cells. Which of the following answer choices describes this zone(s)?","o":["A. Vermillion Zone","B. Esophageal sphincter","C. Pyloric sphincter","D. Pectinate Line","E. Hilton's Line"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal sphincter and Pectinate Line (two answers)","a":"B,D","why":"<b>B and D. Esophageal sphincter region and pectinate line.</b> Two places join <b>simple columnar to nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b>: the esophagogastric junction at the lower esophageal sphincter, and the pectinate (dentate) line of the anal canal, next to the goblet-cell-rich rectum.<br>A. Vermilion zone: skin to oral mucosa, all stratified squamous.<br>C. Pyloric sphincter: simple columnar on both sides.<br>E. Hilton's line: nonkeratinized to keratinized stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":57,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":141,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"11 extra 164"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Serous cells: basophilic base, watery protein fluid","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-8.jpg","cap":"Serous acini: pyramidal cells, round nuclei, dark basal cytoplasm, apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 8","lg":[["a","parotid, plastic section, toluidine blue-based stain (granules sharp)"],["A","serous acini"],["ID","intercalated duct"],["b","parotid, hematoxylin and eosin"],["CT","connective-tissue septum"],["Microvilli, Intercellular canaliculi (labels along the top)","left over from a serous-cell drawing hidden behind the photos; they point to nothing here"]]},{"src":"sl/F0722-150.jpg","cap":"Serous versus mucous cells side by side: shape, nuclei, staining, secretion","where":"2024 TA review, slide 150","lg":[["(left) a","parotid, thin plastic section"],["(left) b","parotid, H&E"],["A","serous acini"],["(left) ID","intercalated duct"],["CT","connective tissue septum"],["M","mucous cells in tubules (sublingual gland)"],["SM","skeletal muscle of the tongue"],["(right) ID","small intralobular ducts (arrows)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-148-1","s":"What is true about the cells below?","o":["A. They are columnar cells","B. They secrete viscous mucus","C. Nuclei are present basally","D. The basal domain is basophilic with abundant RER","E. They stain poorly"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The basal domain is basophilic with abundant RER","a":"D","why":"D. These are <b>serous cells</b>: pyramidal, round nuclei, and a <b>basal domain that stains dark blue (basophilic)</b> because it is packed with <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER)</b> for making protein.<br>A. Columnar shape is a mucous cell trait; serous cells are pyramidal.<br>B. Viscous mucus comes from mucous cells; serous cells make watery protein fluid.<br>C. Nuclei pressed to the base is the mucous look; the lecture gives serous nuclei as round and central.<br>E. Poor (pale) staining is the mucous look; serous cells stain darkly.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-148.jpg","in":"Two panels of serous acini (A) of pyramidal granular cells with round nuclei.","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":148,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["a","parotid, thin plastic section"],["b","parotid, H&E"],["A","serous acini"],["ID","intercalated duct"],["CT","connective tissue septum"]],"like":"F0743-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 1"},{"id":"F4081-30-1","s":"A histologic section shows pyramidal cells with round, central nuclei. Their basal domains are basophilic due to abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, and their apical cytoplasmic domains contain many granules. They are arranged in spherical acini. Based on these structural features, which functional outcome is most likely?","o":["A. Secretion of watery proteinaceous fluid","B. Formation of mucous droplets","C. Loss of microvilli","D. Flattening of round, central nuclei","E. Breakdown of pyramidal cell shape"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of watery proteinaceous fluid","a":"A","why":"A. Pyramidal cells, round central nuclei, a <b>dark-blue (basophilic) base full of rough endoplasmic reticulum</b>, apical granules and round acini mean <b>serous cells</b>. That machinery makes protein, so they secrete a <b>watery, protein-rich fluid</b>.<br>B. Mucous droplets come from pale columnar mucous cells.<br>C. Serous cells keep short apical microvilli; nothing here means losing them.<br>D. Flattened basal nuclei are a mucous cell trait.<br>E. The pyramidal shape is the normal, lasting form; it points secretion at the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":30,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0743-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 2"},{"id":"F0743-2-1","s":"Under the microscope, you analyze a biopsy slide stained by H&E. You observe a group of single-layered epithelial cells organized around a lumen. The cells are pyramidal in shape and present a certain polarity, with basophilic features and a well-rounded nucleus at the base. Concomitantly, a concentration of eosinophilic vacuoles in seen in their apical portion. This description best corresponds to:","o":["A. Endocrine holocrine glands","B. Exocrine mucous glands","C. Tensed urothelium","D. Exocrine serous glands","E. Exocrine mixed glands"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine serous glands","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Exocrine serous glands.</b> Every clue in the description fits a <b>serous acinus</b>. The cells are pyramidal and sit in one layer around a lumen. The base is basophilic (purple) and holds a round nucleus. The top is filled with eosinophilic (pink) granules, which are stored protein. A lumen means the product leaves through a duct, and that is the meaning of <b>exocrine</b>.</p><p><b>What the two colors mean.</b> Hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) is the routine stain. Hematoxylin is a blue-purple dye that sticks to acidic material such as DNA and RNA. Whatever takes it up is called <b>basophilic</b>. Eosin is a pink dye that sticks to protein. Whatever takes it up is called <b>eosinophilic</b>.</p><p><b>Why a serous cell is purple below and pink on top.</b> A serous cell is a protein factory, and its parts lie in the order of the work.</p><ol><li><b>Base:</b> the round nucleus and a large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum, the membrane sheets studded with ribosomes where the protein is made. Ribosomes are rich in RNA, so the base stains purple.</li><li><b>Top (apex):</b> small, dense <b>zymogen granules</b>, packets of finished protein waiting to be released. Protein takes up eosin, so the top stains pink.</li><li><b>Lumen:</b> the granules empty here, and the secretion flows on into the ducts.</li></ol><p>This one-way layout is the polarity mentioned in the description.</p><p><b>What serous cells make.</b> A thin, watery fluid rich in protein, mostly enzymes. In the salivary glands the main one is amylase, which begins the digestion of starch. Under the electron microscope the granules are small and dark (electron-dense).</p><p><b>The shape of a serous unit.</b> The cells form a round ball called an acinus, with a very small lumen. Tiny channels called intercellular canaliculi run between the cells and also lead to the lumen.</p><p><b>Words used to classify glands.</b></p><ul><li><b>Exocrine:</b> the product leaves through a duct. <b>Endocrine:</b> there is no duct and no lumen, and the product (a hormone) enters the blood.</li><li><b>Serous:</b> dark, granular cells with round nuclei that make a watery protein fluid. The parotid gland and the exocrine pancreas are purely serous.</li><li><b>Mucous:</b> pale, foamy cells with nuclei flattened against the base that make thick mucus.</li><li><b>Mixed:</b> both serous and mucous units in one gland, as in the submandibular and sublingual glands.</li><li><b>Holocrine:</b> the whole cell breaks down to become the secretion, as in the sebaceous (oil) glands of the skin.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Mucous cells: pale mucin, flat basal nuclei","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-9.jpg","cap":"Mucous acini (M): pale foamy cells, flat basal nuclei, beside skeletal muscle (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 9","lg":[["M","mucous cells in tubules (pale, foamy)"],["ID","small intralobular ducts (arrows)"],["SM","skeletal muscle bundles of the tongue"]]},{"src":"sl/F0722-150.jpg","cap":"Serous versus mucous cells side by side: shape, nuclei, staining, secretion","where":"2024 TA review, slide 150","lg":[["(left) a","parotid, thin plastic section"],["(left) b","parotid, H&E"],["A","serous acini"],["(left) ID","intercalated duct"],["CT","connective tissue septum"],["M","mucous cells in tubules (sublingual gland)"],["SM","skeletal muscle of the tongue"],["(right) ID","small intralobular ducts (arrows)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-9-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes a key histological difference between mucous and serous glands?","o":["A. Mucous acini have round, basophilic nuclei and stain darkly due to abundant zymogen granules","B. Serous acini have pale, foamy cytoplasm because they secrete glycoproteins","C. Mucous acini have flattened, basally located nuclei and stain lightly due to mucin content","D. Serous acini lack intercalated ducts, while mucous acini have highly developed striated ducts"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucous acini have flattened, basally located nuclei and stain lightly due to mucin content","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Mucous acini have flattened, basally located nuclei and stain lightly due to mucin content.</b> A mucous cell is filled with large granules of <b>mucin</b>. The granules take up most of the cell, so they press the nucleus flat against the base. Mucin stains poorly with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E), the routine stain, and much of it washes out when the tissue is prepared. That leaves the cell <b>pale and foamy</b>. A serous cell is the reverse: dark and granular, with a round nucleus.</p><p><b>The two cells side by side.</b></p><ul><li><b>Shape:</b> serous cells are pyramidal. Mucous cells are columnar.</li><li><b>Nucleus:</b> round in a serous cell. Flattened and pressed against the base in a mucous cell.</li><li><b>Staining:</b> a serous cell is dark. Its base is basophilic (purple) because it is full of rough endoplasmic reticulum, the organelle that makes protein. Its top is eosinophilic (pink) because of small, dense zymogen granules. A mucous cell is pale and foamy.</li><li><b>Electron microscope:</b> serous granules are small and dark (electron-dense). Mucous granules are large and pale (electron-lucent).</li><li><b>Shape of the unit:</b> serous cells form round acini with a small lumen and tiny intercellular canaliculi between the cells. Mucous cells form longer, cylindrical tubules with a wider lumen.</li><li><b>Secretion:</b> serous cells make a watery fluid rich in protein, such as the enzyme amylase. Mucous cells make thick, viscous mucus.</li></ul><p><b>What mucin is.</b> Mucins are proteins coated with many sugar chains. (A protein with sugars attached is a glycoprotein.) The sugars bind a great deal of water, so the granules are hydrophilic, which means water-loving. Once released, mucin swells with water and becomes mucus, a slippery gel that coats and lubricates food.</p><p><b>Why the granules look empty.</b> The fluids used to prepare the tissue dissolve much of the mucin, and H&amp;E colors sugars poorly. So the upper part of the cell shows as pale, bubbly spaces. Only the thin walls between the granules and the flat nucleus at the base take up stain.</p><p><b>Using this to name a salivary gland.</b> Compare the number of dark units with the number of pale units.</p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> all dark serous acini.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> mostly dark serous acini, with some pale mucous tubules.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> mostly pale mucous tubules.</li></ul><p><b>Ducts in serous and mucous glands.</b> Every salivary gland has intercalated and striated ducts. They are long and numerous in the serous parotid. The mostly mucous sublingual gland has few.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":9,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"},{"id":"F4081-32-1","s":"The histologic image shows mucous cells from a salivary gland. Why are the apical granules in these cells hydrophilic?","o":["A. Because they contain large mucin granules","B. Because they contain abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum","C. Because their nuclei are round and central","D. Because they form spherical acini","E. Because their basal domains are basophilic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Because they contain large mucin granules","a":"A","why":"A. Mucous cells store <b>large granules of mucin</b>, a heavily sugar-coated protein that binds water. That makes the granules <b>hydrophilic</b> and leaves them pale in routine stains.<br>B. Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum is the serous cell's basal feature.<br>C. Round, central nuclei describe serous cells; mucous nuclei are flat and basal.<br>D. Spherical acini are the serous shape; mucous cells form cylindrical tubules.<br>E. A dark-blue (basophilic) base is a serous trait.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Mucous acini with pale, foamy cytoplasm and flattened basal nuclei (not needed to answer).","tg":["H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":32,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 3"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"EM: pale mucous vs dark serous granules","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-10.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph of a mixed acinus: pale mucous (M), dark serous (S), myoepithelial (My)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 10","lg":[["M","mucous cells (large pale granules)"],["L","lumen of the acinus"],["S","serous cells (small dark granules)"],["My","myoepithelial cell"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-212-1","s":"Don't forget EM! Which one is which? (cells labelled A and B in the electron micrograph of a mixed acinus: serous or mucous)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = Mucous, B = Serous","a":null,"why":"A = <b>mucous</b> cell, B = <b>serous</b> cell. In electron microscopy (EM), mucous cells are packed with <b>large, pale (electron-lucent) mucin granules</b>. Serous cells hold <b>small, dark (electron-dense) zymogen granules</b> and sit near the base of the acinus.<br>Common mix-up: in EM, dark means dense protein. So the dark-granule cell is serous, just as serous cells look darker in light microscopy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-212.jpg","in":"TEM of a mixed salivary acinus: cells labelled A are filled with large pale, poorly electron-dense secretory granules; cells labelled B (at the base) contain smaller dark electron-dense granules.","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)","H16 slide 10 (Ultrastructure)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":212,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","mucous cells (large pale granules)"],["L","lumen of the acinus"],["B","serous cell (small dark granules)"],["My","myoepithelial cell"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 4"},{"id":"F0722-151-1","s":"Label the three acini (epithelia cells) -","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Mucous cells (M, large pale granules); serous cells (S, small dense granules); myoepithelial cell (My, at the base)","a":null,"why":"Purple box = <b>mucous cell</b> (large pale mucin granules). Red box = <b>serous cell</b> (small dark, electron-dense granules near the lumen). Orange box = <b>myoepithelial cell</b>: a flat cell at the base, between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. On a nerve signal it contracts and squeezes secretion out.<br>Common mix-up: the myoepithelial cell is not a secretory cell; it has no secretory granules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-151.jpg","in":"TEM of a mixed acinus: many pale mucous granules (M), a cluster of dark serous granules (S), a flattened myoepithelial cell (My) at the base.","tg":["H16 slide 10 (Ultrastructure)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["purple box","mucous cells (large pale granules)"],["L","lumen of the acinus"],["red box","serous cell (small dark granules)"],["orange box","myoepithelial cell at the base"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 5"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Myoepithelial cells squeeze out secretion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-11.jpg","cap":"Myoepithelial basket cells hug acini and intralobular ducts; they contract to squeeze secretion","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-85-1","s":"What is the function of Myoepithelial cells?","o":["A. Their function is unknown","B. They secrete amylase","C. They squeeze out secretion from acini and ducts in response to nervous stimuli","D. They produce mucous"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"They squeeze out secretion from acini and ducts in response to nervous stimuli","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. They squeeze out secretion from acini and ducts in response to nervous stimuli.</b> The name gives the job. \"Myo\" means muscle, and \"epithelial\" means the cell belongs to the epithelium. A <b>myoepithelial cell</b> is an epithelial cell that can contract like smooth muscle. It wraps around a secretory unit. When nerves stimulate it, it tightens and <b>pushes the secretion out</b> of the acinus and along the first ducts.</p><p><b>What they look like and where they sit.</b></p><ul><li>They are flat cells with long contractile arms. The arms hold the acinus the way fingers hold a ball, so they are also called <b>basket cells</b>.</li><li>They surround the acini and the intercalated ducts, the first small ducts that leave the acini.</li><li>They lie <b>between the secretory cells and the basal lamina</b>, the thin sheet on which the epithelium rests. So they are inside the unit's own wrapping, not out in the connective tissue.</li><li>On a routine section they are hard to see. Usually only a thin, flat nucleus at the edge of the acinus shows.</li></ul><p><b>Why a gland needs them.</b> A secretory cell can release its granules, but it cannot push the fluid along. The lumen of an acinus is very small, and mucus is thick. The squeeze of the myoepithelial cells empties the unit quickly, for example when food enters the mouth.</p><p><b>The glands they work in.</b> There are three pairs of major salivary glands: the <b>parotid</b>, the <b>submandibular</b> and the <b>sublingual</b>. Many small minor glands also lie in the lining of the mouth. Each major gland is a compound tubuloacinar gland. \"Compound\" means that its ducts branch like a tree. \"Tubuloacinar\" means that the branches end in secretory units that are round (acini) or longer (tubules).</p><p><b>How a salivary gland is organized.</b></p><ul><li>A connective tissue <b>capsule</b> covers the gland.</li><li><b>Septa</b>, which are walls of connective tissue, run inward from the capsule. They divide the gland into lobes and then into lobules.</li><li>Each <b>lobule</b> holds the secretory units and the small ducts that drain them.</li><li>The connective tissue between the units carries blood vessels, nerves and fat cells. It is also rich in <b>plasma cells</b>, which make immunoglobulin A (IgA), the antibody found in saliva.</li></ul><p><b>Who does the other jobs.</b> Serous cells make the watery, enzyme-rich fluid that contains amylase. Mucous cells make the mucus. Myoepithelial cells secrete nothing; they only contract. The pancreas has no myoepithelial cells, which is one way to tell it from a salivary gland.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":85,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":236,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"},{"id":"F4081-34-1","s":"A histologic image shows basket cells located between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. These flattened cells with contractile processes are found in acini and intralobular ducts. What is their functional role?","o":["A. Producing viscid mucus","B. Forming cylindrical tubules","C. Squeezing out secretion in response to nervous stimuli","D. Compressing nuclei to the basal region","E. Forming intercellular canaliculi"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Squeezing out secretion in response to nervous stimuli","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Myoepithelial (basket) cells</b> sit between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. Their contractile processes <b>squeeze secretion out of acini and intralobular ducts</b> when nerves fire.<br>A. Viscid mucus is made by mucous cells.<br>B. Cylindrical tubules are the shape of mucous secretory units.<br>D. Flattened basal nuclei are a mucous cell feature.<br>E. Intercellular canaliculi are channels between serous cells that lead to the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":34,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-85-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 6"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Intercalated vs striated duct functions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-20.jpg","cap":"Functions list: acini, intercalated ducts, striated ducts and plasma cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: intercalated absorb chloride, secrete bicarbonate; striated reabsorb sodium","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-154-1","s":"What do the intercalated ducts do?","o":["A. Secrete HCO3- (Bicarbonate); absorb Cl-","B. Absorb Na+; secrete K+","C. Secrete Pâté","D. Secrete H+; Absorb K+"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secrete HCO3- (Bicarbonate); absorb Cl-","a":"A","why":"A. Per the lecture, <b>intercalated ducts absorb chloride (Cl−) and secrete bicarbonate (HCO3−)</b>.<br>B. Absorbing sodium (Na+) and secreting potassium (K+) is the <b>striated duct</b>'s job.<br>C. A joke option; ducts do not secrete pâté.<br>D. Secreting hydrogen ions (H+) and absorbing K+ is what alpha-intercalated cells of the kidney's collecting duct do, not salivary ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":154,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 7"},{"id":"F4081-45-1","s":"Match the structures with functions","o":["A. Acini","B. Intercalated ducts","C. Striated ducts","D. Plasma cells","E. Reabsorbs Na & secretes K and HCO3","F. IgA","G. Absorbs Cl & secrete HCO3","H. Secrete sero/mucous fluid"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Acini - secrete sero/mucous fluid; Intercalated ducts - absorb Cl & secrete HCO3; Striated ducts - reabsorb Na & secrete K and HCO3; Plasma cells - IgA","a":null,"why":"Acini → <b>secrete sero/mucous fluid</b> (H). Intercalated ducts → <b>absorb chloride (Cl−) and secrete bicarbonate (HCO3−)</b> (G). Striated ducts → <b>reabsorb sodium (Na+) and secrete potassium (K+) and HCO3−</b> (E), making saliva hypotonic. Plasma cells → <b>immunoglobulin A (IgA)</b> (F).<br>Common mix-up: both duct types add bicarbonate; the sodium pump that makes saliva hypotonic is in the striated duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":45,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 8"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Identify a striated duct by its basal striations","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD) among serous acini, with drawing of basal striations","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13","lg":[["a","light micrograph, hematoxylin and eosin"],["SD","striated duct (faint basal striations)"],["b","scanning electron micrograph, duct cells seen from the side"],["L","lumen"],["B","basal ends with interlocking membrane folds"],["c","scanning electron micrograph of the cell bases (basal lamina removed)"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-23.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD): pale columnar cells with basal striations among serous acini","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 23","lg":[["A","serous acini (dark, granular)"],["M","mucous cells (pale, foamy)"],["S","serous demilunes (dark crescents capping mucous tubules)"],["arrows (top edge)","more serous demilunes (their S labels cropped off)"],["ID","small intralobular duct"],["SD","striated duct (pale columnar cells, basal striations)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-186-1","s":"Question 3 Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Serous gland","B. Mucous gland","C. Striated duct","D. Mixed gland","E. Excretory duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct","a":"C","why":"C. A ring of <b>tall pink columnar cells</b> with central round nuclei and <b>vertical stripes in the basal cytoplasm</b>, around a small lumen inside the lobule, is a <b>striated duct</b>.<br>A. A serous acinus is dark granular pyramidal cells around a tiny lumen.<br>B. Mucous cells are pale and foamy, with flat basal nuclei.<br>D. A mixed gland is a whole organ, not one circled tube.<br>E. Excretory (interlobular) ducts are larger and lie in connective-tissue septa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-186.jpg","in":"High-power H&E: a green outline circles a duct of tall columnar cells with central round nuclei and radially striated pale basal cytoplasm around a small lumen, among dark serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":186,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":4,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["green outline","striated duct (pink columnar cells, central round nuclei, basal striations)"]],"like":"G118-16-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 9"},{"id":"G118-16-1","s":"Identify the structure in the yellow outline.","o":["A. Pancreatic duct","B. Interlobular duct","C. Intercalated duct","D. Striated duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Striated duct.</b> The yellow circle surrounds a round tube that lies <b>inside the lobule</b>, directly among the acini, with no band of connective tissue around it. Its wall is a single layer of <b>tall, pale pink columnar cells</b>. Their nuclei are round and sit in the middle of the cells, and the lumen is open. The tube is also bigger than the acini beside it. A large pink duct of tall cells lying among the acini is a striated duct.</p><p><b>The rest of the picture.</b> The dark, granular units are serous acini. At the top are a few pale, foamy mucous units, each with a dark cap of serous cells (a serous demilune). Dark and pale units together mean a mixed salivary gland. Striated ducts are found only in salivary glands.</p><p><b>The two ducts inside a lobule.</b> Saliva leaves the acinus through a chain of ducts. The first two are intralobular, which means inside the lobule.</p><ul><li><b>Intercalated duct:</b> the first and smallest. It is short and narrow, lined by low simple cuboidal cells, and easy to miss. It links each acinus to a striated duct.</li><li><b>Striated duct:</b> the next one, and much larger. It is lined by simple columnar cells that are wide and pink, with round central nuclei. Fine vertical stripes run through the base of each cell. These basal striations give the duct its name.</li></ul><p><b>What the stripes are.</b> The membrane at the base of the cell folds deeply inward, and long mitochondria stand in rows between the folds. Under the light microscope the rows look like stripes. The folds carry ion pumps, and the mitochondria power them. The duct uses them to pull sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) out of the saliva. The many mitochondria are also why the cells stain so pink.</p><p><b>How to name any duct on a section.</b> Look first at what surrounds it, then at its lining.</p><ul><li>Among the acini, tiny, with low cuboidal cells: intercalated duct.</li><li>Among the acini, large, with tall pink cells and an open lumen: striated duct.</li><li>In a band of pink connective tissue between lobules: interlobular duct. Its lining is often layered.</li></ul><p><b>The cells around the ducts.</b> The connective tissue around the ducts and acini holds many plasma cells. They make immunoglobulin A (IgA), an antibody. The acinar cells add a carrier piece called the secretory component, which takes the IgA across the cell and into the saliva.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-16.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E: dark serous acini, a few pale mucous acini with demilunes at the top; the yellow circle surrounds a large duct inside the lobule, lined by tall, pale pink columnar cells with round central nuclei around an open lumen (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":16,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Striated duct: basal folds, mitochondria, sodium reabsorption","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct cell: basal membrane folds packed with mitochondria; micrograph of striations","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13","lg":[["a","light micrograph, hematoxylin and eosin"],["SD","striated duct (faint basal striations)"],["b","scanning electron micrograph, duct cells seen from the side"],["L","lumen"],["B","basal ends with interlocking membrane folds"],["c","scanning electron micrograph of the cell bases (basal lamina removed)"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-20.jpg","cap":"Functions list: striated ducts reabsorb sodium, secrete potassium and bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/F0754-204.jpg","cap":"Striated duct drawing and micrograph: basal infoldings with mitochondria power sodium reabsorption","where":"2022 TA review, slide 204"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-158-1","s":"What is a key feature of striated ducts?","o":["A. They are interlobular","B. They have a simple columnar epithelium","C. They are markedly present in the pancreas","D. Basal striations are present"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Basal striations are present","a":"D","why":"B and D. <b>Striated ducts</b> are lined by <b>simple columnar</b> cells with <b>basal striations</b>. The striations are folds of the basal membrane packed with mitochondria, which power sodium (Na+) reabsorption.<br>A. Striated ducts are intralobular (inside the lobule), not interlobular.<br>C. The pancreas has <b>no</b> striated ducts; that helps tell it from the parotid.<br>Note: the source's answer slide rewrote B as 'cuboidal'. As printed, B (columnar) is true.","ex":"","fl":["As printed on question slide 158, option B ('They have a simple columnar epithelium') is also TRUE: striated ducts are simple columnar with basal striations (G020:12). The answer slide silently rewrites B as 'simple cuboidal' to make it false. D is the intended single answer, but B as asked is not wrong."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":158,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 10"},{"id":"F0753-89-1","s":"What is the function of striated ducts and which organelle is found in abundance there?","o":["A. Secrete mucous, ER","B. Secrete proteinacoeus fluid, ribosomes","C. Has apical infolding that increases surface area for ion transport; mitochondria","D. Has basal striations that increase the surface area for ion transport; mitochondria"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Has basal striations that increase the surface area for ion transport; mitochondria","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Has basal striations that increase the surface area for ion transport; mitochondria.</b> A striated duct does not make saliva. It <b>changes</b> the saliva that flows through it by moving ions. The work is done at the <b>base</b> of its cells, where the membrane is folded many times to make room for ion pumps. Pumping needs energy, so the base is packed with <b>mitochondria</b>.</p><p><b>What the striations are.</b></p><ul><li>The basal cell membrane folds deeply inward, and the folds interlock with those of the neighboring cells. This greatly enlarges the surface that carries the pumps.</li><li>Long mitochondria line up in rows between the folds. They supply the energy to pump sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>).</li><li>Under the light microscope the rows of folds and mitochondria show as fine vertical stripes in the lower part of the cell.</li></ul><p><b>Why the base.</b> The basal side of the cell rests on the connective tissue and faces the blood vessels. The apical side faces the lumen. Salt taken out of the saliva is handed over to the blood at the basal side, so the pumps and their surface area are there.</p><p><b>What each duct does to the saliva.</b></p><ul><li><b>Intercalated duct:</b> absorbs chloride (Cl<sup>−</sup>) and secretes bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>).</li><li><b>Striated duct:</b> actively reabsorbs sodium and chloride, and secretes potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) and bicarbonate.</li></ul><p><b>The result is hypotonic saliva.</b> The ducts remove more salt than they add, and their walls let little water through. Water cannot follow the salt. So the final saliva is <b>hypotonic</b>, which means less salty than plasma. It is low in sodium and chloride and rich in potassium and bicarbonate.</p><p><b>How to recognize the duct.</b> A striated duct lies inside the lobule, among the acini, and is wider than they are. Its lining is one layer of tall, pink columnar cells with round central nuclei around an open lumen. The intercalated duct that comes before it is much smaller, with low cuboidal cells.</p><p><b>Where striated ducts are found.</b> They belong to the salivary glands. The parotid and the submandibular gland have many. The sublingual gland has few. The pancreas has none, which is one of the best ways to tell the pancreas from the parotid.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":89,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":237,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"},{"id":"F0754-202-1","s":"Which of the following is true in regards to the structure labeled B below?","o":["A. The apical portion has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","B. You can commonly find this structure in the pancreas","C. The basal portion of this structure has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","D. It is commonly associated with centroacinar cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The basal portion of this structure has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","a":"C","why":"C. B is a <b>striated duct</b>: tall pink columnar cells around a lumen. Its <b>basal infoldings hold many mitochondria</b>, which power sodium (Na+) reabsorption. This modifies the saliva; it ends up hypotonic.<br>A. The mitochondria-rich folds are basal, not apical.<br>B. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>D. Centroacinar cells belong to the pancreas, which lacks striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-202.jpg","in":"H&E of a serous (parotid) gland: serous acini (A), connective tissue septum (CT); 'B' labels a duct with a central lumen lined by tall columnar cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":202,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["B","striated duct (pink columnar cells, basal striations)"],["A","serous acini"],["CT","connective tissue septum"]],"like":"F0753-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 11"},{"id":"F3952-93-1","s":"What is the structure labeled w red and what is its function?","o":["A. Intercalated ducts, empty secretions into the duodenum","B. Striated duct, carry zymogens to the duodenum","C. Striated duct, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and add HCO3-","D. Interlobular duct, empty secretions into the oral cavity"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and add HCO3-","a":"C","why":"C. The red-boxed duct is a <b>striated duct</b>: pink columnar cells with <b>basal striations</b>, among serous acini. It <b>reabsorbs sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl−)</b> and adds bicarbonate (HCO3−), making saliva hypotonic.<br>A. Intercalated ducts are small cuboidal tubes, and salivary ducts drain to the mouth, not the duodenum.<br>B. Right duct, wrong job: it carries saliva to the mouth, not zymogens to the duodenum.<br>D. Interlobular ducts lie in septa, not among the acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-93.jpg","in":"Serous salivary gland at high power; a red box marks a duct lined by columnar cells with pale eosinophilic cytoplasm and basal striations, surrounded by dark granular serous acini (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":93,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["red box","striated duct (pale columnar cells, faint basal striations)"]],"like":"F0753-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 12"},{"id":"F3949-23-1","s":"A Patient presents with xerostomia (dry mouth), dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), Ageusia (loss of taste), and swollen parotid glands. A biopsy of the parotid glands show inflammatory infiltrates in the interlobular connective tissue with damage to the acinar cells and striated ducts, reabsorption of which of the following will be most altered?","o":["A. HCO3-","B. Na+","C. Ca2+","D. H2O"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Na+","a":"B","why":"B. The <b>striated ducts</b> actively <b>reabsorb sodium (Na+)</b> and chloride, and secrete potassium and bicarbonate. Damaged striated ducts mainly lose Na+ reabsorption, so the saliva stays saltier.<br>A. Bicarbonate (HCO3−) is secreted into saliva by the ducts, not reabsorbed.<br>C. Calcium (Ca2+) is not what striated ducts pump; they move sodium, potassium and bicarbonate.<br>D. Salivary ducts are nearly water-tight; they do not reabsorb water, which is why saliva ends up hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":23,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-89-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 13"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Duct order: intercalated → striated → interlobular","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-12.jpg","cap":"Duct tree diagram with linings of intercalated, striated, interlobular and main ducts","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: location, lining and secretion of each salivary duct","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-27-1","s":"Which structure found within salivary glands is responsible for connecting secretory acini to the striated ducts?","o":["A. Interlobular duct","B. Intercalated duct","C. Adipose tissue","D. Capsule","E. Plasma-cell–rich connective tissue"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intercalated duct","a":"B","why":"B. The <b>intercalated duct</b> is the first, short duct, lined by simple cuboidal cells. It links each acinus to the striated duct. Order: acinus → intercalated → striated → interlobular → main duct.<br>A. Interlobular ducts are larger, lie in the septa between lobules, and come after the striated ducts.<br>C. Adipose tissue is stroma, not a duct.<br>D. The capsule wraps the whole gland.<br>E. Plasma-cell-rich connective tissue makes immunoglobulin A; it carries no saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":27,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 14"},{"id":"F4081-37-1","s":"Which option correctly represents the flow of saliva through the duct system?","o":["A. Intercalated duct → Intralobular duct → Interlobular duct → Main duct","B. Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct → Main duct","C. Main duct → Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct","D. Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct → Main duct → Interlobular duct","E. Intercalated duct → Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Main duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Intercalated duct → Intralobular duct → Interlobular duct → Main duct","a":"A","why":"A. Saliva flows from small ducts to large: <b>intercalated → intralobular (striated) → interlobular → main duct</b>, which opens into the mouth.<br>B. Starts in the middle and runs backward to the intercalated duct.<br>C. Fully reversed: from the mouth back toward the acinus.<br>D. Puts the intralobular duct before the intercalated duct, and the main duct before the interlobular duct.<br>E. Puts the interlobular duct before the intralobular one; saliva must leave the lobule last.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":37,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 15"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Interlobular duct = duct in the septum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-16.jpg","cap":"Sublingual atlas plate: large interlobular excretory duct running in a connective-tissue septum","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: interlobular duct in a septum, intercalated duct, centroacinar cells, islets","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: interlobular ducts lie between lobules, within the septa","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-206-1","s":"Identify (the structures marked by the orange dot and the blue dots)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Orange = Interlobular duct; Blue = Intralobular duct (striated duct specifically)","a":null,"why":"Orange = <b>interlobular duct</b>: it lies in the thick pink <b>connective-tissue septum between lobules</b>. Blue = <b>intralobular ducts</b>, here <b>striated ducts</b>: they sit <b>inside the lobule</b> among the dark serous acini.<br>Common mix-up: judge a duct by its location first. Size and lining vary, but a duct in the septum is always interlobular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-206.jpg","in":"H&E of a mixed salivary gland lobule with dark serous and pale mucous acini; blue dots sit on small ducts inside the lobule, an orange dot sits on a larger duct in the connective-tissue septum between lobules.","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":206,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["orange dot","interlobular duct (in the connective tissue septum)"],["blue dots","striated ducts (intralobular; pink columnar cells)"]],"like":"F3959-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 16"},{"id":"F0722-153-1","s":"The arrows are pointing to the same structure. What is this structure?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Interlobular ducts","a":null,"why":"<b>Interlobular ducts</b>. Both arrows point to a duct lying <b>in the connective-tissue septum between lobules</b> (left: pancreas; right: parotid). Location is the easiest clue: inside a lobule = intralobular; in the septum = interlobular.<br>Common mix-up: a striated duct is also large and pink, but it sits inside the lobule among the acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-153.jpg","in":"Left: pancreas with an arrow on a duct in a connective-tissue septum; right: salivary gland with an arrow on a large duct running in the interlobular connective tissue.","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","interlobular ducts (in connective tissue septa between lobules)"],["thin black lines (right)","pointer lines whose labels were cropped off"]],"like":"F3959-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 17"},{"id":"F3959-9-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar duct","B. Intralobular duct","C. Striated duct","D. Interlobular duct","E. Intercalated duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Interlobular duct","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Interlobular duct.</b> The arrow tip rests on a small duct: a row of cuboidal cells with round nuclei along a narrow lumen. The duct does not lie among the acini. It runs inside the <b>pale pink band of connective tissue</b> that crosses the field of purple acini. That band is a <b>septum</b>, the wall between two lobules. \"Inter\" means between, so a duct in a septum is an interlobular duct.</p><p><b>Name a duct by its surroundings first.</b></p><ul><li>A duct surrounded by acini is <b>intralobular</b> (inside the lobule).</li><li>A duct sitting in a band of pink connective tissue between lobules is <b>interlobular</b>.</li></ul><p>Then look at the lining. A tiny duct of low cuboidal cells is an intercalated duct. A wide duct of tall pink cells with basal stripes is a striated duct. A larger duct, often with a layered lining, is interlobular.</p><p><b>The lining of interlobular ducts changes with size.</b> The smallest are simple cuboidal, like the one at the arrow. Larger ones are simple columnar. The largest are stratified cuboidal, stratified columnar or pseudostratified columnar. In a salivary gland the main duct becomes nonkeratinized stratified squamous near its opening, where it meets the lining of the mouth.</p><p><b>The whole path in a salivary gland.</b></p><ol><li>Acinus.</li><li>Intercalated duct (inside the lobule).</li><li>Striated duct (inside the lobule).</li><li>Interlobular duct, in the septa.</li><li>Lobar duct.</li><li>Main (excretory) duct, which opens into the mouth.</li></ol><p><b>The path in the pancreas.</b> The field in this picture is pancreas: tightly packed serous acini and no striated ducts.</p><ol><li>Centroacinar cells. These are single pale cells in the center of an acinus, and they are the start of the intercalated duct.</li><li>Intercalated duct.</li><li>Intralobular duct.</li><li>Interlobular duct, in the septa. The larger ones are lined by columnar cells.</li><li>Main pancreatic duct, which reaches the duodenum.</li></ol><p><b>Parts of a secretory unit to be able to label.</b> Serous acinus, mucous tubule, serous demilune (a cap of serous cells on a mucous tubule), myoepithelial cells (contractile cells wrapped around the unit), intercellular secretory canaliculi (tiny channels between serous cells), intercalated duct and striated duct.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-54.jpg","in":"Same pancreas field as Q7; black arrow on a small duct lined by low cuboidal cells inside the pale connective-tissue septum. Asked: the arrowed structure.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":9,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":196,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Name the parts of a salivary secretory unit","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-5.jpg","cap":"Labelled duct tree: serous acinus, demilune, mucous tubule, myoepithelial cells, intercalated and striated ducts","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-25-1","s":"Name the structures [blank labels on a diagram of a mixed salivary gland secretory unit].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Myoepithelial cells; intercellular secretory canaliculi; serous acinus; intercalated duct(s); serous demilune; striated ducts; basal laminae; mucous tubule","a":null,"why":"Top row, left to right: <b>myoepithelial cells</b>, <b>intercellular secretory canaliculi</b>, myoepithelial cells. Left: <b>serous acinus</b> (round purple unit) and <b>intercalated duct</b> (small cuboidal tube). Right: <b>serous demilune</b> (purple cap on a tan mucous end). Middle: intercalated ducts. Bottom: <b>striated duct</b> (green, tall cells with basal stripes), <b>basal laminae</b>, <b>mucous tubule</b> (tan, pale cells, flat basal nuclei).<br>Common mix-up: the intercalated duct is short and cuboidal; the striated duct is wider and columnar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-25.jpg","in":"Diagram of a duct tree: purple serous acini with myoepithelial cells and canaliculi, small intercalated ducts, tan mucous tubules capped by a serous demilune, green striated duct cross-section; blue boxes hide the labels.","tg":["H16 slide 7 (Salivary glands)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":25,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 18"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Parotid purely serous; submandibular mostly serous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-15.jpg","cap":"Parotid: all serous acini with intercalated and striated ducts, no mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15","lg":[["a","parotid, plastic section, toluidine blue-based stain"],["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["ID","intercalated duct"],["b","parotid, hematoxylin and eosin"],["CT","connective-tissue septum"],["lines at left edge of the atlas plate (labels cut off), top to bottom","venule; interlobular excretory duct; fat cells; interlobular excretory duct; serous acini; connective-tissue septa between lobules; myoepithelial cells (on inset I)"],["I","serous acinus"],["II","intercalated duct"],["III","striated duct"],["IV","interlobular excretory duct"]]},{"src":"sl/F0754-211.jpg","cap":"Parotid serous, sublingual mostly mucous, submandibular mostly serous, side by side","where":"2022 TA review, slide 211","lg":[["thin black lines (parotid)","pointer lines whose labels were cropped off"],["M","mucous cells (pale tubules)"],["A","serous acini"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-207-3","s":"15. Which one of the following is a \"pure\" serous gland?","o":["A. parotid gland (adult)","B. palatine gland","C. submandibular gland","D. labial gland","E. sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"parotid gland (adult)","a":"A","why":"A. The adult <b>parotid</b> is the <b>purely serous</b> major salivary gland. All its acini are serous, with zymogen granules rich in amylase.<br>B. Palatine glands are mucous.<br>C. Submandibular is mixed, mostly serous.<br>D. Labial (lip) glands are mostly mucous.<br>E. Sublingual is mixed, mostly mucous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 19"},{"id":"F3980-6-1","s":"A histologic slide shows acini composed almost entirely of serous cells with numerous zymogen granules and well-developed intercalated ducts. Which salivary gland does this slide most likely represent?","o":["A. Parotid","B. Submandibular","C. Sublingual"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid","a":"A","why":"A. A gland made <b>almost entirely of serous acini</b> full of zymogen granules, with long intercalated ducts, is the <b>parotid</b>, the only purely serous major salivary gland.<br>B. Submandibular is mixed, mostly serous: you would also see pale mucous units and serous demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual is mixed but mostly mucous: mainly pale mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":6,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 20"},{"id":"F1221-207-2","s":"14. In humans, which one of the following is correct?","o":["A. the parotid gland is chiefly a mixed gland","B. the submandibular gland is chiefly a mucous gland","C. the sublingual is chiefly a serous gland","D. the parotid is chiefly a mucous gland","E. the submandibular gland is chiefly a serous gland"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"the submandibular gland is chiefly a serous gland","a":"E","why":"E. The <b>submandibular</b> gland is <b>mixed but chiefly serous</b>: mostly dark serous acini, with some mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, not mixed.<br>B. Submandibular is chiefly serous, not mucous.<br>C. The sublingual is chiefly mucous.<br>D. The parotid has essentially no mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 21"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Serous demilune: serous cap on a mucous acinus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-19.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes (S) capping mucous acini (M); mostly sublingual, also submandibular","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19","lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows; dark caps on mucous tubules)"],["M","mucous cells (pale)"],["ID","small intralobular duct (arrow)"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-72.jpg","cap":"Arrows on dark serous crescents capping pale mucous tubules","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-160-1","s":"Identify this structure. What are the arrows point to? What are they surrounding? Where are they found?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Serous demilunes; they surround (cap) mucous acini; found in sublingual and submandibular glands","a":null,"why":"The arrows point to <b>serous demilunes</b>: half-moon caps of dark granular serous cells. They <b>surround (cap) mucous acini</b> (M, pale). They are found in the mixed glands: mostly the <b>sublingual</b>, also the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the parotid is purely serous, so it never has demilunes. A = a purely serous acinus; ID = intercalated duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-160.jpg","in":"Mucous acini (M) capped by crescents of dark serous cells (arrows); serous acinus (A) and intercalated duct (ID) labeled.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":160,"f":"F0722"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":43,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":239,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","serous demilunes (darker serous caps on mucous tubules)"],["A","serous acinus"],["M","mucous cells (pale tubules)"],["ID","small intralobular duct"]],"like":"G024-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 22"},{"id":"G024-9-1","s":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the structure indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. The organ shown in the image is the parotid gland","B. The structure squeezes the secretion from the acini","C. The structure secretes trypsinogen","D. Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","E. It has basal infoldings"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion.</b> The arrow points to a thin, <b>dark purple crescent</b> on the outer edge of a pale, foamy unit. The pale cells are mucous cells. The dark cap on them is a group of serous cells called a <b>serous demilune</b> (demilune means half-moon). Its cells add the watery, protein-rich part to the mucus, so the gland's product is mixed, or seromucous.</p><p><b>The rest of the picture.</b> Most of the field is dark serous acini. In the middle is a cluster of pale mucous units, several of them with dark caps. The empty white spaces are fat cells. The pinker tubes at the lower left are striated ducts. A gland with both dark and pale units is a mixed salivary gland.</p><p><b>How the serous secretion gets out.</b> The demilune caps the closed end of the mucous tubule, behind the mucous cells. Its secretion reaches the lumen through tiny canaliculi that run between the mucous cells. The crescent shape is partly produced by fixation. In living tissue the serous cells stand in line with the mucous cells along the tubule.</p><p><b>Where demilunes are found.</b> Only in the two mixed glands. They are seen mostly in the sublingual gland and also in the submandibular gland. The parotid has no mucous cells, so it has no demilunes.</p><p><b>Nearby structures and their jobs.</b> Myoepithelial cells are the contractile cells that squeeze secretion out of the acini. Striated ducts are the ducts whose cells have basal infoldings for moving ions. Trypsinogen is an inactive enzyme made by the acinar cells of the pancreas.</p><p><b>Three glands, three kinds of saliva.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> exclusively serous. It is the largest gland and makes most of the salivary amylase.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> mixed, mostly serous. Its serous cells add lysozyme, an enzyme that attacks bacteria.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> mixed, mostly mucous. Its serous cells are mainly in demilunes.</li><li><b>Minor glands</b> of the lips, cheeks and palate: mostly mucous. The von Ebner glands of the tongue are the exception; they are serous.</li></ul><p><b>What saliva contains.</b> Water, mucins, amylase, lysozyme, immunoglobulin A (IgA, an antibody) with its secretory component, and ions. Saliva has more potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) and bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>) and less sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) than plasma.</p><p><b>Clinical words.</b></p><ul><li><b>Xerostomia:</b> dry mouth. Causes include Sjögren syndrome (an autoimmune attack on the salivary glands), radiation and drugs.</li><li><b>Sialorrhea:</b> too much saliva, with drooling.</li><li><b>Mumps:</b> a viral infection that swells the parotid glands.</li><li><b>Sialolithiasis:</b> stones in the ducts, most often in the submandibular gland.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G115-8.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E: mostly purple serous acini with a central cluster of pale, foamy mucous acini; the black arrow points to a dark purple crescent of serous cells capping a mucous acinus (serous demilune). Same micrograph as G024 slide 9. On the slide the picture covers the ends of options A and B.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":9,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":9,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":7,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"},{"id":"F0754-208-1","s":"Identify the orange structure. Where was this slide taken from?","o":["A. Serous demilune, submandibular gland","B. Centroacinar cell, pancreas","C. Serous demilune, parotid gland","D. Centroacinar cell, sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Serous demilune, submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. The orange markers sit on <b>serous demilunes</b>: dark serous crescents capping pale mucous acini (M). Demilunes mean a mixed gland, so the <b>submandibular</b> fits (sublingual would too, but is not offered).<br>B. Centroacinar cells are pale cells inside pancreatic acini, not caps on mucous acini.<br>C. The parotid is purely serous, so it has no demilunes.<br>D. Centroacinar cells belong to the pancreas, not the sublingual gland.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-208.jpg","in":"H&E of a mixed gland: pale mucous tubules (M) each capped by a crescent of darker, granular serous cells (orange markers + arrows), plus purely serous acini (A).","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 71 (? Mixed salivary (Submandibular) gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":208,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["orange dots (with arrows)","serous demilunes (darker serous caps on mucous tubules)"],["M","mucous cells (pale tubules)"]],"like":"G024-9-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 23"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Serous demilunes mostly in the sublingual","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-19.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes (S) capping mucous acini (M); mostly sublingual, also submandibular","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19","lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows; dark caps on mucous tubules)"],["M","mucous cells (pale)"],["ID","small intralobular duct (arrow)"]]},{"src":"sl/F0754-210.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes: half-moon serous caps on mucous acini in mixed glands","where":"2022 TA review, slide 210","lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows)"],["M","mucous cells (pale tubules)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-166-3","s":"6. Which one of the following glands would possess numerous demilunes?","o":["A. parotid","B. sublingual","C. posterior lingual","D. von Ebner"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"sublingual","a":"B","why":"B. <b>Serous demilunes</b> cap mucous acini, so they occur only in mixed glands. Per the lecture they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b>, and also in the submandibular.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous: no mucous acini to cap.<br>C. Posterior lingual glands, at the back of the tongue, are mucous.<br>D. Von Ebner glands, at the circumvallate papillae, are purely serous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 24"},{"id":"F4081-43-1","s":"The histologic image shown demonstrates the structure indicated. Where is this structure mostly seen?","o":["A. Parotid gland","B. Sublingual gland","C. Lacrimal gland","D. Minor salivary glands of the palate","E. Parathyroid gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"B","why":"B. The arrows mark <b>serous demilunes</b>: crescents of serous cells (S) capping mucous acini (M). The lecture says they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b> gland, and also in the submandibular.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, so it has no mucous acini to cap.<br>C. The lacrimal gland is serous, without demilunes.<br>D. Minor palatine glands are mucous.<br>E. The parathyroid is an endocrine gland of chief and oxyphil cells, with no acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-43.jpg","in":"Pale mucous acini (M) each capped by a crescent of granular serous cells (arrows, S = serous demilunes); A = serous acinus.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":43,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","serous acinus"],["arrows (top)","serous demilunes (their S labels cut off)"],["M","mucous cells of mucous tubules (pale, foamy)"],["S","serous demilune (purple serous cap on a mucous tubule)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 25"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":32,"label":"Parotid: all-serous acini, fat cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"},{"src":"sl/G020-15.jpg","cap":"Parotid: all serous acini with intercalated and striated ducts, no mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15","lg":[["a","parotid, plastic section, toluidine blue-based stain"],["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["ID","intercalated duct"],["b","parotid, hematoxylin and eosin"],["CT","connective-tissue septum"],["lines at left edge of the atlas plate (labels cut off), top to bottom","venule; interlobular excretory duct; fat cells; interlobular excretory duct; serous acini; connective-tissue septa between lobules; myoepithelial cells (on inset I)"],["I","serous acinus"],["II","intercalated duct"],["III","striated duct"],["IV","interlobular excretory duct"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-28-1","s":"D6- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Parotid gland.</b> This is a whole section seen at low power. Thin pale septa divide it into lobules, and <b>every lobule is the same dark purple</b>. There are no pale patches anywhere. Dark purple means serous acini, and pale would mean mucous units. A salivary gland that is serous throughout is the parotid. The pink rings in the wider septa are the large ducts and blood vessels.</p><p><b>The parotid checklist at higher power.</b></p><ul><li>Every secretory unit is a dark, granular <b>serous acinus</b> with round nuclei. No pale mucous cells are present.</li><li><b>Fat cells</b> lie among the acini. They look like empty white circles, and they increase with age.</li><li>There are many <b>intercalated and striated ducts</b>. The pink striated ducts stand out among the purple acini.</li><li><b>Septa</b> of connective tissue carry the interlobular ducts.</li></ul><p><b>Why color works at low power.</b> Serous cells are full of rough endoplasmic reticulum (the protein-making organelle) and protein granules, which take up stain strongly. Mucous cells are full of mucin, which stains poorly and largely washes out. So the share of dark and pale areas names the gland before a single cell is seen.</p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> all dark.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> mostly dark, with scattered pale mucous tubules. Many of these carry a dark cap of serous cells called a demilune.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> mostly pale.</li></ul><p><b>What the parotid does.</b> It is the largest salivary gland. Its secretion is thin, watery and rich in protein. It makes most of the <b>salivary amylase</b>, the enzyme that begins starch digestion. Mumps, a viral infection, makes the parotid glands swell.</p><p><b>The parotid's closest look-alike.</b> The exocrine pancreas is also made only of dark serous acini. Four things mark the pancreas:</p><ul><li>Pale round islets of Langerhans, its clusters of hormone-making cells.</li><li>Pale centroacinar cells in the middle of its acini.</li><li>No striated ducts.</li><li>Few fat cells.</li></ul><p>The parotid has striated ducts and many fat cells, and it has no islets.</p><p><b>Organs with no acini.</b> The liver is made of plates of hepatocytes with blood channels between them. The spleen is lymphoid tissue. Neither organ has acini.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-15.jpg","in":"Low-power whole section of a lobulated gland: uniformly dark purple serous acini, septa with large ducts and vessels, no pale mucous areas. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":15,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":25,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"},{"id":"F0052-192-1","s":"Question 6 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. Clusters of <b>dark granular serous acini</b> separated by <b>large empty fat cells</b>, with no mucous cells and no islets, mean the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue with red and white pulp.<br>B. Pancreas is tightly packed acini with pale islets and little fat.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-52.jpg","in":"Clusters of dark granular serous acini separated by large clear fat cells, with a large pink striated duct; no mucous cells, no islets. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":192,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 26"},{"id":"F3959-31-1","s":"D9- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. Only <b>dark serous acini</b>, with <b>many fat cells</b> and a duct in connective tissue, mean the <b>parotid</b>. It is purely serous, and fat cells among its acini are typical.<br>A. Submandibular would show pale mucous units among the serous acini.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-17.jpg","in":"Two views of one gland. Left: dark serous acini scattered among many clear fat cells, beside dense connective tissue with a duct. Right: closer view, dark granular serous acini, fat cells and a pink striated duct. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":17,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":31,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":28,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 27"},{"id":"F3959-32-1","s":"D10- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. High power shows <b>dark granular serous acini</b>, <b>fat cells</b> and a <b>pink striated duct</b>, with no mucous cells. That is the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Submandibular would also have pale mucous units and serous demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules, with few striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-29.jpg","in":"High power: dark granular serous acini, fat cells and a pink striated duct. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":32,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":29,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 28"},{"id":"F3952-91-1","s":"What gland is this section and what cell predominates?","o":["A. Pancreas, Beta cells","B. Parotid gland, Serous cells","C. Submandibular gland, Mucous cells","D. Submandibular gland, Serous cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland, Serous cells","a":"B","why":"B. Only <b>dark, granular serous acini</b> (A) with round nuclei, an intercalated duct (ID) and a striated duct (SD), and <b>no mucous cells</b>: the <b>parotid</b>, a purely serous gland.<br>A. Beta cells sit in pale pancreatic islets; there is no islet here, and the pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>C. Mucous cells would look pale and foamy; none are present.<br>D. Submandibular would show some pale mucous units among the serous acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-91.jpg","in":"High-power micrograph: densely granular, basophilic serous acini (A) with round nuclei, an intercalated duct (ID) and a striated duct (SD); no mucous cells.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 75 (Parotid)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["SD","striated duct (pale columnar cells around a lumen)"],["A","serous acini (cells packed with secretory granules)"],["ID","intercalated duct (small, low cuboidal)"]],"like":"F3959-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 29"},{"id":"F0052-188-1","s":"Question 4 Identify the organ:","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Stomach","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. The picture is one <b>striated duct</b> (tall pink columnar cells with basal striations) among dark <b>serous acini</b>. Striated ducts plus serous acini mean a serous salivary gland: the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Duodenum shows villi, crypts and Brunner glands.<br>B. The pancreas has serous acini but <b>no striated ducts</b>.<br>D. Stomach shows pits and gastric glands.<br>E. Liver shows hepatocyte plates and sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-188.jpg","in":"Same high-power image: a striated duct (columnar cells with basal striations) surrounded by granular serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":188,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 30"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Submandibular: mixed, mostly serous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-17.jpg","cap":"Submandibular atlas plate: mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G020-71.jpg","cap":"Submandibular at low power: mostly dark serous lobules with pale mucous patches","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 71"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-29-1","s":"D7- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Mostly dark serous acini</b> with a <b>minority of pale mucous tubules</b> and pink striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous; it has no pale mucous tubules.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate, and striated ducts are few.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-16.jpg","in":"Medium power: mostly dark granular serous acini, a minority of pale foamy mucous tubules with dark demilune caps, and several pink striated ducts. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":16,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":29,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":26,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 31"},{"id":"F0052-184-1","s":"Question 2 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Dark serous acini mixed with pale mucous tubules</b>, some capped by serous demilunes, plus several striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, not acini.<br>C. Parotid is purely serous: no pale mucous tubules.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-48.jpg","in":"Dark purple serous acini mixed with pale foamy mucous units capped by dark crescents (demilunes), plus pink striated ducts. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":184,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 32"},{"id":"F3959-30-1","s":"D8- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. Pale mucous acini <b>capped by dark serous demilunes</b>, among many dark serous acini and pink striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous: no mucous acini, so no demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual also has demilunes, but pale mucous tubules dominate, and serous acini and striated ducts are scarce.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-27.jpg","in":"Pale mucous acini with dark demilune caps among dark serous acini and pink striated ducts. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":30,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":27,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 33"},{"id":"F4081-73-1","s":"What is this?","o":["A. Centrilobular necrosis of the liver","B. Pancreas of a type 1 diabetic","C. Cross-section of healthy colonic crypts","D. Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease","E. Healthy submandibular gland","F. Healthy pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Healthy submandibular gland","a":"E","why":"E. Lobules of acini with <b>pale mucous cells capped by darker serous cells (demilunes)</b>, separated by septa with ducts, mean a <b>mixed salivary gland</b>. The only salivary option is a healthy <b>submandibular</b> gland.<br>A. Centrilobular necrosis: dead liver cells around central veins; no acini.<br>B. Diabetic pancreas: serous acini with small islets; no mucous cells.<br>C. Colonic crypts: straight tubes of goblet cells, not lobules of acini.<br>D. Fatty liver: hepatocytes filled with fat droplets.<br>F. Healthy pancreas: serous acini and islets; no mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-73.jpg","in":"Lobulated gland with many pale mucous acini capped by darker serous cells (demilunes), interlobular septa and ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":73,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-17-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 34"},{"id":"G118-17-1","s":"Identify the organ presented in the image below. [The letters printed on the picture (SA, MA, SD, StD, ID, A) label structures; they are not the answer choices.]","o":["A. Parotid gland","B. Sublingual gland","C. Submandibular gland","D. Exocrine pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Submandibular gland.</b> The picture shows two kinds of secretory units side by side. The dark, granular ones are <b>serous acini</b>. The pale, foamy ones are <b>mucous acini</b>, and several wear a dark cap of serous cells. So the gland is mixed. It also has <b>many whole serous acini</b>, and that is the mark of the submandibular gland.</p><p><b>The letters on the picture.</b></p><ul><li><b>SA:</b> serous acinus. Dark, with round nuclei.</li><li><b>MA:</b> mucous acinus. Pale and foamy, with flat nuclei pushed to the base. The stars (*) sit in the lumens, which are wider than serous lumens.</li><li><b>SD:</b> serous demilune, a crescent of dark serous cells capping a mucous acinus.</li><li><b>ID:</b> intercalated duct, the tiny first duct, with low cuboidal cells.</li><li><b>StD:</b> striated duct, the larger pink duct of tall cells at the lower left.</li><li><b>A:</b> an adipocyte (fat cell), the empty white space.</li></ul><p><b>The submandibular checklist.</b></p><ul><li>Mostly dark serous acini, with scattered pale mucous tubules.</li><li>Many of the mucous tubules are capped by serous demilunes.</li><li>Many striated ducts.</li><li>Lobules separated by connective tissue septa.</li></ul><p><b>Quick key for the three major glands.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> all units dark and serous. No pale units.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> most units dark and serous. Some pale mucous units with demilunes.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> most units pale and mucous. The few serous cells are mostly in demilunes, and whole serous acini are rare.</li></ul><p><b>One pale unit settles a lot.</b> The parotid and the exocrine pancreas are both made only of serous acini. So a single pale mucous unit or a single demilune in the field means the gland is neither of those two. The choice is then between submandibular and sublingual, and it depends on which kind of unit is in the majority. A striated duct points the same way, because the pancreas has no striated ducts.</p><p><b>Two more facts about this gland.</b> Its serous cells add lysozyme, an enzyme that attacks bacteria, to the saliva. And salivary stones (sialolithiasis) form most often in the submandibular gland.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-17.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland, H&E, with the textbook's letter labels left on the picture: SA = dark serous acini, MA = pale mucous acini (lumens marked *), SD = serous demilunes, ID = intercalated duct, StD = striated duct, A = fat cell. Serous and mucous acini side by side, with many serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":17,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Sublingual: mostly pale mucous acini","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-73.jpg","cap":"Sublingual gland: field of pale bluish mucous acini, almost no serous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/G020-9.jpg","cap":"Mucous acini (M) with flat basal nuclei beside skeletal muscle (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 9","lg":[["M","mucous cells in tubules (pale, foamy)"],["ID","small intralobular ducts (arrows)"],["SM","skeletal muscle bundles of the tongue"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-2-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Sublingual gland.</b> A pink connective tissue <b>capsule</b> runs across the top. Under it, almost every secretory unit is <b>pale bluish-gray</b>, with small dark nuclei flattened against its edge. These are mucous tubules. Dark, granular serous units are almost absent. A gland that looks pale even at low power, because it is nearly all mucous, is the sublingual gland.</p><p><b>The sublingual checklist.</b></p><ul><li>Mostly pale, bluish-gray mucous tubules with flattened nuclei at the base.</li><li>Serous cells appear mainly as <b>demilunes</b>, dark crescents that cap the ends of mucous tubules. Whole serous acini are rare.</li><li>Few striated ducts.</li><li>Skeletal muscle of the floor of the mouth may lie next to the gland.</li></ul><p><b>Why mucous units are pale.</b> A mucous cell is filled with large granules of mucin, a protein that carries many sugar chains. Mucin stains poorly with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E), the routine stain, and much of it washes out during processing. What remains is a pale, foamy cell with its nucleus squeezed flat at the base. The mucin that is left often takes a faint blue-gray tint, as it does here.</p><p><b>All three major glands by proportion.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> all dark serous acini.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> mostly dark serous acini, with some pale mucous tubules and demilunes.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> mostly pale mucous tubules, with few serous cells.</li></ul><p>The minor salivary glands of the lips, cheeks and palate are also mostly mucous. The von Ebner glands of the tongue are serous.</p><p><b>What its saliva is like.</b> Thick and viscous. Mucins bind water and form a slippery gel that lubricates food and protects the lining of the mouth.</p><p><b>How a gland differs from the solid organs.</b> A salivary gland is built of acini or tubules, each around a lumen, and of ducts. The <b>liver</b> is built of plates of large pink hepatocytes with blood sinusoids between them. The <b>spleen</b> is lymphoid tissue: dark islands of lymphocytes (white pulp) set in blood-filled red pulp. Neither organ has acini.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-47.jpg","in":"Capsule over a gland made almost entirely of pale bluish-grey mucous tubules with very few dark serous cells. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":47,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":2,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":87,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"},{"id":"F3959-26-1","s":"D4- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"C","why":"C. Pale, bluish <b>mucous tubules fill the whole field</b>, with almost no dark serous cells. A gland that is mostly mucous is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mostly serous: dark purple acini would dominate, with scattered pale units.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous: uniformly dark granular acini, often with fat cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-14.jpg","in":"Two views of one gland. Left: low-power strip of a lobulated, pale bluish gland under a thin capsule. Right: closer view, pale foamy mucous tubules almost throughout with a few small ducts. Asked: which salivary gland (A-C).","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":14,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":23,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 35"},{"id":"F3959-27-1","s":"D5- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"C","why":"C. Even at low power the whole strip stains <b>pale bluish</b>, the color of mucin. A gland that is mostly mucous is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mostly serous, so at low power it looks dark purple with pale patches.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous and looks uniformly dark purple.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-24.jpg","in":"Low-power strip of gland that stains pale bluish (mucous) throughout. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":27,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":24,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3959-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 36"},{"id":"F3952-89-1","s":"What gland is this section and what cell predominates?","o":["A. Parotid Gland, Mucous cells","B. Submandibular gland, Serous cells","C. Submandibular gland, mucous cells","D. Sublingual gland, mucous cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland, mucous cells","a":"D","why":"D. The field is almost all <b>pale, foamy mucous acini</b> (M) with flattened basal nuclei, beside skeletal muscle (SM) of the mouth floor. A mostly mucous gland is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, with no mucous cells.<br>B. The submandibular is mostly serous, so dark acini would dominate.<br>C. A mainly mucous field points to the sublingual, not the mostly serous submandibular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-89.jpg","in":"H&E micrograph: field of pale, foamy mucous tubules/acini (labelled M) with an intralobular duct (ID) and bundles of striated (skeletal) muscle (SM) at lower right; no serous acini evident.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)","H16 slide 73 (Mucous salivary (Sublingual) gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":89,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["M","mucous cells (pale, foamy tubules)"],["ID","intralobular ducts (small)"],["SM","skeletal muscle of the tongue"]],"like":"F3959-2-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 37"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Name all three major salivary glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-16.jpg","cap":"Sublingual atlas plate: mostly pale mucous acini with serous demilunes","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G020-17.jpg","cap":"Submandibular atlas plate: mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/F0754-211.jpg","cap":"Parotid serous, sublingual mostly mucous, submandibular mostly serous, side by side","where":"2022 TA review, slide 211","lg":[["thin black lines (parotid)","pointer lines whose labels were cropped off"],["M","mucous cells (pale tubules)"],["A","serous acini"],["S","serous demilunes (arrows)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-41-1","s":"Name the glands [A, B, C].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = Parotid (exclusively serous); B = Sublingual (mixed but predominantly mucous); C = Submandibular (mixed serous and mucous)","a":null,"why":"A = <b>parotid</b>: all dark purple <b>serous</b> acini, with ducts and some fat. B = <b>sublingual</b>: mostly <b>pale mucous</b> tubules, with a few dark serous caps. C = <b>submandibular</b>: <b>mixed</b>, mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous units.<br>Common mix-up: B and C are both mixed. Decide by the main color: mostly pale = sublingual; mostly dark = submandibular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-41.jpg","in":"A: all dark purple serous acini with ducts and fat; B: mostly pale mucous tubules with a few dark cells and a large duct; C: mostly dark serous acini mixed with some pale mucous units and several ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":41,"f":"F4081"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","parotid gland (all serous acini, fat cells)"],["B","sublingual gland (mostly pale mucous tubules)"],["C","submandibular gland (mostly serous, some mucous)"],["lines","pointer lines left from the source figure; their labels were cut off"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 38"},{"id":"F1221-118-1","s":"Quiz yourself (blank slide): which gland/organ is shown, and what are its identifying features?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left to right: parotid (pure serous acini, striated ducts), submandibular (mixed, mostly serous, serous demilunes), sublingual (mostly mucous, serous demilunes)","a":null,"why":"Left to right: <b>parotid</b> (all dark <b>serous</b> acini, with striated ducts); <b>submandibular</b> (<b>mixed</b>: serous acini plus pale mucous tubules capped by <b>serous demilunes</b>); <b>sublingual</b> (mostly <b>pale mucous</b> acini, beside skeletal muscle of the mouth floor).<br>Memory aid: Parotid = Protein (serous); Submandibular = Serous + Mucous; Sublingual = Slimy (mucous).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-118.jpg","in":"Drawing of the three major salivary glands (boxed) beside three unlabelled H&E panels: all-serous dark acini; mixed serous acini and pale mucous tubules; mostly pale mucous acini with skeletal muscle.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":118,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":120,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":122,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":124,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":126,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":129,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":133,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["boxes","the three major salivary glands in the drawing: parotid (in front of ear), submandibular (under the jaw), sublingual (floor of mouth)"],["A","serous acini (dark, granular)"],["ID","intralobular ducts (small)"],["S","serous demilunes (serous caps on mucous tubules)"],["M","mucous cells (pale, foamy tubules)"],["SM","skeletal muscle of the tongue"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 39"},{"id":"F3980-4-1","s":"Correctly match each histological slide with the type of salivary gland it represents (left → right).","o":["A. Submandibular","B. Sublingual","C. Parotid"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = Parotid gland; middle = Sublingual gland; right = Submandibular gland","a":null,"why":"Left = <b>parotid</b>: every acinar cell has a round central nucleus and granular cytoplasm, around a big pink <b>striated duct</b>; no mucous cells. Middle = <b>sublingual</b>: small ducts among <b>pale, frothy mucous cells</b> with flattened nuclei. Right = <b>submandibular</b>: <b>dark purple serous cells next to pale mucous cells</b> (mixed), around a striated duct.<br>Common mix-up: sublingual and submandibular are both mixed; sublingual is mostly pale, submandibular mostly dark.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-4.jpg","in":"Three high-power panels each centered on a duct: left, pink duct among pale cells; middle, small ducts among pale foamy mucous cells; right, striated duct among dark purple serous acini with a few pale mucous cells.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":4,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 40"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":34,"label":"Islet of Langerhans: pale endocrine cluster","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-24.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: pale islet cells among dark serous acini; exocrine plus endocrine","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G020-77.jpg","cap":"Low-power pancreas: pale round islets scattered among packed acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-14-1","s":"Which of the following structures is outlined in the slide below and what is its function","o":["A. Pancreatic acinus — secretes digestive enzymes into the duodenum","B. Islet of Langerhans — secretes hormones that regulate blood glucose","C. Centroacinar duct cell — initiates bicarbonate-rich fluid secretion","D. Intercalated duct — transports zymogen-rich secretions to intralobular ducts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Islet of Langerhans — secretes hormones that regulate blood glucose","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Islet of Langerhans — secretes hormones that regulate blood glucose.</b> The dashed circle encloses a round cluster that is clearly <b>paler</b> than the tissue around it. Its cells are small, with round nuclei, and they lie in loose cords with thin red capillaries between them. There is no lumen and no duct. Outside the circle the cells are darker and are arranged as acini. A pale, round island of cords among dark acini is an islet of Langerhans, and its hormones control blood glucose.</p><p><b>Two glands in one organ.</b></p><ul><li><b>Exocrine pancreas:</b> most of the organ. Serous acini make digestive enzymes. Ducts carry the enzymes, with a bicarbonate-rich fluid, to the duodenum.</li><li><b>Endocrine pancreas:</b> the islets of Langerhans, small clusters of hormone-making cells scattered among the acini.</li></ul><p><b>How an islet is built.</b> The cells are arranged in cords threaded with many capillaries. A fine capsule of reticular fibers wraps the cluster. An islet has <b>no ducts</b>. Its hormones pass straight into the capillaries, which is what endocrine means. The cells are pale because they lack the heavy rough endoplasmic reticulum and the large enzyme granules that make acinar cells dark.</p><p><b>The islet cells and their hormones.</b></p><ul><li><b>Beta cells:</b> insulin, which lowers blood glucose. They are the most numerous.</li><li><b>Alpha cells:</b> glucagon, which raises blood glucose.</li><li><b>Delta cells:</b> somatostatin, which holds back the release of the other two hormones.</li></ul><p>The three types look alike with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E), the routine stain. Special stains are needed to tell them apart.</p><p><b>How the pancreas is organized.</b> A thin capsule covers it, and septa divide it into lobules. Very little connective tissue lies between the acini, only reticular fibers and capillaries. That is why the acini look so tightly packed.</p><p><b>Recognizing the organ and its parts.</b> A field of tightly packed, dark serous acini with pale round islands scattered through it is pancreas. No salivary gland has islets. Size and shape separate the pale structures of the pancreas. An islet is a solid cluster of many cells. A centroacinar cell is a single pale nucleus in the middle of an acinus. An intercalated duct is a tiny tube of flat cells around a lumen.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-14.jpg","in":"Pancreas H&E: blue dashed circle around a pale-staining cluster of cords of cells with capillaries, surrounded by darker purple/pink serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":14,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["dashed line","islet of Langerhans (pale endocrine cell clump among dark acini)"]],"n":"51"},{"id":"F4081-71-1","s":"Label the cells with endocrine vs exocrine functions: [A, B]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = endocrine (islet of Langerhans; islet cells secrete hormones directly into the blood); B = exocrine (acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes into ducts)","a":null,"why":"A = <b>endocrine</b>: it sits in a <b>pale round cluster</b> of cells, an <b>islet of Langerhans</b>, which releases hormones (insulin, glucagon) straight into capillaries. B = <b>exocrine</b>: a <b>dark acinar cell with pink apical zymogen granules</b>; it secretes digestive enzymes into ducts.<br>Common mix-up: the pale cells make hormones; the dark, granular cells make enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-71.jpg","in":"Pancreas: A sits in a pale round cluster of cells (islet); B sits among dark purple acini with pink apical zymogen granules.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","islet of Langerhans: endocrine cells (pale clump; hormones into blood)"],["B","acinar cells: exocrine (dark base, pink zymogen granules; enzymes into ducts)"]],"like":"F3980-14-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 41"},{"id":"F1221-154-2","s":"Which of the following describes cells of the endocrine pancreas?","o":["A. Acinar cells that feed into a ductal system","B. Serous cells that surround paler central cells like a bullseye on H&E","C. Clusters of paler cells on H&E that have blood supply but no direct ductal system access","D. Cells that appear to have stellate form and exist in the sinusoidal space"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Clusters of paler cells on H&E that have blood supply but no direct ductal system access","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Islets of Langerhans</b> are <b>clusters of paler cells</b> on hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) stain, threaded with capillaries. They <b>release hormones into the blood</b>, so they need no duct.<br>A. Acinar cells that feed a duct system are the exocrine pancreas.<br>B. Serous cells around a pale central (centroacinar) cell form an exocrine acinus.<br>D. Star-shaped cells in the sinusoids are Kupffer cells of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":154,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3980-14-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 42"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":34,"label":"Identify pancreas: packed acini with islets","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-77.jpg","cap":"Low-power pancreas: pale round islets scattered among packed acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77"},{"src":"sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: acini, islets, septa with interlobular duct, centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-6-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. Densely packed serous acini with <b>pale, round clusters of cells (islets of Langerhans)</b> mean <b>pancreas</b>. No other organ has islets.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, with no acini.<br>C. Parotid has serous acini but no islets; look for fat cells and striated ducts.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-51.jpg","in":"Densely packed serous acini with pale, rounded clusters of lightly stained cells (islets) at the left and centre. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":6,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":8,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":190,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 43"},{"id":"F0052-194-1","s":"Question 7 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. A uniform field of <b>tightly packed serous acini</b> with <b>pink apical zymogen granules</b>, thin septa and a duct in connective tissue, but <b>no fat cells, mucous cells or striated ducts</b>: the <b>pancreas</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue without acini.<br>C. Parotid would show fat cells and pink striated ducts among its acini.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-53.jpg","in":"Uniform field of serous acini with basophilic bases and pink apical granules; a pale connective-tissue septum with a small duct at the lower right; no fat cells or mucous cells. Asked: which organ.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":194,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 44"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":35,"label":"Acinar cell: apical zymogen granules face the lumen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Acinar cell electron micrograph and diagram: apical zymogen granules around a small lumen","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27","lg":[["L","lumen of the acinus"],["S","secretory (zymogen) granules"],["G","Golgi apparatus"],["C","condensing vacuoles"],["N","nucleus (round, basal)"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-22.jpg","cap":"Labelled acinar cell electron micrograph: apical zymogen granules, basal rough endoplasmic reticulum","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 22","lg":[["S","secretory (zymogen) granules"],["G","Golgi apparatus"],["C","condensing vacuoles"],["N","nucleus (round, basal)"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-16-1","s":"A histologic section shows pyramidal cells arranged around a small central lumen with apical zymogen granules. Which statement best explains how this structure supports their function?","o":["A. The pyramidal orientation directs enzyme secretion into the central lumen for delivery to ducts","B. The broad basal surfaces maximize nutrient absorption for enzyme modification","C. The narrow lumen allows these cells to regulate pressure and alter secretions","D. The radial pattern enables both endocrine and exocrine release from the same unit"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The pyramidal orientation directs enzyme secretion into the central lumen for delivery to ducts","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. The pyramidal orientation directs enzyme secretion into the central lumen for delivery to ducts.</b> Pyramidal cells around a small lumen with apical zymogen granules form a <b>pancreatic acinus</b>. Each cell is a wedge. Its broad base faces outward, toward the blood supply. Its narrow top faces the lumen. The granules are stored at the top, so when they are released the enzymes can go only one way: <b>into the lumen</b>, and from there into the ducts.</p><p><b>The cell is laid out like an assembly line.</b></p><ol><li><b>Base.</b> The round nucleus and a large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum, where the enzymes are made. Its ribosomes take up the purple dye, so the base is basophilic.</li><li><b>Above the nucleus.</b> The Golgi apparatus, which packs the new proteins into granules.</li><li><b>Top (apex).</b> Many <b>zymogen granules</b>. They take up the pink dye, so the top is eosinophilic.</li><li><b>Lumen.</b> The granules fuse with the top membrane and empty into the small central lumen.</li></ol><p>This one-way arrangement is called polarity.</p><p><b>What a zymogen is.</b> A zymogen is an enzyme stored in an inactive form. The pancreas makes its protein-digesting enzymes this way, for example trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen. They are switched on only after they reach the duodenum, which keeps them from digesting the gland that makes them. The granules also carry amylase, lipase and nucleases.</p><p><b>What makes the cell release.</b> When fat and protein reach the duodenum, cells in its lining release the hormone cholecystokinin (CCK). CCK makes the acinar cells empty their granules.</p><p><b>Where the secretion goes next.</b> In the center of the acinus are pale <b>centroacinar cells</b>, the first cells of the duct system. From them the enzymes pass through intercalated ducts, intralobular ducts and interlobular ducts to the main pancreatic duct and the duodenum.</p><p><b>The acinus is purely exocrine.</b> Its product leaves through ducts. The hormones of the pancreas, insulin and glucagon, come from separate cell clusters called the islets of Langerhans. Islets have no ducts and release into the blood.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":16,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1,"n":"52"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":35,"label":"Centroacinar cell: pale nucleus in acinus center","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreas micrograph with arrow on centroacinar nucleus; acinus diagram with centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["arrow without a letter","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus inside the acinus)"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-24.jpg","cap":"Acinus diagram and micrograph: centroacinar cells begin the intercalated duct inside the acinus","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 24","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini (serous)"],["F","fibroblast in the thin connective tissue (letter shows backwards: micrograph is mirror-flipped)"],["circle","one acinus cut through its centre"],["arrow","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus in the acinus centre, start of the intercalated duct)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-48-1","s":"Which cells are located in the center of pancreatic acini and are characteristic for having no zymogen granules, unlike the surrounding pyramidal cells?","o":["A. Pyramidal cells","B. Zymogen cells","C. Basal rough ER cells","D. Golgi cells","E. Centroacinar cells"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells","a":"E","why":"E. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> sit in the <b>center of pancreatic acini</b>. They are pale and have no zymogen granules because they are the first cells of the <b>intercalated duct</b>, tucked into the acinus.<br>A. Pyramidal cells are the acinar cells, which do have granules.<br>B. Zymogen cells are the granule-filled acinar cells.<br>C. Basal rough endoplasmic reticulum is part of the acinar cell, not a separate cell.<br>D. The Golgi is an organelle, not a cell type.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":48,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 45"},{"id":"F3959-10-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar cell","B. Serous cell","C. Striated duct","D. Intercalated duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cell","a":"A","why":"A. The arrow is on a <b>small pale nucleus in the center of an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>, the first cell of the intercalated duct. The nearby islet confirms pancreas.<br>B. Serous (acinar) cells are the dark pyramidal cells with pink apical granules around the lumen.<br>C. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>D. The intercalated duct is the tube leaving the acinus; inside the acinus its cells are called centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G116-55.jpg","in":"Pancreas with an islet at the top; white arrow on a small pale nucleus sitting in the centre of an acinus. Asked: the arrowed structure.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":10,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":10,"f":"G022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 46"},{"id":"F3952-95-1","s":"What gland is this section and what is the arrow pointing at?","o":["A. Endocrine pancreas, Striated ducts","B. Exocrine Pancreas, centroacinar cells","C. Endocrine pancreas, centroacinar cells","D. Submandibular gland, serous acini"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine Pancreas, centroacinar cells","a":"B","why":"B. Small acini (A) of pyramidal zymogen cells, and the arrow on a <b>pale, flattened nucleus inside an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>, found only in the <b>exocrine pancreas</b>.<br>A. A field of acini is exocrine, and the pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>C. Centroacinar cells belong to the exocrine duct system, not the endocrine islets.<br>D. Submandibular serous acini have no centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-95.jpg","in":"H&E micrograph of exocrine pancreas: small acini (A) of pyramidal zymogenic cells with a fibroblast (F); the arrow points to a pale, flattened nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell).","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","serous acini"],["F","fibroblast (thin dark nucleus in connective tissue)"],["arrow","centroacinar cells (pale nuclei inside the acinus)"]],"like":"G024-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 47"},{"id":"F0722-170-1","s":"Name the structure the arrow is pointing to. Also, where is this found? [Secondary Q: what stimulates the intercalated duct?]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Centroacinar cell (pancreas); centroacinar cells line the initial segment of the intercalated duct and secrete bicarbonate; secretin stimulates the intercalated duct","a":null,"why":"<b>Centroacinar cell</b>, found in the <b>pancreas</b>: the pale nucleus in the center of the acinus. Centroacinar cells line the <b>first part of the intercalated duct</b> and secrete <b>bicarbonate</b>. The intercalated duct is stimulated by <b>secretin</b>.<br>Common mix-up: cholecystokinin (CCK) drives the acinar cells to release enzymes; secretin drives the ducts to release bicarbonate fluid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-170.jpg","in":"Pancreatic acini (A); arrow on a small pale nucleus (circled) in the center of an acinus; F = fibroblast.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","pancreatic acini"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["circle with arrow","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus in the center of an acinus)"]],"like":"G024-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 48"},{"id":"G024-21-1","s":"A biopsy is performed and the specimen below is obtained. Which of the following is true of the organ from which the specimen was taken?","o":["A. The apical cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent RER and Golgi","B. The basal cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent zymogen granules","C. Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","D. Serous demilunes are usually present","E. Striated ducts are present"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts.</b> First name the organ. The picture shows closely packed serous acini. The cells have dark round nuclei near the base and bright pink, granular tops. There are no pale mucous cells and no large pink ducts. In the middle of some acini sit <b>small pale nuclei</b>. They belong to centroacinar cells, which are found only in the <b>pancreas</b>. In the pancreas the centroacinar cells are the first cells of the intercalated duct, so the two are continuous.</p><p><b>Centroacinar cells.</b></p><ul><li>They are pale, flat cells with pale nuclei in the center of the acinus. The name says so: \"centro-acinar\".</li><li>In the pancreas the intercalated duct begins <b>inside</b> the acinus, and the centroacinar cells are its first stretch. In a salivary gland the intercalated duct begins outside the acinus.</li><li>They have no zymogen granules. Their pale staining is normal.</li><li>With the intercalated duct cells, they secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid when the hormone secretin stimulates them.</li></ul><p><b>The layout of the acinar cell.</b></p><ul><li><b>Basal cytoplasm:</b> the round nucleus and abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, where the enzymes are made. This makes the base purple.</li><li><b>Just above the nucleus:</b> the Golgi apparatus, which packs the enzymes into granules.</li><li><b>Apical cytoplasm:</b> the zymogen granules, which hold inactive enzymes. This makes the top pink.</li></ul><p><b>The duct path of the pancreas.</b></p><ol><li>Centroacinar cells.</li><li>Intercalated ducts, lined by simple squamous to low cuboidal cells.</li><li>Intralobular ducts.</li><li>Interlobular ducts, lined by columnar cells.</li><li>Main pancreatic duct. It joins the common bile duct and opens into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla.</li></ol><p>The pancreas has <b>no striated ducts</b> anywhere along this path.</p><p><b>Features that belong to salivary glands.</b> Serous demilunes are dark caps of serous cells on pale mucous tubules. They occur only in the mixed glands, the submandibular and the sublingual. Striated ducts and myoepithelial cells (contractile cells around the acini) also belong to the salivary glands. The marks of the pancreas are different: islets of Langerhans, which are pale clusters of hormone-making cells, and centroacinar cells.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G115-17.jpg","in":"Exocrine pancreas, H&E, high power: serous acinar cells with dark basal nuclei and purple bases, pink granular (zymogen) tops, and small pale nuclei in the centres of acini (centroacinar cells); no striated ducts, no demilunes. Same micrograph as G024 slide 21.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":17,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":21,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":27,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":21,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":23,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"53"},{"id":"G118-13-1","s":"Which description best applies to the cell at the tip of the arrow labeled A?","o":["A. Is part of an intercalated duct","B. Only found in the parotid","C. Secretes digestive enzymes","D. Is abnormally pale-staining"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Is part of an intercalated duct","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Is part of an intercalated duct.</b> The tip of arrow A touches a small, <b>pale oval nucleus in the middle of an acinus</b>. All around it are acinar cells filled with pink granules. A pale cell in the center of a pancreatic acinus is a <b>centroacinar cell</b>. Centroacinar cells are the first cells of the intercalated duct, which in the pancreas begins inside the acinus. So this cell is part of an intercalated duct.</p><p><b>Reading the picture.</b> The tissue is exocrine pancreas. Every unit is a serous acinus, and the bracket at the upper left marks one of them. The pink dots that fill the cells are zymogen granules, the stored digestive enzymes. The nuclei of the acinar cells are darker and sit toward the outer edge of each acinus. The nucleus at A is paler and lies in the center, with almost no visible cytoplasm around it.</p><p><b>Why the cell is pale.</b> It is a duct cell, and it makes no enzymes. It has no zymogen granules and little rough endoplasmic reticulum (the protein-making organelle), so it takes up little stain. This pale look is its normal appearance.</p><p><b>What centroacinar cells do.</b> With the intercalated duct cells, they secrete a watery, <b>bicarbonate-rich fluid</b>. The signal is the hormone secretin, which the duodenum releases when acid arrives from the stomach. The alkaline fluid carries the enzymes down the ducts and neutralizes the acid in the duodenum. The enzymes themselves come from the acinar cells, which respond to another hormone, cholecystokinin (CCK).</p><p><b>Only the pancreas has them.</b> In the salivary glands, the parotid included, the intercalated duct starts outside the acinus. So no duct cell lies in the center of a salivary acinus. A pale nucleus in the middle of a serous acinus identifies the organ as pancreas.</p><p><b>From this cell to the duodenum.</b></p><ol><li>Centroacinar cells, inside the acinus.</li><li>Intercalated duct, a tiny tube of flat to low cuboidal cells.</li><li>Intralobular duct.</li><li>Interlobular duct, in the connective tissue septa, lined by columnar cells.</li><li>Main pancreatic duct, which empties into the duodenum.</li></ol><p><b>Four features that separate pancreas from parotid.</b> The pancreas has islets of Langerhans (pale clusters of hormone-making cells) and centroacinar cells. The parotid has striated ducts and myoepithelial cells. Each organ lacks the other's pair.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-13.jpg","in":"Exocrine pancreas, H&E: serous acini full of pink zymogen granules (a bracket marks one acinus); the arrow labeled A points to a small pale nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell); three more unlabeled arrows at the right edge point to pale strips between acini.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":13,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"54"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Centroacinar cells mean pancreas","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreas micrograph with arrow on centroacinar nucleus; acinus diagram with centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["arrow without a letter","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus inside the acinus)"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Compare table: islets, centroacinar cells in pancreas; myoepithelial cells, striated ducts in salivary","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/F0754-215.jpg","cap":"Parotid striated duct with no centroacinar cells versus pancreas with centroacinar cells","where":"2022 TA review, slide 215","lg":[["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["left yellow arrow","striated duct (parotid)"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["right yellow arrow","centroacinar cell (pancreas)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-93-1","s":"What cell is the distinguishing feature of the pancreas in relation to serous acini?","o":["A. Striated ducts","B. Myoepithelial cells","C. Intercalated duts","D. Centroacinar cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells","a":"D","why":"D. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> are pale cells inside the center of the acinus. Only the <b>pancreas</b> has them, so they tell it apart from a serous salivary gland like the parotid.<br>A. Striated ducts are in salivary glands, not the pancreas.<br>B. Myoepithelial cells wrap salivary acini; the pancreas has none.<br>C. Intercalated ducts are in both, so they do not tell them apart.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":93,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":242,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 49"},{"id":"F0722-167-1","s":"Where can this histological section be found??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pancreas","a":null,"why":"<b>Pancreas</b>. The acini are <b>pyramidal serous cells</b> with pink apical zymogen granules, and the arrows point to <b>pale centroacinar cells in the center of the acini</b>. Only the pancreas has centroacinar cells.<br>Common mix-up: the parotid also has all-serous acini, but it has striated ducts and fat cells and no centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-167.jpg","in":"Serous acini of pyramidal cells with basal basophilia and apical eosinophilic zymogen; arrows from a blacked-out label point to acini.","tg":["H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":167,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","centroacinar cells (small pale nuclei in the centers of acini)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 50"},{"id":"F0754-213-1","s":"Where was this slide taken from?","o":["A. Parotid Gland","B. Liver","C. Sublingual Gland","D. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"D","why":"D. Pyramidal serous acinar cells with pink apical zymogen granules, and the arrow on a <b>pale nucleus in the center of an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>. These are found only in the <b>pancreas</b>.<br>A. The parotid has serous acini too, but no centroacinar cells; it has striated ducts and fat.<br>B. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-213.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of serous acini (A) of pyramidal zymogenic cells with a fibroblast (F); the arrow points to a pale flattened nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell); no striated ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":213,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":39,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":41,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":45,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":47,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":49,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["A","pancreatic acinus"],["upward arrow","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus in the center of an acinus)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 51"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Pancreas vs parotid: islets, centroacinar cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Compare table: islets, centroacinar cells in pancreas; myoepithelial cells, striated ducts in salivary","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/F0754-215.jpg","cap":"Parotid striated duct with no centroacinar cells versus pancreas with centroacinar cells","where":"2022 TA review, slide 215","lg":[["SD","striated duct"],["A","serous acini"],["left yellow arrow","striated duct (parotid)"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["right yellow arrow","centroacinar cell (pancreas)"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-155-1","s":"Which option(s) lack striated ducts?","o":["A. Parotid","B. Sublingual","C. Submandibular","D. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Pancreas.</b> A striated duct is a salivary gland structure. All three major salivary glands have striated ducts. The <b>pancreas has none</b>. Its acini drain through intercalated ducts into plain intralobular ducts lined by pale cells.</p><p><b>What a striated duct is.</b> It is the second duct inside a salivary lobule, after the intercalated duct. It is lined by tall, pink columnar cells with round central nuclei. The base of each cell is deeply folded and holds rows of mitochondria, which show as fine stripes. The cells use this machinery to reabsorb sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) and chloride (Cl<sup>−</sup>) from the saliva and to add potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) and bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>). The result is saliva that is less salty than plasma.</p><p><b>Striated ducts, gland by gland.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> many, and long. The pink ducts stand out among the purple serous acini.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> many.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> present, but few.</li><li><b>Pancreas:</b> none.</li></ul><p><b>The duct path of the pancreas.</b></p><ol><li>Centroacinar cells: pale cells in the center of the acinus that begin the duct.</li><li>Intercalated ducts.</li><li>Intralobular ducts.</li><li>Interlobular ducts.</li><li>Main pancreatic duct, to the duodenum.</li></ol><p>The small ducts of the pancreas have their own job. They add an alkaline, bicarbonate-rich fluid when the hormone secretin stimulates them.</p><p><b>Pancreas or parotid: the four features.</b> Both organs are made of dark serous acini full of zymogen granules (stored enzymes), so they are easy to confuse.</p><ul><li><b>Islets of Langerhans</b> (pale clusters of hormone-making cells): pancreas yes, parotid no.</li><li><b>Centroacinar cells:</b> pancreas yes, parotid no.</li><li><b>Myoepithelial cells</b> (contractile cells around the acini): parotid yes, pancreas no.</li><li><b>Striated ducts:</b> parotid yes, pancreas no.</li></ul><p><b>Extra clues.</b> The parotid has many fat cells among its acini, and the pancreas has few. When no islet is in the field, look at the ducts. A wide duct of tall, bright pink cells among the acini means a salivary gland. Ducts lined by pale cells, together with pale nuclei inside the acini, mean pancreas.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":155,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1,"n":"55"},{"id":"F3980-12-1","s":"A histologic section shows clusters of serous acinar cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm, apical zymogen granules, and well-developed intercalated ducts. No centroacinar cells are visible within the acini. [Which organ?]","o":["A. Pancreas","B. Parotid gland","C. Submandibular gland","D. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. All-serous acini with zymogen granules and well-developed intercalated ducts, but <b>no centroacinar cells</b>: a purely serous salivary gland, the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. The pancreas would show centroacinar cells and islets.<br>C. Submandibular is mixed: pale mucous units and demilunes.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly mucous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":12,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0722-155-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 52"},{"id":"F4081-67-1","s":"Identify the pancreas vs parotid gland:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = pancreas (islet of Langerhans = pale round clump among acini); right = parotid gland (adipocytes and many ducts)","a":null,"why":"Left = <b>pancreas</b>: uniform acini with a <b>pale round islet of Langerhans</b>, which only the pancreas has. Right = <b>parotid</b>: dark serous acini with many <b>fat cells (adipocytes)</b> and many ducts.<br>Common mix-up: both are all-serous glands. Look for islets and centroacinar cells (pancreas) versus fat cells and striated ducts (parotid).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-67.jpg","in":"Left: uniform purple-pink acini with one pale round cluster of cells (islet). Right: dark serous acini with scattered white fat cells and many ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":67,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0722-155-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 53"},{"id":"G118-14-1","s":"Identify the organ presented in the image below.","o":["A. Sublingual gland","B. Submandibular gland","C. Exocrine pancreas","D. Parotid gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine pancreas","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Exocrine pancreas.</b> Every secretory unit in the picture is a serous acinus packed with dark pink granules. There are no pale mucous cells, so the gland is purely serous. The acini are pressed tightly together, with no fat cells between them. Now look at the ducts. The thin pale duct outlined at label A, an <b>intercalated duct</b>, starts right among the acinar cells. The larger ducts at label B are lined by <b>pale cells</b>, paler than the acini around them. No duct here has the bright pink, striped cells of a striated duct. A purely serous gland without striated ducts is the exocrine pancreas.</p><p><b>The two purely serous organs.</b> The exocrine pancreas and the parotid gland both consist of dark serous acini full of zymogen granules (stored enzymes). Four features separate them.</p><ul><li><b>Islets of Langerhans:</b> pale round clusters of hormone-making cells. Pancreas only.</li><li><b>Centroacinar cells:</b> pale duct cells in the center of the acinus. Pancreas only.</li><li><b>Striated ducts:</b> wide ducts of tall, bright pink cells with basal stripes. Parotid only.</li><li><b>Myoepithelial cells:</b> flat contractile cells wrapped around the acini. Parotid only.</li></ul><p><b>When no islet is in the field.</b> This picture has no islet, so the ducts and the spaces between the acini decide.</p><ul><li>In the pancreas the intercalated duct begins <b>inside</b> the acinus, as centroacinar cells. In a salivary gland it begins outside the acinus.</li><li>In the pancreas the ducts among the acini are lined by pale cells. In a salivary gland a wide duct of tall, bright pink cells stands out among the acini.</li><li>The parotid has many fat cells, seen as empty white circles. The pancreas has few.</li><li>The pancreas has very little connective tissue between its acini, so they look tightly packed.</li></ul><p><b>The ducts of this picture, in order.</b> Enzymes leave the acinus past the centroacinar cells and enter the intercalated duct (label A). Intercalated ducts join to form the larger intralobular ducts (label B). These lead to interlobular ducts in the septa, then to the main pancreatic duct and the duodenum.</p><p><b>The mixed salivary glands look different again.</b> The submandibular gland has pale mucous tubules with dark serous caps (demilunes) scattered among its serous acini. The sublingual gland is mostly pale mucous tubules. A field with no pale units at all is either pancreas or parotid.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-14.jpg","in":"Gland made only of serous acini packed with dark pink granules; no mucous acini. Label A (its name is covered) points to a thin pale duct, outlined in black, that leads out of an acinus (intercalated duct); label B (name covered) points to two larger pale ducts with a small lumen.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":14,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Centroacinar cells → pancreas; striated duct → salivary","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Table: pancreas has islets, centroacinar cells; serous salivary has striated ducts, myoepithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Diagram: pale centroacinar cells inside the acinus, continuing into the terminal duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27","lg":[["L","lumen of the acinus"],["S","secretory (zymogen) granules"],["G","Golgi apparatus"],["C","condensing vacuoles"],["N","nucleus (round, basal)"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct cell: basal infoldings packed with mitochondria to reabsorb sodium","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13","lg":[["a","light micrograph, hematoxylin and eosin"],["SD","striated duct (faint basal striations)"],["b","scanning electron micrograph, duct cells seen from the side"],["L","lumen"],["B","basal ends with interlocking membrane folds"],["c","scanning electron micrograph of the cell bases (basal lamina removed)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-138-1","s":"Who am I? (two high-power micrographs)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: exocrine pancreas – pale centroacinar cells in the center of darkly stained serous acini; right: parotid – striated duct (poorly staining basal infoldings full of mitochondria to absorb Na+)","a":null,"why":"<b>Left = exocrine pancreas</b>: small pale <b>centroacinar cells</b> sit in the center of dark serous acini. They are the start of the intercalated duct pushed into the acinus, and only the pancreas has them.<br><b>Right = serous salivary gland (parotid)</b>: a <b>striated duct</b> of tall columnar cells with pale basal striations (folded membrane packed with mitochondria to reabsorb sodium).<br>Mix-up: pancreas has islets and centroacinar cells; salivary glands have striated ducts and myoepithelial cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-138.jpg","in":"Two H&E panels: (left) granular serous acini, one with small pale nuclei at its centre; (right) a duct of tall columnar cells with radially striated pale basal cytoplasm around a small lumen, surrounded by dark serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":138,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 54"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Cholecystokinin → acinar zymogens; secretin → duct bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Acinar cell with apical zymogen granules; centroacinar cells start the duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27","lg":[["L","lumen of the acinus"],["S","secretory (zymogen) granules"],["G","Golgi apparatus"],["C","condensing vacuoles"],["N","nucleus (round, basal)"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-32.jpg","cap":"Cholecystokinin drives acinar enzymes; secretin drives duct water and bicarbonate","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-19-1","s":"This cell is triggered to release its secretory granules by CCK from duodenal cells when food enters that region of the stomach. What are in the secretory granule?","o":["A. Vit A","B. Mucin","C. Zymogens","D. Bile"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zymogens","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zymogens.</b> CCK (cholecystokinin) from duodenal enteroendocrine cells makes <b>pancreatic acinar cells</b> release their apical <b>zymogen granules</b>: inactive proteases plus amylase, lipase and nucleases. Enteropeptidase activates them in the duodenum.<br>A. Vitamin A: stored by Ito (hepatic stellate) cells of the liver.<br>B. Mucin: made by mucous cells, not serous pancreatic acini.<br>D. Bile: made by hepatocytes; CCK only makes the gallbladder contract to release it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Picture of the cell (pancreatic acinar cell) not needed; stem names the CCK trigger. [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":19,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"like":"F4081-51-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 55"},{"id":"F4081-51-1","s":"Which function is carried out by the intercalated ducts of the exocrine pancreas when stimulated by secretin?","o":["A. Secretion of protease, amylase, lipase, and nucleases","B. Activation of zymogens by enteropeptidase in the duodenum","C. Production of trypsin inhibitor for protection","D. Secretion of alkaline fluid to neutralize the gastric content","E. Secretion of enzymes in inactive form"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of alkaline fluid to neutralize the gastric content","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Secretion of alkaline fluid to neutralize the gastric content.</b> Secretin is the hormone released when <b>acid</b> from the stomach enters the duodenum. Its target is the duct system of the pancreas: the <b>intercalated ducts</b> and the centroacinar cells at their start. They answer with a watery fluid rich in bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>). Bicarbonate is a base, so the fluid is alkaline and neutralizes the acid.</p><p><b>Two hormones, two cell types, two products.</b></p><ul><li><b>Secretin</b> acts on the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells. It is released when acid arrives in the duodenum. The cells secrete an alkaline, bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes the acid chyme, the acidic mix of food and gastric juice.</li><li><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> acts on the acinar cells. Enteroendocrine (hormone-making) cells of the duodenum release it when fat and protein arrive. The acinar cells then release their enzymes.</li></ul><p>Each hormone answers the problem that triggered it. Acid calls for a base. Fat and protein call for enzymes.</p><p><b>What the acinar cells secrete.</b> Proteases (protein-digesting enzymes) such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen, plus amylase, lipase and nucleases. The proteases are stored and released as <b>zymogens</b>, which are inactive forms. The acinar cells also make trypsin inhibitor, which protects the gland.</p><p><b>Where the enzymes are switched on.</b> Activation happens in the duodenum, not in the pancreas. Enteropeptidase (also called enterokinase) is an enzyme on the brush border of the duodenal lining. It converts trypsinogen into trypsin. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens.</p><p><b>Why the alkaline fluid matters.</b></p><ul><li>It neutralizes the acid that enters the duodenum and so protects the duodenal lining.</li><li>It keeps the zymogens inactive on their way down the ducts.</li><li>It carries the enzymes along the ducts.</li></ul><p><b>The cells that make it.</b> Centroacinar cells are pale, flat cells in the center of the acinus. They are the first cells of the intercalated duct, which is lined by simple squamous to low cuboidal cells. Neither cell has zymogen granules. They are built to move fluid and ions, and the acinar cells are built to make protein.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":51,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1,"n":"57"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Pancreas self-protection: trypsin inhibitor, alkaline ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-32.jpg","cap":"Lecture list: activation only in duodenum, trypsin inhibitor, alkaline duct pH","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G021-32.jpg","cap":"Protection: inactive zymogens, trypsin inhibitor in granules, high pH in ducts","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-21-1","s":"How can the Pancreas protect itself from autodigestion?","o":["A. Secretion of Trypsin Inhibitor","B. Acidifying pH in the pancreatic duct system","C. Activating the zymogens immediately once released from acinar cells and still in the pancreas","D. Releasing CCK when stimulated"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of Trypsin Inhibitor","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Secretion of Trypsin Inhibitor.</b> The pancreas makes enzymes strong enough to digest protein, and the gland itself is made of protein. The main danger is <b>trypsin</b>, because trypsin switches on all the other enzymes. So the acinar cells pack a <b>trypsin inhibitor</b> together with the enzymes. If any trypsin becomes active too early, inside the gland, the inhibitor blocks it.</p><p><b>The four protections.</b></p><ul><li><b>Inactive storage.</b> The proteases (protein-digesting enzymes) are made and stored as zymogens, which are inactive forms: trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen and others.</li><li><b>Activation only in the duodenum.</b> The enzyme that starts activation is in the duodenum, not in the pancreas.</li><li><b>Trypsin inhibitor.</b> It travels with the zymogens and blocks any trypsin that forms early.</li><li><b>Alkaline duct fluid.</b> The duct cells add bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>), a base. The alkaline fluid keeps the zymogens inactive on their way out.</li></ul><p><b>How activation normally happens.</b></p><ol><li>The zymogens flow down the pancreatic duct into the duodenum.</li><li><b>Enteropeptidase</b> (also called enterokinase), an enzyme on the brush border of the duodenal lining, converts trypsinogen into trypsin.</li><li>Trypsin then activates the other zymogens, and digestion begins.</li></ol><p><b>What drives the secretion.</b> Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a hormone that the duodenum releases when fat and protein arrive. It makes the acinar cells release their enzymes. Secretin is released when acid arrives. It makes the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells secrete the alkaline, bicarbonate-rich fluid. These hormones turn secretion on. The four protections keep it safe.</p><p><b>When protection fails: pancreatitis.</b></p><ul><li><b>Acute pancreatitis:</b> the enzymes become active inside the gland and digest it. The main causes are gallstones first, then alcohol.</li><li><b>Chronic pancreatitis:</b> repeated injury leads to scarring (fibrosis) and loss of acini, and later of the islets, the hormone-making cell clusters. The main cause is long-term alcohol use. Cystic fibrosis is another cause.</li></ul><p><b>Under the microscope.</b> The zymogen granules are the pink granules in the top of each acinar cell. The trypsin inhibitor is packed in with them, so it cannot be seen as a separate structure.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 32 (Protective factors)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":21,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1,"n":"58"},{"id":"F3952-97-1","s":"What is one way the pancreas protects itself against autodigestion?","o":["A. Trypsin inhibitor secreted by endocrine pancreas to keep trypsinogen inactive.","B. Maintain a low pH in the intercalated ducts to keep the zymogens inactive.","C. HCO3- secretion by the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells to keep the zymogens inactivated.","D. Allowing the activation of zymogens in the pancreas intercalated ducts"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"HCO3- secretion by the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells to keep the zymogens inactivated.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells secrete <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b>, keeping the duct system <b>alkaline</b>, which keeps zymogens inactive until the duodenum.<br>A. Trypsin inhibitor is real protection, but it comes from the <b>exocrine acinar cells</b>, not the endocrine islets.<br>B. The ducts are alkaline, not low pH.<br>D. Zymogens must <b>not</b> activate in the ducts; enteropeptidase activates them only in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 32 (Protective factors)","H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":97,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3949-21-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 56"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Alcohol → acute and chronic pancreatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-33.jpg","cap":"Lecture's pancreas clinical correlates: acute and chronic pancreatitis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-172-1","s":"Does Alcoholism lead to acute or chronic pancreatitis?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Both: alcohol is a major cause of acute pancreatitis (second only to gallstones) and the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults.","a":null,"why":"<b>Both.</b> Alcohol causes <b>acute</b> pancreatitis (second most common cause after gallstones) and, after years of drinking, <b>chronic</b> pancreatitis, where it is the most common cause in adults.<br>How: trypsin is activated too early inside the gland and digests acinar tissue; repeated attacks leave fibrosis, calcifications and too little enzyme output (fatty stools).<br>Mix-up: 'acute' alone is incomplete. For chronic pancreatitis with calcifications, think alcohol first.","ex":"","fl":["Incomplete/incorrect as an either-or answer: alcohol causes BOTH. It is the second most common cause of acute pancreatitis (after gallstones) and the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults. Correct answer: both (chronic pancreatitis is the classic long-term result of alcoholism).","The source answered only 'acute'; heavy alcohol use causes both, and it is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 33 (Clinical implications)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":172,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 57"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":38,"label":"Dual blood supply: portal vein + hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-40.jpg","cap":"Portal vein nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor, 75% of flow; hepatic artery oxygen-rich, 25%","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G021-41.jpg","cap":"Portal venule and hepatic arteriole blood mixing in sinusoids, flowing to central vein","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 41","lg":[["white arrows (blue sinusoids)","blood flow from portal vein and hepatic artery branches to the central vein"],["white arrows (green channel)","bile flow toward the bile duct"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-39.jpg","cap":"Liver architecture: triad branches feed sinusoids that drain to the central vein","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 39","lg":[["C","central vein"],["H","plates of hepatocytes"],["PV","portal venule"],["L","small lymphatic"],["B","bile ductule"],["HA","hepatic arteriole"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-55-1","s":"Which of the following describes the dual blood supply of the liver?","o":["A. Hepatic vein and hepatic artery","B. Bile ducts and portal vein","C. Portal artery and hepatic vein","D. Hepatic artery and portal vein","E. Bile ducts and hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic artery and portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatic artery and portal vein.</b> The <b>portal vein</b> brings about three-quarters of the liver's blood: nutrient-rich but oxygen-poor, from the gut and spleen. The <b>hepatic artery</b> brings the other quarter: oxygen-rich.<br>A. The hepatic vein drains blood out of the liver to the inferior vena cava.<br>B. Bile ducts carry bile out; they are not a blood supply.<br>C. There is no 'portal artery', and the hepatic vein is outflow.<br>E. Bile ducts again are not blood vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":55,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 58"},{"id":"F3980-19-1","s":"A liver histology slide shows sinusoids receiving blood from two distinct sources that mix before flowing toward the central vein. The slide also demonstrates a portal triad containing a branch of the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct. Which statement best describes the dual blood supply shown in this tissue?","o":["A. The hepatic artery delivers nutrient-rich blood that mixes with oxygen-rich blood from the portal vein in the sinusoids","B. The portal vein supplies oxygenated blood that drains into sinusoids independently of the hepatic artery","C. Blood from the hepatic artery and portal vein mixes in the sinusoids to provide hepatocytes with oxygen and nutrients simultaneously","D. The hepatic artery drains into the central vein first, followed by portal venous flow entering the sinusoids afterward"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blood from the hepatic artery and portal vein mixes in the sinusoids to provide hepatocytes with oxygen and nutrients simultaneously","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Portal venule and hepatic arteriole both empty into the <b>sinusoids</b>, where the blood <b>mixes</b>, so each hepatocyte gets oxygen and absorbed nutrients at once; the mix then drains to the central vein.<br>A. Reversed: the <b>portal vein</b> brings nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor blood; the <b>hepatic artery</b> brings oxygen-rich blood.<br>B. Portal blood is oxygen-poor, and it mixes with arterial blood rather than flowing separately.<br>D. Neither vessel drains straight into the central vein; both pass through the sinusoids first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":19,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 59"},{"id":"F1221-135-1","s":"Portal triads are composed of three major tubes. Branches of the hepatic artery carry oxygenated blood to the hepatocytes, while branches of the portal vein carry blood with nutrients from the small intestine. The bile duct carries bile products away from the hepatocytes, to the larger ducts and gall bladder. Given that the portal vein carries mostly deoxygenated blood, what do the relative sizes of the portal vein and hepatic artery suggest about oxygen levels in the liver?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The portal vein is larger, suggesting that the blood in the liver is mostly poorly oxygenated. The blood in this vein has already been through the GI tract. The blood in the smaller hepatic artery is better oxygenated.","a":null,"why":"<b>The portal vein is the larger vessel, so most liver blood is oxygen-poor.</b> Lecture: portal vein = <b>nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor, about three-quarters</b> of hepatic blood flow; hepatic artery = <b>oxygen-rich, about one-quarter</b>.<br>The two mix in the sinusoids, and oxygen falls steadily from the portal triad to the central vein. That gradient is why hepatocytes by the central vein (zone 3) suffer first in hypoxia.<br>Mix-up: the portal vein brings nutrients, not oxygen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":135,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 60"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":38,"label":"Portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-43.jpg","cap":"Labelled: central vein versus portal triad (portal venule, bile ductule, hepatic artery)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43","lg":[["lines at left edge (labels cut off), top to bottom","bile ductule, portal venule, hepatic arteriole (one portal triad); connective-tissue septum between lobules; sinusoids; central vein; plates of hepatocytes"],["bracket under the picture","one hepatic lobule"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-39.jpg","cap":"Diagram: triad branches at lobule corners; central vein in the center","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 39","lg":[["C","central vein"],["H","plates of hepatocytes"],["PV","portal venule"],["L","small lymphatic"],["B","bile ductule"],["HA","hepatic arteriole"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-97-1","s":"Which three components make up the portal triad?","o":["A. Hepatic vein. Portal vein and hepatic artery","B. Central vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","C. Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein","D. Central vein. Portal vein, hepatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein.</b> These three branches run together in the connective tissue at each corner of the classic lobule.<br>A. The hepatic vein carries blood out of the liver; it is not in the triad.<br>B. The central vein drains the lobule's center; it is not in the triad, and the portal vein is missing.<br>D. Central vein again, and it leaves out the bile duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"H&E of a portal area with arrows on its vessels and duct (not needed to answer).","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":97,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":244,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":43,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 61"},{"id":"F4081-57-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT included in the portal triad?","o":["A. Portal vein","B. Hepatic artery","C. Bile ductule","D. Central vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Central vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Central vein.</b> It sits in the <b>center</b> of the classic lobule and drains the sinusoids toward the hepatic veins; it is not in the triad.<br>A. Portal vein (venule): triad member; large, thin-walled, nutrient-rich blood.<br>B. Hepatic artery (arteriole): triad member; small, thick-walled, oxygen-rich blood.<br>C. Bile ductule: triad member; cuboidal epithelium carrying bile toward the gallbladder.<br>The triad sits at the lobule's corners.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":57,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","n":"12 extra 62"},{"id":"F0722-175-1","s":"What are the components of the portal triad? Secondary Q: which structure in the portal triad is oxygen rich?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile duct, portal vein, hepatic artery; the hepatic artery is the oxygen-rich one","a":null,"why":"<b>Portal triad = branch of the portal vein + branch of the hepatic artery + bile ductule</b>, held in connective tissue at the lobule corners (with lymphatics and nerves).<br><b>Oxygen-rich member: the hepatic arteriole.</b> The portal venule carries nutrient-rich but oxygen-poor blood from the gut; the bile ductule carries bile, not blood.<br>Mix-up: the central vein is not a triad member; it drains the lobule's center.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labeled liver lobules (central vein, portal triad with portal venule, bile ductule, hepatic artery).","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 63"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":38,"label":"Portal venule on a slide: oxygen-poor, nutrient-rich blood","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: large thin-walled portal vein branch, small hepatic artery, cuboidal bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G116-39.jpg","cap":"Portal venule blood (nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor) and arteriole blood (oxygen-rich) mix in the sinusoid","where":"2026 lab, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-15-1","s":"Which structure brings poor O2/rich nutrient blood into this organ?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3","D. 4"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. 1.</b> Blood that is poor in oxygen but rich in nutrients comes from the gut through the <b>portal vein</b>. Its branch inside the liver is the <b>portal venule</b>, and that is structure 1. In this section, stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E, the routine pink and purple stain), structure 1 is the largest tube in the field. Its wall is very thin, and its wide lumen is nearly empty, with only a little blood in it. A big, thin-walled vessel in a portal area is the portal venule.</p><p><b>The liver has two blood supplies.</b></p><ul><li><b>Portal vein, about 75% of the flow:</b> blood that has already passed through the gut and the spleen. It is rich in absorbed nutrients, and also in absorbed toxins, but it is poor in oxygen.</li><li><b>Hepatic artery, about 25% of the flow:</b> oxygen-rich arterial blood.</li></ul><p>The two vessels branch side by side. Both empty into the <b>sinusoids</b>, the wide blood channels between the liver cells, and there the two kinds of blood mix. So every liver cell (hepatocyte) receives both nutrients and oxygen. The blood then leaves through the central vein, the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava.</p><p><b>The portal triad.</b> At each corner of a liver lobule, a little connective tissue holds three tubes that run together. This group is the portal triad, or portal area. Lymphatic vessels and nerves run there too. The four numbers in this picture mark the three tubes and the tissue around them:</p><ul><li><b>1, portal venule:</b> the largest tube, with a thin wall. It brings the oxygen-poor, nutrient-rich blood in.</li><li><b>2, bile ductule:</b> a ring of tall cuboidal epithelial cells with round nuclei around a clean, empty lumen. It carries bile out of the liver. It holds no blood.</li><li><b>3, hepatic arteriole:</b> small and round, with a thick wall of smooth muscle and a narrow lumen. It brings the oxygen-rich blood in.</li><li><b>4, hepatocytes:</b> the large pink liver cells with round nuclei that fill the rest of the field. They receive the blood.</li></ul><p><b>The liver as a whole.</b> The liver is the largest gland in the body. It is covered by <b>Glisson capsule</b>, a layer of connective tissue. At the hilum, the gate of the liver, this connective tissue enters the organ together with the vessels. Inside, it is built from hepatic lobules. Each lobule is made of plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids between the plates, all held up by a fine framework of reticular fibers.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-15.jpg","in":"Liver portal triad, H&E: 1 = the largest vessel, thin-walled with a wide lumen (portal venule); 2 = duct lined by tall cuboidal epithelium (bile ductule); 3 = small round vessel with a thick muscular wall (hepatic arteriole); 4 = the hepatocytes beside the triad.","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)","H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":15,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"59"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Limiting plate rings the portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Limiting plate: hepatocytes immediately surrounding the portal triad, smooth and uniform","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-53.jpg","cap":"Blue outline: the wall of hepatocytes ringing the portal canal","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["red circle","hepatic artery branch"],["green outline","portal vein branch"],["cyan circle","bile duct"],["blue double line","limiting plate ('mura': ring of hepatocytes around the portal canal)"],["11","slide number of the source set, not a structure"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-25-1","s":"What is the term for the hepatocytes immediately surrounding the portal triad?","o":["A. Kupffer cells","B. Limiting plate","C. Langerhan cells","D. Sinusoids"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Limiting plate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Limiting plate</b>: the ring of hepatocytes immediately around the portal triad, separating the portal connective tissue from the lobule (lecture: 'smooth and uniform').<br>A. Kupffer cells: macrophages inside the sinusoids.<br>C. Langerhans: islets of Langerhans are pancreatic endocrine units; Langerhans cells are skin dendritic cells.<br>D. Sinusoids: blood channels between hepatocyte plates, not a layer of hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":25,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 64"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Classic lobule: central vein center, triads at corners","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-44.jpg","cap":"Pig lobule: central vein centered; venule, arteriole, ductule at a corner","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 44","lg":[["C","central vein"],["A","hepatic arterioles"],["V","portal venule"],["D","bile ductule"],["arrows","thin connective-tissue septa outlining the lobule (pig liver)"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-36.jpg","cap":"Trichrome pig liver: blue septa outline lobules, triads where septa meet","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Hepatocytes: large polygonal, often binucleated cells arranged in plates","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-22-1","s":"Which structures are being shown below?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: hepatocytes (arrows); right: a (classic hepatic) lobule (hexagon outlined around a central vein)","a":null,"why":"<b>Left: hepatocytes</b>: large polygonal cells with round central nuclei (often two) and pink cytoplasm, lined up in plates between pale sinusoids.<br><b>Right: classic hepatic lobule</b>: a roughly hexagonal unit with a <b>central vein</b> in the middle, hepatocyte plates radiating from it, and portal triads at the corners.<br>Mix-up: human lobule borders are faint (little connective tissue), so you judge the hexagon from the central vein and the surrounding triads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-22.jpg","in":"Left: high-power liver with three arrows on large polygonal cells with round central nuclei in plates. Right: low-power liver with a black hexagon drawn around a central vein.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":22,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["arrows","hepatocytes (large polygonal cells, round central nuclei, some with two)"],["hexagon","classic hepatic lobule (central vein in the middle)"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 65"},{"id":"F0744-46-1","s":"Identify the portal triads and central veins in this pig liver. How is a classic hepatic lobule identified?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Central vein = the single vessel in the middle of each polygon; portal triads = the vessel/duct groups at the corners where the blue septa meet. A classic lobule is a hexagon with a central vein at its center and portal triads at its corners (in pig it is outlined by connective tissue; in human the limits are judged from plates radiating from the central vein toward the triads)","a":null,"why":"<b>Central vein</b>: the lone open vessel in the <b>center</b> of each polygon, with hepatocyte plates radiating toward it.<br><b>Portal triads</b>: a venule, an arteriole and a bile ductule grouped in the blue connective tissue at the <b>corners</b> where septa meet.<br><b>Classic lobule</b>: the hexagon drained by one central vein, triads at its corners.<br>Pig vs human: in pig, connective tissue outlines every lobule; in human it is minimal, so you judge the lobule from the central vein and the triads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0744-46.jpg","in":"Pig liver (trichrome): polygonal lobules outlined by blue connective tissue, a white central vein in each, and portal triads at the angles.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 41 (LIVER)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":46,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 66"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Portal triad micrograph: venule, arteriole, ductule","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: large portal vein branch, small artery, cuboidal bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-44.jpg","cap":"Venule (V), arteriole (A), ductule (D) at lobule corner; central vein (C) alone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 44","lg":[["C","central vein"],["A","hepatic arterioles"],["V","portal venule"],["D","bile ductule"],["arrows","thin connective-tissue septa outlining the lobule (pig liver)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-200-1","s":"Question 10 Identify the structures labeled as 1, 2 and 3 respectively:","o":["A. Bile ductule, venule, arteriole","B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","C. Arteriole, capillary, venule","D. Arteriole, venule, capillary"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule.</b> All three labels sit in the pale connective tissue of a <b>portal area</b>, so they are the three tubes of a portal triad. Number 1 is a ring of cuboidal cells with round nuclei around a small, empty lumen, which is a <b>bile ductule</b>. Number 2 is a small vessel with a thick wall for its size and a narrow lumen, which is an <b>arteriole</b> (a branch of the hepatic artery). Number 3 is the largest opening, with a thin wall, an irregular shape and red blood cells inside, which is the <b>venule</b> (a branch of the portal vein).</p><p><b>Telling the tubes of the liver apart.</b></p><ul><li><b>Portal venule:</b> the largest lumen, a thin wall, often an irregular shape.</li><li><b>Hepatic arteriole:</b> small and round, with a thick wall of smooth muscle and a small lumen.</li><li><b>Bile ductule:</b> a ring of cuboidal epithelial cells around a small lumen, with no muscle.</li><li><b>Central vein:</b> alone in the middle of the lobule, with no artery or duct beside it. Its wall is very thin, and the sinusoids open straight into it.</li><li><b>Sinusoids:</b> the pale channels between the plates of liver cells. They are the liver's own kind of capillary, and they lie inside the lobule, not in the portal area.</li></ul><p><b>The classic hepatic lobule.</b> The classic lobule is a hexagon. A central vein sits in the middle, and portal triads sit at the corners. Plates of liver cells (hepatocytes), one or two cells thick, radiate from the central vein toward the corners like the spokes of a wheel. Sinusoids run between the plates. In humans very little connective tissue separates neighboring lobules, so their borders are hard to see. To find a lobule, imagine lines joining the portal areas around one central vein. In the pig, thick walls (septa) of connective tissue outline every lobule.</p><p><b>The limiting plate.</b> The curved arrow in this picture points to the limiting plate. It is the ring of hepatocytes directly around a portal area. It forms a smooth, even border between the portal connective tissue and the lobule.</p><p><b>Special stains of the liver.</b></p><ul><li><b>Silver (reticulin) stain:</b> black reticular fibers outline the plates and the sinusoids.</li><li><b>India ink injection:</b> the ink is eaten by Kupffer cells, the macrophages of the sinusoids, which turn black.</li><li><b>Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) stain:</b> colors the glycogen stored in hepatocytes magenta.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-200.jpg","in":"H&E portal area: #1 a small duct lined by cuboidal cells, #2 a small thick-walled vessel, #3 a large thin-walled vessel containing red cells; curved arrow on the limiting plate.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":200,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":11,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","bile ductule (ring of cuboidal cells)"],["2","arteriole (hepatic artery branch: small, thick wall)"],["3","portal venule (portal vein branch: biggest, thin wall)"],["curved arrow","limiting plate (first row of hepatocytes around the portal tract)"]],"n":"60"},{"id":"F3980-24-1","s":"[Label the structures] 1 ______ 2 ______ 3 ______","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1 = Portal vein (branch); 2 = Hepatic artery (branch); 3 = Bile duct (ductule) ('Simone Biles Duct')","a":null,"why":"<b>1 = portal venule</b>: the <b>largest</b> lumen with a <b>thin wall</b> (branch of the portal vein).<br><b>2 = hepatic arteriole</b>: small, round, <b>thick muscular wall</b>, narrow lumen (branch of the hepatic artery).<br><b>3 = bile ductule</b>: a ring of <b>cuboidal epithelial cells</b> (cholangiocytes) around a small lumen.<br>Mix-up: the big vessel in a triad is the portal venule, not the central vein; the central vein sits alone in the lobule's center, with no artery or duct beside it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-24.jpg","in":"Portal tract: 1 = large thin-walled vessel with wide lumen; 2 = small round vessel with thick muscular wall; 3 = small duct lined by cuboidal epithelium.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":24,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["1","branch of portal vein (large, thin wall)"],["2","branch of hepatic artery (small, thick muscular wall)"],["3","bile duct (ring of cuboidal-to-columnar cells)"]],"like":"F0052-200-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 67"},{"id":"F4081-69-1","s":"Identify the following: What organ is this? Label A, Label B, Label C, Label D","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Liver; A = portal venule; B = sinusoid; C = bile ductule; D = hepatic arteriole","a":null,"why":"<b>Liver</b>: plates of large polygonal hepatocytes with sinusoids between them.<br><b>A = portal venule</b>: the large thin-walled vessel with a ductule and an arteriole beside it, so it belongs to a portal triad (not a central vein).<br><b>B = sinusoid</b>: a thin channel between hepatocyte plates, with red cells inside.<br><b>C = bile ductule</b>: a ring of cuboidal cells.<br><b>D = hepatic arteriole</b>: a small round vessel with a thicker wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-69.jpg","in":"Liver H&E: A = large thin-walled vessel in a portal area; B = bracketed channel between hepatocyte plates; C = small duct of cuboidal cells; D = small round vessel next to it.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":69,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["A","portal venule (large, thin wall)"],["B","sinusoid (red blood cells between hepatocyte plates)"],["C","bile ductule (ring of cuboidal cells)"],["D","hepatic arteriole (small, thick pink muscular wall)"]],"like":"F0052-200-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 68"},{"id":"F0754-221-1","s":"Identify: A: B: C: D: Orange box:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: Bile Duct; B: Hepatic Artery; C: Bile Duct; D: Portal Vein; Orange box: Central Vein","a":null,"why":"Portal area at the bottom, central vein at the top (Junqueira figure 16-12b).<br><b>A and C = bile ductules</b>: tubes lined by cuboidal cells.<br><b>B = hepatic arteriole</b>: small round vessel with its own wall.<br><b>D = portal venule</b> (labelled V): the largest, thin-walled space in the portal connective tissue.<br><b>Orange box = central vein</b>: a lone vessel in the middle of the hepatocyte plates, far from the triad, with no artery or duct beside it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-221.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E of liver: portal area at bottom with structures A, B, C (small vessels/ducts) and D (large thin-walled vein, V); an orange box sits on a vessel at the top of the field, away from the portal area.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["orange box","central vein"],["A","bile ductule"],["B","hepatic arteriole (branch of hepatic artery)"],["C","bile ductule"],["V","portal venule (branch of portal vein)"],["D","the portal venule labeled V"]],"like":"F0052-200-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 69"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Liver stains: sinusoids, central vein, portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-46.jpg","cap":"Same four panels: sinusoids, reticulin stain, central vein, portal triad","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 46","lg":[["a","hematoxylin and eosin"],["H","hepatocyte plates"],["S","sinusoids"],["b","silver stain for reticulin"],["R","reticulin (type III collagen) fibers"],["c","Mallory trichrome"],["C","central vein"],["arrows","sinusoids draining into the central vein"],["d","portal area, hematoxylin and eosin"],["PV","portal venule"],["HA","hepatic arteriole"],["BD","bile ductule"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-43.jpg","cap":"Low power: central vein apart from the triad of venule, ductule, artery","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43","lg":[["lines at left edge (labels cut off), top to bottom","bile ductule, portal venule, hepatic arteriole (one portal triad); connective-tissue septum between lobules; sinusoids; central vein; plates of hepatocytes"],["bracket under the picture","one hepatic lobule"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-178-1","s":"MATCH (answer can be mentioned more than once or not at all): central vein; portal triad; sinusoids; kupffer cells [to images A-D]","o":["A. central vein","B. portal triad","C. sinusoids","D. kupffer cells"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A and B = sinusoids; C = central vein; D = portal triad (Kupffer cells not used)","a":null,"why":"<b>a = sinusoids</b>: pale channels between hepatocyte plates (H&amp;E, hematoxylin and eosin).<br><b>b = sinusoids</b>: silver (reticulin) stain; black reticular fibers outline the sinusoids along the plates.<br><b>c = central vein</b>: one round vessel with plates radiating from it and only a thin rim of connective tissue.<br><b>d = portal triad</b>: connective tissue holding a big thin-walled portal venule, a small thick-walled hepatic arteriole and a cuboidal bile ductule.<br>Kupffer cells need ink or special stains; not shown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-178.jpg","in":"A: H&E liver plates with sinusoids; B: reticulin (silver) stain outlining sinusoids; C: trichrome-like stain around a central vein; D: portal area with PV, HA, BD.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 41 (LIVER)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":178,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["a","hepatocyte plates and sinusoids (H&E)"],["b","reticulin fibers (silver stain)"],["c","central vein (trichrome)"],["d","portal triad (H&E)"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoids"],["R","reticulin (type III collagen) fibers along the hepatocyte plates"],["C","central vein"],["arrows (c)","sinusoids draining into the central vein"],["PV","portal venule (branch of portal vein)"],["HA","hepatic arteriole (branch of hepatic artery)"],["BD","bile ductule"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 70"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Hepatocyte polarity: apical canalicular, basal sinusoidal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal (basal) and canalicular (apical) domains, tight junctions","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte diagram: apical bile canaliculus; basolateral side facing perisinusoidal space","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35","lg":[["1","plasma proteins made and secreted into the perisinusoidal space"],["2","bilirubin, bile acids, toxins taken from blood and sent into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0744-40-1","s":"Is the hepatocyte a polarized cell? Identify the perisinusoidal surfaces and the canalicular surface","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes. The perisinusoidal (basolateral) surfaces face the sinusoids/space of Disse and carry microvilli; the canalicular (apical) surfaces are the small grooves between neighbouring hepatocytes that form the bile canaliculi (green marks)","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes, the hepatocyte is polarized.</b><br><b>Sinusoidal surface = basal (basolateral) domain</b>: faces the space of Disse and is covered with microvilli; it takes up nutrients and releases plasma proteins into the blood.<br><b>Canalicular surface = apical domain</b>: small grooves on two neighboring hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions, form the <b>bile canaliculus</b>, where bile is secreted.<br>Mix-up: bile leaves through the apical (canalicular) side, not the sinusoidal side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Diagram of one hepatocyte between two sinusoids: microvillous surfaces toward the sinusoids/space of Disse; green marks at bile canaliculi between adjacent cells.","tg":["H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":40,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":45,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":2,"like":"G116-28-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 71"},{"id":"G116-28-1","s":"Is the hepatocyte a polarized cell?","o":["A. Yes: the apical domain faces the sinusoid and the basolateral domain faces the bile canaliculus","B. Yes: the apical domain faces the bile canaliculus and the basolateral domain faces the sinusoid","C. Yes: the apical domain faces the central vein and the basolateral domain faces the portal triad","D. No: all of its surfaces are alike, because it does not line a duct or the gut lumen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes: the apical domain faces the bile canaliculus and the basolateral domain faces the sinusoid","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Yes: the apical domain faces the bile canaliculus and the basolateral domain faces the sinusoid.</b> A polarized cell is a cell whose surfaces differ in structure and in the work they do. The hepatocyte (liver cell) fits that definition. It secretes <b>bile</b> from one surface, into the bile canaliculus, and that surface is its <b>apical</b> domain. Its <b>basolateral</b> domain faces the sinusoid, the blood channel, and trades material with the blood. So the hepatocyte is polarized even though it does not line a duct or the gut.</p><p><b>The three surfaces of a hepatocyte.</b></p><ul><li><b>Sinusoidal surface (basal, or basolateral, domain):</b> faces the space of Disse, the thin gap next to the sinusoid. It carries many microvilli. Here the cell takes up material from the blood and releases plasma proteins and glucose into it.</li><li><b>Intercellular (lateral) surface:</b> faces the neighboring hepatocytes and is held to them by cell junctions.</li><li><b>Canalicular surface (apical domain):</b> faces the bile canaliculus, a tiny groove between two hepatocytes. Tight junctions seal the groove, so bile cannot leak toward the blood. Bile is secreted here.</li></ul><p><b>What a hepatocyte looks like.</b> It is a large, polygonal cell with a round central nucleus, and it often has two nuclei. Hepatocytes are stable cells. They rarely divide, but they can start dividing again to regrow the liver after injury or after part of it is removed.</p><p><b>The organelles and their jobs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Rough endoplasmic reticulum, with the Golgi:</b> makes plasma proteins, which are albumin, clotting factors and lipoproteins.</li><li><b>Smooth endoplasmic reticulum:</b> detoxification and drug metabolism, bile acid synthesis, and conjugation of bilirubin. Conjugation makes bilirubin, the yellow pigment from old red blood cells, water-soluble so that it can leave in the bile.</li><li><b>Glycogen granules:</b> stored glucose, taken up from the blood in the space of Disse. They stain magenta with the periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) stain.</li><li><b>Mitochondria, lysosomes and peroxisomes:</b> energy, breakdown of old material, and fatty acid metabolism.</li></ul><p><b>What the liver does.</b> It forms bile. It stores glycogen and steadies the blood glucose. It makes urea from ammonia, and the kidneys then excrete the urea. It metabolizes cholesterol and fat. It releases plasma proteins and insulin-like growth factor (IGF) straight into the blood, which is an endocrine type of secretion. It detoxifies drugs and poisons. It also processes several steroid hormones and vitamin D.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Line drawing of one hepatocyte: sinusoids and the space of Disse (with microvilli) at the top and the lower corners, green bile canaliculi between neighbouring hepatocytes at the sides; slide 29 adds the labels 'Apical' (at the canaliculi) and 'Basolateral' (at the sinusoidal surfaces). Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":28,"f":"G116"}],"tier":1,"n":"61"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Hepatocyte organelles: proteins, detoxification, glycogen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-49.jpg","cap":"Organelle jobs: glycogen granules, plasma-protein synthesis, detoxification and bile acids","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 49","lg":[["arrow 1","plasma proteins made on rough endoplasmic reticulum, released into the perisinusoidal space"],["arrow 2","toxins, bilirubin and bile acids taken up from the perisinusoidal space, processed in smooth endoplasmic reticulum, secreted into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["double arrow 3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte functions and stores: bile, plasma proteins, glycogen, lipid droplets","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35","lg":[["1","plasma proteins made and secreted into the perisinusoidal space"],["2","bilirubin, bile acids, toxins taken from blood and sent into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0744-41-1","s":"Correlate hepatocyte ultrastructure and function: What organelles are engaged in synthesis of plasma proteins? What organelle processes toxic compounds? Bile and bile acids are secreted into what structure? Glucose is stored as what material? Triglyceride is stored in what form?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Rough ER (with Golgi) makes plasma proteins; smooth ER processes toxic compounds; bile and bile acids go into the bile canaliculus; glucose is stored as glycogen granules; triglyceride is stored as lipid droplets","a":null,"why":"<b>Plasma proteins</b> → <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> (with Golgi), released into the space of Disse.<br><b>Toxic compounds</b> → <b>smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b> (detoxification, bile acid synthesis, bilirubin conjugation).<br><b>Bile and bile acids</b> → secreted into the <b>bile canaliculus</b> at the apical surface.<br><b>Glucose</b> → stored as <b>glycogen granules</b> (PAS, periodic acid-Schiff, positive).<br><b>Triglyceride</b> → stored as <b>lipid droplets</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labeled hepatocyte EM-style diagram (RER, SER, Golgi, mitochondria, glycogen, lipid, lysosomes, bile canaliculus, tight junctions, perisinusoidal space, fenestrated endothelium).","tg":["H16 slide 49 (Hepatocytes)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":41,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 72"},{"id":"F4081-62-1","s":"What function of the liver is demonstrated best with PAS staining?","o":["A. Synthesis of plasma proteins","B. Synthesis of glycogen","C. Detoxification of drugs","D. Synthesis of bile"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesis of glycogen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glycogen synthesis.</b> PAS (periodic acid-Schiff) stains <b>carbohydrates</b> magenta, so it lights up the <b>glycogen granules</b> hepatocytes build from glucose taken up from the perisinusoidal space.<br>A. Plasma proteins are made on rough endoplasmic reticulum; PAS does not show them.<br>C. Drug detoxification happens in smooth endoplasmic reticulum.<br>D. Bile synthesis (smooth endoplasmic reticulum, secretion into canaliculi) is not what PAS shows.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 49 (Hepatocytes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":62,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 73"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Liver makes urea, does not break it down","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-60.jpg","cap":"Lecture list of liver functions, including synthesis of urea","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte functions: bile, plasma proteins, urea formation, detoxification, storage","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35","lg":[["1","plasma proteins made and secreted into the perisinusoidal space"],["2","bilirubin, bile acids, toxins taken from blood and sent into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-176-1","s":"Which of the following is not a function of the liver?","o":["A. forms bile","B. stores glycogen and is a buffer for blood glucose","C. breaking down Urea","D. detoxification","E. processing of steroid hormones and vitamin D"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"breaking down Urea","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Breaking down urea</b> is not a liver job. The liver <b>makes</b> urea from ammonia (urea cycle); the kidneys excrete it.<br>A. Forms bile: yes, hepatocytes secrete it into canaliculi.<br>B. Stores glycogen and buffers blood glucose: yes.<br>D. Detoxification: yes, by smooth endoplasmic reticulum enzymes.<br>E. Processes steroid hormones and vitamin D: yes (the liver does the first activation step of vitamin D).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 60 (FUNCTIONS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":176,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 74"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Hepatic sinusoid on a slide: blood channel between hepatocyte plates","slides":[{"src":"sl/G115-10.jpg","cap":"Hepatic sinusoids: pale blood channels between plates of hepatocytes one or two cells thick","where":"2026 interactive, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G116-33.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte plates and sinusoids on H&E; reticulin stain outlines them in black","where":"2026 lab, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-11-1","s":"Identify the structure at the tip of the pointer","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Red pulp","C. Hepatic sinusoid","D. Fenestrated capillary"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic sinusoid","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Hepatic sinusoid.</b> First name the organ. The field is filled with plates of large pink cells with round central nuclei. These are hepatocytes (liver cells), so this is liver. The section is stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E, the routine pink and purple stain). The tip of the arrow lies in a <b>pale channel between two plates of hepatocytes</b>, and the channel holds bright red blood cells. A blood channel between the plates of the liver is a hepatic sinusoid.</p><p><b>What a sinusoid is.</b> Sinusoids are wide, irregular capillaries that run between the plates of hepatocytes. Blood from the portal vein and the hepatic artery mixes in them. It flows through them from the portal triads at the edge of the lobule to the central vein in the middle. In this picture you can follow the same pale channels, dotted with red cells, all across the field.</p><p><b>How its wall is built.</b> The lining is a <b>fenestrated endothelium</b>, which means an endothelium pierced by pores. Here the pores are large and open, and there are gaps between the cells. There is <b>no continuous basal lamina</b> under them. (A basal lamina is the thin supporting sheet on which an endothelium normally rests.) This type of vessel is called a discontinuous capillary, or sinusoid. An ordinary fenestrated capillary, as found in the kidney, the gut and the endocrine glands, is narrower, and its pores sit on a continuous basal lamina. So \"sinusoid\" is the exact name for the wide, leaky channel of the liver.</p><p><b>The cells in and around a sinusoid.</b></p><ul><li><b>Endothelial cell:</b> lines the sinusoid.</li><li><b>Kupffer cell:</b> the macrophage of the liver. It sits inside the sinusoid as part of its lining and faces the lumen.</li><li><b>Ito cell (hepatic stellate cell):</b> lies outside the endothelium, in the space of Disse. The space of Disse is the thin gap between the endothelium and the hepatocytes, where blood plasma and hepatocytes exchange material.</li></ul><p><b>Telling liver from tissues that look similar.</b></p><ul><li><b>Liver:</b> plates of polygonal hepatocytes, sinusoids, central veins and portal triads.</li><li><b>Red pulp:</b> part of the spleen. It is a mass of red blood cells in cords and sinuses, with no plates of hepatocytes.</li><li><b>Lamina propria:</b> the loose connective tissue that lies directly under a lining epithelium, as in the gut or the gallbladder. It is made of connective tissue cells and fibers.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-11.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E: plates of pink hepatocytes with round nuclei; the arrow points into a pale channel between the plates that holds red blood cells (hepatic sinusoid).","tg":["H16 slide 41 (LIVER)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":11,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"62"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Space of Disse: blood-hepatocyte exchange","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-53.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: fenestrated endothelium; space of Disse full of hepatocyte microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["red circle","hepatic artery branch"],["green outline","portal vein branch"],["cyan circle","bile duct"],["blue double line","limiting plate ('mura': ring of hepatocytes around the portal canal)"],["11","slide number of the source set, not a structure"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Diagram: fenestrated endothelium, space of Disse between sinusoid and hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-246-1","s":"What is the function of the Space of Disse","o":["A. Allows for the passage of bile","B. Allows for exchange of materials between hepatocytes and blood","C. It is where basal stem cells reside","D. Allows for the exchange of materials between the blood and the canal of hering"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Allows for exchange of materials between hepatocytes and blood","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Exchange between hepatocytes and blood.</b> The space of Disse lies between the fenestrated sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes; hepatocyte microvilli project into it and plasma bathes them.<br>A. Bile runs in bile canaliculi between hepatocytes, never in the space of Disse.<br>C. No basal stem cells there; it holds Ito cells. Liver progenitor cells sit near the canals of Hering.<br>D. The canal of Hering carries bile; it does not exchange with blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":246,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G116-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 75"},{"id":"F1221-137-1","s":"The sinusoids have a discontinuous endothelium. This means that the sinusoidal endothelial cells do not have a continuous basement membrane; they are separated from the hepatocytes by an area known as the space of Disse. What functional role does the discontinuous endothelium serve in the liver sinusoids?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"It allows maximal contact between the blood and the hepatocytes, which absorb materials from it and secrete materials into it.","a":null,"why":"<b>Maximal exchange between blood and hepatocytes.</b> Sinusoid endothelium is <b>fenestrated and discontinuous</b> with a sparse basal lamina, so plasma passes freely into the <b>space of Disse</b> and bathes hepatocyte microvilli.<br>There hepatocytes take up nutrients, bilirubin and toxins, and release plasma proteins (albumin, clotting factors) and glucose into the blood.<br>Mix-up: blood cells stay in the sinusoid; only plasma enters the space of Disse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":137,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"G116-35-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 76"},{"id":"G116-35-1","s":"An electron micrograph of the liver shows a sinusoid. A narrow space packed with hepatocyte microvilli lies between the thin, fenestrated wall of the sinusoid and the hepatocyte. What is this space?","o":["A. Space of Mall (periportal space)","B. Bile canaliculus","C. Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)","D. Lumen of the sinusoid","E. Canal of Hering"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space).</b> The stem gives the location exactly. The space lies <b>between the thin, fenestrated wall of the sinusoid and the hepatocyte</b> (liver cell), and it is <b>packed with hepatocyte microvilli</b>. That is the space of Disse. \"Perisinusoidal\" means around the sinusoid, and \"fenestrated\" means pierced by pores (fenestrae).</p><p><b>Reading the electron micrograph from the blood outward.</b></p><ol><li><b>Lumen of the sinusoid.</b> It holds the blood cells.</li><li><b>Endothelial cell.</b> It is very thin and has large open pores. There are gaps between the cells, and there is no continuous basal lamina (the thin supporting sheet on which an endothelium normally rests) under them.</li><li><b>Space of Disse.</b> A narrow space filled with the microvilli of the hepatocyte.</li><li><b>Hepatocyte.</b> Its cytoplasm shows mitochondria, smooth endoplasmic reticulum and rough endoplasmic reticulum.</li></ol><p><b>What the space of Disse is for.</b> Its job is exchange between the hepatocytes and the blood. Plasma passes freely through the pores of the endothelium into the space, while the blood cells stay in the lumen. No solid barrier stands in the way, so contact between plasma and hepatocyte is as close as possible. The microvilli enlarge the surface that takes up material from the blood and releases plasma proteins and glucose.</p><p><b>What else lies in it.</b> The space holds <b>reticular fibers</b>, which support the sinusoid. It also holds the <b>Ito cells</b> (hepatic stellate cells), which store fat and vitamin A.</p><p><b>The other small spaces and channels of the liver.</b></p><ul><li><b>Bile canaliculus:</b> it also holds microvilli, but it is a tiny groove between two hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions, away from the sinusoid. It carries bile.</li><li><b>Space of Mall (periportal space):</b> lies at the edge of the lobule, between the connective tissue of the portal area and the first row of hepatocytes (the limiting plate). Fluid from the space of Disse drains into it, and liver lymph begins there.</li><li><b>Canal of Hering:</b> a small bile channel at the edge of the lobule, lined partly by hepatocytes and partly by cuboidal bile duct cells.</li></ul><p><b>The interstitium in general.</b> The interstitium is everything that lies between the cells and outside the vessels of an organ. It is made of ground substance (glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans), interstitial fluid, fibers (collagen, reticular and elastic) and the fibroblasts that make the fibers.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Only a labeled version exists (electron micrograph with the labels Lumen of sinusoid, Fenestrae, Endothelial cell, Space of Disse, Microvilli on hepatocyte, Mitochondria, sER, rER), so the view is described in the stem.","tg":["H16 slide 53 (SPACES)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":35,"f":"G116"}],"tier":1,"n":"63"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Sinusoids: endothelium + Kupffer cells, drain centrally","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Sinusoid from portal field to central vein: endothelium, Kupffer cell, space of Disse","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47","lg":[["arrows","flow: blood (blue venous, red arterial) along the sinusoid into the central vein; bile (green) the other way, to the bile ductule"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrographs: endothelial cells line the sinusoid; Kupffer cell inside it","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-16.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte plates one to two cells thick between sinusoids","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-12-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the structures identified by the 2 thin arrows and the arrowhead?","o":["A. The structures marked by the arrows and the structure marked by the arrowhead are not continuous","B. The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","C. The structure marked by the arrowhead is within zone 1 of the hepatic acinus","D. The structure marked by the arrowhead contains 1-2 layers of smooth muscle in its wall","E. The structures marked by the arrows are apposed to plates of hepatocytes that are usually 3-4 layers thick"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells.</b> Start with the arrowhead. It sits in a large opening that stands alone in the middle of the field, with a thin wall and with no artery or duct beside it. That is the <b>central vein</b>. The two thin arrows lie on pale channels that run between the plates of liver cells toward the central vein. Those are <b>sinusoids</b>. The lumen of a sinusoid is lined by <b>endothelial cells</b>, and <b>Kupffer cells</b>, the macrophages of the liver, sit in that lining and face the lumen.</p><p><b>The cells of a sinusoid.</b></p><ul><li><b>Endothelial cells:</b> thin cells with large open pores and gaps between them. They have no continuous basal lamina (the thin supporting sheet under an ordinary endothelium), so plasma passes through freely.</li><li><b>Kupffer cells:</b> star-shaped macrophages inside the sinusoid. They eat aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris that arrive from the gut.</li><li><b>Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells):</b> lie just outside the endothelium, in the space of Disse, the thin gap between the endothelium and the liver cells. They store fat and vitamin A.</li></ul><p><b>Sinusoids and the central vein are one continuous path.</b> Blood enters the sinusoids from the portal triads at the edge of the lobule. It flows toward the middle, and the sinusoids open straight into the central vein. This picture shows it well: the pale channels converge on the central vein from every side, like the spokes of a wheel. From the central vein the blood goes to the hepatic veins and then to the inferior vena cava.</p><p><b>The wall of the central vein.</b> It is very thin. It is a layer of endothelium with a little connective tissue, and it has no layers of smooth muscle. A thick wall of smooth muscle belongs to the hepatic arteriole, which lies in the portal triad.</p><p><b>The plates of hepatocytes.</b> Hepatocytes (liver cells) are arranged in plates that are only <b>one or two cells thick</b>. Because the plates are so thin, every hepatocyte touches a sinusoid.</p><p><b>Where the central vein lies in the zones.</b> The hepatic acinus is a way of dividing the liver by blood supply. Zone 1 is next to the portal triad and gets the most oxygen. <b>Zone 3</b> surrounds the central vein and gets the least oxygen. So the arrowhead in this picture lies in the middle of zone 3.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G115-9.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E (PathPedia): a black arrowhead inside a thin-walled central vein; two thin black arrows on sinusoids that run between the hepatocyte plates toward it. Same micrograph as G024 slide 12.","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":9,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":12,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":16,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":12,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":13,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"64"},{"id":"G116-35-2","s":"An electron micrograph of the liver shows a sinusoid. A very thin cell with open pores (fenestrae) separates the lumen of the sinusoid from the microvillus-filled perisinusoidal space. Which cell is it?","o":["A. Kupffer cell","B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cell","C. Hepatocyte","D. Sinusoidal endothelial cell","E. Cholangiocyte"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sinusoidal endothelial cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sinusoidal endothelial cell.</b> The wall of the sinusoid is a <b>very thin endothelial cell with open pores (fenestrae)</b> and no continuous basal lamina. It is the only layer between the lumen and the space of Disse.<br>A. Kupffer cell: a bulky macrophage that bulges into the lumen and holds engulfed material.<br>B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cell: lies outside the endothelium, in the space of Disse, and holds lipid droplets.<br>C. Hepatocyte: the large cell beyond the space; it sends the microvilli into it.<br>E. Cholangiocyte: the cuboidal cell that lines bile ductules, not sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Only a labeled version exists (electron micrograph with the labels Lumen of sinusoid, Fenestrae, Endothelial cell, Space of Disse, Microvilli on hepatocyte, Mitochondria, sER, rER), so the view is described in the stem.","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":35,"f":"G116"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-12-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 77"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Kupffer in sinusoid; Ito in space of Disse","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-50.jpg","cap":"Diagram: Kupffer cell in the sinusoid; stellate cell in the space of Disse","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 50"},{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cell (HS) with lipid droplets in the perisinusoidal space (PS)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52","lg":[["HS","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell with lipid droplets"],["K","Kupffer cells"],["PS","perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)"],["E","endothelial cell"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoid"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-182-1","s":"Where do you find Kupffer cells?","o":["A. Inside the perisinusoidal space","B. In the hepatic artery","C. In the bile duct","D. In the sinusoidal space"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"In the sinusoidal space","a":"D","why":"<b>D. In the sinusoidal space.</b> Kupffer cells are macrophages lying <b>within the sinusoid</b>, part of its lining, where they meet gut-derived blood (and injected India ink).<br>A. The perisinusoidal space (space of Disse) holds Ito cells and hepatocyte microvilli.<br>B. The hepatic artery carries oxygen-rich blood into the triad; macrophages don't live in it.<br>C. Bile ducts are lined by cholangiocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":182,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":54,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":16,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 78"},{"id":"F1221-182-2","s":"Where do you find Hepatic Stellate cells?","o":["A. Inside the perisinusoidal space","B. In the hepatic artery","C. In the bile duct","D. In the sinusoidal space"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Inside the perisinusoidal space","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Inside the perisinusoidal space</b> (space of Disse), between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells store vitamin A and fat and cause fibrosis in cirrhosis.<br>B. The hepatic artery is a portal triad vessel.<br>C. Bile ducts are lined by cholangiocytes.<br>D. The sinusoid itself holds Kupffer cells (macrophages), not stellate cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":182,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 79"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Interstitium: matrix, fluid, fibroblasts, not endothelium","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3980-29.jpg","cap":"Interstitium: extracellular matrix and fluid between capillaries, lymphatics and cells","where":"2025 TA review, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-27-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT considered part of the interstitium?","o":["A. Ground substance composed of glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans","B. Capillary endothelial cells forming the microvascular barrier","C. Fibroblasts producing extracellular matrix components","D. Collagen fibers forming the structural scaffold between cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Capillary endothelial cells forming the microvascular barrier","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Capillary endothelial cells</b> line the vessel and form the barrier around the blood; they are not part of the space outside it. The <b>interstitium</b> is the tissue space between cells and vessels: extracellular matrix (ground substance plus fibers), interstitial fluid and resident cells.<br>A. Ground substance of glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans: part of it; its gel slows fluid flow.<br>C. Fibroblasts: part of it; they make the matrix.<br>D. Collagen fibers: part of it; the scaffold.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":27,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 80"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":42,"label":"Kupffer cells: sinusoidal macrophages, antigen-presenting","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-51.jpg","cap":"India ink: black Kupffer cells (K) along sinusoids; macrophage functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 51","lg":[["K","Kupffer cells (black from injected India ink)"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: Kupffer cell (macrophage) inside the sinusoid lumen","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14","lg":[["sER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-101-1","s":"Which of these cells function as macrophages in the liver?","o":["A. Kupffer cells","B. Ito cells","C. Stellate cells","D. Langerhans cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kupffer cells</b>: the liver's resident macrophages, lying within the sinusoid lining; they eat aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris and present antigens.<br>B and C. Ito cells = hepatic stellate cells (one cell, two names): fat- and vitamin A-storing cells in the space of Disse that cause fibrosis. Kupffer cells are 'stellate macrophages', but plain 'stellate cells' means Ito cells.<br>D. Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells of the skin epidermis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":101,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":245,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 81"},{"id":"F4081-64-1","s":"Which statement accurately describes the residential macrophages of the liver?","o":["A. Found within the sinusoids, present antigens, dispose of old RBCs","B. Found within the space of Disse and secrete albumin and insulin","C. Specialized star-shaped hepatocytes, degrade vitamin A and bile","D. Compose the walls of the capillaries, have flattened nuclei, filter blood"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Found within the sinusoids, present antigens, dispose of old RBCs","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Kupffer cells are <b>stellate macrophages inside the sinusoid lining</b>; they present antigens and phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris.<br>B. The space of Disse holds Ito cells, not Kupffer cells; albumin comes from hepatocytes and insulin from pancreatic beta cells.<br>C. Star-shaped cells that store vitamin A are Ito (hepatic stellate) cells, not hepatocytes, and they store it rather than degrade it.<br>D. Flattened cells forming the sinusoid wall are endothelial cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 82"},{"id":"F0754-225-1","s":"What is the name and function of the cell highlighted below?","o":["A. Ito Cell, secrete vitamin A","B. Kupffer Cell, macrophage","C. Hepatocyte, contains glycogen granules","D. Cholangiocytes, epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer Cell, macrophage","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Kupffer cell, macrophage.</b> On this electron micrograph the cell lies <b>inside the sinusoid lumen</b>, against the hepatocytes, which is where Kupffer cells live.<br>A. An Ito cell would sit <b>outside</b> the endothelium in the perisinusoidal space, filled with lipid droplets.<br>C. The hepatocyte is the large cell with the round nucleus, labelled separately.<br>D. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts, not sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-225.jpg","in":"TEM of rat liver: a cell lying inside the liver sinusoid lumen next to a hepatocyte nucleus; its label is covered by an orange box (perisinusoidal space and lipid droplet also labelled).","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":225,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":227,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["orange box with pointer line","Kupffer cell (macrophage in the sinusoid lumen)"],["rER","rough endoplasmic reticulum of the hepatocyte"]],"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 83"},{"id":"F0722-173-1","s":"Name this cell and its function. Also, found where?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Kupffer cells: stellate macrophages of the liver that act as antigen-presenting cells and phagocytose old RBCs, bacteria and debris; located within the sinusoids","a":null,"why":"<b>Kupffer cells</b>, shown <b>black</b> because they swallowed injected India ink particles.<br><b>Function</b>: resident liver macrophages; they phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris, and act as antigen-presenting cells.<br><b>Location</b>: <b>within the sinusoids</b>, part of the sinusoid lining between hepatocyte plates.<br>Mix-up: Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells) sit outside the endothelium in the space of Disse and store vitamin A and fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-173.jpg","in":"Liver from an ink-injected animal: black-stained cells (K) along the sinusoids between hepatocyte plates.","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["K","Kupffer cells (black: swallowed India ink)"]],"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 84"},{"id":"F3952-99-1","s":"What are the cells stained with india ink, and what is their function?","o":["A. Kupffer cells, antigen presenting cell","B. Hepatocyte, secrete elastin","C. Kupffer cell, regeneration capacity","D. Ito cells, antigen presenting cell"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells, antigen presenting cell","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kupffer cells, antigen-presenting cells.</b> Liver macrophages phagocytose injected India ink, so the Kupffer cells in the sinusoids turn black.<br>B. Hepatocytes don't take up ink, and they don't secrete elastin.<br>C. Right cell, wrong job: regeneration comes from hepatocytes dividing and from progenitor cells near the canals of Hering.<br>D. Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells) store vitamin A and fat and make matrix; they are not the ink-eating antigen-presenting cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver section after India-ink injection: black ink-laden cells (K) lining the sinusoids between hepatocyte plates; a grey box marks one. The stem's 'India ink' already identifies phagocytic Kupffer cells.","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 85"},{"id":"F3949-27-1","s":"Identify the cells stained black, what is their function?","o":["A. Hepatocytes, detox the blood that flows through","B. Kupffer cells, secrete Vit A","C. Hepatic stellate cells, secrete elastin","D. Kupffer cells serve as APC in the sinusoids"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells serve as APC in the sinusoids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Kupffer cells, antigen-presenting cells in the sinusoids.</b> Black cells in a liver section are macrophages that ate injected India ink.<br>A. Hepatocytes detoxify, but they don't take up ink; they are the large polygonal cells in plates.<br>B. Right cell, wrong job: vitamin A belongs to Ito (hepatic stellate) cells.<br>C. Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space of Disse and make collagen-rich matrix after injury, not elastin, and they don't stain black.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver with black (ink-laden) cells along the sinusoids, per key and notes. [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":27,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"like":"G118-1-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 86"},{"id":"G118-1-1","s":"What is the function of the cell at the tip of the arrow?","o":["A. Phagocytosis","B. Replenishing stem cell","C. Fat storage","D. Bile production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Phagocytosis","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Phagocytosis.</b> This is liver at high power, stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E, the routine pink and purple stain). The big pink cells with round nuclei are hepatocytes (liver cells), and the pale gap between them is a sinusoid, a blood channel. The arrow points to a cell that lies <b>inside the sinusoid</b>, and its cytoplasm is <b>packed with brown-yellow granules</b>. The granules are material that the cell has swallowed. A cell in a liver sinusoid that is full of engulfed particles is a <b>Kupffer cell</b>, the macrophage of the liver. The work of a macrophage is phagocytosis, which means cell eating.</p><p><b>What Kupffer cells are.</b> They are the resident macrophages of the liver. They are star-shaped, with long processes, so they are also called stellate macrophages. They come from blood monocytes. They sit within the sinusoids as part of the lining, facing the lumen, so all the blood that crosses the liver flows past them.</p><p><b>Their two jobs.</b></p><ul><li>They <b>phagocytose</b> aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris. Much of this arrives from the gut in the portal blood.</li><li>They act as <b>antigen-presenting cells</b>, which show pieces of what they have eaten to the immune system.</li></ul><p><b>How to find them.</b></p><ul><li><b>Routine H&amp;E:</b> a cell lying in the sinusoid, between the hepatocytes, with engulfed brown or yellow particles in its cytoplasm, as in this picture.</li><li><b>India ink or carbon injection:</b> the Kupffer cells eat the particles and show up as black cells along the sinusoids.</li><li><b>Electron micrograph:</b> a large cell inside the sinusoid lumen, against the endothelium, with phagocytic vacuoles.</li></ul><p><b>The other cells of the liver lobule and their jobs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Hepatocytes:</b> the large pink cells with round nuclei. They make bile and plasma proteins.</li><li><b>Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells):</b> lie in the space of Disse, the thin gap just outside the sinusoid lining. They store fat and vitamin A and hold clear fat droplets.</li><li><b>Progenitor (stem) cells:</b> lie in the canals of Hering, small bile channels at the edge of the lobule. They help the liver regenerate.</li></ul><p><b>Names that sound alike.</b> A <b>Langerhans cell</b> is an antigen-presenting cell of the epidermis of the skin. An <b>islet of Langerhans</b> is an endocrine cluster of the pancreas. Neither is found in the liver.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-1.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E, high power: pink hepatocytes with round nuclei around a sinusoid; the arrow points to a cell lying in the sinusoid whose cytoplasm is full of engulfed brown-yellow granules (Kupffer cell).","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":1,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"65"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Ito cells: vitamin A, fat, fibrosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cell (HS) with lipid droplets in the space of Disse; functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52","lg":[["HS","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell with lipid droplets"],["K","Kupffer cells"],["PS","perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)"],["E","endothelial cell"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoid"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-7.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: hepatic stellate cell in the perisinusoidal space of Disse","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 7","lg":[["HSC","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell, packed with pale lipid droplets (vitamin A store)"],["arrow","perisinusoidal space of Disse (gap between the sinusoid's endothelial lining and the hepatocytes, where the stellate cell sits)"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Trichrome: spider-web pericellular fibrosis laid down by activated stellate cells","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-101-1","s":"What perisinusoidal liver cells are important for synthesis of extracellular matrix components and storage of fat-soluble vitamins?","o":["A. Hepatocytes","B. Hepatic stellate, Ito cells","C. Kupffer cells","D. Canal of Herring"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic stellate, Ito cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells</b>: perisinusoidal cells in the space of Disse that store <b>fat and vitamin A</b> in lipid droplets and make <b>extracellular matrix</b> (fibrosis after injury).<br>A. Hepatocytes form the plates; they are not the perisinusoidal cells.<br>C. Kupffer cells are macrophages inside the sinusoid.<br>D. The canal of Hering is a small bile channel lined by cholangiocytes, linking canaliculi to ductules; it is not a cell.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":101,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-5-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 87"},{"id":"F0722-181-1","s":"Identify. FUNCTION??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells, perisinusoidal lipocytes) in the space of Disse: store fat and vitamin A; secrete matrix after injury, leading to fibrosis","a":null,"why":"<b>Ito cell (hepatic stellate cell, perisinusoidal lipocyte)</b>, labelled HS: a cell full of clear <b>lipid droplets</b> in the <b>perisinusoidal space (space of Disse)</b>, between the endothelium (E) and the hepatocytes (H).<br><b>Function</b>: stores fat and vitamin A; after injury it secretes extracellular matrix (collagen), causing <b>fibrosis</b>.<br>Mix-up: Kupffer cells (K) sit inside the sinusoid (S) itself and are macrophages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-181.jpg","in":"Plastic section of liver: a cell with large clear lipid droplets (HS) in the perisinusoidal space (PS) between hepatocytes (H) and sinusoid (S); Kupffer cells (K) and endothelium (E) labeled.","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":181,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["HS","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell (pale fat droplets store vitamin A)"],["K","Kupffer cells (macrophages in the sinusoid)"],["PS","perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)"],["E","endothelial cell lining the sinusoid"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoid"]],"like":"G024-5-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 88"},{"id":"F3949-37-1","s":"A 60 year old patient comes to the office for evaluation of jaundice. Medical history is significant for uncontrolled type 2 DM and morbid obesity. There is no family history of liver disease. Mild scleral icterus is present. The abdomen is nontender and nondistended. Lab revealed elevated transaminases. A liver biopsy is obtained and trichrome staining shows the following. Which of the following cells is directly responsible for the findings indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Cholangiocytes","B. Hepatocytes","C. Ito Cells","D. Sinusoidal endothelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ito Cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ito cells.</b> Trichrome stains collagen; the arrowed scar in fatty liver disease (obesity, diabetes) is laid down by activated <b>Ito (hepatic stellate) cells</b> in the space of Disse, which secrete matrix after injury.<br>A. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts.<br>B. Hepatocytes are the injured cells (fat, inflammation); they don't make the scar.<br>D. Sinusoidal endothelial cells are fenestrated cells that let plasma reach hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Trichrome-stained liver with an arrow on fibrous (collagen) bands, per the answer slide (cirrhosis). [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":37,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-5-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 89"},{"id":"G024-5-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-4: 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and central obesity; fatigue, dull RUQ pain; transaminases twice the upper limit of normal; ultrasound shows fatty liver (NAFLD); biopsy shows steatosis, inflammation and 'spider web like' pericellular fibrosis on trichrome = NASH.] An activated hepatic stellate cell with a myofibroblast-like phenotype has been implicated as a primary effector cell in hepatic fibrogenesis. Given this information and the \"spider web like\" histologic appearance of fibrosis in this patient's biopsy, The most likely location of this early fibrosis is:","o":["A. Portal triads","B. Canals of Hering","C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse","D. Hepatocyte cytoplasm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Perisinusoidal space of Disse","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse.</b> The stem names the cell that lays down the scar: the activated <b>hepatic stellate cell</b>, also called the Ito cell. That cell lives in the <b>space of Disse</b>, the thin gap between the lining of a sinusoid (blood channel) and the liver cells (hepatocytes). So the first collagen appears there. Sinusoids run along every hepatocyte, so thin strands of collagen come to wrap single liver cells. Under the microscope that fine net looks like a spider web. It is called pericellular fibrosis, which means scarring around individual cells.</p><p><b>The Ito cell at rest.</b> In a healthy liver the Ito cell sits quietly in the space of Disse. It stores fat droplets and <b>vitamin A</b>. It is the body's main store of vitamin A, and it releases the vitamin as needed. Its other names are hepatic stellate cell and perisinusoidal lipocyte, which means the fat cell beside the sinusoid.</p><p><b>How injury turns it into a scar-making cell.</b></p><ol><li>Something injures the liver over a long time: alcohol, fat or a virus.</li><li>The Ito cells <b>activate</b>. They change into cells that resemble myofibroblasts, which are fibroblasts with some features of smooth muscle.</li><li>The activated cells secrete <b>collagen</b> and other matrix (the material that lies between cells) into the space of Disse.</li><li>This scar tissue is <b>fibrosis</b>. It appears first around the central veins.</li></ol><p><b>How the scar is seen.</b> A <b>trichrome stain</b> colors collagen blue, so the thin blue strands along the sinusoids stand out against the hepatocytes.</p><p><b>What this patient has.</b> He has central obesity, type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome. His fatty liver is not caused by alcohol, so it is called nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). His biopsy shows three things: fat inside the hepatocytes (steatosis), inflammation and fibrosis. Fat together with inflammation and hepatocyte injury is called <b>nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH)</b>. NASH is the form of fatty liver that can scar.</p><p><b>Where the scarring can end.</b> If the injury continues, the fibrosis spreads and joins into bands. The end stage is <b>cirrhosis</b>: bands of fibrous scar surround nodules of regrowing hepatocytes, and the normal lobule pattern is lost.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":5,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":5,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":5,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1,"n":"66"},{"id":"G118-9-1","s":"Identify the cells at the tip of the pointers.","o":["A. Stave cells","B. Kupfer cells","C. Thymic epithelioreticular cells","D. Stellate cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stellate cells","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Stellate cells.</b> This is liver at high power (the scale bar is 20 µm), stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E, the routine pink and purple stain). Each white arrow points to a cell that holds <b>one large, clear, round droplet</b>. A small dark nucleus is pushed to the edge of the droplet, and the cell lies <b>beside a sinusoid</b> (blood channel), between the liver cells. The droplet is fat. Fat dissolves out when the tissue is prepared, so it leaves an empty white circle. The fat-storing cells that lie beside the sinusoids of the liver are the <b>hepatic stellate cells</b>, also called Ito cells.</p><p><b>One cell, several names.</b> Ito cell, hepatic stellate cell and perisinusoidal lipocyte all mean the same cell. \"Perisinusoidal\" means around the sinusoid, and \"lipocyte\" means fat cell. \"Stellate\" means star-shaped. In the liver, the plain name \"stellate cell\" means the Ito cell.</p><p><b>Where it lives and what it stores.</b> The Ito cell lies in the <b>space of Disse</b>, the thin gap between the lining of the sinusoid and the liver cells. It stores fat droplets and <b>vitamin A</b>. It is the body's main vitamin A store, and it releases the vitamin as needed.</p><p><b>What it does after injury.</b> Alcohol, fat or viruses can injure the liver. The Ito cells then activate into cells that resemble myofibroblasts, and they secrete collagen and other matrix. This scar tissue is called fibrosis. A trichrome stain shows the collagen in blue.</p><p><b>The neighbors in this picture.</b></p><ul><li><b>Hepatocytes (liver cells):</b> the large polygonal cells that fill the field. Each has a round central nucleus and grainy pink-purple cytoplasm.</li><li><b>Sinusoids:</b> the narrow pale channels between the plates of hepatocytes. A few red blood cells lie in them.</li><li><b>Kupffer cells:</b> the macrophages of the liver. They sit inside the sinusoid lumen and hold engulfed brown or yellow debris.</li></ul><p><b>Cell names that belong to other organs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Stave cells:</b> the long endothelial cells that line the venous sinuses of the spleen.</li><li><b>Epithelioreticular cells:</b> the cells that form the framework of the thymus.</li></ul><p>Neither of these is found in the liver.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-9.jpg","in":"Liver, H&E, high power (scale bar 20 µm): four white arrows on cells that hold one large, clear, round fat droplet with a small dark nucleus pushed to its edge, lying beside the sinusoids between the hepatocytes (fat-storing stellate cells, also called Ito cells).","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":9,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"67"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Fatty liver: ultrasound finds fat, biopsy confirms steatohepatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Steatohepatitis biopsy: fat droplets, inflammatory clusters, spider-web trichrome fibrosis","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-2-1","s":"A 45 year-old man visits his physician as routine follow-up for management of his type 2 diabetes mellitus and metabolic syndrome. He noted he had been feeling more tired than usual with mild dull pain in the right upper quadrant. The physical examination of the abdomen was limited by the patient's abdominal (central) obesity (waist circumference 42 inches). Although the patient's serum transaminase levels had always been within the normal range, at this visit the values were twice the upper limit of normal. Concerned about these new findings, the physician orders an abdominal ultrasound examination. Why?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Abdominal ultrasonography can detect fat in the liver (increased echogenicity), i.e., hepatic steatosis / non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)","a":null,"why":"<b>To detect fat in the liver.</b> Fatty liver looks <b>brighter (increased echogenicity)</b> on ultrasound. With diabetes, metabolic syndrome, central obesity and newly raised liver enzymes (transaminases), the likely cause is <b>NAFLD</b> (nonalcoholic fatty liver disease): fat in hepatocytes without heavy drinking or another cause.<br>Fat builds up first in <b>zone 3</b> hepatocytes, around the central vein.<br>Next step: ultrasound can't separate simple fat from NASH (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis); that needs a biopsy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)","H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":2,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":2,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":2,"f":"G025"}],"tier":2,"like":"G115-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 90"},{"id":"G024-3-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-4: 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and central obesity; fatigue, dull RUQ pain; transaminases twice the upper limit of normal; ultrasound shows fatty liver (NAFLD); biopsy shows steatosis, inflammation and 'spider web like' pericellular fibrosis on trichrome = NASH.] The term nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) is applied to NAFLD patients who demonstrate steatohepatitic injury which confers an increased risk of advanced fibrotic liver disease and its serious complications (e.g., decompensated cirrhosis, carcinoma). How can the physician determine if his patient with NAFLD has NASH and the associated risk of advanced liver disease?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Liver biopsy (gold standard; ultrasound cannot distinguish NAFL from NASH): steatosis + inflammation + pericellular ('spider web') fibrosis on trichrome = NASH","a":null,"why":"<b>Liver biopsy</b> is the gold standard. Ultrasound sees fat but cannot tell simple fatty liver (NAFL, nonalcoholic fatty liver) from <b>NASH</b> (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis).<br>NASH on biopsy = <b>steatosis</b> (large fat droplets in hepatocytes) + <b>inflammation</b> (clusters of inflammatory cells) + <b>pericellular 'spider web' fibrosis</b> on trichrome stain (collagen stains blue).<br>That fibrosis is laid down by activated Ito (hepatic stellate) cells in the space of Disse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":3,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":3,"f":"G025"}],"tier":2,"like":"G115-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 91"},{"id":"G115-2-1","s":"A 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus, metabolic syndrome and abdominal (central) obesity feels more tired than usual and has mild dull pain in the right upper quadrant. His serum transaminase levels, previously normal, are now twice the upper limit of normal. His physician orders an abdominal ultrasound examination. What can the ultrasound detect in his liver?","o":["A. Activated hepatic stellate cells in the space of Disse","B. Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity","C. Steatohepatitis (inflammation and injury of hepatocytes)","D. \"Spider web like\" fibrosis around the hepatocytes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fat in the liver (steatosis), seen as increased echogenicity.</b> A fatty liver reflects more sound, so it looks <b>brighter</b> on ultrasound. With type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, central obesity and newly raised transaminases (liver enzymes), fat in the liver is the likely cause.<br>A. Activated stellate cells are single cells in the space of Disse; only a microscope shows them.<br>C. Steatohepatitis (inflamed, injured hepatocytes) cannot be told from simple fat on ultrasound; that takes a biopsy.<br>D. Fibrosis around single hepatocytes shows on a biopsy stained with trichrome, not on ultrasound.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G115"}],"tier":2,"like":"G115-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 92"},{"id":"G115-3-1","s":"A man with metabolic syndrome and raised serum transaminases, who drinks no alcohol, has fat in the liver (steatosis) on ultrasound: non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). Why is this finding important?","o":["A. Fat in the liver proves that cirrhosis is already present","B. Fat in the liver is the mark of viral hepatitis in the periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes","C. Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, NASH) carries an increased risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma","D. Fat in the liver shows that a gallstone is blocking the common bile duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, NASH) carries an increased risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Fatty liver with steatohepatitic injury (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, NASH) carries an increased risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and carcinoma.</b> Fat in the liver matters because of what can come with it. In some patients the fat is joined by inflammation and injury of the liver cells (hepatocytes), and that combination can scar the liver. This man's raised transaminases point that way. Transaminases are enzymes that leak into the blood from injured hepatocytes.</p><p><b>What fatty liver is.</b> Fat droplets build up inside the hepatocytes. This is called <b>steatosis</b>. It is linked to obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and alcohol. When alcohol is not the cause, the name is nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD).</p><p><b>The two forms of NAFLD.</b></p><ul><li><b>Nonalcoholic fatty liver (NAFL):</b> fat only.</li><li><b>Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH):</b> fat plus inflammation and hepatocyte injury. This form can lead to fibrosis (scarring), and it carries the higher risk of advanced fibrosis, cirrhosis and liver cancer.</li></ul><p>Fat by itself does not prove that any scar is present.</p><p><b>How the two forms are told apart.</b> Ultrasound shows the fat, because a fatty liver looks brighter (more echogenic). But ultrasound cannot tell NAFL from NASH. <b>Liver biopsy is the gold standard.</b> On the biopsy a trichrome stain colors collagen blue and shows any scar.</p><p><b>Where the scar comes from.</b> Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells) lie in the space of Disse, the thin gap between the blood channels and the hepatocytes. Injury activates them, and they secrete collagen. The scar appears first around the central veins.</p><p><b>Cirrhosis, the end stage.</b> Bands of fibrous scar surround nodules of regrowing hepatocytes, and the normal lobule pattern is lost. Three things follow:</p><ul><li><b>Low albumin:</b> fewer working hepatocytes make less of it, which causes swelling and fluid in the belly.</li><li><b>Fewer clotting factors:</b> easy bruising and bleeding.</li><li><b>High portal pressure:</b> the scar blocks blood flow through the liver, which leads to swollen veins called varices.</li></ul><p>The main causes of cirrhosis are long-term heavy alcohol use, fatty liver disease and chronic viral hepatitis.</p><p><b>Regeneration.</b> Hepatocytes are stable cells and can regrow lost liver. After severe injury, progenitor cells in the canals of Hering, small bile channels at the edge of the lobule, help.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)","H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":3,"f":"G115"}],"tier":1,"n":"68"},{"id":"G115-3-2","s":"Ultrasound shows a fatty liver in a man with metabolic syndrome and raised serum transaminases who drinks no alcohol (non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, NAFLD). How can his physician determine whether he has nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), with its risk of advanced liver disease?","o":["A. Repeat the abdominal ultrasound","B. Measure the serum transaminase levels again","C. Liver biopsy","D. Check the metabolic syndrome criteria (waist circumference, fasting glucose, triglycerides)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver biopsy","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Liver biopsy.</b> Steatohepatitis is defined by what the tissue shows: <b>fat droplets in hepatocytes, clusters of inflammatory cells, and fibrosis around the hepatocytes</b> (a blue 'spider web' on a trichrome stain). Only a biopsy shows these, so it is the gold standard.<br>A. Ultrasound shows only that fat is present.<br>B. Transaminases show that hepatocytes are leaking enzymes, not whether there is inflammation or scar.<br>D. The metabolic syndrome criteria describe the patient's risk, not the state of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":3,"f":"G115"}],"tier":2,"like":"G115-3-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 93"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Cirrhosis: fibrosis, low albumin, bruising","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cells secrete matrix after injury, causing fibrosis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52","lg":[["HS","hepatic stellate (Ito) cell with lipid droplets"],["K","Kupffer cells"],["PS","perisinusoidal space of Disse (arrow)"],["E","endothelial cell"],["H","hepatocytes"],["S","sinusoid"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-49.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte rough endoplasmic reticulum makes plasma proteins such as albumin","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 49","lg":[["arrow 1","plasma proteins made on rough endoplasmic reticulum, released into the perisinusoidal space"],["arrow 2","toxins, bilirubin and bile acids taken up from the perisinusoidal space, processed in smooth endoplasmic reticulum, secreted into the bile canaliculus"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"],["SER","smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],["double arrow 3","glucose taken up and stored as glycogen, released when needed"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-78-1","s":"Which of the following statements is most consistent with a patient with chronic and severe alcohol use disorder?","o":["A. Scarred and fibrotic liver, hypoalbuminemia, easy bruising","B. High blood sugar, constant thirst, weight loss","C. Diarrhea, stunted growth, itchy rash on elbows and knees","D. Auto-asplenia, painful enlarged joints, anemia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Scarred and fibrotic liver, hypoalbuminemia, easy bruising","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Long, heavy alcohol use leads to <b>cirrhosis</b>: a <b>scarred, fibrotic liver</b> (scar laid down by Ito cells). Fewer working hepatocytes make less <b>albumin</b> (hypoalbuminemia) and fewer <b>clotting factors</b> (easy bruising).<br>B. High blood sugar, thirst, weight loss: diabetes mellitus.<br>C. Diarrhea, poor growth, itchy rash on elbows and knees: celiac disease (dermatitis herpetiformis).<br>D. Autosplenectomy, painful joints, anemia: sickle cell disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":78,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 94"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Bile canaliculi → canals of Hering → ductules","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-54.jpg","cap":"Canaliculi between hepatocytes; canal of Hering cholangiocytes; interlobular ductule","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 54","lg":[["1, 2, 3 and cut-off words at the edges of the top drawing","labels of an atlas picture hidden behind the drawing; they point to nothing visible"],["three unlabeled lines at top of the lower drawing","bile canaliculi (green channels between hepatocytes; label covered)"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-42.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: canaliculus sealed by tight junctions; drawing of canals of Hering","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-60-1","s":"The structure labeled \"BC\" is a bile canaliculus. What is its function?","o":["A. Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes, and bring it to the hepatic vein","B. Direct bile into the space of Disse for filtration","C. Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes and form the bile ductules","D. Direct bile from the gallbladder to supply the hepatocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes and form the bile ductules","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Bile canaliculi are tiny channels formed by grooves between neighboring hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions. They collect bile and carry it toward the portal triad, into the <b>canals of Hering</b> and then the <b>bile ductules</b>.<br>A. The hepatic vein carries blood, not bile.<br>B. Bile never enters the space of Disse; tight junctions keep bile away from blood.<br>D. Backward: bile flows from hepatocytes to the gallbladder, not the other way.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"TEM of hepatocyte with a small lumen between adjacent hepatocytes (BC) circled; not needed (stem names it).","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":60,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 95"},{"id":"F3949-32-1","s":"What are morphological differences between Bile canaliculi and Canal of Herring differ?","o":["A. Bile canaliculi are lined by a simple squamous, and canals of herring are lined by cholangiocytes","B. Bile canaliculi are lined by hepatocytes bound by tight junctions and Canal of Hering are lined by cholangiocytes (cuboidal epithelium specialized for water reabsorption)","C. Bile canaliculi are lined by Hepatocytes and Canal of Hering are lined by a simple squamous epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile canaliculi are lined by hepatocytes bound by tight junctions and Canal of Hering are lined by cholangiocytes (cuboidal epithelium specialized for water reabsorption)","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Bile canaliculi</b> have no lining cells of their own: they are walled by the membranes of <b>two hepatocytes</b> sealed by tight junctions. The <b>canal of Hering</b> is the first channel lined by <b>cholangiocytes</b> (cuboidal epithelium).<br>A. Canaliculi are not lined by simple squamous cells.<br>C. Canaliculi are hepatocyte-lined, but the canal of Hering is cuboidal, not simple squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":32,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 96"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Bile canaliculus on a slide: thin channel between hepatocytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G116-42.jpg","cap":"Bile canaliculi between hepatocytes: faint on H&E, clear at high power and electron microscopy","where":"2026 lab, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"G118-2-1","s":"Identify the structure in the blue outline in the image below.","o":["A. Bile canaliculi","B. Sinusoidal space","C. Splenic sinus","D. Marrow cavity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile canaliculi","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Bile canaliculi.</b> This is liver at high power. The large cells with round nuclei are hepatocytes (liver cells). The blue outline traces a <b>thin, branching channel that runs between the facing sides of neighboring hepatocytes</b>, inside the plate of cells. It is much narrower than a nucleus, and it has no cells of its own. That is a bile canaliculus. The same kind of thin pink line can be seen between other hepatocytes in the field.</p><p><b>What a bile canaliculus is.</b> It is a tiny groove between two hepatocytes. It has no lining cells of its own, because its wall is simply the membranes of the two hepatocytes. <b>Tight junctions</b> seal it on both sides, so bile cannot leak toward the blood. Hepatocyte microvilli project into it. This is the apical surface of the hepatocyte, the surface that secretes bile. The canaliculi branch and join into a network that runs all through the plate.</p><p><b>Canaliculus or sinusoid.</b> A sinusoid is a blood channel. It is much wider, it lies between the plates and not inside them, it is lined by endothelial cells, and it holds blood cells. In this picture the wide pale spaces at the sides of the plates are sinusoids.</p><p><b>The path of bile.</b></p><ol><li><b>Bile canaliculi,</b> inside the plates.</li><li><b>Canal of Hering</b> (intralobular bile ductule), at the edge of the lobule. It is lined partly by hepatocytes and partly by cuboidal bile duct cells (cholangiocytes). It holds progenitor cells that help the liver regenerate after injury.</li><li><b>Interlobular bile ductule,</b> in the portal triad. It is lined by cuboidal to columnar cholangiocytes.</li><li><b>Right and left hepatic ducts,</b> then the <b>common hepatic duct</b>.</li><li>From there bile goes through the <b>cystic duct</b> to the gallbladder for storage, or down the <b>common bile duct</b> to the duodenum.</li></ol><p><b>Blood and bile flow in opposite directions.</b> Blood runs from the portal triad at the edge of the lobule, through the sinusoids, to the central vein in the middle. Bile runs from the hepatocytes outward, toward the portal triad.</p><p><b>Spaces of other organs.</b> A splenic sinus is a blood channel in the red pulp of the spleen. A marrow cavity is the space inside a bone, filled with blood-forming cells and fat. A field made entirely of hepatocytes is liver.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G118-2.jpg","in":"Liver at high power (University of Wisconsin slide 20): hepatocytes with pale sinusoids between the plates; a blue line traces a thin, branching pink channel that runs between the faces of neighbouring hepatocytes (bile canaliculus); a dark blue box hides the label.","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)","H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clicker","n":"GI histology Kahoot 2026 (Dr. Vidal, 17 picture questions)","sl":2,"f":"G118"}],"tier":1,"n":"69"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Canal of Hering → liver regeneration","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-54.jpg","cap":"Canal of Hering: cuboidal cholangiocytes; contributes to regeneration after injury","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 54","lg":[["1, 2, 3 and cut-off words at the edges of the top drawing","labels of an atlas picture hidden behind the drawing; they point to nothing visible"],["three unlabeled lines at top of the lower drawing","bile canaliculi (green channels between hepatocytes; label covered)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-247-1","s":"Which part of the liver contributes to its regeneration following an injury?","o":["A. Interlobular Bile ductule","B. Bile Canaliculi","C. Space of Mall","D. Intralobular bile ductule"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Intralobular bile ductule","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Intralobular bile ductule (canal of Hering)</b>: a cholangiocyte-lined channel holding progenitor cells that can make new hepatocytes and cholangiocytes after injury (lecture: contributes to regeneration).<br>A. Interlobular bile ductule: sits in the portal triad and drains bile.<br>B. Bile canaliculi: grooves between hepatocytes with no lining cells of their own.<br>C. Space of Mall: the periportal space between the triad's connective tissue and the limiting plate; drains lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":247,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 97"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Blood to central vein; bile out to triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Blood flows portal field to central vein; bile flows back toward ductule","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47","lg":[["arrows","flow: blood (blue venous, red arterial) along the sinusoid into the central vein; bile (green) the other way, to the bile ductule"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Classic lobule, portal lobule and acinus compared: shapes, centers, roles","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G021-41.jpg","cap":"Blood and bile flow in opposite directions along the hepatocyte plate","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 41","lg":[["white arrows (blue sinusoids)","blood flow from portal vein and hepatic artery branches to the central vein"],["white arrows (green channel)","bile flow toward the bile duct"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G024-26-1","s":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the structure of liver?","o":["A. Terminal hepatic venule (central vein) is located at the center of the portal lobule","B. The zone 1 is more susceptible to hypoxic injury","C. Hepatic arteriole carries blood rich in nutrients absorbed from GI tract","D. The flow of bile is always directed towards the center of classic hepatic lobule","E. Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein.</b> Blood enters a liver lobule at its edge, from the vessels of the portal triads. It then flows through the <b>sinusoids</b>, the wide blood channels between the plates of liver cells (hepatocytes). All the sinusoids of a lobule run toward its middle and open straight into the <b>central vein</b>, which is also called the terminal hepatic venule. So this statement is true exactly as written.</p><p><b>The path of blood, in order.</b></p><ol><li><b>Portal venule and hepatic arteriole,</b> in the portal triad at the corner of the lobule.</li><li><b>Sinusoids,</b> where the two kinds of blood mix.</li><li><b>Central vein,</b> in the middle of the lobule.</li><li><b>Hepatic veins,</b> then the inferior vena cava.</li></ol><p><b>What each incoming vessel carries.</b> The <b>portal venule</b> is a branch of the portal vein. It brings blood from the gut that is rich in absorbed nutrients and poor in oxygen, and it supplies about 75% of the flow. The <b>hepatic arteriole</b> brings oxygen-rich arterial blood, about 25% of the flow.</p><p><b>Bile flows the opposite way.</b> Hepatocytes secrete bile into the bile canaliculi, the tiny grooves between neighboring hepatocytes. From there bile runs outward, away from the central vein, to the bile ductule in the portal triad. So in the classic lobule, blood moves toward the center and bile moves toward the edge.</p><p><b>Which structure sits at the center of each lobule model.</b></p><ul><li><b>Classic lobule:</b> a hexagon with the <b>central vein</b> in the center and portal triads at the corners.</li><li><b>Portal lobule:</b> a triangle with a <b>portal triad</b> in the center and three central veins at the corners.</li><li><b>Portal acinus</b> (hepatic acinus): a diamond between two central veins. Its short axis runs between two portal triads.</li></ul><p><b>The zones and oxygen.</b> Blood loses oxygen as it flows along the sinusoid. <b>Zone 1</b>, next to the portal triad, gets the most oxygen. <b>Zone 3</b>, next to the central vein, gets the least. Zone 3 is therefore the zone most easily injured by hypoxia (lack of oxygen), as in shock or heart failure.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 56 (Classical lobule - Anatomical)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)","H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":22,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":26,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":26,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":34,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":26,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":28,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"70"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Portal lobule: bile-drainage triangle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Portal lobule: green triangle of three central veins around one triad","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G020-58.jpg","cap":"Classic lobule, portal lobule and hepatic acinus outlined on one map","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 58","lg":[["PA","portal area (portal triad)"],["CV","central vein"],["1, 2, 3","acinus zones: 1 next to the portal area (most oxygen), 3 next to the central veins (least)"],["yellow lines","borders between the zones"],["dark red arrows","blood flow from the portal area to the central veins"],["blue arrows","blood flow into the central vein of a classic lobule"],["green arrows","bile flow toward the portal area at the center of the portal lobule"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-218-1","s":"What type of hepatic lobule is depicted below?","o":["A. Hepatic Acinus","B. Portal lobule","C. Classic lobule"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal lobule","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Portal lobule.</b> Read the drawing piece by piece. The three hexagons are three classic lobules, and the large blue circle in the middle of each one is its central vein. The shaded <b>triangle</b> joins those three central veins. At the center of the triangle sits a group of three small circles, which is a <b>portal triad</b>: a green bile duct, a red branch of the hepatic artery and a blue branch of the portal vein. A triangle with a portal triad in its center and central veins at its corners is the portal lobule. The green arrows show bile running in toward the bile duct of that triad.</p><p><b>Three ways to draw a liver lobule.</b> The liver tissue is the same in all three. Each model only draws the borders in a different place, to make one function easy to see.</p><ul><li><b>Classic lobule:</b> a hexagon. The central vein is in the center and the portal triads are at the corners. It shows the anatomy and the blood draining toward the central vein.</li><li><b>Portal lobule:</b> a triangle. A portal triad is in the center and three central veins are at the corners. It shows <b>bile secretion</b>: all the bile made inside the triangle drains to the bile duct of the central triad.</li><li><b>Portal acinus</b> (also called the hepatic acinus): a diamond between two central veins. Its short axis runs between two portal triads, along the vessels that feed it. It shows blood supply and metabolism, which means the gradient of oxygen and nutrients.</li></ul><p><b>Why the portal lobule is the bile view.</b> Bile is the exocrine product of the liver, the product that leaves through a duct. Most exocrine glands are drawn with the duct in the middle of the lobule. The portal lobule draws the liver the same way, with the bile duct at the center.</p><p><b>The two flows.</b> Blood enters at the portal triad and runs through the sinusoids, the blood channels between the liver cells, to the central vein. Bile runs the opposite way. Liver cells secrete it into tiny grooves called bile canaliculi, and it flows outward to the bile duct in the triad. That is why the green arrows in this drawing point toward the triad.</p><p><b>The zones of the acinus.</b> Blood loses oxygen and nutrients as it flows. Zone 1 lies nearest the portal triad and gets the most oxygen. Zone 2 is in between. Zone 3 lies nearest the central vein and gets the least.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-218.jpg","in":"Diagram of three adjacent hexagonal classic lobules; a shaded triangle joins their three central veins around a single portal triad at its centre.","tg":["H16 slide 56 (Classical lobule - Anatomical)","H16 slide 55 (Lobules of the liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":218,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["hexagons","classic lobules"],["large blue circles","central veins"],["shaded triangle","portal lobule (centered on one portal triad)"],["small circles at center","portal triad: green bile duct, red hepatic artery, blue portal vein"],["green arrows","bile flow toward the bile duct"]],"n":"71"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 3 least oxygen → hypoxic injury","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Acinus zones: zone 1 by triad, zone 3 by central vein, hypoxia-prone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G024-15.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 circled at the central vein; zone 1 beside the portal tract","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 15","lg":[["1","zone 1 (nearest the portal triad; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (around the central vein; least oxygen)"],["circles","zone 3 around each central vein"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-99-1","s":"Which zone has the poorest oxygen supply?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3 (zone 3, the zone next to the central vein)","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. 3.</b> First find the two landmarks in the picture. At the lower left is a <b>portal tract</b>, which is a portal triad with its connective tissue. It shows as an opening wrapped in connective tissue, with small tubes beside it. At the upper right is a <b>central vein</b>: an opening that stands alone, with a thin wall. The numbers run along the line between them, with 1 next to the portal tract and 3 next to the central vein. Blood enters the liver tissue at the portal tract and flows toward the central vein, and the liver cells take oxygen out of it all along the way. So the cells at number 3, the last to be reached, get the <b>poorest oxygen supply</b>.</p><p><b>Why the oxygen runs down.</b> The branch of the hepatic artery in the portal tract brings oxygen-rich blood, about 25% of the flow. The branch of the portal vein brings oxygen-poor blood from the gut, about 75% of the flow. The two mix in the sinusoids, the blood channels between the plates of liver cells (hepatocytes). So the blood is not rich in oxygen even at the start, and each hepatocyte it passes takes some.</p><p><b>The portal acinus.</b> The zones belong to a model of the liver called the portal acinus (hepatic acinus). It is a diamond-shaped area between two central veins. Its short axis runs between two portal triads, along the vessels that feed it. The model shows blood supply and metabolism.</p><p><b>The three zones.</b></p><ul><li><b>Zone 1 (periportal):</b> nearest the portal triad. It gets the most oxygen and is the first to meet incoming blood. It does oxidative metabolism. It is the zone most affected by viral hepatitis.</li><li><b>Zone 2 (intermediate):</b> in between, with a medium oxygen supply.</li><li><b>Zone 3 (centrilobular):</b> nearest the central vein. It gets the least oxygen. It does the most detoxification of drugs and ammonia, because it has the most drug-metabolizing enzymes.</li></ul><p><b>What injures zone 3.</b> Because it lives on the least oxygen, zone 3 is hit first by <b>hypoxia</b> (lack of oxygen). In shock or heart failure its cells die first. This is called ischemic necrosis, which means cell death from too little blood flow. Fat also builds up in zone 3, and acetaminophen and alcohol damage it.</p><p><b>Finding the zones in any section.</b> A portal triad shows three tubes in connective tissue: a vein, an artery and a bile duct. A central vein stands alone. Zone 1 hugs the triad, and zone 3 hugs the central vein.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-99-1.jpg","in":"Two pictures. Left: drawing of two classic liver lobules with the acinus zones numbered 1, 2, 3 from the line between two portal triads out to each central vein. Right: liver H&E with a portal tract at lower left and a central vein at upper right; the numbers 1, 2, 3 sit along the line between them (1 next to the portal tract, 3 next to the central vein). The question asks which numbered zone gets the least oxygen. The slide's question and answer text are white on a white background, so the slide picture shows no answer. Same H&E micrograph as the bank card F0753-99-1 (checked against F0753 slide 99) and the same slide as G021 slide 40.","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty review","n":"Digestive system supplemental review Fall 2026","sl":42,"f":"G117"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2025 review","n":"Digestive system supplemental review Fall 2025","sl":40,"f":"G021"},{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Accessory glands lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G116"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":47,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":202,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1,"lg":[["1","zone 1 (next to the portal tract, lower left; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (next to the central vein, upper right; least oxygen)"]],"n":"72"},{"id":"F3949-35-1","s":"Which zone is highly oxygenated and which zone is least oxygenated?","o":["A. Zone 3 AKA Centrilobular zone","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 1 AKA Periportal zone"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Highly oxygenated = Zone 1 (periportal, nearest the portal triad); least oxygenated = Zone 3 (centrilobular)","a":"C","why":"<b>Most oxygen: C, zone 1 (periportal)</b>, next to the portal triad, where the hepatic arteriole delivers fresh oxygen. <b>Least oxygen: A, zone 3 (centrilobular)</b>, by the central vein, last in line along the sinusoid.<br>So zone 1 does oxidative work such as protein synthesis and is hit by viral hepatitis; zone 3 does drug and ammonia detoxification and is hit first by hypoxia.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate on both counts.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only C (zone 1, most oxygenated), but the stem also asks for the least-oxygenated zone, which is A (zone 3); both are correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":35,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-99-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 98"},{"id":"F0743-20-1","s":"A 25-year-old man drinks an unknown substance and a few hours later is taken to the ER with acute abdominal pain. The substance induced acute liver damage due to hypoxia (low oxygen supply). Which of the zone's shown below would be the most prone to initially undergo this type of damage?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular), around the central vein. Blood loses oxygen as it moves along the sinusoid from the portal triad, so zone 3 gets the <b>least oxygen</b> and is the <b>first to die in hypoxia</b>.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): best oxygen supply; viral hepatitis targets it.<br>B. Zone 2 (intermediate): middle oxygen supply.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver H&E with 1, 2, 3 marked from portal tract to central vein (stem asks by number, so the picture shows which number is pericentral).","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":20,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-99-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 99"},{"id":"F1221-167-2","s":"Which portion of the hepatic acinus would be most likely affected by ischemic damage?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Danger Zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular): farthest from the oxygen-rich blood entering at the portal triad, so it is the <b>first to undergo ischemic necrosis</b> (shock, heart failure).<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): best oxygen; hit by viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate oxygen.<br>D. 'Danger zone' is not a hepatic zone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":167,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-99-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 100"},{"id":"F0755-249-1","s":"Which zone of the portal acinus is most affected by hypoxia? What is zone three prone to? What is zone one affected by? Where is is nearest to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Zone 3 is most affected by hypoxia; zone 3 is prone to ischemic necrosis, fat accumulation and alcohol/acetaminophen damage; zone 1 is affected by viral hepatitis and is nearest to the portal triad","a":null,"why":"<b>Zone 3 (centrilobular, by the central vein)</b>: least oxygen, so <b>most affected by hypoxia</b>; prone to <b>ischemic necrosis, fat accumulation, and acetaminophen and alcohol damage</b> (it does most drug and ammonia detoxification).<br><b>Zone 1 (periportal)</b>: <b>nearest the portal triad</b>, most oxygen, oxidative metabolism; <b>most affected in viral hepatitis</b>.<br>Memory hook: zone 1 is first to meet incoming blood; zone 3 is last in line for oxygen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":249,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"like":"F0753-99-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 101"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 3: acetaminophen, alcohol, fat, toxins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 by central vein: detoxification, hypoxia, fat, acetaminophen and alcohol damage","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G024-19.jpg","cap":"Zone III: least oxygen, drug metabolism; vulnerable to ischemic and toxic injury","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 19","lg":[["1","zone 1 (nearest the portal triad; most oxygen)"],["2","zone 2 (intermediate)"],["3","zone 3 (around the central vein; least oxygen)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"G024-18-1","s":"A 65 year-old man undergoes an uncomplicated open heart operation for replacement of a stenotic aortic valve. Post-operatively, he is prescribed extra strength acetaminophen (Tylenol) for pain control. Routine measurement of serum transaminase measurements on post-operative day 5 reveal elevations to five times the normal values indicating hepatocellular injury. Which of the acinar zones shown below would be the most likely site of injury?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Zone 3.</b> Zone 3 is the ring of liver cells (hepatocytes) around the central vein. Two facts in the story point to it. First, the injury follows <b>acetaminophen</b>, and zone 3 holds the most drug-metabolizing enzymes, so it is where the drug does its damage. Second, he has just had open heart surgery, when blood flow to the liver can fall, and zone 3 has the <b>least oxygen</b> to begin with. The raised transaminases confirm that hepatocytes are injured. Transaminases are enzymes that leak into the blood from damaged hepatocytes.</p><p><b>How the zones are laid out.</b> Blood enters the liver tissue at the portal triad and flows through the sinusoids (the blood channels between the hepatocytes) to the central vein. It loses oxygen and nutrients along the way. The hepatocytes along this path form the three zones of the portal acinus, a model of the liver based on blood supply. In a section, the numbers 1, 2 and 3 run from the portal triad to the central vein.</p><p><b>What each zone does and what injures it.</b></p><ul><li><b>Zone 1 (periportal):</b> nearest the portal triad. It has the most oxygen and does oxidative metabolism. It meets the incoming blood first, and it is the zone most affected by viral hepatitis.</li><li><b>Zone 2 (intermediate):</b> in between.</li><li><b>Zone 3 (centrilobular):</b> nearest the central vein. It has the least oxygen and detoxifies drugs and ammonia. It is the zone most affected by hypoxia (lack of oxygen) and ischemic necrosis (cell death from too little blood flow), as in shock or heart failure. It is also the zone most affected by fat build-up, acetaminophen and alcohol.</li></ul><p><b>Why the drug enzymes make zone 3 vulnerable.</b> Drugs are processed in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the hepatocyte. The enzymes there turn part of an acetaminophen dose into a reactive, toxic product. The cell normally neutralizes it. When the dose is high or the cell is already stressed, the toxic product builds up and kills the cell. The cells with the most of these enzymes make the most toxic product, and those are the zone 3 cells.</p><p><b>Why low oxygen strikes the same place.</b> Zone 3 is the last stop for the blood, so any fall in blood flow or in blood oxygen is felt there first. Dead hepatocytes around the central veins are the typical finding, called centrilobular necrosis.</p><p><b>Recovery.</b> Hepatocytes are stable cells. They rarely divide, but they can start dividing again to replace lost liver.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver, H&E: a portal tract at lower left and a central vein at upper right; the numbers 1, 2, 3 are placed along the line from the portal tract to the central vein. Same micrograph as G024 slide 18. Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":14,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":18,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":23,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":18,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1,"n":"73"},{"id":"F1221-167-1","s":"Which portion of the hepatic acinus would be most likely affected by ingestion of toxic chemicals?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Danger Zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 1","a":"A","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular). The lecture ties <b>drug detoxification</b> and <b>acetaminophen and alcohol damage</b> to zone 3: its hepatocytes hold the most drug-metabolizing enzymes (smooth endoplasmic reticulum), which turn many chemicals into toxic products, and it has the least oxygen.<br>A. Zone 1: first to meet incoming blood, but the lecture links it to viral hepatitis (board books list a few direct toxins, such as cocaine, there).<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate.<br>D. 'Danger zone' is not a zone.","ex":"","fl":["Both answers have a basis: First Aid says ingested toxins (e.g. cocaine) reach periportal zone 1 first, but the 2026 lecture (G020:59) ties drug, acetaminophen and alcohol injury to centrilobular zone 3 (most CYP450). On this year's exam, zone 3 is the safer answer for drug/alcohol toxicity.","The source keyed A (zone 1, first to meet ingested substances); the 2026 lecture places drug and toxin injury (acetaminophen, alcohol) in zone 3."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":167,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 102"},{"id":"F3952-103-1","s":"Which zone of the hepatic lobule is most affected by cirrhosis?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Portal Triad"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular). The usual causes of cirrhosis in the lecture, <b>alcohol</b> and <b>fatty liver</b>, injure zone 3 first: it gets the least oxygen and gathers fat first, and the scarring starts around the central vein.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): mainly hit by viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate; not the first target.<br>D. The portal triad is connective tissue with vessels, not an acinar zone of hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":103,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-18-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 103"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 1 periportal: viral hepatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Zone 1 periportal, nearest the triad: most affected in viral hepatitis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Hepatic acinus: zone I most oxygenated, zone III least","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-223-1","s":"A patient presents to the ER with fever, nausea, vomiting and RUQ pain. Serologic testing reveals the most likely diagnosis to be viral hepatitis. Which zone of the hepatic acinus is most likely to be damaged?","o":["A. Intermediate zone","B. Centrilobular zone","C. Periportal zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Periportal zone","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Periportal zone (zone 1).</b> It sits next to the portal triad, so it is first to meet incoming blood, with its viruses and the immune cells that attack infected hepatocytes; the lecture lists zone 1 as <b>most affected in viral hepatitis</b>.<br>A. Intermediate zone (zone 2): not a typical first target.<br>B. Centrilobular zone (zone 3): hypoxia, acetaminophen, alcohol and fat injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":223,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 104"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder epithelium: simple columnar, absorbs water","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Simple columnar cells with microvilli and mitochondria; electron micrograph beside","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65","lg":[["LP","lamina propria"],["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium"],["M","muscularis (bracket line)"],["A","adventitia (bracket line)"],["G","mucus secretory granules"],["MV","microvilli"],["(b) arrows and arrowheads","spaces between folded lateral cell membranes (water leaves the cells here)"]]},{"src":"sl/G021-44.jpg","cap":"Columnar cells with microvilli and folded basolateral membrane for water uptake","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 44","lg":[["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium lining the folds"],["(a) LP","lamina propria (no muscularis mucosae)"],["(a) M","muscularis, bundles in all directions"],["(a) A","adventitia (liver side)"],["(b) G","mucus secretory granules"],["(b) MV","apical microvilli"],["(b) arrows","intercellular spaces along folded basolateral membranes"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-3","s":"3. The epithelium of the gallbladder is composed of","o":["A. simple columnar epithelium","B. psuedostratified epithelium","C. ciliated columnar epithelium","D. stratified squamous epithelium","E. simple squamous epithelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"simple columnar epithelium","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Simple columnar epithelium</b>, with microvilli, on a highly folded mucosa.<br>B. Pseudostratified epithelium lines the airways and epididymis, not the gallbladder.<br>C. Ciliated columnar lines the uterine tube and airways; gallbladder cells have microvilli, not cilia.<br>D. Stratified squamous lines the esophagus and skin.<br>E. Simple squamous lines vessels and body cavities (endothelium, mesothelium).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 105"},{"id":"F0722-165-1","s":"What type of epithelium is this? Found where?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli (abundant mitochondria); gallbladder","a":null,"why":"<b>Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli</b>, from the <b>gallbladder</b>. Clues: one layer of tall cells with apical <b>microvilli</b> (MV), many <b>mitochondria</b> and lateral infoldings; the drawing shows highly folded mucosa over smooth muscle with no glands.<br>Why: these cells pump sodium and absorb water to <b>concentrate bile</b>.<br>Mix-up: intestinal enterocytes also have microvilli, but intestinal mucosa has goblet cells, intestinal glands (crypts) and a muscularis mucosae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-165.jpg","in":"TEM of columnar cells with apical microvilli (MV) and many mitochondria, plus a drawing of the folded gallbladder mucosa with the epithelium highlighted.","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":165,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["MV","microvilli"],["G","apical mucus granules"],["arrows","intercellular spaces between folded side membranes (absorbed water exits here)"],["yellow box","simple columnar epithelium lining the mucosal folds"],["thin black lines (right)","pointer lines whose labels were cropped off"]],"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 106"},{"id":"F3952-107-1","s":"Cholangiocytes of the gallbladder mucosa are made of what tissue type and have what function?","o":["A. Stratified squamous epithelium specialized for bile secretion","B. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption","C. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for bile secretion","D. Simple cuboidal epithelium specialized for serous secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption.</b> Gallbladder lining cells have microvilli, many mitochondria and lateral infoldings; they pump sodium and absorb water to <b>concentrate bile</b>.<br>A. The gallbladder lining is not stratified squamous.<br>C. Right epithelium, wrong job: hepatocytes secrete bile; the gallbladder only stores and concentrates it.<br>D. Simple cuboidal serous secretion fits gland acini, not the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":107,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 107"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder wall: no muscularis mucosae or submucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-64.jpg","cap":"Labelled wall: epithelium, lamina propria, smooth muscle; no muscularis mucosae or submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 64"},{"src":"sl/G021-44.jpg","cap":"Folded mucosa, lamina propria (no muscularis mucosae), muscularis, serosa or adventitia","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 44","lg":[["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium lining the folds"],["(a) LP","lamina propria (no muscularis mucosae)"],["(a) M","muscularis, bundles in all directions"],["(a) A","adventitia (liver side)"],["(b) G","mucus secretory granules"],["(b) MV","apical microvilli"],["(b) arrows","intercellular spaces along folded basolateral membranes"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-164-1","s":"Does the Gallbladder have a muscularis mucosa or submucosa??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"No. The gallbladder mucosa is highly folded and it lacks a muscularis mucosa and a submucosa, but has a muscular layer and an adventitia","a":null,"why":"<b>No, neither.</b> The gallbladder wall is only: <b>mucosa</b> (simple columnar epithelium + lamina propria, highly folded) → <b>muscular layer</b> (randomly arranged smooth muscle with elastic fibers) → <b>adventitia</b> (serosa on the free surface).<br>Mix-up: the gut tube has a muscularis mucosae, then a submucosa, then the muscularis externa. Missing those two layers is a key way to call 'gallbladder' on a slide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":164,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3952-105-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 108"},{"id":"F3952-105-1","s":"What are the walls of the gallbladder?","o":["A. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, muscularis externa,","B. Lamina propria, muscularis propria, external adventitia","C. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, muscularis externa","D. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, adventitia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lamina propria, muscularis propria, external adventitia","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Lamina propria, muscularis propria, external adventitia.</b> The wall of the gallbladder has only <b>three layers</b>. The inner layer is the mucosa: a simple columnar epithelium resting on a <b>lamina propria</b> of loose connective tissue. The middle layer is a single muscular coat, the muscularis, which this answer calls the <b>muscularis propria</b>. The outer layer is the <b>adventitia</b>. The gallbladder has <b>no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa</b>. That is why the list goes straight from the lamina propria to the muscle.</p><p><b>The three layers in detail.</b></p><ul><li><b>Mucosa:</b> simple columnar epithelium plus lamina propria. It is highly folded when the gallbladder is empty. The branching folds look like villi, but they are not true villi.</li><li><b>Muscular layer (muscularis):</b> thin bundles of smooth muscle that run in random directions, with elastic fibers between them.</li><li><b>Adventitia:</b> thick connective tissue with blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves. It lies where the gallbladder is attached to the liver. The free surface is covered instead by serosa, the smooth lining of the abdominal cavity (peritoneum).</li></ul><p><b>How this differs from the gut tube.</b> The stomach and intestine have four layers: a mucosa that ends in a thin sheet of muscle called the muscularis mucosae, a submucosa, a thick muscularis externa in orderly layers, and a serosa or adventitia. The gallbladder has no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa, so its lamina propria lies directly on the muscle. It also has no glands, except a few mucous glands near its neck.</p><p><b>An epithelium built to absorb water.</b> The columnar cells have apical microvilli, many mitochondria and lateral infoldings (folded side membranes). With these the gallbladder absorbs water and salt from bile and concentrates it up to about 10 times.</p><p><b>Naming each digestive gland on a section.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parotid:</b> all dark serous acini (round clusters of cells that secrete a watery, protein-rich fluid), with fat cells and striated ducts.</li><li><b>Submandibular:</b> mostly serous acini, with some pale mucous tubules capped by half-moons of serous cells (demilunes), and striated ducts.</li><li><b>Sublingual:</b> mostly pale mucous tubules, with demilunes.</li><li><b>Pancreas:</b> dark serous acini with centroacinar cells (pale duct cells in the middle of the acinus), and pale islets (clusters of endocrine cells). Islets are found only here. The pancreas has no striated ducts, no myoepithelial cells and no mucous cells.</li><li><b>Liver:</b> plates of polygonal hepatocytes, sinusoids, central veins and portal triads. It has no acini around a lumen.</li><li><b>Gallbladder:</b> folded simple columnar mucosa and thin irregular smooth muscle.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Micrograph of gallbladder wall: folded mucosa with lamina propria (LP), smooth-muscle layer (M) and adventitia (A).","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":105,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1,"n":"74"},{"id":"F0754-231-1","s":"A 16 y/o female presents to the urgent care clinic with fatty, foul-smelling stool. She was diagnosed many years ago with Cystic Fibrosis and you are concerned she has a mucus plug blocking her bile duct. Describe the organization of the gallbladder.","o":["A. Simple cuboidal epithelium with cilia","B. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosa","C. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with microvilli","D. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and smooth muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and smooth muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, smooth muscle</b>, then adventitia: that is the gallbladder wall. Microvilli and many mitochondria help it absorb water to concentrate bile.<br>A. Simple cuboidal with cilia: not gallbladder; its lining cells are tall columnar with microvilli.<br>B. Lists a muscularis mucosae, which the gallbladder lacks.<br>C. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous lines the mouth and esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":231,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"like":"F3952-105-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 109"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder: folded mucosa, no true villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-74.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: branching mucosal folds that mimic villi, no glands beneath","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 74"},{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Mucosa folds mimic villi; simple columnar epithelium with microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65","lg":[["LP","lamina propria"],["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium"],["M","muscularis (bracket line)"],["A","adventitia (bracket line)"],["G","mucus secretory granules"],["MV","microvilli"],["(b) arrows and arrowheads","spaces between folded lateral cell membranes (water leaves the cells here)"]]},{"src":"sl/G024-31.jpg","cap":"Folded mucosa, lamina propria, random muscle bundles, adventitia against the liver","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 31","lg":[["arrows","simple columnar epithelium covering the mucosal folds"],["E","epithelium (simple columnar)"],["LP","lamina propria"],["SM","smooth muscle layer (muscularis; bundles run in all directions)"],["Ad","adventitia (where it lies against the liver)"],["BM","basement membrane"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-162-1","s":"Identify this structure. What's its function?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gallbladder; it stores and concentrates bile and contracts in response to CCK from the duodenum","a":null,"why":"<b>Gallbladder</b>: a <b>highly folded mucosa</b> of tall simple columnar cells; the branching folds mimic villi, but there are <b>no glands, goblet cells or muscularis mucosae</b> beneath them (unlike the intestine).<br><b>Function</b>: <b>stores and concentrates bile</b> by absorbing water; its smooth muscle <b>contracts in response to CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) from the duodenum after a fatty meal, pushing bile out through the cystic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-162.jpg","in":"Low-power pale section of a highly folded mucosa (branching folds that mimic villi) with no glands below.","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":162,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"lg":[["small black arrow","mouse cursor left in the screenshot, not a label"]],"like":"G024-30-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 110"},{"id":"G024-30-1","s":"A 45 year-old man with a history of cholelithiasis presents to the ED with severe right upper quadrant abdominal pain radiating to the right shoulder, nausea and fever. He is taken to the operating room where a cholecystectomy is performed. The resected gallbladder and a representative histologic section are displayed at right. All of the following are true EXCEPT:","o":["A. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile","B. Inflammation of the gallbladder is usually associated with gallstones","C. The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","D. The gallbladder is covered partly by serosa and partly adventitia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area.</b> The question asks for the one statement that is not true, and this is it. The gallbladder has <b>no true villi</b>. When the organ is empty, its mucosa is thrown into tall, branching <b>folds</b>, and in a section these folds look like villi. They are only folds of the whole mucosa, and they flatten as the gallbladder fills. True villi are the permanent finger-like projections of the small intestine.</p><p><b>What the gallbladder does.</b> It stores bile and concentrates it, up to about 10 times, by absorbing water and salt. Its lining is a simple columnar epithelium built for that work: the cells have apical microvilli, many mitochondria and folded side membranes (lateral infoldings). After a fatty meal, enteroendocrine (hormone-secreting) cells of the duodenum release the hormone <b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>. CCK makes the gallbladder contract and empty its bile into the duodenum.</p><p><b>Its wall and its covering.</b> The wall has three layers: the folded mucosa (epithelium and lamina propria), a thin layer of randomly arranged smooth muscle, and an outer layer of connective tissue. There is no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa. Where the gallbladder is attached to the liver, the outer layer is a thick <b>adventitia</b>. Its free surface is covered by <b>serosa</b>, the smooth lining of the abdominal cavity (peritoneum). So the organ is covered partly by serosa and partly by adventitia.</p><p><b>Gallstones (cholelithiasis).</b> There are two kinds.</p><ul><li><b>Cholesterol stones:</b> the most common.</li><li><b>Pigment stones:</b> made from the breakdown products of red blood cells, for example in sickle cell anemia.</li></ul><p>Stones cause pain in the right upper part of the abdomen after fatty meals, because that is when the gallbladder contracts.</p><p><b>Cholecystitis.</b> This is inflammation of the gallbladder. It usually happens when a stone blocks the cystic duct, the duct that leaves the gallbladder. It causes right upper quadrant pain that spreads to the right shoulder, with nausea and fever. That is this patient's picture. His treatment was cholecystectomy, the surgical removal of the gallbladder.</p><p><b>A related disease.</b> In cystic fibrosis, thick secretions can block the pancreatic ducts and the bile ducts. Digestive juices then fail to reach the gut, and the stools become fatty and foul-smelling.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened gallbladder packed with faceted brown gallstones (top) and a section of the inflamed gallbladder wall (bottom; Sternberg's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology, Fig. 38-9). Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2026","sl":28,"f":"G115"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":30,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":30,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":45,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":30,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":32,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1,"n":"75"},{"id":"F4081-76-1","s":"A 43 year old female with a history of sickle cell anemia presents with right upper quadrant pain. She says it's especially worse after eating fatty foods. She undergoes surgery, and the patient sample is pictured below. Which describes the histological features of the affected tissue?","o":["A. Sac-like groups of cells with a granular, basophilic basolateral surface, extensive RER, and centroacinar cells","B. A layer of simple cuboidal epithelium, highly folded lamina propria, a thick layer of smooth muscle, thick adventitia and serosa","C. Stratified squamous epithelium with layers of mucosa, mucus glands, submucosa, skeletal muscle and smooth muscle","D. Mucous cells, chief cells, parietal cells, enteroendocrine cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A layer of simple cuboidal epithelium, highly folded lamina propria, a thick layer of smooth muscle, thick adventitia and serosa","a":"B","why":"<b>B, the gallbladder.</b> Right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals plus sickle cell disease (pigment stones from hemolysis) means gallstones and cholecystectomy. Gallbladder: <b>highly folded mucosa, no muscularis mucosae or submucosa, smooth muscle layer, adventitia and serosa</b>. Wording slip: its epithelium is simple <b>columnar</b> with microvilli, and the muscle layer is thin.<br>A. Acini with centroacinar cells: pancreas.<br>C. Stratified squamous, skeletal muscle: esophagus.<br>D. Mucous, chief, parietal, enteroendocrine cells: stomach.","ex":"","fl":["B is still the best choice (the only gallbladder option), but its wording is wrong in one point: gallbladder mucosa is lined by simple COLUMNAR epithelium with microvilli (G020:65), not simple cuboidal; its muscle layer is thin and irregularly arranged."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened gallbladder filled with dark pigment gallstones (pigment stones fit sickle cell hemolysis).","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"like":"G024-30-1","kind":"repeat","n":"12 extra 111"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":47,"label":"Pancreas acini vs liver plates and sinusoids","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic acini with centroacinar cells; diagram of centroacinar cells in the acinus","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26","lg":[["A","pancreatic acini"],["F","fibroblast (arrow)"],["arrow without a letter","centroacinar cell (pale nucleus inside the acinus)"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-46.jpg","cap":"Liver: hepatocyte plates with sinusoids; central vein with radiating plates","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 46","lg":[["a","hematoxylin and eosin"],["H","hepatocyte plates"],["S","sinusoids"],["b","silver stain for reticulin"],["R","reticulin (type III collagen) fibers"],["c","Mallory trichrome"],["C","central vein"],["arrows","sinusoids draining into the central vein"],["d","portal area, hematoxylin and eosin"],["PV","portal venule"],["HA","hepatic arteriole"],["BD","bile ductule"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-199-1","s":"What's all this then!? (identify the three micrographs)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: serous acini with centroacinar cells in the center of the acinus – exocrine pancreas; middle: central vein surrounded by columns of hepatocytes and sinusoids (liver); right: hepatocytes and sinusoids (liver)","a":null,"why":"Left: <b>exocrine pancreas</b>: serous acini with pale <b>centroacinar cells in their centers</b>. Middle: <b>liver</b>: plates of hepatocytes radiating from a <b>central vein</b>, with sinusoids between them. Right: <b>liver</b> at high power: polygonal <b>hepatocytes</b> separated by narrow <b>sinusoids</b>.<br>Common mix-up: liver shows cell plates and sinusoids, not round glandular acini around a lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-199.jpg","in":"Three H&E panels: (left) serous acini of granular pyramidal cells with pale small nuclei in the acinar centres; (middle) liver parenchyma radiating from a round empty vein; (right) high-power plates of polygonal cells separated by narrow vascular channels.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 76 (Central vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":199,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 112"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":47,"label":"Four images: triad, hepatocyte, pancreas, gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: portal vein branch, hepatic artery branch, bile duct branch","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic acinar cell electron micrograph with zymogen granules; centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27","lg":[["L","lumen of the acinus"],["S","secretory (zymogen) granules"],["G","Golgi apparatus"],["C","condensing vacuoles"],["N","nucleus (round, basal)"],["RER","rough endoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: folded mucosa, simple columnar cells with microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65","lg":[["LP","lamina propria"],["(a) arrows","simple columnar epithelium"],["M","muscularis (bracket line)"],["A","adventitia (bracket line)"],["G","mucus secretory granules"],["MV","microvilli"],["(b) arrows and arrowheads","spaces between folded lateral cell membranes (water leaves the cells here)"]]}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-140-1","s":"WHAT IS GOING ON HERE?! (identify the four images)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Top left: portal triad (hepatic artery, portal venule, bile duct); top right: hepatocytes EM (mitochondria, bile canaliculus, junctional complex); bottom left: pancreatic acinar cells (intercalated duct, rough ER, zymogen granules, centroacinar cell); bottom right: gallbladder (smooth muscle, epithelial cells, villus-like mucosal folds)","a":null,"why":"<b>Top left: portal triad</b>: big thin-walled portal venule, small thick-walled hepatic arteriole, bile duct of cuboidal cells.<br><b>Top right: hepatocytes on electron micrograph</b>: many mitochondria; a tiny bile canaliculus between two cells, sealed by junctional complexes.<br><b>Bottom left: pancreatic acinar cells</b>: basophilic base (rough endoplasmic reticulum), apical zymogen granules, pale centroacinar cells.<br><b>Bottom right: gallbladder</b>: tall simple columnar cells on branching folds that mimic villi, smooth muscle below, no glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-140.jpg","in":"Four panels: an H&E portal area with three tubes; a TEM of two adjoining hepatocytes with mitochondria and a small canaliculus between them; an H&E of basophilic acinar cells with apical granules and a small duct; an H&E of a folded mucosa of tall columnar cells over a smooth-muscle wall.","tg":["H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":140,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor","n":"12 extra 113"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The four layers of the wall","p":"","s":"The plan of the digestive tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The gut's own nervous system","p":"","s":"The plan of the digestive tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lining of the mouth","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lips","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The tongue: muscle, surfaces and tonsils","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lingual papillae","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Taste buds","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Teeth","p":"","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.6"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Lining, glands and outer coat","p":"","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The muscle changes from top to bottom","p":"","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Where the esophagus meets the stomach","p":"","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Reflux, esophagitis and metaplasia","p":"","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Barrett esophagus in the clinic, and cancer","p":"","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The overall design of the stomach","p":"","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three regions, three kinds of mucosa","p":"","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Gland cells of the body and fundus","p":"","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enteroendocrine cells, and the parietal cell at work","p":"","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Diseases of the stomach","p":"","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three levels of folding, and the crypts","p":"","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enterocytes and fat absorption","p":"","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Crypt cells: stem cells, Paneth cells and hormone cells","p":"","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Duodenum and jejunum","p":"","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Ileum: Peyer patches and M cells","p":"","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's lining","p":"","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's wall: teniae coli and diverticula","p":"","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The appendix","p":"","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Rectum and anal canal","p":"","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"One table for the whole tube","p":"","s":"Putting the tube together","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Plan of a salivary gland, and its secretory cells","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts inside the lobule: intercalated and striated","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts between lobules, and the whole path","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three glands, three kinds of saliva","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the parotid","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the submandibular and sublingual, and all three together","p":"","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Two glands in one: acini and islets","p":"","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Acinar cells and centroacinar cells","p":"","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Pancreas or parotid?","p":"","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"What the pancreas secretes, and how it protects itself","p":"","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Blood supply and the portal triad","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The classic lobule on a slide","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Hepatocytes","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Sinusoids and the space of Disse","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Kupffer cells","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ito cells, fatty liver and cirrhosis","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Bile flow","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.7"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three ways to draw a lobule, and the zones","p":"","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.8"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The gallbladder","p":"","s":"Gallbladder","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Review: the digestive glands side by side","p":"","s":"Gallbladder","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"}]};